2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iotapa#Daughter_of_King_Mithridates_III_of_Commagene
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter of King Mithridates III of Commagene
2 CONT
2 CONT Iotapa was a princess from the Kingdom of Commagene, who lived between the 1st century BC and 1st century. She was the daughter of King Mithridates III of Commagene and Queen Iotapa of Commagene and was of Armenian, Greek and Mede
2 CONC s descent.
2 CONT
2 CONT Iotapa was most probably born, raised and educated in Samosata, the capital of the Kingdom of Commagene. Her brother was Antiochus III of Commagene, whom she married. When her father died in 12 BC, her brother succeeded their fath
2 CONC er as King of Commagene.
2 CONT
2 CONT With Iotapa’s reign with Antiochus III, she was the last independent Queen of Commagene, as her brother ruled as the last independent King of Commagene. From her marriage to her brother, she had two children: a son, prince Antioch
2 CONC us IV of Commagene and a daughter, princess Iotapa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antiochus III died in 17, his death became unsettling for the kingdom. At the time of Antiochus’ death, Commagene was in political turmoil. The reasons for this development is unknown, however it appears at that time, their childr
2 CONC en were too young to succeed their father and there was no authority to prevent civil unrest and unite the citizens of Commagene.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the death of Antiochus III, two fractions had appeared. One fraction led by noblemen wanted Commagene to be placed under the rule of the Roman Empire and the another fraction led by the citizens wanted to retain the rule of t
2 CONC he King.
2 CONT
2 CONT The political fractions from Commagene peacefully had sent embassies to Rome, seeking the advice and assistance of the Roman Emperor Tiberius, to decide the future of Commagene. When the political fractions addressed the Emperor o
2 CONC f the future of Commagene, they were in line, were with the political reality of Roman rule and were prepared to live with the decision that Tiberius made. They were also prepared to live in the rule of the Roman Empire. When the 
2 CONC political fractions had sent embassies to Rome, this marked the end of the independence of Commagene.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tiberius decided to make Commagene a part of the Roman province of Syria. The decision that Tiberius made was welcomed by many citizens by Commagene, however some, particularly those who were supporters of the royal family were un
2 CONC happy with this outcome.
2 CONT
2 CONT The whereabouts of Iotapa after the annexation of Commagene are unknown. Her children were raised and lived in Rome. In 38, the Roman Emperor Caligula restored the kingdom to her children.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Iotapa=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Iotapa (born around 20 BC), a daughter of King Mithridates III of Commagene, reigned as Queen of Commagene after marrying her King brother Antiochus III.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Iotapa was a princess from the Kingdom of Commagene, who lived in the second half of the 1st century BC and the first half of the 1st century. She was the daughter of King Mithridates III of Commagene and Queen Iotapa of Commagene
2 CONC  and was of Armenian, Greek and Median descent.
2 CONT
2 CONT Iotapa was most probably born, raised and educated in Samosata, the capital of the Kingdom of Commagene. Her brother was Antiochus III of Commagene, whom she married. When her father died in 12 BC, her brother succeeded their fath
2 CONC er as King of Commagene. With Iotapa’s reign with Antiochus III, she was the last independent Queen of Commagene, as her brother ruled as the last independent King of Commagene. From her marriage to her brother, she had two childr
2 CONC en: a son, prince Antiochus IV of Commagene and a daughter, princess Iotapa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antiochus III died in 17; his death became unsettling for the kingdom. At the time of Antiochus’ death, Commagene was in political turmoil. The reasons for this development are unknown, however it appears at that time, their child
2 CONC ren were too young to succeed their father and there was no authority to prevent civil unrest and unite the citizens of Commagene. From the death of Antiochus III, two factions had appeared. One faction led by noblemen wanted Comm
2 CONC agene to be placed under the rule of the Roman Empire and the other faction led by the citizens wanted to retain the rule of the King.
2 CONT
2 CONT The political factions from Commagene peacefully had sent embassies to Rome, seeking the advice and assistance of the Roman emperor Tiberius, to decide the future of Commagene. When the political factions addressed the Emperor of 
2 CONC the future of Commagene, they were in line, were with the political reality of Roman rule and were prepared to live with the decision that Tiberius made. They were also prepared to live in the rule of the Roman Empire. When the po
2 CONC litical factions had sent embassies to Rome, this marked the end of the independence of Commagene.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tiberius decided to make Commagene a part of the Roman province of Syria. The decision that Tiberius made was welcomed by many citizens by Commagene, however some, particularly those who were supporters of the royal family were un
2 CONC happy with this outcome. The whereabouts of Iotapa after the annexation of Commagene are unknown. Her children were raised and lived in Rome. In 38, the Roman Emperor Caligula restored the kingdom to her children.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iotapa_(spouse_of_Antiochus_III)
