1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Berenice was the daughter of Salome I, sister of Herod the Great. She married her cousin Aristobulus, who was executed by his father in 6 BC. She was accused of complicity in his murder.
2 CONT
2 CONT By Aristobulus she was the mother of Herod Agrippa I, Herod III, king of Chalkis, Herodias, Mariamne III and Aristobulus Minor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her second husband, Theudion (brother of Herod I's first wife Doris, and thus uncle of Antipater), was put to death for conspiring against Herod. Subsequently she went to Rome and enjoyed the favor of the imperial household.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources===
2 CONT Josephus, Antiq. Jud.,16,2
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berenice_(daughter_of_Salome)
2 CONT
2 CONT >>><nowiki>--------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT Wagner, Sir Anthony Richard; ‘Pedigree and Progress: Essays in the Genealogical Interpretation of History’ puts her dates as: b c 31 BCE - d 29, Rome, Roma, Italy [http://www.geni.com/discussions/107300?msg=783322  See Discussion 
2 CONC Requesting Sources for the 17 BCE birthdate.]
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>_______________
2 CONT
2 CONT Berenice (I) was the daughter of SALOME (I) and her second husband, Costobarus. Sometime after 17 B.C.E. she married Aristobulus I, Herod’s second son by Mariamme the Hasmonean. According to Josephus, this was not a happy marriage
2 CONC , since Berenice assisted her mother in spying on her husband. By inference we may learn that after the execution of her husband in 7 B.C.E. she moved with her family to Rome, where she found a congenial atmosphere for her childre
2 CONC n’s upbringing within the imperial court. Her special friend was Antonia, Mark Anthony’s daughter and the future Emperor Claudius’ mother (Ant. 18:164). This is perhaps the reason why Strabo, the geographer and historian, found it
2 CONC  necessary to mention her in passing (Geographic 16:46). http://jwa.org/encyclopedia/article/herodian-women
2 CONT
