2 CONT ==Herod "the Great" or Herod  "the Impious"==
2 CONT
2 CONT King Herod was known to the Romans as "the Great", but in the eyes of the people over whom he ruled he was always known as "the Impious", despite his costly restoration of the Temple in Jerusalem. Herod was a Roman citizen, Govern
2 CONC or of Galilee by 47 BC, and then King of Judea from 37 to 4 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was one of the major figures in politics of Palestine in the early years of the Roman Empire. In 7 BC he strangled to death two of his sons, Aristobulus and Alexander, drawing a comment from Roman Emperor Augustus (27 BC-14 AD)
2 CONC  that it was safer to be one of Herod’s pigs than one of his sons. Another son was later born to Herod and, for his safety, his mother dispatched him to the care of her family in Ariminum, a city near Ravenna in northern Italy.31 
2 CONC He was Prince Joseph, the Joseph of Arimathea in the Gospels, and he later became the unseen power behind his father’s throne. 
2 CONT
2 CONT __________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.dailymail.co.uk/sciencetech/article-2880605/Entrance-King-Herod-Great-s-palace-unearthed-Complex-set-corridors-leads-elaborate-hall-ruler.html  _(2014)_
2 CONT
2 CONT __________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herod_the_Great#Marriages_and_descendants  '''Herod the Great'''] (born 73 or 74 BCE, died 4 BCE in Jericho, was an Edomite (a Semitic group from the region between the Dead Sea and the Gulf of Aqaba)
2 CONC  client king of the Roman provinces of Judea, Galilee and Samaria (near present-day [Nablus]). Important details of his biography are gleaned from the works of the 1st century CE Roman-Jewish historian Josephus Flavius.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was described as "a madman who murdered his own family and a great many rabbis."  He is also known for his colossal building projects in Jerusalem and elsewhere, including the rebuilding of the Second Temple in Jerusalem (somet
2 CONC imes referred to as Herod's Temple).
2 CONT
2 CONT Herod the Great appears in the Gospel according to Matthew (Ch. 2), which describes an event known as the Massacre of the Innocents.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to this account, shortly after the birth of Jesus, Magi from the East visited Herod to inquire the whereabouts of "the one having been born king of the Jews", because they had seen his star in the east and therefore want
2 CONC ed to pay him homage. Herod, as King of the Jews, was alarmed at the prospect of a usurper. Herod assembled the chief priests and scribes of the people and asked them where the "Anointed One" (the Messiah, Greek: Ο Χριστός (ho chr
2 CONC istos)) was to be born. They answered, in Bethlehem, citing Micah 5:2.
2 CONT
2 CONT Herod therefore sent the Magi to Bethlehem, instructing them to search for the child and, after they had found him, to "report to me, so that I too may go and worship him". However, after they had found Jesus, the Magi were warned
2 CONC  in a dream not to report back to Herod.
2 CONT
2 CONT Similarly, Joseph was warned in a dream that Herod intended to kill Jesus, so he and his family fled to Egypt. When Herod realized he had been outwitted by the Magi, he gave orders to kill all boys of the age of two and under in B
2 CONC ethlehem and its vicinity. Joseph and his family stayed in Egypt until Herod's death, then moved to Nazareth in Galilee in order to avoid living under Herod's son Archelaus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Regarding the Massacre of the Innocents, although Herod was certainly guilty of many brutal acts, including the killing of his wife and two of his sons, no other known source from the period makes any reference to such a massacre.
2 CONC  Since Bethlehem was a small village, the number of male children under the age of 2, would probably not exceed 20. This may be the reason for the lack of other sources for this history, although Herod's order in Matthew 2:16 incl
2 CONC udes those children in Bethlehem's vicinity making the massacre larger numerically and geographically.
2 CONT
