1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Cleopatra of Jerusalem was a woman who lived in the 1st century BC during the Roman Empire. She is remembered as one of the wives of King of Judea Herod the Great.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a possibility that Cleopatra could have been a daughter of a local noble from Jerusalem. She was born and raised in the city and could have been of Jewish or Edomite-Phoenician origins but was not related to the Hasmonaea
2 CONC n Dynasty (please cite the source of this information). Cleopatra was called Cleopatra of Jerusalem, to distinguish her from the Ptolemaic Greek Queen [https://www.geni.com/people/Cleopatra-VII-Philopator-Pharaoh-of-Egypt/60000000
2 CONC 01336815278 Cleopatra VII of Egypt].
2 CONT
2 CONT >However, it is also possible that Cleopatra of Jerusalem really was Queen Cleopatra VII of Egypt. Josephus mentions "Cleopatra of Jeruslaem" twice: once in Antiquities 17.1.3 and once in War 1.28.4. It is interesting to note that
2 CONC  according to Josephus, Cleopatra VII and King Herod became intimate, and that Cleopatra "upon the whole, seemed overcome with love for him (King Herod),"(Antiquities 15.4.2). Herod is said to have had a son named Philip with Cleo
2 CONC patra of Jerusalem while it is believed that Cleopatra had a third child with Marc Anthony also named Philip. It is during Marc Anthony's absence that Cleopatra, according to Josephus, spent a great deal of time with King Herod. T
2 CONC he dates seem to make this unlikely [http://www.geni.com/discussions/108133 Please contribute any thoughts on this possibility here.]
2 CONT
2 CONT She had married King Herod the Great in 25 BC (please cite the source of this date). Herod most probably married her as a part of a political alliance.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cleopatra bore Herod two sons who were:
2 CONT *Herod (b. 24 BC/23 BC), of which very little is known. (Please site the source of this information.)
2 CONT *Herod Philip II (b. 22 BC/21 BC - 34) who later became the Tetrarch of Ituraea and Trachonitis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cleopatra’s children by Herod were raised and educated in Rome. After the death of her husband in 4 BC, her second son inherited some of his father’s dominion and ruled as a Roman client king until his death in 34. Cleopatra becam
2 CONC e the mother-in-law of Philip’s wife and niece Salome. Philip and Salome had no children.
2 CONT ===Sources===
2 CONT
2 CONT *Josephus: Antiquities 15.4.2
2 CONT *Josephus: War 1.28.4
2 CONT *http://virtualreligion.net/iho/herod2.html
2 CONT *http://www.historyofthedaughters.com/69.pdf
2 CONT *http://www.livius.org/he-hg/herodians/herod_the_great02.html
2 CONT *http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/view.jsp?artid=537&letter=C
2 CONT *http://books.google.com.au/books?id=Qc-0KWxP3kAC&pg=PA235&lpg=PA235&dq=Cleopatra+of+Jerusalem&source=web&ots=FvTOVlpR3h&sig=Ps5PdYwErG6PFJiAMzZ7OL-3s7E&hl=en&sa=X&oi=book_result&resnum=6&ct=result#v=onepage&q=Cleopatra%20of%20Jer
2 CONC usalem&f=false
2 CONT *http://books.google.com.au/books?id=wCa-DjDJkT0C&pg=PA180&lpg=PA180&dq=Cleopatra+of+Jerusalem&source=web&ots=VxV5ccBwTP&sig=AwJn7ECtDkF7a0KTzmIRt7dYIvQ&hl=en&sa=X&oi=book_result&resnum=5&ct=result#v=onepage&q=Cleopatra%20of%20Jer
2 CONC usalem&f=false
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cleopatra_of_Jerusalem
