2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Juba II (Yuba in Berber, Iuba in Latin; Ιóβας (Ιóβα) or Ιουβας in Greek)[1] or Juba II of Numidia (52 BC/50 BC-23) was a king of Numidia and then later moved to Mauretania. His first wife was Cleopatra Selene II, the last Ptolemai
2 CONC c Monarch and daughter to Greek Ptolemaic Queen Cleopatra VII of Egypt and Roman triumvir Mark Antony.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Early Life
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Restored to the Throne
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Mauretania
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Marriages and Children
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 Author 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.1 Natural History
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 References
2 CONT
2 CONT 7 Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Early Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Juba II was the only child and heir to King Juba I of Numidia. His mother is unknown. In 46 BC, his father committed suicide as he was defeated by Julius Caesar (in Thapsus, North Africa) and Numidia became a Roman Province.[1] Hi
2 CONC s father was an ally to the Roman General Pompey. Juba II claimed to be a descendant to the sister of General Hannibal (Scol. Lucan, Pharsalia 8.287).
2 CONT
2 CONT Juba II was brought to Rome by Julius Caesar and took part in Caesar’s triumphal procession. In Rome, he learned Latin and Greek, became romanized and was granted Roman citizenship.[1] Through dedication to his studies, he is said
2 CONC  to have become one of Rome's best educated citizens, and by age 20 he wrote one of his first works entitled Roman Archaeology.[1] He was raised by Julius Caesar and later by his great-nephew Octavius (future Emperor Caesar August
2 CONC us). Juba II while growing up, accompanied Octavius on military campaigns, gaining valuable experience as a leader. He fought alongside Octavius in the battle of Actium in 31 BC. Throughout the years, Juba II and Octavius became l
2 CONC ifelong friends.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Restored to the Throne
2 CONT
2 CONT Coin of Juba II.Augustus restored Juba II as the king of Numidia between 29 BC-27 BC. Juba II established Numidia as an ally of Rome. Juba II would become one of the most loyal client kings that served Rome. Between 26 BC-20 BC, A
2 CONC ugustus arranged for him to marry Cleopatra Selene II, giving her a large dowry and appointing her queen. Juba II and Cleopatra did not enjoy a long rule over Numidia. It was probably due to his services with Augustus in a campaig
2 CONC n in Spain that led Augustus to make him King of Mauretania.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Mauretania
2 CONT
2 CONT When they moved to Mauretania, they renamed their new capital to Caesaria (modern Cherchell, Algeria). The city was named in honor of Augustus. The construction and sculpture projects at Caesaria and another city Volubilis, displa
2 CONC y a rich mixture of Egyptian, Greek and Roman architectural styles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cleopatra is said to have exerted considerable influence on Juba II's policies. Juba II encouraged and supported the performing arts, research of the sciences and research of natural history. Juba II also supported Mauretanian tra
2 CONC de. The Kingdom of Mauretania was of great importance to the Roman Empire. Mauretania traded all over the Mediterranean, particularly with Spain and Italy. Mauretania exported fish, grapes, pearls, figs, grain, wooden furniture an
2 CONC d purple dye harvested from certain shellfish, which was used in the manufacture of purple stripes for senatorial robes. Juba II sent a contingent to Iles Purpuraires to re-establish the ancient Phoenician dye manufacturing proces
2 CONC s.[3] Tingis, a town at the Pillars of Hercules (modern Strait of Gibraltar) became a major trade centre. In Gades, (modern Cádiz) and Carthago Nova (modern Cartagena) Spain, Juba II was appointed by Augustus as an honorary Duovir
2 CONC . A Duovir was a chief magistrate of a Roman colony or town, most probably involving with trade and was also a Patronus Colonaie.
2 CONT
2 CONT The value and quality of Mauretanian coins became distinguished. The Greek historian Plutarch describes him as 'one of the most gifted rulers of his time'. Between 2 BC-2, he travelled with Gaius Caesar (a grandson of Augustus), a
2 CONC s a member of his advisory staff to the troubled Eastern Mediterranean. In 21, Juba II made his son Ptolemy co-ruler and Juba II died in 23. Juba II was buried alongside his first wife in the Royal Mausoleum of Mauretania. Ptolemy
2 CONC  then became the sole ruler of Mauretania.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriages and Children
2 CONT
2 CONT First marriage to Greek Ptolemaic princess Cleopatra Selene II (40 BC-6). Their children were Ptolemy of Mauretania (1 BC-40) and Drusilla of Mauretania (born 5).
2 CONT
2 CONT Second marriage to princess of Cappadocia Glaphyra. Glaphyra’s first husband was prince Alexandros (a son of King of Judea Herod the Great), Alexandros was executed in 7 BC. Glaphyra married Juba II either in 6 or 7. Glaphyra fell
2 CONC  in love with the tetrarch of Judea Herod Archelaus (another son of Herod the Great). Glaphyra ended her brief marriage to Juba II and divorced him to marry Herod Archelaus. Juba II had no children with Glaphyra.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Author
2 CONT
2 CONT Juba II wrote a number of books in Greek and Latin on history, natural history, geography, grammar, painting and theatre. His guide to Arabia became a bestseller in Rome. Only fragments of his work survived. He collected a substan
2 CONC tial library on a wide variety of topics, which no doubt complemented his own prolific output. Pliny the Elder refers to him as an authority 65 times in the Natural History and in Athens, a monument was built in recognition of his
2 CONC  writings. His writings are published and translated in Roller: Scholarly Kings (Chicago 2004).
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Natural History
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Pliny the Younger, Juba II sent an expedition to the Canary Islands and Madeira.[4] Juba II had given the Canary Islands that name because he found particularly ferocious dogs (canarius - from canis - meaning of the d
2 CONC ogs in latin) on the island.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is also known to have written a book about a Canarian spurge which he named Euphorbia after his personal physician. It was later called Euphorbia regis-jubae (‘King Juba's euphorbia’) in his honor (it is now Euphorbia obtusifol
2 CONC ia ssp. regis-jubae). The palm tree genus Jubaea was also named after him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juba_II
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Juba II n.52Ac -26DC   Rey de Numibia Cleopatra influenció a Juba II para ejercer una buena política de gobierno. Juba II fomentó y apoyó las artes escénicas, la investigación científica y el conocimiento de la historia natural, y
2 CONC  fomentando el comercio mauritano. Fue un erudito, estudioso de la Gramatica , Artes y  Letras, incluso Historia escribiendo varios textos. Según Plutarco;  fue  uno de los  grandes  gobernantes  de  su tiempo, Mauritania comerció
2 CONC   con toda la polis  el  Mar Mediterráneo. Con Hispania y Roma exportando Garam,pescado, uvas, perlas, higos, grano, madera para muebles y tinte púrpura para las vestiduras de senadores romanos y patricios. Envió expediciones  a l
2 CONC as Isla Canarias e hizo expediciones  a  los Atlas. Falleció el 26 DC..
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Juba II=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Juba II (Berber: Yuba, ⵢⵓⴱⴰ; Iuba in Latin; Ancient Greek: Ἰóβας, Ἰóβα or Ἰούβας) or Juba II of Numidia (52/50 BC-AD 23) was a king of Numidia and then later moved to Mauretania. His first wife was Cleopatra Selene II, daughter of
2 CONC  Greek Ptolemaic Queen Cleopatra VII of Egypt and Roman triumvir Mark Antony.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Early life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Juba II was an Ancient Libyan prince from North Africa. He was the only child and heir of King Juba I of Numidia; his mother's identity is unknown. In 46 BC, his father was defeated by Julius Caesar (in Thapsus, North Africa). Num
2 CONC idia became a Roman Province. His father had been an ally of the Roman General Pompey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Juba II was brought to Rome by Julius Caesar and he took part in Caesar’s triumphal procession. In Rome he learned Latin and Greek, became romanized and was granted Roman citizenship.[1] Through dedication to his studies, he is sa
2 CONC id to have become one of Rome's best educated citizens, and by age 20 he wrote one of his first works entitled Roman Archaeology. He was raised by Julius Caesar and later by his great-nephew Octavian (future Emperor Caesar Augustu
2 CONC s). While growing up, Juba II accompanied Octavian on military campaigns, gaining valuable experience as a leader. He fought alongside Octavian in the battle of Actium in 31 BC. They became longtime friends.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Restored to the throne'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Augustus restored Juba II as the king of Numidia between 29-27 BC. Juba II established Numidia as an ally of Rome. Juba II would become one of the most loyal client kings that served Rome. Probably due to his services with Augustu
2 CONC s in a campaign in present-day Spain, between 26 BC and 20 BC Augustus arranged for him to marry Cleopatra Selene II, giving her a large dowry and appointing her queen.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Mauretania'''
2 CONT
2 CONT When Juba II and his queen moved to Mauretania, they renamed their new capital Caesaria (modern Cherchell, Algeria), in honor of Augustus. The construction and sculpture projects at Caesaria and another city, Volubilis, display a 
2 CONC rich mixture of Egyptian, Greek and Roman architectural styles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cleopatra is said to have exerted considerable influence on Juba II's policies. Juba II encouraged and supported the performing arts, research of the sciences and research of natural history. Juba II also supported Mauretanian tra
2 CONC de. The Kingdom of Mauretania was of great importance to the Roman Empire. Mauretania traded all over the Mediterranean, particularly with Spain and Italy. Mauretania exported fish, grapes, pearls, figs, grain, wooden furniture an
2 CONC d purple dye harvested from certain shellfish, which was used in the manufacture of purple stripes for senatorial robes. Juba II sent a contingent to Iles Purpuraires to re-establish the ancient Phoenician dye manufacturing proces
2 CONC s.[3] Tingis (modern Tangier), a town at the Pillars of Hercules (modern Strait of Gibraltar) became a major trade centre. In Gades, (modern Cádiz) and Carthago Nova (modern Cartagena) Spain, Juba II was appointed by Augustus as a
2 CONC n honorary Duovir (a chief magistrate of a Roman colony or town), probably involving trade, and was also a Patronus Colonaie.
2 CONT
2 CONT The value and quality of Mauretanian coins became distinguished. The Greek historian Plutarch describes him as 'one of the most gifted rulers of his time'. Between 2 BC-AD 2, he travelled with Gaius Caesar (a grandson of Augustus)
2 CONC , as a member of his advisory staff to the troubled Eastern Mediterranean. In 21, Juba II made his son Ptolemy co-ruler and Juba II died in 23. Juba II was buried alongside his first wife in the Royal Mausoleum of Mauretania. Ptol
2 CONC emy then became the sole ruler of Mauretania.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriages and children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT First marriage to Greek Ptolemaic princess Cleopatra Selene II (40 BC – 6 AD). Their children were:
2 CONT * Ptolemy of Mauretania born in ca 10 BC – 5 BC
2 CONT * A daughter of Cleopatra and Juba, whose name has not been recorded, is mentioned in an inscription. It has been suggested that Drusilla of Mauretania was that daughter, but she may have been a granddaughter instead. Drusilla is 
2 CONC described as a granddaughter of Antony and Cleopatra, or may have been a daughter of Ptolemy of Mauretania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Second marriage to Glaphyra, a princess of Cappadocia, and widow of Alexander, son of Herod the Great. Alexander was executed in 7 BC for conspiracy against his father. Glaphyra married Juba II in 6 AD or 7 AD. She then fell in lo
2 CONC ve with Herod Archelaus, another son of Herod the Great and Ethnarch of Judea. Glaphyra divorced Juba to marry him in 7 AD. Juba had no children with Glaphyra.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Author'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Juba wrote a number of books in Greek and Latin on history, natural history, geography, grammar, painting and theatre. His guide to Arabia became a bestseller in Rome. Only fragments of his work survived. He collected a substantia
2 CONC l library on a wide variety of topics, which no doubt complemented his own prolific output. Pliny the Elder refers to him as an authority 65 times in the Natural History and in Athens, a monument was built in recognition of his wr
2 CONC itings. His writings are published and translated in Roller: Scholarly Kings (Chicago 2004).
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Natural history''
2 CONT
2 CONT Juba II was a noted patron of the arts and sciences and sponsored several expeditions and biological research. He also was a notable author, writing several scholarly and popular scientific works such as treatises on natural histo
2 CONC ry or a best-selling traveller's guide to Arabia.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Pliny the Younger, Juba II sent an expedition to the Canary Islands and Madeira. Juba II had given the Canary Islands that name because he found particularly ferocious dogs (canarius – from canis – meaning of the dogs
2 CONC  in Latin) on the island.
2 CONT
2 CONT Juba's Greek physician Euphorbus wrote that a succulent spurge found in the High Atlas was a powerful laxative. In 12 BC, Juba named this plant Euphorbia after Euphorbus, in response to Augustus dedicating a statue to Antonius Mus
2 CONC a, Augustus's own personal physician and Euphorbus' brother.[6] Botanist and taxonomist Carl Linnaeus assigned the name Euphorbia to the entire genus in the physician's honor. Euphorbia was later called Euphorbia regisjubae ("King
2 CONC  Juba's euphorbia") to honor the king's contributions to natural history and his role in bringing the genus to notice. It is now Euphorbia obtusifolia ssp. regis-jubae. The palm tree genus Jubaea is also named after Juba.
2 CONT
2 CONT Flavius Philostratus recalled one of his anecdotes: "And I have read in the discourse of Juba that elephants assist one another when they are being hunted, and that they will defend one that is exhausted, and if they can remove hi
2 CONC m out of danger, they anoint his wounds with the tears of the aloe tree, standing round him like physicians."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In fiction'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Juba is a lead character in Michael Livingston's 2015 historical fantasy novel The Shards of Heaven.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
