1 NOTE Demetrius I (of Syria), called Soter (circa 187-150 bc), king of Syria 
2 CONT (162-150 bc), son of Seleucus IV Philopator (222?-175 bc). Held a hostage 
2 CONT in Rome during the reigns of his father and of his uncle, Antiochus IV 
2 CONT Epiphanes, he escaped in 163 bc and deposed and killed his cousin, 
2 CONT Antiochus V Eupator (173-162 bc), who had occupied the Syrian throne on 
2 CONT the death of his father, Antiochus IV. The reign of Demetrius I was marked 
2 CONT by warfare against the Jews, who were then ruled by the Maccabees, against 
2 CONT the Cappadocians, and against the Medes. He was named Soter (Greek, 
2 CONT “Savior”) because he liberated the Babylonians from the Median satrap 
2 CONT Timarchus (died about 160 bc). Demetrius I was killed in battle while 
2 CONT resisting the usurpation of Alexander Balas (reigned 150-145 bc), a youth 
2 CONT from Smyrna pretending to be the son of Antiochus IV Epiphanes and backed 
2 CONT by Rome and Syria's neighbors.
