1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Seleucus IV Philopator (Greek: Σέλευκος Δ' Φιλοπάτωρ), ruler of the Hellenistic Seleucid Empire, reigned from 187 BC to 175 BC over a realm consisting of Syria (now including Cilicia and Judea), Mesopotamia, Babylo
2 CONC nia and Nearer Iran (Media and Persia).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was compelled by financial necessities, created in part by the heavy war-indemnity exacted by Rome, to pursue an ambitious policy. In an effort to collect money to pay the Romans, he sent his minister Heliodorus to Jerusalem to
2 CONC  seize the temple treasury. On his return, Heliodorus assassinated Seleucus, and seized the throne for himself.
2 CONT
2 CONT The true heir Demetrius, son of Seleucus, now being retained in Rome as a hostage, the kingdom was seized by the younger brother of Seleucus, Antiochus IV Epiphanes, who managed to oust Heliodorus, even though an infant son, also 
2 CONC named Antiochus, was formal head of state for a few years until Epiphanes had him murdered.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seleucus_IV_Philopator
