1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Avidius Cassius
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Avidius Cassius 
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor of the Roman Empire 
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign April to July 175 
2 CONT
2 CONT Full name Gaius Avidius Cassius 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born c. 130 
2 CONT
2 CONT Birthplace Cyrrhus, Syria 
2 CONT
2 CONT Died July 175 
2 CONT
2 CONT Place of death Egypt / Syria 
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor Marcus Aurelius 
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor Marcus Aurelius 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife Volusia Vettia Maeciana 
2 CONT
2 CONT Father Gaius Avidius Heliodorus 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother Julia Cassia Alexandra 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children 
2 CONT
2 CONT -1. Avidius Heliodorus, 
2 CONT
2 CONT -2. Avidius Maecianus, 
2 CONT
2 CONT -3. Avidia Alexandra, 
2 CONT
2 CONT -4. Volusia Laodice 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT A native of Cyrrhus, Syria, he was the son of Gaius Avidius Heliodorus, a noted orator who was Prefect of Egypt from 137 to 142 under Hadrian, and wife Junia Cassia Alexandra.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Life and Career
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a distinguished military career under Marcus Aurelius during the Parthian War, as the Legatus (General) of Legio III Gallica, capturing Seleucia and Ctesiphon, and managed to enter the Senate. He became Consul Suffectus in 
2 CONC 160 and sometime between 161 and 163 and Governor of Syria in 166 and suppressed a revolt of the Bucoli in Egypt that had broken out in 172.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Emperor
2 CONT
2 CONT In 175 he was proclaimed Roman Emperor after the premature news of the death of Marcus Aurelius; the sources also indicate he was encouraged by Marcus's wife Faustina, who was concerned about her husband's ill health, believing hi
2 CONC m to be on the verge of death, and felt the need for Cassius to act as a protector in this event, since her son Commodus was still young (13). The evidence, including Marcus's own Meditations, supports the idea that Marcus was ind
2 CONC eed quite sick, but contrary to the report that reached Cassius, he had not died. Cassius chose to continue his revolt even after he learned that Marcus was still alive, however.
2 CONT
2 CONT At first, according to Cassius Dio, Marcus, who was on campaign against tribes in the north, tried to keep the rebellion a secret from his soldiers, but after the news had spread among them, he addressed them. In this speech that 
2 CONC Dio attributes to Marcus, he laments the disloyalty of "a dearest friend", while at the same time expressing his hope that Cassius would not be killed or commit suicide, so that he could show mercy. The Senate declared Cassius a p
2 CONC ublic enemy.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is known that Cassius was recognized as emperor by May 3, since a document of that date is recorded as being in the first year of Cassius's reign. The beginning of his rebellion have been in April 175.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although he seized control of some of the most vital parts of the Roman east — Egypt being an important source of grain for the city of Rome — Cassius failed to find widespread support for his rebellion. The governor of Cappadocia
2 CONC , Martius Verus, remained loyal to Marcus Aurelius. Clearly Marcus was in a stronger position, with many more legions available to him than were available to Cassius. "After a dream of empire lasting three months and six days", Ca
2 CONC ssius was murdered by a centurion; his head was sent to Marcus, who refused to see it and ordered it buried.
2 CONT
2 CONT The events of his life are known from Cassius Dio's Roman History, and the Historia Augusta.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through his maternal great-great grandmother Junia Lepida (a great grandmother to Cassia Alexandria), Avidius Cassius was a direct descendant (sixth great-grandson) of the first Roman emperor Augustus Caesar, and thus the Julian b
2 CONC loodline would carry on through Avidius' children.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage and Children
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Volusia Vettia or Volusia Maeciana (c. 135 – aft. 175), daughter of Lucius Volusius Maecianus and wife, and had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT -1.)... Avidius Heliodorus
2 CONT
2 CONT -2.)... Avidius Maecianus
2 CONT
2 CONT -3. Avidia Alexandra
2 CONT
2 CONT -4. Volusia Laodice, born c. 165, married to Quintus Tineius Sacerdos
2 CONT
