1 OCCU Ruler of the Roman Empire, Roman Dictator, (technically not Emperor but sometimes called 1st EMPEROR of Rome), Kejsare, Dictator of Rome (This is THE Julius Caesar immortalized by William Shakespeare, Eerste keizer van Rome (Caesa
2 CONC r)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ides_of_March  '''Ides of March]
2 CONT *[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assassination_of_Julius_Caesar  '''Assassination of Julius Caesar]
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julius_Caesar  '''Gaius Julius Caesar] ( July 100 BC – 15 March 44 BC ) Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julius_Caesar   '''Gaius Julius Caesar'''], one of the most influential political and military leaders in history,  helped establish the vast empire ruled by Rome. Caesar’s triumph in a civil war in t
2 CONC he 40s bc made him the absolute ruler of Rome, but political jealousies among his opponents motivated them to assassinate him.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Julius Caesar was born in Rome on July 12, 100 Bc, and he was assassinated on the ides of March, the 15th of March, in 44Bc. He was born to Gaius Julius Caesar and Aurelia putting him in probably the most prestigious and strong ro
2 CONC oted clans, the Julian clan. His uncle by marriage (oxford classical, Hornblower p.925) was the famous military leader and seven times consul, Gaius Marius and in an effort to keep Julius from becoming a great man in the history o
2 CONC f Rome, Marius appointed him flamen diales, or priest of Jupiter. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As a young man, Caesar distinguished himself in roman society. He could declaim and recite poems very well. He also wrote poetry and became a favorite among the women of Rome. His first wife was Cornelia whom he married early. She
2 CONC  was the daughter of Lucius Cornelius Cinna , therefore, Caesar was associated with the popular party in his early political career. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At first life was great for him in Rome; he was young, well liked, and he had his wife, but soon Caesar heard of Sulla's hostility toward him and he fled to Bythinia, under orders from the praetor of Asia, where he sought to raise
2 CONC  a fleet under King Nicomedes, which was his first military campaign, in 81Bc (Caesars-might and madness-Brownjohn p. 48). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Later Caesar's enemies accused him of giving in to Nicomedes's unnatural desires. In an effort to disprove these rumors Caesar allegedly tried to have an affair with every patrician woman in Rome. Gaius Suetonius Tranquillius wrot
2 CONC e, "Not even in the provinces were married women safe from him." 
2 CONT
2 CONT When he left Asia, pirates captured him and demanded a ransom of 20 talents (Ceasars-might and madness p. 49). Arrogant and confident, Caesar mocked them and he laughed at their demands. He told them that they did not know whom th
2 CONC ey had caught and told them his ransom should have been much more. Caesar manipulated the pirates and he began to have so much control over them that they would stop talking or making noise upon his demands if he was trying to sle
2 CONC ep or read. He warned these pirates that when freed, he would find them and kill them. They should have heeded his warning, because upon his release, Caesar gathered men and went to find the pirates. After defeating them he crucif
2 CONC ied the pirates.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Julius Caesar'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruthlessly ambitious, Julius Caesar used war, intrigue and political guile to make himself the most powerful man in Rome. Too powerful for some. Find out the facts about his rise and fall.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Roman Republic'''
2 CONT
2 CONT When Caesar was born in 100BC, Rome ruled much of the Mediterranean. It was a Republic ruled by officials called magistrates (the most senior of which were two consuls) who were elected by assemblies of the people. Magistrates hel
2 CONC d office for one year before joining Rome's powerful advisory council, the Senate. Caesar would later fight these institutions to become dictator of Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Rise to power'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Hailing from a prominent family, Caesar quickly rose to political power. He was elected into many public offices and, in 63BC, bribed his way to become Pontifex Maximus (high priest). He financed himself by plundering Rome's Spani
2 CONC sh provinces.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Caesar as consul'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Popular with the army and the people, Caesar was elected joint consul with Bibulus in 60BC. But he had many enemies in the Senate, including the orators Cato the Younger and Cicero, who feared his growing strength. Caesar sideline
2 CONC d Bibulus and took steps to limit the power of the Senate.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Triumvirate'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 59BC, Caesar formed a coalition (Triumvirate) with two important Roman citizens: Crassus, a rich banker and Pompey, Rome's leading general. They controlled Rome's public affairs and divided the provinces between them.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Growing power'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Triumvirate gave Caesar the Roman provinces in northern Europe and several legions. Between 58 and 50BC, Caesar enlarged his powerbase by conquering Gaul (much of modern France and Belgium). He even invaded Britain twice.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Dictator'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Triumvirate ended when Crassus was killed fighting the Parthians in the east. In 50BC, the Senate, with support from Pompey, demanded that Caesar return to Rome without his army and surrender his office. Caesar, fearing that h
2 CONC e would be put on trial, invaded Italy, defeated Pompey and the Senate, and became sole ruler.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Murder'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Caesar's problem was that he became too powerful, alienating men who previously had a share of power. On 15 March, 44BC, a group of Republicans stabbed Caesar to death in the Senate.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Legacy'''
2 CONT
2 CONT By concentrating the power of the Republic in one man, Caesar opened the way for the creation of the Roman Empire ruled by an emperor. The first of these was his adopted successor, Octavian, in 31BC
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>''Info from http://www.genealogy4u.com/genealogy/getperson.php?personID=I52733&tree=western2007
2 CONT ''
2 CONT
2 CONT Bio below.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Parents
2 CONT
2 CONT -Father Gaius Julius Caesar the Elder
2 CONT
2 CONT -Mother Aurelia (related to the Aurelia Cottae)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sisters
2 CONT
2 CONT -Julia Caesaris "Maior" (the elder)
2 CONT
2 CONT -Julia Caesaris "Minor" (the younger)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Wives
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''First marriage to''''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT -Cornelia Cinnilla, from 83 BC until her death in childbirth in 69 or 68 BC
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Second marriage to''''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT -Pompeia, from 67 BC until he divorced her around 61 BC
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Third marriage to''''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT -Calpurnia Pisonis, from 59 BC until Caesar's death
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Children
2 CONT
2 CONT -1. Julia with Cornelia Cinnilla, born in 83 or 82 BC
2 CONT
2 CONT -2. Caesarion, with Cleopatra VII, born 47 BC. He was killed at age 17 by Caesar's adopted son Octavianus.
2 CONT
2 CONT -adopted: Gaius Julius Caesar Octavianus, his great-nephew by blood, who later became Emperor Augustus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcus Junius Brutus: The historian Plutarch notes that Caesar believed Brutus to have been his illegitimate son, as his mother Servilia had been Caesar's lover during their youth.[128]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Grandchildren
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandson from Julia and Pompey, dead at several days, unnamed.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Lovers
2 CONT
2 CONT Cleopatra VII
2 CONT
2 CONT Servilia Caepionis mother of Brutus
2 CONT
2 CONT Eunoë, queen of Mauretania and wife of Bogudes
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notable relatives
2 CONT
2 CONT Gaius Marius (married to his Aunt Julia)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mark Antony
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius Julius Caesar
2 CONT
2 CONT Julius Sabinus, a Gaul of the Lingones at the time of the Batavian rebellion of AD 69, claimed to be the great-grandson of Caesar on the grounds that his great-grandmother had been Caesar's lover during the Gallic war.
2 CONT
