1 NOTE Ptolemy II (309-246 bc), called Ptolemy Philadelphus (brotherly), king of 
2 CONT Egypt (285-246 bc), the son of Ptolemy I by Berenice I (died before 283 
2 CONT bc). His wars with the Seleucid king Antiochus I established Ptolemaic 
2 CONT Egypt as the dominant maritime power in the eastern Mediterranean Sea. The 
2 CONT economy of the country was brought under government control and the 
2 CONT cultural life at the Alexandrian court flourished under Ptolemy II; the 
2 CONT Greek poets Callimachus and Theocritus were among the literary figures 
2 CONT connected with the court. Ptolemy increased the number of books in the 
2 CONT Alexandrian library and was an active patron of literature and 
2 CONT scholarship.
