2 CONT =Mamercus Aemilius Lepidus Livianus=
2 CONT '''Mamercus Aemilius Lepidus Livianus, Consul 77 BC''' and possibly Princeps Senatus was a well connected and influential figure in Late Republican politics. Brother of Marcus Livius Drusus (tribune) and son of '''Marcus Livius Dr
2 CONC usus (censor)''', he was adopted into the Aemilii Lepidi. He married '''Cornelia Sulla''', daughter of Lucius Cornelius Sulla, and may have been (the evidence is inconclusive) princeps senatus in the Sullan senate. He was consul i
2 CONC n 77 BC, the year after Sulla's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lepidus Livianus appears in Colleen McCullough's novel The Grass Crown (novel) and its sequels.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Mamercus Aemilius Lepidus Livianus=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Mamercus Aemilius Lepidus Livianus, (died c. 62 BC) was a Roman politician and military commander who was consul in 77 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Livianus was a well connected and influential figure in Late Republican politics. A member of the aristocratic party, brother of the tribune Marcus Livius Drusus and son of Marcus Livius Drusus, he was adopted into the Aemilii Lep
2 CONC idi. His influence was such that he was able to intercede with Lucius Cornelius Sulla on the young Julius Caesar's behalf, getting Sulla to spare Caesar's life. He was also married to Cornelia Sulla, Sulla's daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 91 BC he succeeded his brother Marcus Livius Drusus as one of the pontifices in the College of Pontiffs. He served with distinction in the Social War (91–88 BC), probably serving as legate under Quintus Caecilius Metellus P
2 CONC ius. He achieved some victories, most notably he was credited with killing the general of the Marsi, Quintus Poppaedius Silo, during the storming of Venusia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although having failed once to be elected praetor, he tried again, achieving the office by 81 BC. He ran for the office of consul in 77 BC, achieving it only after Gaius Scribonius Curio withdrew his candidature for that year in f
2 CONC avor of Livianus. Although during his term as consul he commanded a considerable number of military forces, there is no evidence that he obtained a provincial command after his term was concluded.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following his term as consul he was probably a promagistrate serving under Marcus Antonius Creticus in 74 BC, who had been given an extraordinary commission to clear the Mediterranean Sea of the Ligurian pirates. By 70 BC, he may 
2 CONC have been Princeps Senatus, although the evidence is inconclusive. He was called as a hostile witness against Gaius Cornelius in 65 BC, as part of the events surrounding the First Catilinarian Conspiracy.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Depictions in fiction'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Lepidus Livianus appears in Colleen McCullough's novel The Grass Crown and its sequels.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mamercus_Aemilius_Lepidus_Livianus
