2 CONT Gaius Julius Caesar Strabo Vopiscus From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia For other men called Gaius Julius Caesar (Strabo), see Gaius Julius Caesar Gaius Julius Caesar Strabo Vopiscus (ca. 130 BC  – 87 BC) was the younger son to 
2 CONC Lucius Julius Caesar II and his wife Poppilia and younger brother to Lucius Julius Caesar III. His cognomina indicate he was possibly cross-eyed, and the surviving member of a set of twins. In 103 BC, he was on a committee to supe
2 CONC rvise the implementation of the Lex frumentaria, an agrarian bill, proposed by tribune Lucius Appuleius Saturninus. Strabo became a pontifex in 99 BC; a quaestor in 96 BC and an aedile in 90 BC. When the war between Sulla and Mari
2 CONC us started, Strabo stood for the consulship, although he needed to be a praetor before. Sulla supported him and this caused great civil unrest. Along with his brother he was killed fighting in the streets at the beginning of the C
2 CONC ivil War by partisans of Marius in 87 BC. According to Livy, their heads were exposed on the speaker’s platform. Caesar Strabo Vopiscus wrote at least 3 tragedies with Greek themes. These plays were Adrastus, Tecmesa and Teutras. 
2 CONC Only fragments of the plays survive. According to Cicero, he was an orator known for his wit and humor. Cicero published a dialogue called De Oratore, in which Strabo explains why humor is important in speech. He was an uncle to L
2 CONC ucius Julius Caesar IV, Julia Antonia and a great-uncle to Mark Antony, Gaius Antonius, Lucius Antonius and Lucius Julius Caesar V.
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaius_Julius_Caesar_Strabo_Vopiscus
