1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Serapio==
2 CONT '''For Sources for this Wikipedia information, click:''' [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Publius_Cornelius_Scipio_Nasica_Serapio]
2 CONT
2 CONT Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Serapio (182/181–132 BC) was a politician of the Roman Republic best remembered today for leading a mob that assassinated the tribune Tiberius Gracchus, and hunted and killed Tiberius' supporters af
2 CONC terwards.
2 CONT
2 CONT A member of the great patrician gens Cornelia, he was the son of Scipio Nasica Corculum, the pontifex maximus and princeps senatus. As with many other Cornelii Scipiones, Serapio obtained several prominent offices; he notably succ
2 CONC eeded his father as pontifex maximus in 141 BC, and became consul in 138 BC. A firm conservative, like his father and his cousin Scipio Aemilianus, he led the opposition to the tribune of the plebs Tiberius Gracchus, whom he final
2 CONC ly murdered in 133 BC. However, he was sent to Asia by the senate to avoid his prosecution by Gracchus' supporters, and died in Pergamum soon after. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family Background'''
2 CONT Not much is known about the early life of Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Serapio. He was a member of the gens Cornelia and the son of Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Corculum and his wife Cornelia, daughter of Scipio Africanus. S
2 CONC cipio Nasica was born in 182 or 181 BC.[1] Scipio Nasica Serapio was the third member of his family to bear the agnomen Nasica (pointed nose). He succeeded his father as Pontifex Maximus in 141 BC, possibly because of his illustri
2 CONC ous family name and his father's great reputation.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is likely that his branch of the Cornelii Scipiones had drifted away from the majority of the family, on account of politically opposing views towards the Third Punic War. Corculum was opposed to the invasion of Carthage, where
2 CONC as Scipio Aemilianus actually led the siege of Carthage.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Political Career'''
2 CONT ne important reference to Scipio Nasica's participation in politics is as a mysterious “Cornelius” by the historian Appian. This “Cornelius” is credited with a great Roman defeat at the hands of the widely feared “Pannonians.” Aft
2 CONC er some deduction, one can identify the leader of the Roman forces to be Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Serapio, who in 141 BC was the praetor of Macedonia. Some debate would classify a more likely candidate for the terrible “Pan
2 CONC nonians” to be actually from the region of Illyria, just south of Pannonia proper.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the same year, Scipio Nasica would be awarded the title of Pontifex Maximus, inheriting it from his father who had just died. In 138 BC, Nasica would be elected to the office of consul, part of a series of “strong men” to recti
2 CONC fy the recent bouts of unrest and defeats abroad. During his consulship, Scipio Nasica would attempt to avenge his own defeat as praetor; in doing so, he attempted to raise harsh levies on the Romans. Discontent with his demands, 
2 CONC opposition would rise against him under the leadership of probably Nasica's greatest political rival, the tribune Curiatius. Curiatius had Nasica arrested on the grounds of withholding the legal privileges of the tribunes against 
2 CONC the levy. While in custody, Nasica was given the name “Serapio” as an insult, after a swine-dealer's slave he was said to resemble.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Scipio Nasica's next major political involvement would be that in the murder of Tiberius Gracchus. Gracchus rose to office at a time when the Roman Republic was bloated with the effects of extensive expansion abroad; a huge influx
2 CONC  of slave labor and foreign wealth, a change in the function of agriculture and devaluation in the crop market were causing a massive domestic crisis, challenging the fundamental values of Roman culture (there is a debate over whe
2 CONC ther it was a grain crisis or a manpower crisis).[5] Gracchus had taken the office of tribune and was passing laws of reform to help rectify this domestic crisis, though his legislation was empowering the plebs of Roman society. T
2 CONC he majority of the Senate, who favored the patricians, felt threatened and aligned with Scipio Nasica and his cousin Scipio Aemilianus, who would lead the opposition against Gracchus. Nasica eventually became responsible for the a
2 CONC ssassination of Gracchus during elections in 133 BC. Scipio Nasica had gathered the senators to Gracchus’ bloody death claiming that the tribune desired to become king of Rome. To commit the assassination Scipio Nasica covered his
2 CONC  head with the hood of his pontifex maximus robe, which possibly denoted the killing as a ritualized sacrifice for the good of Rome. After his assassination, Scipio led a witch hunt to eradicate any surviving supporters of Gracchu
2 CONC s. Those who escaped the purge afterwards demanded that Scipio be held responsible for murder. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Death'''
2 CONT Eventually the prolonged conflict between political parties caught up with Nasica, and the Senate sent him away to Pergamon on a mission. This was unusual, as a Pontifex Maximus would never normally be sent away from Rome. He late
2 CONC r died there, allegedly at the hands of some of Gracchus’ supporters. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Scipio Nasica Serapio was succeeded by his son of the same name, who became consul in 111 BC. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT =Book Sources=
2 CONT * '''Cicero''', Marcus Tullius. 2016. ''On Duties''. Translated by Benjamin Patrick Newton. Ithaca, NY: Cornell University Press.[https://www.google.com/books/edition/On_Duties/3xfMDAAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=Publius+Cornelius+Scipio
2 CONC +Nasica+Serapio&pg=PA53&printsec=frontcover]
2 CONT * Taylor, Don. 2017. ''Roman Republic at War: A Compendium of Roman Battles from 498 to 31 BC''. Barnsley, England: Pen & Sword Military.The Roman Republic at War [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Roman_Republic_at_War/vZ7BDgA
2 CONC AQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=Publius+Cornelius+Scipio+Nasica+Serapio&pg=PT8&printsec=frontcover]
2 CONT * '''Cicero''', Marcus Tullius. ''On the Republic and On the Laws'' (Translated work)) [https://www.google.com/books/edition/On_the_Republic_and_On_the_Laws/Rm1UAgAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=Publius+Cornelius+Scipio+Nasica+Serapio&pg=P
2 CONC T169&printsec=frontcover]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scipio_Nasica
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Scipio Nasica was the name of several members of the Scipiones''', a branch of the patrician Roman gens Cornelia. Metellus Scipio was born into this family, but was later adopted out to the gens Caecilia. He still retained his 
2 CONC former name by combining it with that of his adoptive father.
2 CONT
2 CONT This name may refer to:
2 CONT * Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica (consul 191 BC) (b. 227 BC), son of Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Calvus.
2 CONT * Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Corculum (d. 141 BC), son of the above, consul in 162 and 155 BC, pontifex maximus in 150 BC, and princeps senatus in 147 BC.
2 CONT * Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Serapio, son of the above, pontifex maximus in 141 BC and consul in 138 BC. The murderer of Tiberius Gracchus.
2 CONT * Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica (consul 111 BC), son of the above. Some sources mistakenly call him Serapio like his father.
2 CONT * Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius Scipio Nasica (d. 46 BC), grandson of the above, consul in 52 BC, adopted by Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT =Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Serapio =
2 CONT
2 CONT Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Serapio (ca. 183-132 BC Pergamum, Asia Minor), the son of Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Corculum and his wife Cornelia Africana Major, was a member of the gens Cornelia and a politician of the anc
2 CONC ient Roman Republic. He was consul in 138 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT The gens Cornelia was a family of patrician descent. Notable figures from his family line include Scipio Africanus, the first conqueror of Carthage and Scipio Aemilianus, the third conqueror of Carthage and main opponent of Tiberi
2 CONC us Gracchus. Gracchus himself was Scipio Nasica's cousin.
2 CONT
2 CONT His accomplishments are sparsely recorded or discussed in the scholarly world, though he still played an integral role in the overthrow of Tiberius Gracchus, as well as holding many important offices within the Cursus honorum.
2 CONT
2 CONT Scipio Nasica Serapio was the third member of his family to bear the agnomen Nasica (pointed nose). He succeeded his father as Pontifex Maximus in 141 BC, possibly because of his illustrious family name and his father's great repu
2 CONC tation.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Early life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Not much is known about the early life of Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Serapio. He is the child of Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Corculum and his wife Cornelia Africana Major. Scipio Nasica was born in 183 BC. He was the mate
2 CONC rnal grandson of Publius Cornelius Scipio Africanus.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is likely that his branch of the gens Scipio had drifted away from the majority of the family, on account of politically opposing views towards the Third Punic War. Corculum was opposed to the invasion of Carthage, where Scipio
2 CONC  Aemilianus actually led the siege of Carthage.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Political career'''
2 CONT
2 CONT One important reference to Scipio Nasica’s participation in politics is as a mysterious “Cornelius” by the historian Appian. This “Cornelius” is credited with a great Roman defeat at the hands of the widely feared “Pannonians.” Af
2 CONC ter some deduction, one can identify the leader of the Roman forces to be Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Serapio, who in 141 BC was the praetor of Macedonia. Some debate would classify a more likely candidate for the terrible “Pa
2 CONC nnonians” to be actually from the region of Illyria, just south of Pannonia proper.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the same year, Scipio Nasica would be awarded the title of Pontifex Maximus, inheriting it from his father who died in the same year. In 138 BC, Nasica would be elected to the office of consul, part of a series of “strong men” 
2 CONC to rectify the recent bouts of unrest and defeats abroad. During his consulship, Scipio Nasica would attempt to avenge his own defeat as praetor; in doing so, he attempted to raise harsh levies on the Romans. Discontent with his d
2 CONC emands, opposition would rise against him under the leadership of probably Nasica’s greatest political rival, the tribune Curiatius. Curiatius had Nasica arrested on the grounds of withholding the legal privileges of the tribunes 
2 CONC against the levy. While in custody, Nasica was given the name “Serapio” as an insult, referring to the shape of his nose.
2 CONT
2 CONT Scipio Nasica’s next major political involvement would be that in the murder of Tiberius Gracchus. Gracchus rose to office at a time when the Roman Republic was bloated with the effects of extensive expansion abroad; a huge influx
2 CONC  of slave labor and foreign wealth, a change in the function of agriculture and devaluation in the crop market were causing a massive domestic crisis, challenging the fundamental values of Roman culture (there is a debate over whe
2 CONC ther it was a grain crisis or a manpower crisis). Tiberius Gracchus had taken the office of tribune and was passing laws of reform to help rectify this domestic crisis, though his legislation was empowering the plebs of Roman soci
2 CONC ety. Feeling threatened, the patrician favoring majority of the Senate aligned with Scipio Nasica and his cousin Scipio Aemilianus, who would lead the opposition against Gracchus. Nasica would be the one responsible for gathering 
2 CONC senators to assassinate Gracchus during elections in 133 BC. Scipio Nasica had gathered the senators to Gracchus’ bloody death claiming that the tribune desired to become king of Rome. To commit the assassination Scipio Nasica cov
2 CONC ered his head with the hood of his pontifex maximus robe which possibly denoted the killing as a ritualized sacrifice for the good of Rome. After his assassination, Scipio would lead a witch hunt to eradicate any surviving members
2 CONC  of Gracchus’ supporters. Those supporters would demand that Scipio be held responsible for murder, though modern scholars believe that the majority of the Senate supported both of the Scipio men in the controversy.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Death'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually the prolonged conflict between political parties caught up with Nasica, and the Senate sent him away to Pergamon on a mission. This was unusual, as a Pontifex Maximus would never normally be sent away from Rome. He late
2 CONC r died there in Pergamon, allegedly at the hands of some of Gracchus’ supporters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Scipio Nasica Serapio was succeeded by his son of the same name, who became consul in 111 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
