1 NOTE Konge av Pontos i Lilleasia.
1 NOTE parthia.com : Fifth king of Parthia, and the king who established Parthia as an ancient world power. He was the brother of Phraates I, and the son of Phriapatius. He was the father of Phraates II. 
2 CONT We do not know the exact date of his accession, but he is mentioned by Justin [XLI.6] as a contemporary of Bactria's Eucratides and Antiochus IV Epiphanes. Antiochus IV campaigned in Iran to return the territories lost by Arsaces 
2 CONC II. 
2 CONT Mithradates I may have campaigned into Tapuria and Traxiana shortly after succeeding to the throne. The death of Antiochus IV in 164 B.C. would certainly have reduced pressure on Parthia from the west. From 161 - 155 B.C. Mithrada
2 CONC tes waged a long war with Media, the success of which was long unsure. The best account appears to be that Timarkhos, Seleucid satrap of Media lost some territory in the area of Rhagae and farther east, to Mithradates I, while the
2 CONC  Seleucid reconquest did not recover this land but did recapture Ecbatana. According to Justin,1  Mithradates I had to fight many times in Media and Frye2  believes it conceivable that the Seleucids held only the city and the lowl
2 CONC ands to the west of Ecbatana at the time the relief at Herakles was carved, approximately 148 B.C. 
2 CONT The acquisition of Media opened the door to Mesopotamia for Parthian expansion into that fertile territory.3  Mithradates entered the royal city of Seleucia and was recognized as King on or before July 8, 141 B.C. Before October 1
2 CONC 41 B.C., Mithradates sovereignty was acknowledged as far south as Uruk. But by December, incursions of the  Sakae into Hyrcania forced him away to the east. Mithradates turned over military command to a Parthian commander and the 
2 CONC remainder of his reign was occupied with campaigns in southern and central Parthia.4  Mithradates was no sooner gone than Demetrius returned to the attack, joined by Elymais and Persis (and perhaps Bactrian troops), but Demetrius 
2 CONC was soon captured and sent to Mithradates in Hyrcania. He was well treated, however, and given Rhodogune, Mithradates' daughter, in marriage. As punishment for joining Demetrius against the Parthians, Elymais was looted, where the
2 CONC  temples of Artemis and Athena yielded "ten thousand talents."5  Since the Parthians were established at Susa shortly after the death of Mithradates, that territory was probably added to the empire by him.6  At his death, the Part
2 CONC hian empire included Parthia proper, Hyrcania, Media, Babylonia, Assyria, Elymais, Persis (?), and the districts of Tapuria and Traxiana. 
2 CONT Scholars originally accepted Mithradates I died peacefully in 138/137 B.C., a date fixed by the date of his final tetradrachm (S13.5, dated 174 Seleucid era), and cuneiform evidence, but recent works by Dabrowa8 and others suggest
2 CONC  the end of Mithradates I's reign was in 132 B.C. 
2 CONT With the name Mithradates, we may safely assume that worship of the god Mithra was officially sanctioned in Parthia.7
1 NOTE He reigned 171-138 BC.
