1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''
2 CONT =Paullus Fabius Persicus=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Paullus Fabius Persicus (2/1 BCE - ?(reign of Claudius)) was the only son of Paullus Fabius Maximus and Marcia, a maternal cousin of Augustus (daughter of his aunt Atia and L. Marcius Philippus) and great-niece of Julius Caesar. A
2 CONC s such, Persicus was a first-cousin-once-removed of Augustus and a great-great-nephew of Julius Caesar.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Birth and Name'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Paullus Fabius Persicus is believed to have been born in 2 or 1BC. His cognomen - like the praenomen (Paullus) he shared with his father - was given to him to advertise his natural paternal descent from Lucius Aemilius Paullus Mac
2 CONC edonicus, who had defeated the last Macedonian monarch, Perseus, in 146BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life and career'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The first appearance of Persicus is in June of the year 15, when he was co-opted into the Arval Brethren aged c. 15 to replace his then recently deceased father. At around that time, he was also made a member of the College of Pon
2 CONC tiffs and of the Sodales Augustales. He subsequently held the posts of quaestor under Tiberius and praetor, though the details of these posts are unknown. His next dated post is in 34, when he became ordinary consul with Lucius Vi
2 CONC tellius, the father of the later Roman Emperor, Vitellius.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his consulship, his next post was proconsul of Asia in the reign of Claudius (c. 44). An edict written by Persicus from his time as proconsul of Asia is extant.
2 CONT
2 CONT He seems to have died sometime in the reign of Claudius.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Character'''
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Seneca the Younger, Persicus was a particularly vile person,[7] who owed his career more to his ancestry than to his own merit.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paullus_Fabius_Persicus
