1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Pompeius Strabo From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT  (January 2009) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gnaeus Pompeius Strabo (died 87 BC), whose cognomen means "cross eyed", is often referred to in English as Pompey Strabo to distinguish him from Strabo, the geographer. Strabo lived in the Roman Republic. Strabo was born and raise
2 CONC d into a noble family in Picenum (modern Marche & Abruzzo) a rural district in Northern Italy, off the Adriatic coast. Strabo's mother was called Lucilia. Lucilia’s family originated from Suessa Aurunca (modern Sessa Aurunca) and 
2 CONC she was a sister of satire poet Gaius Lucilius. Lucilius was a friend of Roman general Scipio Aemilianus Africanus. Strabo’s paternal grandfather was Gnaeus Pompeius, while his father was Sextus Pompeius. His elder brother was Sex
2 CONC tus Pompeius and his sister was Pompeia. His brother was the father of another Sextus Pompeius and Quintus Pompeius, the first of whom was the father of another Sextus Pompeius, Consul in 35 BC, in turn the father of still another
2 CONC  Sextus Pompeius.
2 CONT
2 CONT Strabo became the first of his branch of the gens Pompeia to achieve senatorial status in Rome, despite the anti-rural prejudice of the Roman Senate. After proving his military talent, Strabo climbed the cursus honorum and became 
2 CONC promagistrate in Sicily 93 BC and consul in the year 89 BC, in the midst of the Social War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Strabo commanded Roman forces against the Italian Allies in the northern part of Italy. His three Roman legions were instrumental in Rome's victory. After his consulship and the war, Strabo retired to Picenum with all of his veter
2 CONC an soldiers. He remained there until 87 BC, when he responded to dictator Lucius Cornelius Sulla's request for help against general Gaius Marius. Strabo had the habit of playing both ends against the middle in the intense politics
2 CONC  of the period. Sulla arranged to remove Strabo from command and replace him with a handpicked confederate. Strabo left camp on "personal business" while his soldiers killed the replacement. Later, when Strabo died of plague, a mo
2 CONC b dragged his body through the streets until a tribune interceded. His son took the legions back to Picenum once again.
2 CONT
2 CONT Strabo married a Roman woman whose name is unknown. He had at least two children: a son, Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus, also known as Pompey the Great or Pompey the triumvir, who took as his fourth wife Julia (the daughter of dictator Ga
2 CONC ius Julius Caesar), and a daughter called Pompeia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pompeius_Strabo
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pompeius_Strabo
2 CONT
2 CONT Strabo’s paternal grandfather was Gnaeus Pompeius, while his father was Sextus Pompeius. His elder brother was Sextus Pompeius and his sister was Pompeia. His brother was the father of another Sextus Pompeius and Quintus Pompeius,
2 CONC  the first of whom was the father of another Sextus Pompeius, Consul in 35 BC (not to be confused with Sextus Pompeius, enemy of the Triumvirate, who died the same year), in turn the father of still another Sextus Pompeius.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT G. Pompeius Strabo was a Roman from the rural Italian district of Picenum, that lay between the Apennines and the Adriatic. He became the first of his branch of the gens Pompeia to achieve senatorial status in Rome, despite the an
2 CONC ti-rural prejudice of the Roman Senate. After proving his military talent, Strabo climbed the cursus honorum and became propraetor in Sicily 93 BC and consul in the year 89 BC, in the midst of the Social War.”
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Pompeius Strabo=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Gnaeus Pompeius Strabo (135–87 BC), whose cognomen means "cross eyed", is often referred to in English as Pompey Strabo to distinguish him from Strabo the geographer, and was the father of Pompey the Great. He lived in the Roman R
2 CONC epublic and was born and raised into a noble family in Picenum (modern Marche & Abruzzo) a rural district in Central Italy, off the Adriatic Coast. Strabo’s mother was called Lucilia. Lucilia’s family originated from Suessa Aurunc
2 CONC a (modern Sessa Aurunca) and she was a sister of satire poet Gaius Lucilius. Lucilius was a friend of Roman general Scipio Aemilianus Africanus. Strabo’s paternal grandfather was Gnaeus Pompeius, while his father was Sextus Pompei
2 CONC us. His elder brother was Sextus Pompeius and his sister was Pompeia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Strabo became the first of his branch of the gens Pompeia to achieve senatorial status in Rome, despite the anti-rural prejudice of the Roman Senate. After proving his military talent, Strabo climbed the cursus honorum and became 
2 CONC promagistrate in Sicily 93 BC and consul in the year 89 BC, in the midst of the Social War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Strabo commanded Roman forces against the Italian Allies in the northern part of Italy. His three Roman legions were instrumental in Rome's victory. After his consulship and the war, he retired to Picenum with all of his veteran s
2 CONC oldiers. Optimates gave his army to Quintus Pompeius Rufus, the new consul. This caused Pompeius Rufus to be murdered by Strabo’s soldiers. He remained there until 87 BC, when he responded to the Optimates' request for help agains
2 CONC t the Marian Party. Strabo had the habit of playing both ends against the middle in the intense politics of the period. For this Rutilius Rufus referred to him as "the vilest man alive".
2 CONT
2 CONT In 87 BC Strabo was killed by a disease outbreak in his army. His avarice and cruelty had made him hated by the soldiers to such a degree that they tore his corpse from the bier and dragged it through the streets. His son took the
2 CONC  legions back to Picenum once again.
2 CONT
2 CONT Strabo married and his wife is an unnamed Roman woman. He had a least two children: a son, Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus, also known as Pompey the Great or Pompey the triumvir who married Julia (the daughter of dictator Gaius Julius Caes
2 CONC ar) as his fourth wife and a daughter called Pompeia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his honor his name was given to the cities of Alba Pompeia and Laus Pompeia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pompeius_Strabo
