2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius Cornelius Scipio Asiaticus (2nd century BC-aft. 183 BC) was a Roman general and statesman. He was the son of Publius Cornelius Scipio and the younger brother of Publius Cornelius Scipio Africanus. He was elected consul in 1
2 CONC 90 BC, and later that year led (with his brother) the Roman forces to victory at the Battle of Magnesia.
2 CONT
2 CONT While his career may be eclipsed by the shadow of his elder brother, Lucius' life is noteworthy in several respects.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early career
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius and his brother both became aediles at a very young age; Scipio became curule aedile in 214 or 213 BC, but Lucius's aedileship is undated.
2 CONT
2 CONT Asiaticus served under his brother in Spain, and in 208 BC took a town on his own. He was sent to the Senate with the news of the victory in the Spanish war, c. 206 BC. In 193 BC, he was elected praetor, with Sicily as his provinc
2 CONC e, with the influence of his brother; however, Scipio's declining influence was not sufficient to get him elected consul in 191 BC. He was finally elected consul in 190 BC with his co-consul being his brother's old second-in-comma
2 CONC nd Gaius Laelius.
2 CONT
2 CONT Consul and proconsul
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Smith:
2 CONT
2 CONT The senate had not much confidence in his abilities (Cic. Phil. xi. 7), and it was only through the offer of his brother Africanus to accompany him as a legate that he obtained the province of Greece and the conduct of the war aga
2 CONC inst Antiochus. The loser was therefore his co-consul Gaius Laelius who was not a rich man, and who had hoped to make his family fortunes in the East.
2 CONT
2 CONT As consular commander of the forces sent against Antiochus III, Asiaticus was a bitter enemy of the Aetolians. He refused the peace negotiated with the Aetolians by his brother, thus proving him to be of a strong nature.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was supreme commander at Magnesia and thus received full credit (at his brother's insistence) for the victory over Antiochus. Upon his return to Rome, he celebrated a full triumph and requested the title "Asiaticus" to signify 
2 CONC his conquest of Western Asia Minor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Political fall
2 CONT
2 CONT Towards the end of his brother's life, Lucius was accused of misappropriating the funds collected from Antiochus as an indemnity. Africanus, then Princeps Senatus, was outraged, going as far as destroying the campaign's financial 
2 CONC records while on the floor of the Senate as an act of defiance.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his brother's death (c. 183 BC), Lucius was thrown in prison for this supposed theft. He was eventually pardoned by the tribune Tiberius Gracchus, although he was forced to sell his property and pay the state a lump sum. Rom
2 CONC an historians report that he refused to accept any gifts or loans from his friends to pay the penalty.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his brother's lifetime in 185 BC, Asiaticus celebrated with great splendour the games which he had vowed in his war with Antiochus. Valerius of Antium related that he obtained the necessary money during an embassy on which 
2 CONC he was sent after his condemnation, to settle the disputes between the kings Antiochus and Eumenes.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a candidate for the censorship in 184 BC, but was defeated by the old enemy of his family, M. Porcius Cato, who deprived Asiaticus of his Public Horse at the review of the equites. It appears, therefore, that even as late a
2 CONC s this time an eques did not forfeit his horse by becoming a senator.
2 CONT
2 CONT His coins are the only ones of his family to survive.
2 CONT
2 CONT Descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT Asiaticus had descendants, the Cornelii Scipiones Asiatici, the last of whom was the consul Lucius Cornelius Scipio Asiaticus who had an adoptive son. This son passed into obscurity after 82 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius Cornelius Scipio Asiaticus II
2 CONT
2 CONT Livy records that the quaestor Lucius Cornelius Scipio was sent to meet King Prusias II of Bithynia and conduct him to Rome, when this monarch visited Italy in 167 BC. Smith reports that this quaestor is probably to be identified 
2 CONC with the Lucius Cornelius Scipio, son of Lucius, grandson of Publius, who is commemorated in the elogia Scipionum from the Tomb of the Scipios in Rome. His father was the conqueror of Antiochus. The inscription is:
2 CONT
2 CONT Epitaph of Asiaticus II from the Tomb of the Scipios.
2 CONT
2 CONT L·CORNELI L·F P SCIPIO·QVAIST TR·MIL·ANNOS GNATOS XXX·III MORTVOS·PATER REGEM ANTIOCO SUBEGIT
2 CONT
2 CONT A transliteration into modern upper and lower case letters with punctuation, with an understood letter in brackets, is:
2 CONT
2 CONT L. Corneli. L. f. P. [n] Scipio, quaist., tr. mil., annos gnatus XXXIII mortuos. Pater regem Antioco subegit. A translation into classical Latin is:
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius Cornelius Lucii filius Publii nepos Scipio. Quaestor Tribunis Militum annos natus XXXIII mortuus. Pater regem Antiochum subegit.
2 CONT
2 CONT A translation into English is:
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius Cornelius, son of Lucius, grandson of Publius, Scipio, quaestor, military tribune, died aged 33 years. His father conquered king Antiochus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius Cornelius Scipio Asiaticus III
2 CONT
2 CONT He is only known from the Fasti Capitolini.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius Cornelius Scipio Asiaticus IV
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius Cornelius Scipio Asiaticus, is first mentioned in 100 BC, when he took up arms with the other members of the senate against Saturninus (Cic. pro Rabir. Perd. 7). In the Social War he was stationed with L. Acilius in the tow
2 CONC n of Aesernia, from which they escaped on the approach of Vettius Scato in the dress of slaves. He belonged to the Marian party in the civil wars, and was appointed consul in 83 BC with C. Norbanus. In this year Sulla returned to 
2 CONC Italy, and advanced against the consuls. He defeated Norbanus in Italy, but seduced the troops of Scipio to desert their general.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was taken prisoner in his camp along with his son Lucius, but was dismissed by Sulla uninjured. He was, however, included in the proscription in the following year, 82 BC, whereupon he fled to Massilia, and passed there the rem
2 CONC ainder of his life. His daughter was married to P. Sestius. Cicero speaks favourably of the oratorical powers of this Scipio.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.google.com/books/edition/Livy_Book_XXXI_XXXVIII/K39JAAAAIAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=Lucius+Cornelius+Scipio+Asiaticus&pg=PA7&printsec=frontcover
