2 CONT
2 CONT =Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius Scipio Nasica=
2 CONT
2 CONT Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius Cornelianus Scipio Nasica (c. 100/98-46 BC), in modern scholarship often as Metellus Scipio, was a Roman consul and military commander in the Late Republic. During the civil war between Julius Caesa
2 CONC r and the senatorial faction led by Pompeius Magnus ("Pompey the Great"), he remained a staunch optimate. He led troops against Caesar's forces, mainly in the battles of Pharsalus and Thapsus, where he was defeated. He later commi
2 CONC tted suicide. Ronald Syme called him "the last Scipio of any consequence in Roman history."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family connections and name'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Metellus Scipio was born Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica. His grandfather was the P. Cornelius Scipio Nasica Serapio who was consul in 111 BC; his father Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica (born 128 BC) married Licinia Crassa, daught
2 CONC er of the L. Licinius Crassus who was consul in 95 BC. The father died not long after his praetorship (c. 93 BC), and was survived by two sons and two daughters. The brother was adopted by their grandfather Crassus, but left littl
2 CONC e mark on history.
2 CONT
2 CONT Publius Scipio, as he was referred to in contemporary sources early in his life, was adopted in adulthood through the testament of Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius, consul in 80 BC and pontifex maximus. He retained his patrician st
2 CONC atus: "Scipio's ancestry," notes Syme, "was unmatched for splendour." As Jerzy Linderski has shown at length, this legal process constitutes adoption only in a loose sense; Scipio becomes a Caecilius Metellus in name while inherit
2 CONC ing the estate of Metellus Pius, but was never his "son" while the pontifex maximus was alive. He was called "Metellus Scipio" but also sometimes just "Scipio" even after his adoption. The official form of his name as evidenced in
2 CONC  a decree of the senate was "Q. Caecilius Q. f. Fab. Metellus Scipio."
2 CONT
2 CONT Scipio married Aemilia Lepida, daughter of Mamercus Aemilius Lepidus Livianus (consul 77 BC), but was not without rival in the match. The virginal Cato had also wanted to marry Aemilia and lost out in the seduction:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''When [Cato] thought that he was old enough to marry,— and up to that time he had consorted with no woman,— he engaged himself to Lepida, who had formerly been betrothed to Metellus Scipio, but was now free, since Scipio had reje
2 CONC cted her and the betrothal had been broken. However, before the marriage Scipio changed his mind again, and by dint of every effort got the maid. Cato was greatly exasperated and inflamed by this, and attempted to go to law about 
2 CONC it; but his friends prevented this, and so, in his rage and youthful fervour, he betook himself to iambic verse, and heaped much scornful abuse upon Scipio … .''
2 CONT
2 CONT The couple had one son, a Metellus Scipio who seems to have died when he was only 18. Another son may have been born around 70, or a son may have been adopted. The couple's much more famous daughter was born around that time as we
2 CONC ll. Scipio first married off the celebrated Cornelia Metella to Publius Crassus, the son of Marcus Licinius Crassus. After Publius's premature death at Carrhae, Scipio decided to succeed Caesar as the father-in-law of Pompeius, wh
2 CONC o was at least thirty years older than Cornelia. The marriage is one of the acts by which Pompeius severed his alliance to Caesar and declared himself the champion of the optimates. He and Scipio were consuls together in 52.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Political career'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Cicero names "P. Scipio" among the young nobiles on his defense team when Sextus Roscius was prosecuted in 80 BC. He is placed in the company of M. Messalla and Metellus Celer, both future consuls.
2 CONT
2 CONT Metellus Scipio has been listed as tribune of the plebs in 59, but his patrician status argues against his holding the office. It is possible that Scipio's 'adoption' into a plebeian gens may have qualified him for a tribunate on 
2 CONC a technicality. He was possibly curule aedile in 57 BC, when he presented funeral games in honor of his adopted father's death six years earlier. He was praetor, most likely in 55 BC, during the second joint consulship of Pompeius
2 CONC  and Marcus Crassus.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 53 BC, he was interrex with M. Valerius Messalla. He became consul with Pompeius in 52 BC, the year he arranged the marriage of his newly widowed daughter to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Indisputably aristocratic and conservative, Metellus Scipio had been at least symbolically a counterweight to the power of the so-called triumvirate before the death of Crassus in 53. "Opportune deaths," notes Syme, "had enhanced 
2 CONC his value, none remaining now of the Metellan consuls."
2 CONT
2 CONT He is known to have been a member of the College of Pontiffs by 57 BC, and was probably nominated upon the death of his adoptive father in 63 and subsequently elected.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Role in civil war'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 49 BC, Metellus Scipio persuaded the senate to issue the ultimatum to Caesar that made war inevitable. That same year, he became proconsul of the province of Syria. In Syria and in the province of Asia, where he took up
2 CONC  winter quarters, he used often oppressive means to gather ships, troops, and money:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''He put a per capita tax on slaves and children; he taxed columns, doors, grain, soldiers, weaponry, oarsmen, and machinery; if a name could be found for a thing, that was seen as sufficient for making money from it.''
2 CONT
2 CONT Scipio put to death Alexander of Judaea, and was acclaimed Imperator for "alleged" victories in the Amanus Mountains — as noted disparagingly by Caesar.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 48 BC, he brought his forces from Asia to Greece, where he maneuvered against Gn. Domitius Calvinus and L. Cassius until the arrival of Pompeius. At the Battle of Pharsalus, he commanded the center. After the optimates' defeat 
2 CONC by Caesar, Metellus fled to Africa. With the support of his former rival-in-romance Cato, he wrested the chief command of Pompeius's forces from the loyal Attius Varus, probably in early 47. In 46 BC, he held command at the Battle
2 CONC  of Thapsus "without skill or success," and was defeated along with Cato. After the defeat he tried to escape to the Iberian Peninsula to continue the fight, but was cornered by the fleet of Publius Sittius. He committed suicide b
2 CONC y stabbing himself so he would not fall at the hands of his enemies.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Dignity in death''
2 CONT
2 CONT Facing death, Metellus Scipio achieved an uncharacteristic dignity, famously departing from his soldiers with a nonchalant Imperator se bene habet ("Your general's just fine"). These last words elicited strong praise from the Stoi
2 CONC c moral philosopher Seneca:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Take, for example, Scipio, the father-in-law of Gnaeus Pompeius: he was driven back upon the African coast by a head-wind and saw his ship in the power of the enemy. He therefore pierced his body with a sword; and when they aske
2 CONC d where the commander was, he replied: 'All is well with the commander.' These words brought him up to the level of his ancestors and suffered not the glory which fate gave to the Scipios in Africa to lose its continuity. It was a
2 CONC  great deed to conquer Carthage, but a greater deed to conquer death. 'All is well with the commander!' Ought a general to die otherwise, especially one of Cato's generals?''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Assessment'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Classical scholar John H. Collins summed up the character and reputation of Metellus Scipio:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''From all that can be learned of this Scipio, he was as personally despicable and as politically reactionary as they come: a defender of C. Verres (In Ver. II. 4. 79–81), a debauchee of singular repulsiveness (Valerius Maximus, 9
2 CONC .1.8), an incompetent and bull-headed commander (Plutarch, Cato Min. 58), an undisciplined tyrant in the possession of authority (Bell. Afr. 44–46), an extortioner of the provinces (BC 3.31–33), a proscription-thirsty bankrupt (At
2 CONC t. 9.11), a worthy great grandson des hochmütigen, plebejerfeindlichen Junkers (Münzer, RE 4.1502) who had led the lynching of Tiberius Gracchus, and a most unworthy father of the gentle Cornelia. Only in the Imperator se bene hab
2 CONC et with which he met death is there any trace of the nobler character of his great forebears (Seneca Rhet., Suas. 7.8).''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source''': https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quintus_Caecilius_Metellus_Pius_Scipio_Nasica
2 CONT ----
2 CONT =Other Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Roberts, John Maddox. SPQR X: A Point of Law. New York: Thomas Dunne Books, 2006. [https://www.google.com/books/edition/SPQR_X_A_Point_of_Law/v8x9RGR6X-4C?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=Quintus+Caecilius+Metellus+Pius+Scipio&pg=PR10&printsec=f
2 CONC rontcover]
2 CONT * Uberto Decembrio. Four Books on the Commonwealth: De re publica libri IV. Paolo Ponzù Donato, ed. & trans. Boston, MA | Leiden, Netherlands: Brill, 2020. [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Uberto_Decembrio_Four_Books_on_the_C
2 CONC ommo/p8y8DwAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=Quintus+Caecilius+Metellus+Pius+Cornelianus+Scipio+Nasica&pg=PA277&printsec=frontcover]
2 CONT * Plutarchus. Plutarch’s Lives, Vol IV. A. H. Clough trans., William W. Goodwin ed. New York: Little, Brown, & Co., 1909. [https://archive.org/details/plutarchslives04ahcl/page/n9/mode/2up?q=Metellus]
2 CONT * Harris, Robert. Imperium Roman. München: Penguin Random House Deutschland, 2006. ISBN 978-3-641-10832-8. Note: The following German and English translation can be found at this URL: [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Imperium
2 CONC /TqJbhtmkuegC?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=Quintus+Caecilius+Metellus+Pius+Cornelianus+Scipio+Nasica&pg=PT79&printsec=frontcover]
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Nachdem Cato geganen war, sagte Cicero zu mir: “Der junge Mann ist so begierig darauf, jedem seine Prinzipientreue vor Augen zu führen, wie ein Säufer auf eine Kneipenschlägerei aus ist.” Dennoch war Cato einverstanden gewesen, 
2 CONC dass Cicero sich in seinem Namen an Scipio wante. Ich sah Cicero an, das die Gelegenheit, den Aristokraten persönlich unter die Lupe nehmen zu können, ganz nach seinem Geschmack war. Es gab buchstäblich keinen Mann in Rom, dessen 
2 CONC Stammbaum imposanter war als der des Quintus Caecilius Mettelu Pius Cornelianus Scipio Nasica – Nasica bedeutet “Spitznase”, die er im Übrigen sehr hoch trug. Er war nicht nur der leibliche Sohn eines Scipio, sonder nauch der Adop
2 CONC tivsohn von Metellus Pius, dem nominellen Oberhaupt ddes Geschlechts dder Metelli. Vater und Adoptivsohn waren erst kürzlich aus Spanien zurückgekehrt und hielten sich derzeit auf Pius’ riesigem Landgut in Tibur auf. Man erwartete
2 CONC , das sie bei Pompeius’ Triumph am neunundzwanzigsten. Dezember direct hinter dem General in die Stadt reiten würden. Cicero beschloss, ein Treffen für den dreißigsten zu vereinbaren.''
2 CONT
2 CONT After Cato left, Cicero said to me, "The young man is as eager to demonstrate his principles to everyone as a drunkard is to a bar fight." Nevertheless, Cato had agreed that Cicero wanted to approach Scipio on his behalf. I looked
2 CONC  at Cicero, feeling that the opportunity to scrutinize the aristocrat in person was to his liking. There was literally no man in Rome whose pedigree was more imposing than that of Quintus Caecilius Mettelus Pius Cornelianus Scipio
2 CONC  Nasica - Nasica means "pointed nose", which incidentally he carried very high. He was not only the biological son of a Scipio, but also the adopted son of Metellus Pius, the nominal head of the family of the Metelli. The father a
2 CONC nd adopted son had recently returned from Spain and were currently staying at Pius' vast estate in Tibur. It was expected at Pompey's triumph on the twenty-ninth. December would ride right behind the general into town. Cicero deci
2 CONC ded to set a meeting for the thirtieth.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In the above work''', “Metellus” frequently is the shortened name used. See: [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Imperium/TqJbhtmkuegC?hl=en&gbpv=1&bsq=Metellus]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Stevenson, Tom. Julius Caesar and the Transformation of the Roman Republic. New York, Routledge, 2015. See subheading “Pompey’s Sole Consulship (52 BCE)” [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Julius_Caesar_and_the_Transformation
2 CONC _of/0xkhBQAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=Quintus+Caecilius+Metellus+Pius+Scipio&pg=PT267&printsec=frontcover]
