1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Lucius Volusius Saturninus''' (38/37 BC[1] - 56[2]) was a noted Ancient Roman statesman of his day.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Family background=
2 CONT
2 CONT Saturninus was the son of suffect consul Lucius Volusius Saturninus and Nonia Polla.[4] He had a sister called Volusia who married a certain Cornelius,[5] while his paternal cousins were Lollia Saturnina and Lollia Paulina, the th
2 CONC ird wife of the Roman emperor Caligula.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Political career=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Augur	Before 3
2 CONT * Proconsul of Anatolia[7]	9 - 10
2 CONT * Augustalis, Titius[8]	14 - 56
2 CONT * Legatus pro praetore, probably of Illyricum[9]	14 - 15
2 CONT * Legatus pro praetore of Dalmatia[10]	34 - 50
2 CONT * Prefect of Rome[11]	ca. 42 - 56
2 CONT
2 CONT =Reputation and Posthumous Honors=
2 CONT
2 CONT Saturninus had a distinguished reputation,[12] who was in good graces of all of the Roman emperors who avoided the malevolence of them and had a great fortune.[13] He died at the age of 93.[14] When he died, the Roman Senate under
2 CONC  the sponsorship of Roman emperor Nero ordered a state funeral and the erection of a number of statues of him in major temples, theatres and civic buildings throughout Rome. The statues included a bronze one in the Forum of August
2 CONC us, two marble statues in the temple of the Deified Augustus, one consular statue in the temple of the Deified Julius, another on the Palatium intra Tripylum, a third in the forecourt of Apollo in sight of the curia, a statue as A
2 CONC ugur, an Equestrian statue and a statue on a curule chair sitting near the Theatre of Pompey.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Family and Issue=
2 CONT
2 CONT Saturninus married the noblewoman Cornelia Lentula, the daughter of the consul of 3 BC, Lucius Cornelius Lentulus.[15] Cornelia bore Saturninus three sons who were: Lucius Volusius Saturninus who became a Pontifex Maximus, Quintus
2 CONC  Volusius Saturninus who became a consul of 56 and an unnamed son.[16] Saturninus with his sons, ran the columbarium on the Appian Way.[17]
2 CONT
2 CONT =References, Sources, and External links=
2 CONT
2 CONT * for a full list, please see: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucius_Volusius_Saturninus_(suffect_consul_3)
