1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brogitarus '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Brogitarus''' (/brəˈdʒɪtərəs/; Classical Latin: [brɔˈɡɪtaɾʊs])[1] was king of Galatia in Asia Minor between 63 BC and 50 BC, reigning concurrently with his father-in-law Deiotarus Philoromaeus,[2] who was also tetrarch of the T
2 CONC olistobogii. By Deiotarus' daughter Adobogiona, Brogitarus was the father of Amyntas, tetrarch of the Trocmi and king of Galatia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cicero claims that Brogitarus obtained his elevation to the kingship of Galatia alongside Deiotarus by bribing P. Clodius Pulcher, who was then tribune of the plebs at Rome.[2] Brogitarus also became high priest of the Great Mothe
2 CONC r at Pessinus after the incumbent was removed through a law introduced Clodius Pulcher. Cicero impugns not only this procedure but also Brogitarus' character, claiming that the priesthood "was sold for a large sum to Brogitarus, a
2 CONC  profligate man, and unworthy of any such sacred character, especially as he had desired it not for the purpose of doing honour to the goddess, but only of profaning her temple."[3] Deiotarus subsequently intervened to remove Brog
2 CONC itarus as high priest on the grounds that the latter had "polluted" its sacred ceremonies.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The name Brogitarus may be understood as brogi-taros 'border-crosser' or (less likely) brogi-taruos 'border-bull'.[4
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Charlton T. Lewis and Charles Short (1879). "Brogitarus" from A Latin Dictionary, retrieved on 2010-05-31.
2 CONT # M. Tullius Cicero. De Haruspicum Responsis 13.
2 CONT # M. Tullius Cicero. Pro Sestio 25:56. Quoted text is from C. D. Yonge and B. A. London (1891). The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero, literally translated. The original is ...uenditum pecunia grandi Brogitaro, impuro homini atque
2 CONC  indigno illa religione, praesertim cum eam sibi ille non colendi, sed uiolandi causa adpetisset.
2 CONT # Xavier Delamarre (2003). Dictionnaire de la langue gauloise. Editions Errance, Paris, pp. 90, 290.
2 CONT * Christian Settipani, Les Ancêtres de Charlemagne (France: Éditions Christian, 1989).
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Brogitarus=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Brogitarus (/brəˈdʒɪtərəs/; Classical Latin: [brɔˈɡɪtaɾʊs]) was king of Galatia in Asia Minor between 63 BC and 50 BC, reigning concurrently with his father-in-law Deiotarus Philoromaeus, who was also tetrarch of the Tolistobogii.
2 CONC  By Deiotarus' daughter Adobogiona, Brogitarus was the father of Amyntas, tetrarch of the Trocmi and king of Galatia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cicero claims that Brogitarus obtained his elevation to the kingship of Galatia alongside Deiotarus by bribing P. Clodius Pulcher, who was then tribune of the plebs at Rome. Brogitarus also became high priest of the Great Mother a
2 CONC t Pessinus after the incumbent was removed through a law introduced Clodius Pulcher. Cicero impugns not only this procedure but also Brogitarus' character, claiming that the priesthood "was sold for a large sum to Brogitarus, a pr
2 CONC ofligate man, and unworthy of any such sacred character, especially as he had desired it not for the purpose of doing honour to the goddess, but only of profaning her temple."[3] Deiotarus subsequently intervened to remove Brogita
2 CONC rus as high priest on the grounds that the latter had "polluted" its sacred ceremonies.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name Brogitarus may be understood as brogi-taros 'border-crosser' or (less likely) brogi-taruos 'border-bull'.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
