1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Lucius Cornelius Scipio Barbatus=
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius Cornelius Scipio Barbatus (died c. 280 BC) was one of the two elected Roman consuls in 298 BC. He led the Roman army to victory against the Etruscans near Volterra. A member of the noble Roman family of Scipiones, he was th
2 CONC e father of Lucius Cornelius Scipio and Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Asina and great-grandfather of Scipio Africanus.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''General of the third Samnite war'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Barbatus rose to preeminence as a patrician officer of the Roman Republic during the crucial period of the Third Samnite War, when Rome finally defeated a coalition of neighboring states: the Etruscans, Umbrians and Samnites assis
2 CONC ted by the Gauls, thereby extending its leadership and sovereignty over most of Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Battle of Volterrae, 298 BC''
2 CONT
2 CONT Prior to 298 BC war had already broken out between Rome and Etruria when the Etruscans decided to invade Rome in combination with some Gallic allies they had purchased. The planned attack was a violation of a former treaty with Ro
2 CONC me. The Gauls reneged and the Etruscans found themselves facing a Roman army under consul Titus Manlius who however died after a fall from his horse in a display of horsemanship. The election held to replace him made Marcus Valeri
2 CONC us Corvus consul. He joined the army in Etruria and began to waste the country hoping to provoke the Etruscans to battle, which they refused.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 298 BC Appius Claudius followed by Publius Sulpicius became interreges for reasons unknown. Sulpicius held an election, which brought Barbatus and Gnaeus Fulvius Maximus Centumatus into consular office. The Lucanians spoke befo
2 CONC re the Senate saying that the Samnites were devastating their country and asking for the protection of Rome in exchange for a treaty and hostages. The Senate assented after a few moments' deliberation and dispatched heralds to tel
2 CONC l the Samnites to withdraw. Encountering the Samnite army they were told that if they spoke in Samnium they would never leave there alive; consequently, the Senate declared war on Samnium. In a casting of lots as to which consul w
2 CONC ould take which war Barbatus won command of the army in Etruria while Centumatus undertook the initial campaign in the Third Samnite War.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Etruscans attacked immediately before Volterra. A day-long battle brought no victory but in the night the Etruscans withdrew to their fortified cities leaving their camp and equipment to the Romans. Encamping his army at the E
2 CONC trurian border Barbatus led a lightly armed force in the devastation of the countryside.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Battle of Tifernum, 297 BC''
2 CONT
2 CONT In the next year the Etruscans sued for peace. The newly elected consuls for 297 BC, Quintus Fabius Maximus and Decius Mus led both armies against Samnium, Barbatus going as lieutenant general (legatus) under Maximus. As they adva
2 CONC nced into Samnium laying waste to the country the Samnites were hoping to catch them in an ambush in a valley at Tifernum (Samniticum). Stationing a force there to entice the Romans they hid their main force in the hills behind. F
2 CONC abius saw through the ruse and brought his army up in quadrangular formation before the "hiding place" of the Samnites, who then came down to fight a conventional battle, line-to-line.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unable to obtain a victory, Fabius withdrew the spearmen of the First Legion from the line and sent them under the command of Barbatus stealthily around the enemy flank into the hills behind, whence the latter had earlier descende
2 CONC d. They were ordered to coordinate an attack from behind with an especially vigorous cavalry charge to the front of the Samnite line. The plan went entirely wrong: the charge came too soon and was repulsed. A counterattack was beg
2 CONC inning to break the Roman line when Barbatus' men appeared on the hills and were mistaken for the second Roman army under Mus, a disaster for the Samnites if true. They abandoned the field posthaste leaving behind 23 standards and
2 CONC  3400 slain, while 830 were taken prisoner. In fact Publius Decius Mus was far away in south Samnium.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Campaigns under Claudius and Flamma, 296 BC''
2 CONT
2 CONT Having routed the Samnite army both consuls proceeded to the systematic reduction of Samnium over a period of five months until the next election. Mus travelled over the country conducting operations from 45 camps successively whi
2 CONC le Maximus utilized 86.[3] After elections the new consuls ordered them to continue the war in Samnium for six months, each with the rank of proconsul. The Samnite army under Gellius Egnatius, unable to remain in Samnium, offered 
2 CONC its services to Etruria, which were accepted; under Egnatius' leadership the Umbrians were brought in and Gallic mercenaries were hired. Calling a meeting of all the chief men in Etruria Egnatius declared that war for freedom was 
2 CONC better than peace with servitude and announced his intention to attack Rome. The Etruscans assented.
2 CONT
2 CONT Receiving intelligence of the new dangerous circumstances the Senate dispatched Appius Claudius into Etruria in command of the First and Fourth Legions and 12,000 allied troops. Several inconclusive engagements were fought. The se
2 CONC cond consul for 296, Lucius Volumnius Flamma, was assisting the two proconsuls in the reduction of Samnium when the Lucanians defected, influenced by an appeal from the ordinary people of Samnium. Flamma claimed to have received a
2 CONC  letter from Claudius asking for military assistance, a claim which was later denied by Claudius. Sending Maximus (presumably still with Barbatus) to the reduction of Lucania he departed for Etruria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Claudius was ill-pleased to see him and had ordered him away when all the officers of his own army met to insist that he be retained. The men took a voice vote of such magnitude that it alarmed the nearby enemy camp and they prepa
2 CONC red for battle. The Romans went out to fight immediately, with Claudius giving in to a situation he had to accept. The Romans attacked so fiercely with Claudius, it is said, fighting in the front ranks along with the men and conti
2 CONC nually invoking the goddess of war, Bellona, with hands upraised to heaven, that they routed the combined enemy force and drove them from their camp, killing 7300 and taking 2120 prisoners.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, the reduced forces of Maximus and Mus failed to restrain the Samnites, who raised another army with which they invaded and plundered Campania. Arriving there by forced marches Flamma learned that the Samnite army was en
2 CONC camped at the river Volturnus on its way back to Samnium. In the Battle of the Volturnus of 296 BC Flamma's army waited in ambush outside the gates of the Samnite camp. Flamma had sent in native spies the night before, who ascerta
2 CONC ined that the Samnites would make a dawn march. At dawn Flamma allowed part of the Samnite army to march out, splitting their forces, before he launched an attack that had such a momentum it was soon being fought in the camp. 7400
2 CONC  Roman prisoners taken previously by the Samnites freed themselves and joined in the fighting. At the end of the day the Romans had killed 6000, taken 2500 prisoners including four military tribunes and the commander, Statius Mina
2 CONC cius, and captured 30 standards. They redistributed the Samnite booty to claimants and gave the unclaimed property to the soldiers. Samnite hopes in the south had been thwarted. News was received at Rome however that Gellius Egnat
2 CONC ius had raised another army in the north consisting of Samnites, Etruscans, Umbrians and Gauls. The Senate in a mood of despair prepared to mobilize the last of the Roman forces. They ordered a draft of all males, including adoles
2 CONC cents, the elderly and the sons of freedmen. For the first time they began to debate the permanent depopulation of Samnium (a measure that was never carried out).
2 CONT
2 CONT ''The turning point, 295 BC''
2 CONT
2 CONT The elections of 295 BC were now upon the city. Flamma was recalled to conduct them. Maximus and Mus were elected, with Appius Claudius in the office of praetor. Maximus insisted on commanding in Etruria without the casting of lot
2 CONC s that normally apportioned duties to consuls and after an intense public debate the Senate granted his request. He proceeded to Etruria, relieved Claudius of his command and sent him home on the grounds that he was a do-nothing c
2 CONC ommander who had allowed his men to sit in camp without even the exercise of marches for patrols and training. Thanks to Claudius, Maximus was soon recalled to account for his conduct of the Etrurian campaign and receive any furth
2 CONC er orders. Barbatus suddenly appears again in the account, indicating that he had been under Maximus' command all along. Maximus assigns Barbatus as propraetor of the Second Legion stationed temporarily at Clusium. He then departs
2 CONC  for Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Patrician censor'''
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of his death Barbatus was the patrician censor of 280 BC. His censorate is notable because it is the first one of which we have a reliable record, though the position was quite old by that time.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sarcophagus and Epitaph'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Sarcophagus of Lucius Cornelius Scipio Barbatus was discovered in the Tomb of the Scipios (the only one to survive complete there), and is now in the Vatican Museums. It preserves his epitaph, written in Old Latin Saturnian me
2 CONC ter (for traslation see article on Sarcophagus).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source'': https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucius_Cornelius_Scipio_Barbatus
2 CONT
2 CONT =Acimovic, Alexander. 2007. ''Scipio Africanus''. Lincoln, NE: iUniverse.=
2 CONT [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Scipio_Africanus/9yFKDwAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=Lucius+Cornelius+Scipio+Barbatus&pg=PA1&printsec=frontcover]
2 CONT
2 CONT "Publius Cornelius Scipio was born in 236 B.C. He was descended from one of the most renowned patrician ''gens'' of ancient Rome, the Cornelii. The Scipios were most likely an offshoot from an older line within the ''gens'', possi
2 CONC bly the Malugienses.[1] the name Scipio means 'staff'; it is said to have originated when a blind member of the family held onto his son for support; its oldest recorded usage is found in accounts of the early 4th century B.C.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The first surly attested Scipios, brothers named Publius and Lucius, held high office in the mid 4th century; the former was master of the horse in 350 [BC], the latter was consul in the same year.[2] There is considerable confus
2 CONC ion about the members of the next generation or two of the family and the offices they may or may not have held,[3] but one whom we can identify with certainty is a Cnaeus Cornelius Scipio, whose son, Lucius Conrnelius Scipio Barb
2 CONC atus, served as consul in 298 [BC]. Scipio Barbatus, besides his consulship, served severl times as a legate under the legendary Fabius Maxximus Rullianus and his son Fabius Gurges, and was also censor, probably in 280;[4] he late
2 CONC r built the famous Tomb of the Scipios, which can still be seen today on the Via Appia. Following his death, he was buried in a magnificent sarcophagus in the center of the Tomb, which is now housed in the Vatican Museum.[5] Barba
2 CONC tus' son, also Lucius, was consul in 259, during the early years of the First Pubic War; his tenure included a successful attack upon the Carthaginians in Sardinia and Corsica, for which he was awarded a triumph.[6]  In 258, he se
2 CONC rved as censor; after his death, he was also interred in the family tomb.[7[ This Lucius Scipio begat two sons, Cnaeus and Publius, the eldest son of the latter being the Publius of whose life we are writing."
2 CONT
2 CONT =Inscriptions of Scipio Tombs=
2 CONT [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Roman_Civilization/2Dg6TRHG9MQC?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=Lucius+Cornelius+Scipio+Barbatus&pg=PA523&printsec=frontcover]
2 CONT
