2 CONT =Gnaeus Pompeius=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Gnaeus Pompeius (ca. 75-April 12, 45 BC), also known as Pompey the Younger (sometimes spelled Cneius, Gneius), was a Roman politician and general from the late Republic (1st century BC).
2 CONT
2 CONT Gnaeus Pompeius was the elder son of Pompey the Great (Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus) by his third wife, Mucia Tertia. Both he and his younger brother Sextus Pompey grew up in the shadow of their father, one of Rome's best generals and n
2 CONC ot originally a conservative politician who drifted to the more traditional faction when Julius Caesar became a threat. When Caesar crossed the Rubicon in 49 BC, thus starting a civil war, Gnaeus followed his father in their escap
2 CONC e to the East, as did most of the conservative senators. Pompey's army lost the Battle of Pharsalus in 48 BC, and Pompey himself had to run for his life, only to be murdered in Egypt on September 29th of the same year.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the murder, Gnaeus and his brother Sextus joined the resistance against Caesar in the Africa Province. Together with Metellus Scipio, Cato the Younger and other senators, they prepared to oppose Caesar and his army to the en
2 CONC d. Caesar defeated Metellus Scipio and Cato, who subsequently committed suicide, at the Battle of Thapsus in 46 BC. Gnaeus escaped, this time to the Balearic Islands, where he joined Sextus. Together with Titus Labienus, former ge
2 CONC neral in Caesar's army, the Pompey brothers crossed over to Hispania (the Iberian Peninsula, comprising modern Spain and Portugal), where they raised yet another army.
2 CONT
2 CONT Caesar soon followed and, on March 17, 45 BC, the armies met in the Battle of Munda. Both armies were large and led by able generals. The battle was closely fought, but eventually a cavalry charge by Caesar turned events to his si
2 CONC de. In the battle and the panicked escape that followed, Titus Labienus and an estimated 30,000 men of the Pompeian side died. Gnaeus and Sextus managed to escape another time but supporters were difficult to find. It was by now c
2 CONC lear Caesar had won the civil war. Within a few weeks, Gnaeus Pompeius was caught and executed by Lucius Caesennius Lento for treason. Sextus Pompeius was able to keep one step ahead of his enemies, and survived his brother for an
2 CONC other decade.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gnaeus Pompeius married Claudia Pulchra, who survived him; they had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
