2 CONT
2 CONT 6For a discussion of Gallus' fall from grace and the sources that treat the event, see ibid , 232ff, and idem. , "The Antiochene Connection: Zonaras, Ammianus Marcellinus, and John of Antioch on the Reigns of the Emperors Constant
2 CONC ius II and Julian," Byzantion , 50 (1980), 170ff. See also R. N. Mooney, "Gallus Caesar's Last Journey," Classical Philology 53 (1958): 175-177. For a disputed plot of the usurper Magnentius to kill Gallus, see Robert Frakes, "Amm
2 CONC ianus Marcellinus and Zonaras on a Late Roman Assassination plot," Historia 46 (1997): 121-128.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7For a listing of sources on Eusebia and her influence on Constantius, see A.H.M. Jones, J.R. Martindale, and J. Morris, PLRE ,s.v. "Eusebia" 1.300-01.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8For a discussion of the rise of Julian and the events leading up to the death of Constantius, see R.C. Blockley, "Constantius Gallus and Julian as Caesars of Constantius II," Latomus , 21 (1972), 445ff; Ilse Müller-Seidel, "Die U
2 CONC surpation Julians des Abtrünnigen im Lichte seiner Germanenpolitik," HZ , 180 (1955). 227ff; and DiMaio, Zonaras , 329ff.
2 CONT
2 CONT For a listing of sources on Faustina, see A.H.M. Jones, J.R. Martindale, and J. Morris, PLRE ,s.v. "Faustina" 1.326.
2 CONT
2 CONT 9Extant Books XIV through XXI of Ammianus' Res Gestae treat the reign of Constantius. At least his lost Book XIII did also; see further, Robert Frakes, "Cross-References to the Lost Books of Ammianus Marcellinus," Phoenix 49 (1995
2 CONC ): 232-246.
2 CONT
2 CONT 10For a discussion of Constantius' character, see DiMaio and Arnold, Byzantion , 62 (1992), 168ff. For a summary of his virtues and vices, see Ammianus 21.16.1-9
2 CONT
2 CONT Barnes' Athanasius and Constantius is the locus classicus for any discussion of Constantius' involvement with the Christian faith.
2 CONT
2 CONT Copyright (C) 1998, Michael DiMaio, Jr. and Robert Frakes. This file may be copied on the condition that the entire contents,including the header and this copyright notice, remain intact.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: b. February 317, Arelate, Viennensis [now Arles, Fr.]
2 CONT
2 CONT d. 340 
2 CONT
2 CONT Roman emperor from 337 to 340.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second son of Constantine the Great (ruled 306-337), he was given the title of caesar by his father on March 1, 317. When Constantine the Great died in 337, Constantine II and his brothers, Constans and Constantius II, each ad
2 CONC opted the title augustus and divided the empire among themselves. Constantine II became ruler of Britain, Gaul, and Spain. He soon claimed Italy and Africa from Constans and, early in 340, unexpectedly invaded Italy. Penetrating t
2 CONC o Aquileia, Constantine was met by the vanguard of the army of Constans and was killed in the ensuing battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Copyright © 1994-2001 Encyclopædia Britannica, Inc.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: b. Aug. 7, 317, Sirmium, Savia [now Sremska Mitrovica, Yugos.]
2 CONT
2 CONT d. Nov. 3, 361, Mopsucrenae, Honorias [now in Turkey] 
2 CONT
2 CONT original name FLAVIUS JULIUS CONSTANTIUS , Roman emperor from 337 to 361, who at first shared power with his two brothers, Constantine II (d. 340) and Constans I (d. 350), but who was sole ruler from 353 to 361.
2 CONT
2 CONT The third son of Constantine I the Great and Fausta, Constantius served under his father as caesar from 323 to 337. When Constantine died on May 22, 337, the troops massacred many of his relatives, allegedly at Constantius' instig
2 CONC ation. Constantius then divided the empire with his brothers, taking the eastern provinces (Thrace, Macedonia, Greece, Asia, and Egypt) for himself. Between 338 and 350 he was engaged in inconclusive but extremely bloody warfare w
2 CONC ith the Persian king Shapur II.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 350 Constantius returned to Europe to confront two usurpers. Vetranio, commander of the Danube forces, had taken power in Illyricum (now western Yugoslavia); the rest of Europe was seized by the barbarian officer Magnentius, wh
2 CONC o in 350 executed Constans, the ruler in the West. At Naissus (modern Nis, Yugos.), Constantius persuaded Vetranio to abdicate, and in 351 he crushed Magnentius at Mursa (modern Osijek, Yugos.). During this struggle Constantius ap
2 CONC pointed his cousin, Gallus Caesar, administrator of the East. But Caesar proved to be a despotic ruler, and in 354 Constantius recalled him and had him executed. After campaigning against the Sarmatian, Suebi, and Quadi tribes on 
2 CONC the Danube in 357-358, Constantius returned east to fight Shapur, who had renewed his attacks on the eastern frontier (359). In 361 Constantius was recalled to the West by the revolt of Julian, his caesar in Gaul, but became ill o
2 CONC n the way and died.
2 CONT
2 CONT As an advocate of Arian Christianity, Constantius passed laws against paganism and exiled many Roman Catholic bishops. He drove the influential anti-Arian bishop of Alexandria, Athanasius, into hiding in 356. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Copyright © 1994-2001 Encyclopædia Britannica, Inc.
