1 NOTE 521 - 465. KONGE AV PERSIA.
1 NOTE OR "XERXES I"; GREAT KING OF PERSIA 486-485 BC; PRESENT AT BATTLES OF
2 CONT THERMOPYLAE AND SALAMIS 480 BC, AND PLATAEA AND MYKALE 479 BC; b. 521 BC-d.
2 CONT 8/465 BC
1 NOTE He ruled from 485 to 464 b.c.
1 NOTE (Persian Khshayarsha)  Ascending the throne upon the death of his father, 
2 CONT he subdued a rebellion in Egypt, and then spent three years preparing a 
2 CONT great fleet and army to punish the Greeks for aiding the Ionian cities in 
2 CONT 498 bc and for their victory over the Persians at Marathon in 490 bc. The 
2 CONT Greek historian Herodotus gives as the combined strength of Xerxes' land 
2 CONT and naval forces the incredible total of 2,641,610 warriors. Xerxes is 
2 CONT said to have crossed the Hellespont by a bridge of boats more than a 
2 CONT kilometer in length and to have cut a canal through the isthmus of Mount 
2 CONT Athos. During the spring of 480 bc he marched with his forces through 
2 CONT Thrace, Thessaly, and Locris. At Thermopylae 300 Spartans, under their 
2 CONT king, Leonidas I, made a courageous but futile stand, delaying the 
2 CONT Persians for ten days. Xerxes then advanced into Attica and burned Athens, 
2 CONT which had been abandoned by the Greeks. At the Battle of Salamís later in 
2 CONT 480 bc, however, his fleet was defeated by a much smaller contingent of 
2 CONT Greek warships commanded by the Athenian Themistocles. Xerxes thereupon 
2 CONT retired to Asia Minor, leaving his army in Greece under the command of his 
2 CONT brother-in-law, Mardonius (fl. 500-479 bc), who was slain at Plataea the 
2 CONT following year. Xerxes was murdered at Persepolis by Artabanus (died 464 
2 CONT bc), captain of the palace guard; he was succeeded by his son Artaxerxes I 
2 CONT (reigned 465-425 bc). Xerxes is generally identified as the Ahasuerus of 
2 CONT the Book of Esther.
1 NOTE He ruled from 485 to 464 b.c.
1 NOTE RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT "the Great" King of Persia 486-465
