2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helena_of_Constantinople
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Saint Helena (Latin: Flavia Iulia Helena Augusta) also known as Saint Helen, Helena Augusta or Helena of Constantinople (ca. 246/50 – 18 August 330) was the consort of Emperor Constantius, and the mother of Emperor Constantine I. 
2 CONC She is traditionally credited with finding the relics of the True Cross.
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena's birthplace is not known with certainty. The sixth-century historian Procopius is the earliest authority for the statement that Helena was a native of Drepanum, in the province of Bithynia in Asia Minor. Her son Constantin
2 CONC e renamed the city "Helenopolis" after her death in 328, giving rise to the belief that the city was her birthplace. Although he might have done so in honor of her birthplace, Constantine probably had other reasons for doing so. T
2 CONC he Byzantinist Cyril Mango has argued that Helenopolis was refounded to strengthen the communication network around his new capital in Constantinople, and was renamed to honor Helena, not to mark her birthplace. There is another H
2 CONC elenopolis, in Palestine, but its exact location is unknown. This city, and the province of Helenopontus in the Diocese of Pontus, were probably both named after Constantine's mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT The bishop and historian Eusebius of Caesarea states that she was about 80 on her return from Palestine. Since that journey has been dated to 326–28, Helena was probably born in 248 or 250. Little is known of her early life. Fourt
2 CONC h-century sources, following Eutropius' "Breviarium," record that she came from a low background. Saint Ambrose was the first to call her a stabularia, a term translated as "stable-maid" or "inn-keeper". He makes this fact a virtu
2 CONC e, calling Helena a bona stabularia, a "good stable-maid." Other sources, especially those written after Constantine's proclamation as emperor, gloss over or ignore her background.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is unknown where she first met Constantius. The historian Timothy Barnes has suggested that Constantius, while serving under Emperor Aurelian, could have met her while stationed in Asia Minor for the campaign against Zenobia. B
2 CONC arnes calls attention to an epitaph at Nicomedia of one of Aurelian's protectors, which could indicate the emperor's presence in the Bithynian region soon after 270. The precise legal nature of the relationship between Helena and 
2 CONC Constantius is also unknown. The sources are equivocal on the point, sometimes calling Helena Constantius' "wife", and sometimes calling her his "concubine." Jerome, perhaps confused by the vague terminology of his own sources, ma
2 CONC nages to do both. Some scholars, such as the historian Jan Drijvers, assert that Constantius and Helena were joined in a common-law marriage, a cohabitation recognized in fact but not in law. Others, like Timothy Barnes, assert th
2 CONC at Constantius and Helena were joined in an official marriage, on the grounds that the sources claiming an official marriage are more reliable.
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena gave birth to the future emperor Constantine I on the 27th of February of an uncertain year soon after 270 (probably around 272). At the time, she was in Naissus (Niš, Serbia). Constantius divorced Helena at some time befor
2 CONC e 289, when he married Theodora, Maximian's daughter. (The narrative sources date the marriage to 293, but the Latin panegyric of 289 refers to the couple as already married.) Helena never remarried and lived for a while in obscur
2 CONC ity, though close to her only son, who had a deep regard and affection for her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine was proclaimed Augustus of the Roman Empire in 306 by Constantius' troops after the latter had died, and following his elevation his mother was brought back to the public life and the imperial court. She received the t
2 CONC itle of Augusta in 325 and died in 330 with her son at her side. Her sarcophagus is on display in the Pio-Clementine Vatican Museum, although the connection is often questioned. The elaborate reliefs contain hunting scenes. During
2 CONC  her life, she gave many presents to the poor, released prisoners and mingled with the ordinary worshipers in modest attire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sainthood
2 CONT
2 CONT She is considered by the Eastern Orthodox, Oriental Orthodox and Roman Catholic churches as a saint, famed for her piety. Her feast day as a saint of the Orthodox Christian Church is celebrated with her son on 21 May, the "Feast o
2 CONC f the Holy Great Sovereigns Constantine and Helen, Equal to the Apostles." Her feast day in the Roman Catholic Church falls on 18 August. Her feast day in the Coptic Orthodox Church is on 9 Pashons. Eusebius records the details of
2 CONC  her pilgrimage to Palestine and other eastern provinces (though not her discovery of the True Cross). She is the patron saint of new discoveries.
2 CONT
2 CONT Relic discoveries
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine appointed his mother Helen as Augusta, and gave her unlimited access to the imperial treasury in order to locate the relics of Judeo-Christian tradition. In 325, Helena was in charge of such a journey to Jerusalem by h
2 CONC er son. Upon the request of the monks in the region, Helena ordered the construction of a church in Egypt to identify the Burning Bush of Sinai. The chapel, at St. Catherine's Monastery often referred to as the Chapel of Saint Hel
2 CONC en, is dated to the year AD 330.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jerusalem was still rebuilding from the destruction of Emperor Hadrian, who had built a temple to Venus over the site of Jesus's tomb near Calvary. According to legend, Helena entered the temple with Bishop Macarius, ordered the t
2 CONC emple torn down and chose a site to begin excavating, which led to the recovery of three different crosses. Refused to be swayed by anything but solid proof, the empress had a woman from Jerusalem, who was already at the point of 
2 CONC death from a certain disease, brought to her. When the woman touched the first and second crosses, her condition did not change, but when she touched the third and final cross she suddenly recovered[citation needed], and Helena de
2 CONC clared the cross with which the woman had been touched to be the True Cross. On the site of discovery, Constantine built the Church of the Holy Sepulchre as well as on other sites detected by Helena.
2 CONT
2 CONT She also found the nails of the crucifixion. To use their miraculous power to aid her son, Helena allegedly had one placed in Constantine's helmet, and another in the bridle of his horse. Helena left Jerusalem and the eastern prov
2 CONC inces in 327 to return to Rome, bringing with her large parts of the True Cross and other relics, which were then stored in her palace's private chapel, where they can be still seen today. Her palace was later converted into the c
2 CONC hurch Santa Croce in Gerusalemme.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to one tradition, Helena acquired the Holy Tunic on her trip to Jerusalem and sent it to Trier.
2 CONT
2 CONT Several of Saint Helena's treasures are now in Cyprus, where she spent some time. Some of them are a part of Jesus Christ's tunic, pieces of the holy cross and the world's only pieces of the rope to which Jesus was tied with on th
2 CONC e Cross. The latter has been held at the Staurovouni monastery, which was also founded by Saint Helena.
2 CONT
2 CONT A Cathedral was named after her in Helena, Montana.
2 CONT
2 CONT Depictions in British folklore
2 CONT
2 CONT In Great Britain, later legend, mentioned by Henry of Huntingdon but made popular by Geoffrey of Monmouth, claimed that Helena was a daughter of the King of Britain, Cole of Camulodunum, who allied with Constantius to avoid more w
2 CONC ar between the Britons and Rome. Geoffrey further states that she was brought up in the manner of a queen, as she had no brothers to inherit the throne of Britain. The source for this may have been Sozomen's Historia Ecclesiastica
2 CONC , which however does not claim Helena was British but only that her son Constantine picked up his Christianity there. Constantine was with his father when he died in Eboracum (York), but neither had spent much time in Britain. The
2 CONC re is no other surviving evidence to support this legend, which may be due to confusion with Saint Elen, wife of the usurper Magnus Maximus.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT At least twenty-five holy wells currently exist in the United Kingdom that are dedicated to Saint Helena. She is also the patron saint of Colchester and Abingdon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adrian Gilbert has argued that Helena traveled to Nevern in Wales where she hid the True Cross. near the local Norman church of St Brynach, where a cross is carved into a rock formation. Named the Pilgrim's Cross, religious pilgri
2 CONC ms once came here to pray for visions. Names of local places are abundant with cross imagery, including "River of the Empress," "Mountain of the Cross," "Pass of the Cross" and others. The True Cross, however, has not been found i
2 CONC n this region.
2 CONT
2 CONT Depictions in fiction
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena is the protagonist of Evelyn Waugh's novel Helena. She is also the main character of Priestess of Avalon (2000), a fantasy novel by Marion Zimmer Bradley and Diana L. Paxson. She is given the name Eilan and depicted as a tr
2 CONC ained priestess of Avalon. In the anime and manga, Hellsing, the Nail of Helena is a powerful artifact used by the Paladin Alexander Anderson to gain supernatural power.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT At age 80, Helen travelled over 1400 miles from Rome to Jerusalem to gather Christian relics.  According to legend, she discovered the true cross of Christ.  Her feast falls on August 18.  She is the patron saint of archaeologists
2 CONC .  She is also the patron saint of our own Helen Budz!
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Reference:  http://familytrees.genopro.com/318186/jarleslekt/default.htm?page=toc_families.htm
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT (Latin: Flavia Iulia Helena Augusta) also known as Saint Helen, Helena Augusta or Helena of Constantinople
2 CONT
2 CONT See: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helena_of_Constantinople
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/c/i/p/Susan-D-Cipp/WEBSITE-0001/UHP-0678.html
2 CONT
2 CONT http://larryvoyer.com/genealogy/getperson.php?personID=I46631&tree=v7_28
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Sainte Hélène & Reine d'Angleterre -
2 CONT
2 CONT Heilige Helena & Koningin van Engeland -
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Helen & Queen of England
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Helena's birthplace is not known with certainty. The sixth-century historian Procopius is the earliest authority for the statement that Helena was a native of Drepanum, in the province of Bithynia in Asia Minor. Her son Constantin
2 CONC e renamed the city "Helenopolis" after her death in 328, giving rise to the belief that the city was her birthplace.[2]  Although he might have done so in honor of her birthplace, Constantine probably had other reasons for doing s
2 CONC o. The Byzantinist Cyril Mango has argued that Helenopolis was refounded to strengthen the communication network around his new capital in Constantinople, and was renamed to honor Helena, not to mark her birthplace.[3]  There was 
2 CONC also a Helenopolis in Palestine (modern Daburiyya)[4][5]  and a Helenopolis in Lydia.[6]  These cities, and the province of Helenopontus in the Diocese of Pontus, were probably both named after Constantine's mother.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The bishop and historian Eusebius of Caesarea states that she was about 80 on her return from Palestine.[7] Since that journey has been dated to 326–28, Helena was probably born in 248 or 250. Little is known of her early life.[8]
2 CONC  Fourth-century sources, following Eutropius' "Breviarium," record that she came from a low background. Saint Ambrose was the first to call her a stabularia, a term translated as "stable-maid" or "inn-keeper". He makes this fact a
2 CONC  virtue, calling Helena a bona stabularia, a "good stable-maid".[9] Other sources, especially those written after Constantine's proclamation as emperor, gloss over or ignore her background.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT It is unknown where she first met Constantius.[10] The historian Timothy Barnes has suggested that Constantius, while serving under Emperor Aurelian, could have met her while stationed in Asia Minor for the campaign against Zenobi
2 CONC a. Barnes calls attention to an epitaph at Nicomedia of one of Aurelian's protectors, which could indicate the emperor's presence in the Bithynian region soon after 270.[11] The precise legal nature of the relationship between Hel
2 CONC ena and Constantius is also unknown. The sources are equivocal on the point, sometimes calling Helena Constantius' "wife", and sometimes calling her his "concubine".[10] Jerome, perhaps confused by the vague terminology of his own
2 CONC  sources, manages to do both.[12] Some scholars, such as the historian Jan Drijvers, assert that Constantius and Helena were joined in a common-law marriage, a cohabitation recognized in fact but not in law.[13] Others, like Timot
2 CONC hy Barnes, assert that Constantius and Helena were joined in an official marriage, on the grounds that the sources claiming an official marriage are more reliable.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena gave birth to the future emperor Constantine I on the 27th of February of an uncertain year soon after 270[15] (probably around 272).[16] At the time, she was in Naissus (Niš, Serbia).[17] Constantius divorced Helena at som
2 CONC e time before 289, when he married Theodora, Maximian's daughter.[18] (The narrative sources date the marriage to 293, but the Latin panegyric of 289 refers to the couple as already married).[19] Helena never remarried and lived f
2 CONC or a while in obscurity, though close to her only son, who had a deep regard and affection for her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine was proclaimed Augustus of the Roman Empire in 306 by Constantius' troops after the latter had died, and following his elevation his mother was brought back to the public life and the imperial court. She received the t
2 CONC itle of Augusta in 325 and died in 330 with her son at her side. Her sarcophagus is on display in the Pio-Clementine Vatican Museum, although the connection is often questioned, next to her is the sarcophagus of her granddaughter 
2 CONC Saint Constantina (Saint Constance). The elaborate reliefs contain hunting scenes. During her life, she gave many presents to the poor, released prisoners and mingled with the ordinary worshipers in modest attire.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT When Constantius was named caesar, or successor to the throne of the Roman Empire, in 293, he divorced her because of her non patrician origin. She devoted the rest of her life to religious pilgrimages, visiting Jerusalem about 32
2 CONC 5 and founding there the Church of the Holy Sepulcher and the Church of the Nativity. According to some legends, she was the discoverer of the True Cross in Palestine. Her feast day is August 18.
2 CONT --------------------
