2 CONT =Quintus Caecilius Metellus=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Quintus Caecilius Metellus (b. ca 130 BC), was the owner of a palace in Tampillium on the Quirinal Hill, with a beautiful hanging garden in oriental style, with towers and terraces surrounded by a grove of trees, besides a house a
2 CONC nd a tomb at the Fifth Mile of the Appian Way, where he was buried, having made his fortune thanks to favours made to him during his campaign in Asia by his relative, perhaps his first cousin, Lucius Licinius Lucullus, son of Luci
2 CONC us Licinius Lucullus, Praetor in 103 BC, and wife Caecilia Metella.
2 CONT
2 CONT Not having issue, he adopted his maternal nephew - son of his sister Caecilia Metella (ca 130-ca 50 BC) and husband Titus Pomponius - Titus Pomponius Atticus, who for that reason became called Quintus Caecilius Pomponianus Atticus
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Filiation'''
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of a Caecilius Metellus (b. ca 160 BC). There is nothing that conclusively identifies his father; he may be the son of Lucius Caecilius Metellus Dalmaticus, Lucius Caecilius Metellus Diadematus or Marcus Caecilius M
2 CONC etellus, or, most likely, Lucius Caecilius Metellus Calvus, given the close connection to the famous Lucius Licinius Lucullus, son of one Caecilia Metella and maternal grandson of Lucius Caecilius Metellus Calvus.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
