2 CONT Note 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1Licinius' full name: ILS , 675, 678-80; such variations as Iovius Licinius ( ILS , 676) or Licinianus Licinius ( ILS , 677, 712-14, 6788) appear on inscriptions. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For Licinius' antecedents and the sources that treat them, see A.H.M. Jones, J.R. Martindale, and J. Morris, The Prosopography of the Later Roman Empire , [Cambridge, 1971], s.v. "Val. Licinianus Licinius 3" 1.509, and T.D. Barnes
2 CONC , New Empire of Diocletian and Constantine , (Cambridge, 1982), 43ff. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For a discussion of the Conference at Carnuntum, see, for example, Barnes, Constantine and Eusebius , (Cambridge, 1981), 31ff, O. Seeck, RE 13, s.v. "Licinius (31a)," 222.40ff, and E Groag, RE 14, s.v. "Maxentius,"col.2430.48ff. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For a discussion of the reign of Licinius, see O. Seeck, RE 13, 222ff, R. Andreotti, Dizinario epigrafico di antichitâ romane 4, s.v. "Licinius[Valerius Licinianus]," 1000ff, and Barnes, Constantine and Eusebius , 32ff. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For a listing of the chronology of the reign of Licinius, see Barnes, New Empire , 80ff; for the proper dating of the events between 314 and 317, see infra n n. 3-4. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2]}For a listing of the sources that treat the chronology of 310 and 312, see supra , n.1. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For a listing of the sources that treat the marriage of Licinius and Constantia, see supra , n.1. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For a discussion of Licinius' second child, see Michael DiMaio, Jörn Zeuge, and Jane Bethune, "The Proelium Cibalense et Proelium Campi Ardiensis : The First Civil War of Constantine I and Licinius I," AncW , 21(1990), 90-91. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For a discussion of the Batle of the Milvian Bridge, the sources that treat it, and the character of Maxentius, see Michael DiMaio, Jörn Zeuge, and Natalia Zotov, " Ambiguitas Constantiniana : the Caeleste Signum Dei of Constantin
2 CONC e the Great," Byzantion , 58(1989), 333ff. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The so-called Edict of Milan is discussed, for example, by O. Seeck, RE 13, col. 222.37ff, idem. , "Das sogenannte Edikte von Mailand," Zeitschrift f&uumlr Kirchengeschichte; ,12(1891, 181ff, DiMaio, Zeuge, and Zotov, Byzantion , 
2 CONC 58(1988), 35ff, and, in passing, by Barnes, Constantine and Eusebius , 318, n. 4. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3Licinius' campaign against Daia is discussed by Barnes, Constantine and Eusebius , 62ff, Seeck, RE 4, s.v. "Daia," col. 1989.56ff, and idem ,RE 13, 233.56ff. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The chronology of Licinius' reign in 313 and early 314 is discussed by Michael DiMaio, et al. ,AncW , 21(1990), 74ff, 83ff. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4The dating of Constantine's civil wars against Licinius is a major source of debate among scholars which will probably never be resolved; this is due to the fact that the literary, numismatic, and legal evidence is so ambiguous. 
2 CONC It has been traditional to date the civil wars between the two rulers to 314 and 324 respectively, following the magisterial arguments of Otto Seeck( Regesten der Kaiser und Päpste für die Jahre 311 bis 476 N. Chr.: Vorarbeit zu e
2 CONC iner Prosopographie der christlichen Kaiserzeit , [Stuttgart, 1919], 162ff; idem. ,RE 13, col. 224.36ff). Based primarily on the numismatic evidence, Patrick Bruun has argued that the campaign of 314 actually occurred in 316( Cons
2 CONC tantinian Coinage of Arelate , [Helsinki, 1953], 17ff; idem. ,Studies in Constantinian Chronology , [New York, 1961], 10ff; idem. ,Roman Imperial Coinage 7: Constantine and Licinius A.D. 313-337 , [London, 1966]), a position follo
2 CONC wed by Barnes("Lactantius and Constantine," Journal of Roman Studies , 63[1973], 36ff; idem. ,Constantine and Eusebius , 65ff, 318ff, and idem. ,New Empire , 71ff, 81ff). Following the lead of R. Andreotti( Dizinario epigrafico di
2 CONC  antichitâ romane 4, 1000ff), I and others have argued the evidence makes more sense if the first campaign was actually fought in 314 and 316( AncW , 21[1990], 87ff). Pohlsander has challenged some of the conclusions of the aforem
2 CONC entioned article (Hans A.Pohlsander, "The Date of the Bellum Cibalense : A Reexamination," AncW 25 (1995), 89-101). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5For a discussion of the chronology of the period between 315 and 317 and the Battle of the Campus Ardiensis is dicussed by ibid. , 79ff. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I and others have written, "Zosimus notes that...Licinius would rule Thrace, the east, and the regions in that area (2.20.1);... Anonymus Valesianus says that Licinius' sphere of influence would include the east, Asia, Thrace, Moe
2 CONC sia, and Scythia Minor (5.18)" ( ibid , 80, n 127.). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6For the chronology of the remainder of Licinius' reign, see Barnes, New Empire , 82. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Licinius and his anti-Christian legislation is discussed by idem. ,Constantine and Eusebius , 72ff, Seeck, RE 13, col. 225.9ff, B.J. Kidd, A History of the Church to A.D. 461 ,(Oxford, 1922), 2.4ff, A. Alföldi, The Conversion of C
2 CONC onstantine and Pagan Rome , (Oxford, 1969), 36ff, 53ff, 82ff, and Joseph Vogt, Reallexicon für Antike und Christentum 3, s.v. "Constantinus der Grosse," col. 337ff.< 
2 CONT
2 CONT The consular Fasti for the period between 319 and 324 are discussed by Michael DiMaio, Zonaras' Account of the Neo-Flavian Emperors , (Ph.D. diss., University of Missouri-Columbia, 1977), 162-163. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7The second conflict between Licinius and Constantine is discussed by Barnes, Constantine and Eusebius , 76ff, Seeck, RE 13, col. 229.5ff, idem. ,RE 4, "Crispus (9).", col. 1723.39ff, Vogt, RAC 3, 337, idem. , "Die Vita Constantin
2 CONC i des Eusebius über den Konflict zwischen Constantin und Licinius," Historia , 2(1953), 463ff, and J. Moreau, " Zum Problen der Vita Constantini," Historia , 4(1955), 237ff. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The arrest and death of Licinius and Martinianus are discussed by Barnes, Constantine and Eusebius , 214, Seeck, RE 13, col. 230.18ff and F. Görres, "Die Verwandtenmorde Constantin's des Grossen," ZWT , 30(1887), 360ff. 
2 CONT
