2 TITL Cyrus the Great - Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 NOTE Cyrus the Great (circa 600-529 bc), king of Persia (550-529 bc). He was 
2 CONT the son of Cambyses I, a descendant of Achaemenes (Hakhamanish) 
2 CONT (flourished 7th century bc), and a member of the Achaemenid dynasty. When 
2 CONT Cyrus became (558 bc) ruler of the Persian district of Anshan, the 
2 CONT district was subject to the Medes; five years later he led a rebellion 
2 CONT against the Medes that resulted in the capture of King Astyages (reigned 
2 CONT about 584-c. 550 bc) and the overthrow (550 bc) of the Median Empire. 
2 CONT Thereafter Cyrus called himself king of Persia and ruled a territory 
2 CONT extending from the Halys River in Asia Minor, eastern border of Lydia, to 
2 CONT the Babylonian Empire on the south and east. Babylon, Egypt, Lydia, and 
2 CONT the city-state of Sparta in Greece combined to curb the power of Cyrus, 
2 CONT but in 546 bc the Persians added Lydia to their realm, and in 539 bc the 
2 CONT kingdom of Babylon fell to Cyrus. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Persian Empire was the most powerful state in the world until its 
2 CONT conquest two centuries later by Alexander the Great. Cyrus was an able and 
2 CONT merciful ruler. Significant among his deeds was his granting of permission 
2 CONT to the Jews to return from their exile in Babylon to their native Israel 
2 CONT to rebuild the Temple of Solomon. Cyrus died while leading an expedition 
2 CONT against the eastern tribe, the Massagetae, and was succeeded by his son, 
2 CONT who became Cambyses II.
1 NOTE SOURCE NOTES:
2 CONT http://www.american-pictures.com/genealogy/persons/per00539.htm#0
