2 CONT Bio at WikipediaHe was put on the throne under the regency of his mother by Theodosius II, Roman emperor of the East (401-50). Valentinian was weak; from 433 to 454 his general Flavius Aetius (396?-454) was the actual ruler. Durin
2 CONC g his reign, much of the Western Empire was ravaged by invaders; Africa was seized (429) by Gaiseric, king of the Vandals, and the Danubian provinces, Gaul, and Italy were overrun (441) by Attila, king of the Huns. Aetius won a gr
2 CONC eat victory over Attila in 451 at Chalons-sur-Marne, Gaul. Valentinian killed Aetius in 454 but was murdered the following year by two of the general's supporters.  --Other Fields Ref Number: + EVEN: Reigned Date: 425/455
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Valentinian III was born in AD 419, the son of Constantius III and Aelia Galla Placidia, who was the half-sister of Honorius.
2 CONT
2 CONT With Honorius having no children of his own, he proclaimed Valentinian III his heir (AD 421 or shortly after). Though in AD 423, when Placidia fled from the court of Honorius due to his advances toward her, she took the young Vale
2 CONC ntinian III and her daughter with her. They took refuge at the court of Theodosius II at Constantinople.
2 CONT
2 CONT Honorius died later in AD 423 and, with the heir apparent absent, his throne was usurped by John, a civil servant who was manoeuvered into position by Castinus, the powerful 'Master of Soldiers' of the west, who was an enemy of Pl
2 CONC acidia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodosius II, opposed to the usurper, now belatedly recognized Constantius III (who had died in AD 421) as Augustus, in order to champion the cause of Valentinian III. The young Valentinian III, in preparation for his taking the 
2 CONC western throne, was elevated to the rank of Caesar in Thessalonica in AD 424. A military expedition was then organized by the eastern emperor to conquer the western capital Ravenna and depose John. Placidia and her children sailed
2 CONC  on the very fleet which set out to Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The two generals Ardaburius and Aspar succeeded in conquering Ravenna and John was captured, sentenced to death by Placidia in Aquileia and executed in May or June AD 425.
2 CONT
2 CONT Valentinian III was hailed Augustus in Rome in AD 425 at the age of six. Placidia became the regent of the western empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Valentinian III, like other emperors of that period (for example Honorius and Theodosius II) was a ruler, who left the governing of his empire to others. At first, during his childhood, he should have his mother Placidia to take c
2 CONC are of government and later rule lay much in the hands of his military chief Aetius. While others governed Valentinian III chose far more to have a good time. He enjoyed physical activity, was said to have been a good runner, and 
2 CONC a skilled horseman and archer. Further he liked the company of astrologers and magicians. And though his wife, Licinia Eudoxia, whom he married in AD 437, was famed for her ravishingly good looks, he is said to have repeatedly had
2 CONC  affairs with married women.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first crisis in Valentinian III's reign was soon to come, when general Aetius arrived with his relief force of Huns only to find that emperor John, whom he sought to save, had already been executed. Naturally the arrival of a 
2 CONC man at the head of such a large army was a threat to the new government. And so Placidia made a deal with Aetius to regain control of this dangerous situation.
2 CONT
2 CONT And so Flavius Aetius was made 'Master of Horse' and sent to Gaul, whilst his army of Huns was paid a large bribe to simply return home.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Gaul Aetius should achieve remarkable successes against the Franks and Visigoths during AD 427-8. Though this should only make Placidia's yet more suspicions towards him. For she evidently feared he sought to usurp the throne. 
2 CONC In spite of this, Aetius' successes forced her to promote him to 'Master of Soldiers' in AD 429.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the same year AD 429 the Vandal leader Geiseric crossed from Spain to north Africa with his people. There he declared himself an independent king, free from Rome or any allegiance to Valentinian III, and set out to conquer Rome
2 CONC 's African domains.
2 CONT
2 CONT In AD 431 Geiseric's Vandals moving east heavily defeated the Romans in north Africa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile back in Europe Placidia hoped for her main army supporter, Bonifatius (Boniface), to rid her of Aetius. Bonifatius had returned from Africa to Italy in AD 429 and ever since war between him and Aetius had been brewing. T
2 CONC he two finally clashed in AD 432. Bonifatius was defeated, wounded and later died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aetius went on to drive the Germans back behind the Upper Danube, put down revolts in Gaul and in AD 437 he smashed the Burgundians as they sought to invade Gaul. Considering the military difficulties of the time and the shortage 
2 CONC of soldiers in the Roman army, these victories were outstanding achievements.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yet the deterioration in north Africa continued. In AD 439 Geiseric conquered Carthage and in AD 441 once again heavily defeated a Roman force sent against him.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the western empire suffering from an ever more desperate shortage of military manpower things only got worse. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Then, in AD 450, the eastern emperor Marcian cancelled the annual subsidy to the Huns.
2 CONT
2 CONT Was this inevitably to encourage Hun aggression, then Justa Grata Honoria, the sister of Valentinian III, played a vital part in the way things should unfold.
2 CONT
2 CONT For Valentinian III sought to arrange a marriage for her, a marriage to an elderly dignitary of which Honoria disapproved.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hence she now took the drastic step of sending her signet ring to Attila, king of the Huns, urging him to come to her rescue.
2 CONT
2 CONT Attila interpreted this as an offer of marriage and demanded no less than half the western empire as his dowry. Naturally this was rejected, which caused Attila to lead his terrifying Huns into Gaul. In AD 451 on the Catalaunian P
2 CONC lains (Châlons) Aetius, together with the Visigoths, met Attila's Huns who were reinforced by many German tribes.
2 CONT
2 CONT The battle saw the death of the Visigoth king Theodoric I, but Attila was decisively beaten. Though instead of withdrawing into back into Germany, Attila in AD 452 moved into Italy, with Aetius unable to stop him.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Huns captured and sacked Mediolanum (Milan), destroyed Aquileia and then marched on Rome. Aetius, without the Visigoths, could not possibly take on an army the size of Attila's and was forced to stand by as this happened. But 
2 CONC as Rome lay helpless to the menacing Huns, pope Leo I somehow persuaded Attila to turn back and leave the ancient city unscathed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Attila withdrew and in AD 453 died. His empire fell apart almost at once, the Hun menace to the empire disappearing as fast at it had arisen.
2 CONT
2 CONT In AD 453 Aetius son was betrothed to the Valentinian III's daughter Placidia. Aetius thereafter was granted his fourth consulship in AD 454. He stood at the height of his power, having effectively ruled the western empire at the 
2 CONC head of the military for two decades.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, at court he had influential and deadly enemies, foremost among them, the former praetorian and city prefect Petronius Maximus and the emperor's chamberlain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Having been threatened by Aetius they conspired with Valentinian III, who himself felt very intimidated by Aetius to murder him.
2 CONT
2 CONT In September AD 454, while Aetius was delivering a financial account, Valentinian III and Heraclius attacked him in the throne room and stabbed him to death.
2 CONT
2 CONT But with the powerful Aetius no longer in control, the court was gripped by intrigues.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the death of Aetius, the powerful Petronius Maximus now hoped to take his place, but Heraclius prevented that, managing to persuade Valentinian III that this would be a dangerous appointment.
2 CONT
2 CONT Angry, Maximus sought revenge. And so he found two Scythians (or Huns), called Optila and Thraustila, who whose loyalty still demanded vengeance for the murder of their master Aetius.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 16 March AD 455, Optila and Thraustila were among the guards of the imperial entourage as the emperor practised archery on the Campus Martius at Rome. Everyone was taken by surprise as Thraustila stepped forward and murdered He
2 CONC raclius and Optila struck the head of Valentinian III, causing wounds from which he died.
2 CONT
