2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopediaJump to: navigation, search 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gratian from "Promptuarii Iconum Insigniorum "Gratianus Funarius, also known as Gratianus Major (Major Latin for the elder), also known as Gratian the Elder, was a soldier of the Roman Empire who flourished in the 4th century. He 
2 CONC is called Gratian the Elder, to distinguish himself from his grandson the Roman Emperor Gratian.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT Gratianus originated from an obscure Christian family from the town of Cibalae or Cibalis, Southern Pannonia (modern Vinkovci, Croatia). There Gratianus seems to have married a local woman, and they had two sons Flavius Valentinia
2 CONC nus (321-375) and Flavius Iulius (Julius) Valens (328-378). His first son would become Roman Emperor of the West and his second son would become Roman Emperor of the East.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gratianus obtained the cognomen Funarius, which means ‘the rope-man’, because prior to his military career, Gratianus was a rope salesman. On one occasion, he successfully resisted the efforts of five soldiers to wrestle him. That
2 CONC  occasion inspired Gratianus to enlist in the Roman Army.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Military & Political Career
2 CONT
2 CONT Gratianus was renowned for his strength and his wrestling skills, and had an illustrious army career. He rose from Staff Officer to be promoted to Military Tribune and became a Comes Africae. In the 340s, Gratianus was sent to Bri
2 CONC tannia and was given the title Comes Britanniarum. He commanded a field army (comitatenses). The crisis that precipitated this is not known, however.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his army career ended, Gratianus returned to his birthplace and lived as a private citizen who had earned a good reputation from his military career. Gratianus could have earned the praetorship.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Gratianus’ retirement, Roman Emperor Constantius II (reigned 337-360) confiscated all of his estates because of his suspected support of Roman usurper Magnentius. Gratianus was kept in high regard with fellow soldiers due to hi
2 CONC s popularity within the army; this could have contributed to the elevation of his sons and his descendants in becoming Roman Emperors. When Valens became Emperor, the Roman Senate in Constantinople decreed a brass statue of him. H
2 CONC is son became emperor on February 26, 364.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gratian_the_Elder
