2 CONT c)         '''ATTILA''' (-453).  Iordanes names "Attila patre genitus Munduzco"[91].  The Gesta Hungarorum names "ducis…Ethela…Bendacuz filius…de genere Erd oriundi" as one of the four leaders of the "Huni in Scitia"[92].  The Chr
2 CONC onicle of Marcellinus records that "Bleda et Attila fratres" raided Illyria and Thrace in 442[93].  Paulus Diaconus records that "rex Hunnorum Attila…cum fratre Bleba" ruled a kingdom "intra Pannonias Daciamque" and devastated "Ma
2 CONC cedoniam Misiamque et Achaiam utrasque etiam Tracias", in a later passage recounting his attack on the kingdom of Burgundy[94].  Iordanes records that Attila unsuccessfully proposed marriage to "Honoriam Valentiniani principis ger
2 CONC manam, filiam Placidiæ Augustæ"[95].  The exploits of Attila are also fully recorded in later chronicles such as that of Sigebert of Gembloux, who states that he died of apoplexy on his wedding night in 455[96].  This is based on 
2 CONC the account of Iordanes who names "[https://www.geni.com/people/Ildico/6000000180274872831 Ildico]" as Attila's new bride[97].  
2 CONT
