2 CONC  of his military actions in the Notitia Dignitatum, his subordinates Bonifacius and Flavius Aetius were considered more significant in this regard.[1] In 426 he ordered the death of Patroclus, bishop of Arelate, and of Titus, deac
2 CONC on in Rome. The following year he opposed Bonifacius' rebellion in Northern Africa sending some troops to this province
2 CONT
2 CONT According to a recent reconstruction of his familiar bonds, he was an ancestor of Felix, Consul in 511. He was a son of Ennodius and he might have been the son of his father (b. 380) who was the husband of a daughter (b. 385) of F
2 CONC lavius Julius Agricola, Consul of Rome in 421 and perhaps the father of Emperor Avitus, being the parents of Flavius Magnus, Consul of Rome in 460 and Felix Ennodius, Proconsul in Africa in ca 420 or 423.[4]
