1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The Burgundians proclaimed an independent kingdom of Burgundy with its capital at Lyons soon after 476. The kingdom included all of what is now southwestern France and most of Switzerland. Chilpéric and his wife we
2 CONC re murdered by his brother '''[http://www.geni.com/people/Gundobad-King-of-the-Burgundians/6000000011401260582 Gondebad]'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is some confusion around the names and ancestries of Chilperic's wife or wives. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * FMG assumes he had only one wife: The name of Chilperich´s wife is not known. Gregory of Tours records that Chilperich's wife was drowned by her brother-in-law King Gundobad, after he tied a stone around her neck. The ''Liber Hi
2 CONC storiæ Francorum'' records the same event.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Wagner gives only one wife, whom he calls Caratene (died 506), no ancestry given. She was the mother of St. Clotilda, wife of Clovis I. Some Geni contributors say she was a daughter of Gundicar.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Some Geni contributors, on unknown authority show a second wife, Agrippina, said to have been a ''sister'' of Ricimer, not a daughter. However, Wagner says Chilperich was a son of an unnamed sister of Ricimir.
2 CONT
2 CONT = Sources =
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BURGUNDY%20KINGS.htm#_Toc311018094 Chilperich 473-486] at Foundation for Medieval Genealogy, visited Aug. 20, 2013.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sir Anthony Wagner, ''Pedigree and Progress: Essays in the genealogical interpretation of history'' (1975), Pedigree 27.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The Burgundians proclaimed an independent kingdom of Burgundy with its capital at Lyons soon after 476. The kingdom included all of what is now southwestern France and most of Switzerland. Chilpéric and his wife were murdered by h
2 CONC is brother Gondebad.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is some confusion around the names and ancestries of Chilperic's wife or wives.
2 CONT
2 CONT FMG assumes he had only one wife: The name of Chilperich´s wife is not known. Gregory of Tours records that Chilperich's wife was drowned by her brother-in-law King Gundobad, after he tied a stone around her neck. The Liber Histor
2 CONC iæ Francorum records the same event.
2 CONT Wagner gives only one wife, whom he calls Caratene (died 506), no ancestry given. She was the mother of St. Clotilda, wife of Clovis I. Some Geni contributors say she was a daughter of Gundicar. Some Geni contributors, on unknown 
2 CONC authority show a second wife, Agrippina, said to have been a sister of Ricimer, not a daughter. However, Wagner says Chilperich was a son of an unnamed sister of Ricimir. Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Chilperich 473-486 at Foundation for Medieval Genealogy, visited Aug. 20, 2013. Sir Anthony Wagner, Pedigree and Progress: Essays in the genealogical interpretation of history (1975), Pedigree 27.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The Burgundians proclaimed an independent kingdom of Burgundy with its capital at Lyons soon after 476. The kingdom included all of what is now southwestern France and most of Switzerland. Chilpéric and his wife were murdered by h
2 CONC is brother Gondebad.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is some confusion around the names and ancestries of Chilperic's wife or wives.
2 CONT
2 CONT FMG assumes he had only one wife: The name of Chilperich´s wife is not known. Gregory of Tours records that Chilperich's wife was drowned by her brother-in-law King Gundobad, after he tied a stone around her neck. The Liber Histor
2 CONC iæ Francorum records the same event. Wagner gives only one wife, whom he calls Caratene (died 506), no ancestry given. She was the mother of St. Clotilda, wife of Clovis I. Some Geni contributors say she was a daughter of Gundicar
2 CONC . Some Geni contributors, on unknown authority show a second wife, Agrippina, said to have been a sister of Ricimer, not a daughter. However, Wagner says Chilperich was a son of an unnamed sister of Ricimir. Sources
