2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia entry for Clotaire I:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clotaire_I
2 CONT
2 CONT Chlothar I (or Chlothachar, Chlotar, Clothar, Clotaire, Chlotochar, or Hlothar, giving rise to Lothair; 497 – 561), called the Old (le Vieux), King of the Franks, was one of the four sons of Clovis. He was born about 497 in Soisso
2 CONC ns (now in Aisne département, Picardie, France).
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of his father in 511, he received, as his share of the kingdom, the town of Soissons, which he made his capital; the cities of Laon, Noyon, Cambrai, and Maastricht; and the lower course of the Meuse River. But he was 
2 CONC very ambitious, and sought to extend his domain.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the chief instigator of the murder of his brother Chlodomer's children in 524, and his share of the spoils consisted of the cities of Tours and Poitiers. He took part in various expeditions against Burgundy and, after the d
2 CONC estruction of that kingdom in 534, obtained Grenoble, Die, and some of the neighbouring cities.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Ostrogoths ceded Provence to the Franks, he received the cities of Orange, Carpentras, and Gap. In 531, he marched against the Thuringii with his nephew Theudebert I and in 542, with his brother Childebert I against the V
2 CONC isigoths of Spain. On the death of his great-nephew Theodebald in 555, Clotaire annexed his territories. On Childebert's death in 558 he became sole king of the Franks.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also ruled over the greater part of Germany, made expeditions into Saxony, and for some time exacted from the Saxons an annual tribute of 500 cows. The end of his reign was troubled by internal dissensions, his son Chram rising
2 CONC  against him on several occasions. Following Chram into Brittany, where the rebel had taken refuge, Clotaire shut him up with his wife and children in a cottage, which he set on fire. Overwhelmed with remorse, he went to Tours to 
2 CONC implore forgiveness at the tomb of St Martin, and died shortly afterwards.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Clotaire's first marriage was to Guntheuc, widow of his own brother Chlodomer, sometime around 524. They had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT His second marriage, which occurred around 532, was to Radegund, daughter of Bertachar, King of Thuringia, whom he and his brother Theuderic defeated. She was later canonized. They had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT His third and most successful marriage was to Ingund, by whom he had five sons and two daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT *1. Gunthar, predeceased father
2 CONT *2. Childeric, predeceased father
2 CONT *3. Charibert, King of Paris
2 CONT *4. Guntram, King of Burgundy
2 CONT *5. Sigebert, King of Austrasia
2 CONT *6. Chlothsind, married Alboin, King of the Lombards
2 CONT
2 CONT His next marriage was to a sister of Ingund, Aregund, with whom he had a son:
2 CONT
2 CONT *7. Chilperic, King of Soissons
2 CONT
2 CONT His last wife was Chunsina (or Chunsine), with whom he had one son:
2 CONT
2 CONT *8. Chram, who became his father's enemy and predeceased him
2 CONT
2 CONT Chlothar may have married and repudiated Waldrada.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Bachrach, Bernard S. (1972). Merovingian Military Organization, 481–751. Minneapolis: University of Minnesota Press, ISBN 0 81660 621 8.
2 CONT * Geary, Patrick J. (1988). Before France and Germany: The Creation and Transformation of the Merovingian World. Oxford: Oxford University Press, ISBN 0 19504 458 4.
2 CONT * James, Edward (1991). The Franks. London: Blackwell, ISBN 0 63114 872 8.
2 CONT * Oman, Charles (1914). The Dark Ages, 476–918. London: Rivingtons.
2 CONT * Wallace-Hadrill, J. M. (1962). The Long-Haired Kings, and Other Studies in Frankish History. London: Methuen.
2 CONT * Wood, Ian N. (1994). The Merovingian Kingdoms, 450–751. London: Longman, ISBN 0 58221 878 0.
2 CONT
2 CONT Chlothar I, Merovingian Dynasty
2 CONT *Born: 497 Died: 561 
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by Clovis I  King of Soissons 511–558  Succeeded by Chilperic I 
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by Chlodomer  King of Orleans 524–558  Succeeded by Guntram 
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by Theudebald  King of Reims 555–558  Succeeded by Sigebert I, Moved to Metz 
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by Childebert I  King of Paris 558  Succeeded by Charibert I  Vacant: Title last held by Clovis I King of the Franks 558–561 Vacant  Title next held by Clotaire II 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT De la página de Wikipedia en español para Clotario I:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clotario_I
2 CONT
2 CONT Clotario I LeVieux, nacido hacia el año 497 y fallecido el 29 de noviembre del 561, fue un monarca del reino de los francos, hijo menor de Clodoveo y de Clotilde. Fue rey de Neustria (511-561), de Orleans (532-561), de Borgoña, co
2 CONC mpartido con su hermano Childeberto (534-558), de Austrasia (555-561) y de París y de Borgoña (558-561).
2 CONT
2 CONT Reinado 
2 CONT
2 CONT En el reparto del Regnum Francorum, a la muerte de su padre en el 511, Clotario heredó la Neustria. Se casó con Ingonda en el 517.
2 CONT
2 CONT A la muerte de su hermano Clodomiro (524), Clotario se casó con su viuda, Gondioque, pero ni siquiera de este modo obtuvo el territorio de su hermano difunto, puesto que la ley sálica impuso la partición del reino entre los hijos 
2 CONC de Clodomiro. Para evitarlo, Clotario se alió con Childeberto para organizar el asesinato de sus jóvenes herederos (532). De los tres hermanos, dos fueron asesinados, y el último (Clodoaldo) renunció a su parte y escogió la vida m
2 CONC onástica. Así, Clotario y Childeberto, se pudieron repartir libremente el territorio de su hermano.
2 CONT
2 CONT En el año 531, se casó con Radegunda, quien prefirió retirarse a un convento en vez de convivir a su lado, fundando en Poitiers la abadía de la Santa Cruz, la primera abadía femenina de Europa. Fue canonizada como Santa Radegunda.
2 CONC  Clotario se volvió a casar en el 532 con Ingonda y luego con su hermana Arnegonda.
2 CONT
2 CONT La muerte de Teodobaldo (nieto de Teodorico, su hermano difunto) en el 555, y la de Childeberto (sin descendencia) en el 558, permitieron a Clotario reunificar el Regnum Francorum de su padre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aún volvió a contraer un nuevo matrimonio con Chunsina, y luego con Vulderade (555) viuda de Teodobaldo, rey de Austrasia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Su reinado estuvo marcado por numerosas campañas militares, entre las que cabe destacar la guerra contra los burgundios (523-526); la campaña de Turingia (530); la invasión de Borgoña (534), en compañía de su hermano Childeberto (
2 CONC donde se repartieron la corona); o la tentativa fallida de la invasión de España (542), con Childeberto,aunque fueron rechazados en Zaragoza.
2 CONT
2 CONT A finales de su reinado tuvo problemas con su hijo Cramne, que se reveló contra él en numerosas ocasiones. Siguió a Cramne hasta Inglaterra, donde se había refugiado, y Clotario lo encerró a él y a su mujer e hijos en una granja y
2 CONC  le prendió fuego. Sobrecogido por el remordimiento, fue a Tours a implorar perdón sobre la tumba de San Martín. Murió poco después, en Compiègne, el 29 de noviembre.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Encyclopedia Britannica (Forrás / Source):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.britannica.com/EBchecked/topic/113816/Chlotar-I
2 CONT
2 CONT Chlotar I
2 CONT *Merovingian king
2 CONT *born c. 500?
2 CONT *died  late 561, Compiègne, Fr.
2 CONT
2 CONT Merovingian king of Soissons from 511 and of the whole Frankish kingdom from 558, who played an important part in the extension of Frankish hegemony.
2 CONT
2 CONT The youngest of Clovis I’s sons, Chlotar shared in the partition of his father’s kingdom in 511, receiving the old heartlands of the Salian Franks in modern northern France and Belgium. After the death of his brother, Clodomir, in
2 CONC  524, he murdered his nephews and shared the kingdom of Orléans with his two remaining brothers, Childebert I and Theodoric I. The deaths without heirs of the latter’s grandson, Theodebald, in 555 and of Childebert in 558 brought 
2 CONC all the Frankish lands finally under Chlotar’s sway.
2 CONT
2 CONT Chlotar’s principal campaigns were against the Burgundians in 532–534 (when he and Childebert finally imposed Frankish rule), against the Visigoths in 541 (with Childebert), and against the Thuringians in 531 (with Theodoric); he 
2 CONC married the Thuringian princess, Radegunda. In 535 he joined the other Merovingian kings in a military treaty with Justinian. Chlotar was ruthless and brutal, and the family rivalries and sometimes open hostilities in which he fig
2 CONC ured prominently were characteristic of Merovingian history; in 560 he even had his rebellious son, Chram, together with Chram’s family, put to death. Gregory of Tours describes Chlotar, wracked with fever on his deathbed, asking—
2 CONC whether in outrage or in admiration—what manner of heavenly king it was who would bring great rulers to their deaths in such a fashion.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Unknown Spanish biography:
2 CONT
2 CONT Quando seu pai morreu em 511, ele recebeu, como sua parte do reino, a cidade de Soissons, da qual ele fez sua capital; as cidades de Laon, Noyon, Cambrai e Maastricht; e o baixo curso do rio Mosa. Mas ele era muito ambicioso, e te
2 CONC ntou ampliar seus domínios.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ele foi o principal instigador do assassinato dos filhos de seu irmão Clodomiro em 524, e sua parte na divisão dos espólios consistiu nas cidades de Tours e Poitiers. Ele tomou parte em várias expedições contra a Borgonha e, depoi
2 CONC s da destruição daquele reino em 534, obteve Grenoble, Die e algumas das cidades vizinhas. Quando os ostrogodos cederam Provença aos francos, ele recebeu as cidades de Orange, Carpentras e Gap.Em 531, ele marchou contra os turíngi
2 CONC os junto ao seu irmão Teodorico I e em 542, com seu irmão Childeberto I contra os visigodos da Espanha. Com a morte de seu sobrinho neto Teodebaldo em 555, Clotário anexou seus territórios. Com a morte de Chideberto em 558 ele se 
2 CONC tornou rei único dos francos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ele também governou sobre grande parte da Alemanha, fez expedições no interior da Saxônia e por algum tempo exigiu dos saxões um tributo anual de 500 vacas. O fim de seu reinado foi perturbado por divergências internas. Seu filho 
2 CONC Cram se levantou contra ele em várias ocasiões. Seguindo Cram na Borgonha, onde o rebelde havia se refugiado, Clotário o prendeu com sua mulher e filhos numa cabana, queimando-a. Impressionado de remorso, ele retornou a Tours para
2 CONC  implorar perdão na tumba de Saint Martin, morrendo pouco tempo depois.
2 CONT
