1 NOTE Source: THE RUFUS PARKS PEDIGREE by Brian J.L. Berry, chart page 61.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 66:
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.   CHLOTAIRE I, 499-561.  Upon the death of CLOVIS the kingdom was divided among his four sons, but as each died a redistribution took place until CHLOTAIRE, the youngest had it all in 558.  His realm extended from the Rhinme to
2 CONC  the Channel and southward into Aquitaine.  He mar. (1) St. REDEGONDE, dau of a Thuringian Frankish king whom he had deteated in battle, taking the princess as booty.  Soon after their marriage, she founded a convent at Poitiers w
2 CONC here she became a nun in 554 and where she died.  When CHLOTAIRE d., the kingdom again was divided among four sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT !Availability: The libraries of Ken, Karen, Kristen, Kevin, Brian, Amy, Adam and FAL.
2 CONT !no Weis
1 NOTE Original individual @P3960234247@ (@MS_TREE2.GED0_15GM2@) merged with @P3960391565@ (@MS_TREE2.GED0_15GM2@)
1 NOTE 511 King of Soisson
2 CONT 524 King ofOrleans
2 CONT 558 King of France
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Soissons 511-558
2 CONT King of Austrasia 555-558
2 CONT King of Franks 558-561
2 CONT
2 CONT Chlotar, King of Franks
2 CONT Born: ?
2 CONT Died: 561
2 CONT Father: Clovis I, King of Franks
2 CONT Mother: Saint Clotilda
2 CONT Married (1): Guntheuc
2 CONT Children: ?
2 CONT Married (2): Radegund
2 CONT Children: ?
2 CONT Married (3): Ingund
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT Gunthar
2 CONT Childeric
2 CONT Charibert I, King of Paris
2 CONT Guntram, King of Burgundy
2 CONT Sigebert I, King of Metz
2 CONT Chlothsind
2 CONT Married (4): Aregund
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT Chilperic I, King of Soissons
2 CONT Married (1): Chunsina
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT Chramn
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Clovis I, King of Franks, he inherited Soissons on his death in511. He, with his four brothers, attacked and defeated Burgundy under thekings Sigisbert and Godomar early in his reign. With his oldest brotherTheuderic I, Kin
2 CONC g of Metz, he attacked the Thuringian Franks under KingHermanfrid, took the kingdom, and took his daughter Radegund. Next, withhis brother Childebert I, King of Paris, Chlotar murdered his nephews whowere under the care of Queen C
2 CONC lotilda his mother. When Theuderic died,the kingdom was up for grabs. Chlotar and Childebert each received only asmall part, the most of it going to Theudebert, his son. In 555,Theudebald, who had succeeded his father Theudebert i
2 CONC n Austrasia, died,and Austrasia passed to Chlotar. When Childebert died in 558, Paris fellto Chlotar as well, thus making him sole ruler of the Franks. WhenChlotar died in 561, the kingdom was divided among his 4 living sons:Chari
2 CONC bert (Paris), Guntram (Burgundy), Chilperic (Soissons), and Sigebert(Metz).
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.ghg.net/shetler/oldimp/009.html
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Clothaire I, King of The Franks Franks [King of The Sessions] , son ofClovis I "The Christian", King of The Franks, and the Saint Queen of TheFranks, Clothilde, was born about 497 in Rheims, Marne, Loire-Alantique,France. He died 
2 CONC on 23 Nov 561 in Braines, Loire Atlantique, France, aboutage 64, and was buried in St. Medard Abbey, Soissons, Aisne, France.Another name for Clothaire was Clotaire I King of Franks.
2 CONT
2 CONT General Notes: He was a man of shocking character, "sensual and a brute",the Abbe Aigrain justly calls him. His matrimonial alliances have neverbeen properly disentangled; he was married at least 5 times and it iseven possible tha
2 CONC t his union with Radegund was polygamous.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 531 Theodoric, King of Austrasia, and his half-brother, Clotaire I,King of Neustria, fell upon Hermenefrid, vanquished him, and carried homea great booty. Among the prisoners, Radegund, then about 12 years old,fell to the lot o
2 CONC f King Clotaire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among other enormities, he burned alive in a cottage his own son andgrandchildren. He is said to have died penitent; during his last illnesshe showed great alarm and disturbance of mind at the remembrance of hiscrimes. (Lives of t
2 CONC he Saints)
2 CONT
2 CONT Alt Birth: Abt 487 of, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Noted events in his life were:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Fact 1: Reigned As Sole King 558-561.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Fact 2: Aka Clotaire I King of The Franks.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Fact 3: King of Soissons 511-561.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Fact 4: Had Six Wives.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clothaire married Ingonde (Ingunde) Thuringia [Queen of The Franks] about518 in France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children from this marriage were:
2 CONT
2 CONT         i. Guntramm of the Franks King of Orléans (born of Orléans,Loiret, France - died on 28 Mar 592 in France)
2 CONT         ii. Gonthier (Gunther), Prince of The Franks Prince of Franks(born about 519 France - died in 561)
2 CONT        iii. Charibert I , King of Paris (born in 520 Paris, Seine, France- died on 7 May 567-568 , buried in St. Vincent Abbey, Paris, Seine,France)
2 CONT         iv. Gontran , King of Orléans (born in 525 of, Orléans, Loiret,France - died on 28 Mar 592-593 in France)
2 CONT         v. Clododinde, Queen of The Lombards of The Franks (born about530 France - died)
2 CONT         vi. Sigebert I, King of Metz King of Austrasia (born about 535 ofMetz, Moselle, France - died about 575-579 in Metz, Moselle, France)
2 CONT        vii. Blithildus , Princess of Sessions (born about 531 - died in570)
2 CONT        viii. Chilperic I De Soissons King of Franks (born about 523 ofSoissons, Aisne, France - died in Oct 584 in France)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Clothaire next married Chunsena (Gunsine, Gunsinde) of The Franks, notMarried. Chunsena (Gunsine, Gunsinde) of The Franks died.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Clothaire next married Valtrude (Valdrade) ,Princess of The Lombards[Concubine 3], not Married.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [FAVthomas.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT    Merovingian king of Soissons from 511 and of the whole Frankishkingdom from 558, who played an important part in the extension ofFrankish hegemony.
2 CONT    The youngest of Clovis I's sons, Chlotar shared in the partition ofhis father's kingdom in 511, receiving the old heartlands of the SalianFranks in modern northern France and Belgium. After the death of hisbrother, Clodomir, in
2 CONC  524, he murdered his nephews and shared the kingdomof Orléans with his two remaining brothers, Childebert I and Theodoric I.The deaths without heirs of the latter's grandson, Theodebald, in 555 andof Childebert in 558 brought all
2 CONC  the Frankish lands finally underChlotar's sway.
2 CONT    Chlotar's principal campaigns were against the Burgundians in 523(with Childebert and
2 CONT Clodomir) and 532/534 (when he and Childebert finally imposed Frankishrule), against the Visigoths in 532 (with Theodoric) and 542 (withChildebert), and against the Thuringians in c. 531 (with Theodoric); hemarried the Thuringian 
2 CONC princess, Radegunda. Chlotar was ruthless andbrutal, and the family rivalries and sometimes open hostilities in whichhe figured prominently were characteristic of Merovingian history; in 560he even had his rebellious son, Chram, t
2 CONC ogether with Chram's family, putto death. Gregory of Tours describes Chlotar, wracked with fever on hisdeathbed, asking—whether in outrage or in admiration, what manner ofheavenly king it was who would bring great rulers to their 
2 CONC deaths in sucha fashion.
2 CONT
2 CONT To cite this page: "Chlotar I" Encyclopædia Britannica
2 CONT  <http://www.britannica.com/eb/article?eu=84440&tocid=0&query=chlotar%20i>
