2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000205596575871&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The striding lion, with its tail curved upwards, was prominently used as a royal symbol during the Neo-Babylonian Empire, appearing on a large number of bricks, seals and contracts. Such lions were also, together with other animal
2 CONC s, prominently used in the reliefs of Babylon's Procession Street. The symbol was also used to denote royal property, with objects belonging to the king being marked with a striding lion, together with a short inscription naming t
2 CONC he king.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Lion in the bas-relief on the Ishtar Gate at the Pergamon Museum in Berlin'''.
