2 CONT ===Profile images 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/photo/view?album_type=photos_of_me&id=6000000002235950530&photo_id=6000000162663237007&position=1 “Corona férrea”] -  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron_Crown The Iron Crown (in Italian, Latin, and Lombard:
2 CONC  Corona Ferrea; German: Eiserne Krone) is a reliquary votive crown, traditionally considered one of the oldest royal insignia of Christendom. It was made in the Middle Ages, consisting of a circlet of gold and jewels fitted around
2 CONC  a central silver band, which tradition held to be made of iron beaten out of a nail of the True Cross. In the later Middle Ages, the crown came to be seen as a heritage from the Kingdom of the Lombards and was used as regalia for
2 CONC  the coronation of some Holy Roman Emperors as kings of Italy. It is kept in the Duomo of Monza. … Old research dates the crown to the 8th or early 9th century.[note 2] However, according to a more recent study, the crown in its c
2 CONC urrent state is the result of two different works made between the 4–5th and the 9th century. This seems to validate the legends about the origin of the crown, that date it back to the Lombard era.[1] In reality, the Lombard royal
2 CONC ty ignored the coronation ceremonies.
2 CONT ** 1. Corona Ferrea, Museo e Tesoro del Duomo di Monza" (in Italian). < [http://www.museoduomomonza.it/corona-ferrea/ link] > Recenti indagini scientifiche fanno prospettare che la Corona, che così come si presenta deriva da inter
2 CONC venti realizzati tra il IV–V e il IX secolo, possa essere un'insegna reale tardo-antica, forse ostrogota, passata ai re longobardi... [Recent scientific investigations envisage that the Crown, which as it is now is derived from wo
2 CONC rks carried between the 4th–5th and 9th centuries, can be a royal insignias of Late Antiquity, perhaps Ostrogoths, passed to the Lombard kings...] 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/photo/view?album_type=photos_of_me&id=6000000002235950530&photo_id=6000000219134271852&position=0 Les royaumes barbares, vers 560] - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Audoin Map of Europe at the end of Audoin's
2 CONC  reign; his kingdom is marked ''Royaume Lombard'' (Kingdom of the Lombards)
