2 CONC f Tours names Childebert as son of King Clovis and his wife Clotilde, listed after Chlodomer and before Clotaire[120].  "Theodorico, Chlomiro, Hildeberto, Hlodario" are named (in order) as sons of "Chlodoveus" in the Regum Merowin
2 CONC gorum Genealogia[121].  He succeeded his father in 511 as CHILDEBERT I King of the Franks, at Paris, his territory covering the Seine and Somme valleys as well as the northern coast of France as far as Brittany, Nantes and Angers.
2 CONC   Gregory of Tours records that King Childebert attacked and defeated Amalric King of the Visigoths[122], which marked the end of the Visigothic government in France and the start of the transfer of their power-base to Spain.  He 
2 CONC and his brother Clotaire launched a third attack on Burgundy, besieged Autun and occupied the whole kingdom, deposing King Gondemar II[123].  Gregory of Tours records that the childless King Childebert adopted his nephew Theodeber
2 CONC t as his heir after the death of the latter's father[124].  He founded the monastery in Paris which, from the end 11th century, was called Saint-Germain-des-Prés.  Gregory of Tours records the death of King Childebert in Paris and
2 CONC  his burial in the church of St Vincent[125].  The Marii Episcopi Aventicensis Chronica records the death in 558 of "Childebertus rex Francorum"[126].  '''m [https://www.geni.com/people/Ultragotha/6000000009034387059?through=60000
