1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://www.geni.com/people/M%C3%A9rovech-I-King-of-the-Salian-Franks/6000000002143186115?through=6000000005913253971 MEROVECH] (-[451/57]).  m ---.  The name of Merovech's wife is not known. Merovech is recorded 
2 CONC as having one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >1.         [https://www.geni.com/people/Child%C3%A9ric-I-King-of-the-Franks/6000000005913253971?through=6000000005913350166 CHILDERICH] (-Tournai [481/82], bur Tournai). Gregory of Tours records that Merovech was the father of Ch
2 CONC ilderich.  The Liber Historiæ Francorum names "Merovechus…filium…Childerico".  He succeeded in [451/57] as leader of the Franks in Roman Gaul, and subsequently adopted the title CHILDERICH I King of the Franks, confirmed by his un
2 CONC dated seal which bears the title "Childerici Regiz".  Gregory of Tours records that King Childerich's "private life was one long debauch" and that he was forced into exile in Thuringia by his subjects who chose as his replacement 
2 CONC the Roman General Ægidius, named magister militum per Gallias in [456/57] and who ruled for 8 years.  Gregory of Tours records Childerich's restoration as king in Gaul, followed by his alliance with "Odovacar…[and] his Saxons" (in
2 CONC dicating that this is unlikely to refer to the Ostrogoth leader in Italy), and their joint subjugation of the Alamans[.  A letter from Remigius Bishop of Reims to Childerich's son Clovis congratulates the latter on taking over his
2 CONC  father's position in "Belgica Secunda", implying that Childerich's authority was limited to the north-east part of Gaul.  The Liber Historiæ Francorum records that "Childericus rex" reigned for 24 years. [http://fmg.ac/Projects/M
2 CONC edLands/MEROVINGIANS.htm#ClovisIB MEDLANDS]
2 CONT
2 CONT > m ([464]) as her second husband, [https://www.geni.com/people/Basina-II-of-the-Th%C3%BCringians/6000000003772264436?through=6000000005913350166 BASINA], formerly wife of BASINUS King of Thuringia, daughter of ---.  Gregory of To
2 CONC urs names Basina as wife of Basinus King of Thuringia, with whom King Childerich sought refuge after being deposed, Basina deserting her first husband to join Childerich after he was restored as king in Gaul.  The marriage date is
2 CONC  estimated on the basis of how long Childerich was allegedly in exile, assuming that the date of his deposition is accurate, and appears to be consistent with the estimated dates of birth of the couple's descendants.  The Liber Hi
2 CONC storiæ Francorum records that "Childericus rex" committed adultery with "in Toringam…Basina regina uxorem Bisini regis" who abandoned her husband to join Childerich.  Assuming that Basina existed, it is unlikely that her first nam
2 CONC e is correct considering that it is the feminine form of her first husband's name.[http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MEROVINGIANS.htm#ClovisIB MEDLANDS]  King Childerich & his wife had four children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>a)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Clovis-I-the-Great-King-of-the-Franks/6000000005913350166?through=6000000005913253971 CHLODOVECH] [Clovis] ([464/67]-Paris [27 Nov] 511, bur Paris, basilique des Saints-Apôtres [later égli
2 CONC se de Sainte-Geneviève]).  Gregory of Tours names Clovis as son of Childerich & Basina.  He succeeded his father in [481/82] as CLOVIS I King of the Franks. CHLODOVECH [Clovis], son of CHILDERICH I King of the Franks & his wife Ba
2 CONC sina --- ([464/67]-Paris [27 Nov] 511, bur Paris, basilique des Saints-Apôtres [later église de Sainte-Geneviève]).  Gregory of Tours names Clovis as son of Childerich & Basina.  The Liber Historiæ Francorum names "Childerico" as 
2 CONC father of "Chlodovecho rege"[38].  He succeeded his father in [481/82] as CLOVIS I King of the Franks.  He defeated Syagrius, ruler at Soissons, in 486.  The Liber Historiæ Francorum records that "Chlodovechus" expanded his kingdo
2 CONC m "usque Sequanam" and afterwards "usque Ligere fluvio"[.  He remained a pagan after his marriage to a Catholic wife, but converted to Christianity in [496] allegedly having vowed to do so if successful in a battle against the Ala
2 CONC mans[40].  He allied with Godegisel against Gondebaud King of Burgundy in [500].  He defeated and killed Alaric II King of the Visigoths at the campus Vogladensis, probably Voulan, near Poitiers, athough this is popularly known as
2 CONC  the battle of Vouillé, in 507.  Gregory of Tours records that Clovis took control of the territory of Sigebert King of the Franks of the Rhine, after persuading Sigeric's son Chloderic to kill his father and then killing Chloderi
2 CONC c, as well as the territory of Chararic King of the Salian Franks.  Gregory of Tours records the death of King Clovis in Paris "five years after the battle of Vouillé" and his burial in the church of the Holy Apostles, which he an
2 CONC d Queen Clotilde had built. Gregory of Tours records that Queen Clotilde became a nun at the church of St Martin at Tours after her husband died, and in a later passage records her death in Tours and burial in Paris next to her hu
2 CONC sband in the church which she had built.  She was canonised by the Catholic church, feast day 3 Jun. [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MEROVINGIANS.htm#ClovisIB MEDLANDS]
2 CONT
2 CONT >>b)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Lant%C3%A9childe/6000000003998835912?through=6000000005913253971 LANDECHILDIS] [Lantilde].  Gregory of Tours names Lanthechild as sister of King Clovis, specifying that she was baptised wi
2 CONC th him after having followed the Arian faith[27].  She converted to Arianism, according to the title of one of the sermons of Avitus Bishop of Vienne[28]. [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MEROVINGIANS.htm#ClovisIB MEDLANDS]
2 CONT
2 CONT >>c)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Audofledis-of-the-Salian-Franks/6000000002377207214?through=6000000005913253971 AUDOFLEDIS] . Iordanes records the marriage of Theodoric and "Lodoin Francorum regem filiam eius Audefledam"
2 CONC  and names her brothers "Celdebertum et Heldebertum et Thiudebertum"[30], although this is presumably an incorrect reference to her nephews and great-nephew with similar names.  m ([492]) THEODORIC King of the Ostrogoths in Italy,
2 CONC  illegitimate son of THEODEMIR King of the Ostrogoths in Pannonia & his concubine Ereleuva -- ([454]-30 Aug 526). [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MEROVINGIANS.htm#ClovisIB MEDLANDS]
2 CONT
2 CONT >>d)        [https://www.geni.com/people/Albofl%C3%A8de/6000000047122259831?through=6000000005913253971 ALBOFLEDIS] (-after 496).  Gregory of Tours names Albofled as sister of King Clovis, specifying that she was baptised but died
2 CONC  soon after, St Rémy sending a letter of condolence to her brother[31].  From the context, it would appear that her baptism took place around the same time as her brother was baptised.  “Remigius Episcopus” wrote to “Chlodoveo Reg
2 CONC i” consoling him on the death of “germana vestra…Albochledis”[32]. [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MEROVINGIANS.htm#ClovisIB MEDLANDS]
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MEROVINGIANS.htm#ClovisIB 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Childeric_I
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Childeric I''' (c. 440– c. 481) was the Merovingian king of the Salian Franks from 457 until his death, and the father of Clovis.
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father Merovech as king, traditionally in 457 or 458. With his Frankish warband he was established with his capital at Tournai, on lands which he had received as a foederatus of the Romans, and for some time he ke
2 CONC pt the peace with his allies.
2 CONT
2 CONT In about 463 in Orléans, in conjunction with the Roman General Aegidius, who was based in Soissons, he defeated the Visigoths, who hoped to extend their dominion along the banks of the Loire River. After the death of Aegidius, he 
2 CONC first assisted Comes ("count") Paul of Angers, together with a mixed band of Gallo-Romans and Franks, in defeating the Goths and taking booty. Odoacer reached Angers but Childeric arrived the next day and a battle ensued. Count Pa
2 CONC ul was killed and Childeric took the city. Childeric, having delivered Angers, followed a Saxon warband to the islands on the Atlantic mouth of the Loire, and massacred them there. In a change of alliances, he also joined forces w
2 CONC ith Odoacer, according to Gregory of Tours, to stop a band of the Alamanni who wished to invade Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The stories of his expulsion by the Franks, whose women he was taking; of his eight-year stay in Thuringia with King Basin and his wife Basina; of his return when a faithful servant advised him that he could safely do so by sendin
2 CONC g to him half of a piece of gold which he had broken with him; and of the arrival in Tournai of Queen Basina, whom he married, come from Gregory of Tours' Libri Historiarum (Book ii.12).
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 481 and was buried in Tournai, leaving a son, Clovis, afterwards king of the Franks.
2 CONT Tomb
2 CONT
2 CONT Childeric's tomb was discovered in 1653 (May 27) by a mason doing repairs in the church of Saint-Brice in Tournai, a city in modern Belgium. Numerous precious objects were found, including a richly ornamented sword, a torse-like b
2 CONC racelet, jewels of gold and garnet cloisonné, gold coins, a gold bull's head and a ring with the inscription CHILDERICI REGIS ("of Childeric the king"), which identified the tomb. Some 300 golden bees were also found. Archduke Leo
2 CONC pold William, governor of the Southern Netherlands (today's Belgium), had the find published in Latin. The treasure went first to the Habsburgs in Vienna, then as a gift to Louis XIV, who was not impressed with the treasure and st
2 CONC ored it in the royal library, which became the Bibliothèque Nationale de France during the Revolution. Napoleon was more impressed with Childeric's bees and when he was looking for a heraldic symbol to trump the Bourbon fleur-de-l
2 CONC ys. he settled on Childeric's bees as symbols of the French Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the night of November 5–6, 1831, the treasure of Childeric was among 80 kilos of treasure stolen from the Library and melted down for the gold. A few pieces were retrieved from where they had been hidden in the Seine, including
2 CONC  two of the bees. The record of the treasure, however, now exists only in the fine engravings made at the time of its discovery and in some reproductions made for the Habsburgs. [edit] External links
2 CONT # Northvegr website: Gregory of Tours, Historia Francorum
2 CONT # "A note on Childeric's bees": the discovery of his tomb: follow the links for the engravings of Childeric's treasure and the two remaining gold bees.
2 CONT
2 CONT King of the Salian Franks	 King at Tournai	 King of France
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: 440 or 436
2 CONT
2 CONT Photo: Signet ring of Childeric I. Inscription CHILDERICI REGIS ("of Childeric the king"). Found in his tomb at Tournai, now in the Monnaie de Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT Childeric I (c. 440– c. 481) was the Merovingian king of the Salian Franks from 457 until his death, and the father of Clovis.
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father Merovech as king, traditionally in 457 or 458. With his Frankish warband he was established with his capital at Tournai, on lands which he had received as a foederatus of the Romans, and for some time he ke
2 CONC pt the peace with his allies.
2 CONT
2 CONT His tomb was discovered in the church of Saint-Brice in Tournai, Belgium.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Childeric I (c. 440– c. 481) was the Merovingian king of the Salian Franks from 457 until his death, and the father of Clovis.
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father Merovech as king, traditionally in 457 or 458. With his Frankish warband he was established with his capital at Tournai, on lands which he had received as a foederatus of the Romans, and for some time he ke
2 CONC pt the peace with his allies.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Childerico_I
2 CONT
2 CONT Childerico I (hacia 436 - 481, Tournai, Bélgica), Rey de los Francos Salios en el año 457.
2 CONT
2 CONT Quizás fue hijo de Meroveo. Se casa con Basina de Turingia y tiene por hijo a Clodoveo I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Este príncipe fue secuestrado cuando era niño por un destacamento del ejército de los hunos y liberado de forma milagrosa por un bravo francés llamado Viomade. Tenía valor y coraje, pero era de corazón tierno, y los señores france
2 CONC ses molestos por los amoríos que tenía con sus mujeres, se aliaron para destronarlo, por lo que se retiró a Alemania, donde volvió a caer en los mismos errores y sedujo a Basina, esposa del rey de Turingia, su huésped y amigo el r
2 CONC ey Basino.
2 CONT
2 CONT Childerico se hizo de nuevo con el trono, del que le habían echado sus galanterías, después de ganar una batalla. La reina de Turingia, Basina, abandona a su marido y se casa con Childerico, con gran escándalo de las gentes de bie
2 CONC n, que reclaman en vano los derechos matrimoniales y las leyes inviolables de la amistad. De este matrimonio nació el gran Clodoveo I.
2 CONT
2 CONT El final de su reinado estuvo señalado por muchos éxitos gloriosos. Derrota cerca de Orleans al ejército de Odoacro, rey de los hérulos (y conquistador en el año 476 del Imperio Romano de Occidente), toma Angers y la somete al pil
2 CONC laje. Mata con su propia mano al conde Paul, que estaba al mando de las tropas del Emperador. Firma un tratado de paz con Odoacro y se une con los sajones para exterminar a los alamanes que habían invadido una parte de Italia. La 
2 CONC conquista de Alemania fue el último hecho memorable de este príncipe. Murió poco después, en el vigésimo cuarto año de su reinado y fue enterrado en la villa de Tournai. Su tumba fue descubierta el año 1653.
2 CONT
2 CONT Le sucede su hijo Clodoveo I.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Childerik_I
2 CONT
2 CONT Childerik I (ca. 436-481 of 482) was een koning (hertog) van de Salische Franken. Hij volgde zijn vader Merovech op als heerser van de Saliërs in de omgeving van Doornik. Childerik diende vermoedelijk als generaal onder de Romeins
2 CONC e keizer Majorianus en in die hoedanigheid ook onder de Gallo-Romeinse heersers Aegidius en Paulus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Childerik had vier kinderen, zijn zoon Chlodovech en zijn dochters Lantechilde, Audoflede (gehuwd met Theodorik de Grote) en Abboflede. Childerik stierf op 26 november 481 en werd opgevolgd door zijn zoon Chlodovech.
2 CONT
2 CONT Het graf van Childerik in Doornik werd in 1653 ontdekt, en geldt als het rijkste Frankische koningsgraf dat ooit is gevonden. Het grootste deel van de schat werd in 1831 gestolen, maar de oorspronkelijke beschrijvingen van de voor
2 CONC werpen zijn bewaard gebleven.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Childeric I (c. 437– c. 481) was the Merovingian king of the Salian Franks from 457 until his death, and the father of Clovis.
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father Merovech (Latinised as Meroveus or Merovius) as king, traditionally in 457 or 458. With his Frankish warband he was established with his capital at Tournai, on lands which he had received as a foederatus of
2 CONC  the Romans, and for some time he kept the peace with his allies.
2 CONT
2 CONT In about 463 in Orléans, in conjunction with the Roman General Aegidius, who was based in Soissons, he defeated the Visigoths, who hoped to extend their dominion along the banks of the Loire River. After the death of Aegidius, he 
2 CONC first assisted Comes ("count") Paul of Angers, together with a mixed band of Gallo-Romans and Franks, in defeating the Goths and taking booty. Odoacer reached Angers but Childeric arrived the next day and a battle ensued. Count Pa
2 CONC ul was killed and Childeric took the city. Childeric, having delivered Angers, followed a Saxon warband to the islands on the Atlantic mouth of the Loire, and massacred them there. In a change of alliances, he also joined forces w
2 CONC ith Odoacer, according to Gregory of Tours, to stop a band of the Alamanni who wished to invade Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The stories of his expulsion by the Franks, whose women he was taking; of his eight-year stay in Thuringia with King Basin and his wife Basina; of his return when a faithful servant advised him that he could safely do so by sendin
2 CONC g to him half of a piece of gold which he had broken with him; and of the arrival in Tournai of Queen Basina, whom he married come from Gregory of Tours' Libri Historiarum (Book ii.12).
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 481 and was buried in Tournai, leaving a son Clovis, afterwards king of the Franks.
2 CONT
2 CONT Childeric's tomb was discovered in 1653 (May 27) by a mason doing repairs in the church of Saint-Brice in Tournai, a city in modern Belgium, where numerous precious objects were found, including a richly ornamented sword, a torse-
2 CONC like bracelet, jewels of gold and cloisonné enamel with garnets, gold coins, a gold bull's head and a ring with the inscription CHILDERICI REGIS ("of Childeric the king"), which identified the tomb. Some 300 golden bees were also 
2 CONC found. Archduke Leopold William, governor of the Southern Netherlands (today's Belgium), had the find published in Latin, and the treasure went first to the Habsburgs in Vienna, then as a gift to Louis XIV, who was not impressed w
2 CONC ith them and stored them in the royal library, which became the Bibliothèque Nationale de France during the Revolution. Napoleon was more impressed with Childeric's bees when he was looking for a heraldic symbol to trump the Bourb
2 CONC on fleur-de-lys. He settled on Childeric's bees as symbols of the French Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the night of November 5-6, 1831, the treasure of Childeric was among 80 kilos of treasure stolen from the Library and melted down for the gold. A few pieces were retrieved where they had been hidden in the Seine, including two 
2 CONC of the bees. The record of the treasure, however, now exists only in the fine engravings made at the time of its discovery, and in some reproductions made for the Habsburgs.
