1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 2-2438
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 2-2438
1 NOTE Original individual @P3960392051@ (@MS_TREE2.GED0_15GM2@) merged with @P3960409661@ (@MS_TREE2.GED0_15GM2@)
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 2-2438
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 2-2438
1 NOTE MOTHER PROBABLY A CONCUBINE; KING OF THE FRANKS OF YSSEL 458-496; GRAVE
2 CONT DISCOVERED 1653
1 NOTE Childeric I (c. 437 - c. 482  was the Merovingian </wiki/Merovingian> king of the Salian Franks from 457 until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign
2 CONT He succeeded his father Merovech (Latinised as Meroveus or Merovius) as king, traditionally in 457 or 458 . With his Frankish warband he was established with his capital at Tournai , on lands which he had received as a foederatus 
2 CONC (Foederatus early in the history of the Roman Republic identified one of the tribes bound by treaty (foedus), who were neither Roman colonies  nor had they been granted Roman citizenship (civitas) but were expected to provide a co
2 CONC ntingent of fighting men when trouble arose.)  of the Romans , and for some time he kept the peace with his allies.
2 CONT In about 463  at Orléans , in conjunction with the Roman General Aegidius  who was based at Soissons , he defeated the Visigoths , who hoped to extend their dominion along the banks of the Loire  River; after the death of Aegidius
2 CONC  he first assisted Comes ("count") Paul of Angers  in a mixed band of Gallo-Romans and Franks, defeating the Goths and taking booty. Odoacer reached Angers but Childeric arrived the next day and a battle ensued. Count Paul was kil
2 CONC led and Childeric took the city. Childeric having delivered Angers, he followed a Saxon warband to the islands at the Atlantic mouth of the Loire, and massacred them there. In a change of alliances, he also joined forces with Odoa
2 CONC cer, according to Gregory of Tours, to stop a band of the Alamanni who wished to invade Italy
2 CONT .
2 CONT These are all the facts known about him, and they are not secure. The stories of his expulsion by the Franks, whose women he was taking; of his stay of eight years in Thuringia with King Basin and his wife Basine; of his return wh
2 CONC en a faithful servant advised him that he could safely do so by sending to him half of a piece of gold which he had broken with him; and of the arrival at Tournai of Queen Basine , whom he married, are entirely legendary and come 
2 CONC from Gregory of Tours' Historia Francorum (Book ii.12).
2 CONT After the fall of the Western Empire in 476 there is no doubt that Childeric regarded himself as freed from his engagements towards Rome. He died in 481 and was buried at his capital, Tournai,  leaving a son Clovis, afterwards Clo
2 CONC vis I king of the Franks.
2 CONT
2 CONT His Tomb
2 CONT Childeric's tomb was discovered in 1653, by a mason doing repairs at the church of Saint-Brice in Tournai when numerous precious objects were found, a richly ornamented sword, a torse like bracelet, .....
2 CONT
2 CONT (In heraldry, the torse is a twisted roll of fabric wound around the top of the helm and crest to hold the mantle in place.  Like the mantle, the protective cloth covering worn over a knight's helmet, the torse is represented in t
2 CONC wo colours, generally the same pair of colours used on the mantle. The torse was made up of a pair of ribbons twisted together, tinctured of the principal metal and colors of the shield, the livery colours.  The torse is also ofte
2 CONC n referred to as the wreath.)
2 CONT
2 CONT  ......jewels of gold and cloisonné enamel with garnets, gold coins, a gold bull's head and a ring with the inscription CHILDERICI REGIS ("of Childeric the king"), which identified the tomb. Some 300 golden bees were also in the f
2 CONC ind. Archduke Leopold William, Spanish governor of the Netherlands, had the find published in Latin, and the treasure went first to the Habsburgs in Vienna, then as a gift to Louis XIV, who was not impressed with them and stored t
2 CONC hem in the royal library, which became the Bibliothèque National at the Revolution. Napoleon was more impressed with Childeric's bees: looking for a heraldic symbol to trump the Bourbon fleur-de-lys, he settled on Childeric's bees
2 CONC  as symbols of the French Empire
2 CONT .
2 CONT On the night of November 5-6, 1831, the treasure of Childeric was among 80 kilos of treasure stolen from the Library and melted down for the gold. A few pieces were retrieved where they had been hidden in the Seine, including two 
2 CONC of the bees, but record of the treasure now exists only in the fine engravings made at the time of its discovery, and in some reproductions made for the Habsburgs.
