1 NOTE prt of life story
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=d384ed6e-31ce-4ca1-9064-03643e1e1d92&tid=8764362&pid=-687688461
1 NOTE Source: THE RUFUS PARKS PEDIGREE by Brian J.L. Berry,  chart pg 61.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 66:
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.   DAGOBERT I, 602-38.  His father had made him king of part of Austrasia 623, with ARNULF, Bishop of Metz and PÉPIN of Landen as his advisors in the role of Mayors of the Palace.  This latter institution was to become the real 
2 CONC power of the do-nothing Merovingian kings.  The system finally moved them aside to take the royal dignity in 751 in the person of PÉPIN III le Bref.  DAGOBERT, upon his succession 628, pre-empted the portion of his half-brother Ch
2 CONC aribert ii and forced the Neustrian magnates to recognize him as king, thus controlling both countries.  But he fixed his residence in Paris.  To appease the Austrasians' resentment at this move, he gave them his three-year-old so
2 CONC n SIEGBERT III as their king.  DAGOBERT'S control now extended to Burgundy and Aquitaine, and he held nominal loyalty from Bascony and Brittany.  Prosperity and a revival of the arts marked his reign.  He founded the Abbey of St. 
2 CONC Denis near Paris, where he was bur. with many other Kings of France.  His tomb effigy may be seen there.  He mar. NANTICHILD.  Upon his death the kingdom was split again between SIEGBERT, now 9, and his brother Clovis II, age 5.
2 CONT
2 CONT !Availability: The libraries of Ken, Karen, Kristen, Kevin, Brian, Amy, Adam and FAL
