2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Nanthild (c. 610 – 642) (also known as Nantéchilde, Nanthechilde, Nanthildis, Nanthilde, or Nantechildis) was the third of many wives of Dagobert I, king of the Franks (629-639).
2 CONT
2 CONT She was of Saxon lineage, born about 608 or 610. The Lexikon des Mittelalters calls her ein Mädchen aus dem Dienstpersonal ("a maiden of the royal [ Austrasian ] household"). Her elevation to consort may have given importance to h
2 CONC er relatives: her brother Lanthegisel was an important landowner in the Limousin and a relation of Aldegisel. Dagobert set aside his wife Gomatrud to marry her, ca. 629; to her was born Clovis II, second eldest of Dagobert's survi
2 CONC ving sons and the one who succeeded him in Neustria and Burgundy. After Dagobert's death in January 639, she was initially regent for her son, accompanied by Aega, mayor of the Neustrian palace and an opponent of the powerful cont
2 CONC ingent of nobles headed by Burgundofaro whose seat was at Meaux.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the interest of reducing noble Burgundian independence of the Merovingian palace, she married her niece Ragnoberta to the Frank Flaochad and had the magnates and bishops of the realm of Burgundy acclaim him mayor of the palace 
2 CONC at Orléans in 642. Soon she died at Landry in what was then Burgundy, where she had long resided; her body was translated to the Saint Denis Basilica. Her untimely death allowed her son to fall under the influence of the nobility,
2 CONC  who abhorred a strong royal hand.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nanthild
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Regintrude (Ragintrudis) was a mistress of King Dagobert; she was the mother of King Sigebert III of Austrasia (not our ancestor) as well as of our ancestor Regintrude.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Nanthild (also known as Nantéchilde, Nanthechilde, Nanthildis, Nanthilde, or Nantechildis) was the third of many wives of Dagobert I, King of the Franks (629-639). She was of Saxon lineage, born about 608 or 610. The Lexikon des M
2 CONC ittelalters calls her ein Mädchen aus dem Dienstpersonal ("a maiden of the royal [ Austrasian ] household"). Her elevation to consort may have given importance to her relatives: Her brother Lanthegisel was an important landowner i
2 CONC n the Limousin and a relation of Aldegisel. Dagobert set aside his wife Gomatrud to marry her, ca. 629; to her was born Clovis II, second eldest of Dagobert's surviving sons and the one who succeeded him in Neustria and Burgundy. 
2 CONC After Dagobert's death in January 639, she was initially regent for her son, accompanied by Aega, mayor of the Neustrian palace and an opponent of the powerful contingent of nobles headed by Burgundofaro whose seat was at Meaux.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the interest of reducing noble Burgundian independence of the Merovingian palace, she married her niece Ragnoberta to the Frank Flaochad and had the magnates and bishops of the realm of Burgundy acclaim him mayor of the palace 
2 CONC at Orléans in 642. Soon she died at Landry in what was then Burgundy, where she had long resided; her body was translated to the Saint Denis Basilica. Her untimely death allowed her son to fall under the influence of the nobility,
2 CONC  who abhorred a strong royal hand.
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nanthild for more information.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Regintrude of Austrasia b. circa 612
2 CONT
2 CONT Regintrude of Austrasia|b. c 612| |N. N. of Austrasia|b. c 580| Father N. N. of Austrasia b. circa 580 
2 CONT
2 CONT      Also called Ragnetrude. Regintrude of Austrasia was born circa 612. She was the daughter of N. N. of Austrasia. Regintrude of Austrasia associated with Dagoberchtus I, rex Francorum, vir inluster, son of Chlothacharius II, re
2 CONC x Francorum and Berthetrude of Burgundy, in 630; His 3rd. She was a concubine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Dagoberchtus I, rex Francorum, vir inluster b. circa 603, d. 19 January 639  Children ◦rex Austrasii Sigibertus III des Francs+ b. bt 9 Oct 630 - 19 Jan 631, d. 1 Feb 6563 ◦N. N. des Francs+ b. 632 ◦Regintrude des Francs+ b
2 CONC . c 633
2 CONT
2 CONT http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~cousin/html/p64.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Regintrud (also Reginlind and Regentrud), (b. 660-665 d. 730-740) is something of an enigma.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sources quoted by Schreibner indicate that the records show a Regentrud as the sister of Adela of Pfalzel and daughter of king Dagobert I.
2 CONT
2 CONT This is supported by the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT Another Regentrud being listed in the tradition book of the Nonnberg Abbey as regentrudis regina (marking her a Merovingian princess) The names of her sons, through her marriage to duke Theodo of Bavaria, are also Merowingian, tha
2 CONC t is, from the families of Regentrud and Adela According to these sources, 8th century witnesses only mention Folchaid as the wife of Theodo.
2 CONT
2 CONT The supposition that queen Regintrud, the benefactor of the abbey founded by Rupert of Salzburg and Theodo mentioned above, must therefore be the wife of Theodo, is attributed to the 15th century historians Suntheim and Aventin.
2 CONT
2 CONT As an alternative explanation, the sources suggest that Regintrud was the queen of Theudebert, who may have had his support of the abbey attributed instead to his late father, Theodo.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Theudebert, another Regentrud entered the Nonnberg Abbey and later became abbess in 720-725. This is listed in the Salzburger Verbrüderungsbuch in 784. Assuming the abbess Regentrud and queen Regentrud are one i
2 CONC n the same, she may have entered the abbey on her husbands death.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Regintrud (also Reginlind and Regentrud), (b. 660-665 d. 730-740) is something of an enigma.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sources quoted by Schreibner indicate that the records show a Regentrud as the sister of Adela of Pfalzel and daughter of king Dagobert I.
2 CONT
2 CONT This is supported by the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT Another Regentrud being listed in the tradition book of the Nonnberg Abbey as regentrudis regina (marking her a Merovingian princess) The names of her sons, through her marriage to duke Theodo of Bavaria, are also Merowingian, tha
2 CONC t is, from the families of Regentrud and Adela According to these sources, 8th century witnesses only mention Folchaid as the wife of Theodo.
2 CONT
2 CONT The supposition that queen Regintrud, the benefactor of the abbey founded by Rupert of Salzburg and Theodo mentioned above, must therefore be the wife of Theodo, is attributed to the 15th century historians Suntheim and Aventin.
2 CONT
2 CONT As an alternative explanation, the sources suggest that Regintrud was the queen of Theudebert, who may have had his support of the abbey attributed instead to his late father, Theodo.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Theudebert, another Regentrud entered the Nonnberg Abbey and later became abbess in 720-725. This is listed in the Salzburger Verbrüderungsbuch in 784. Assuming the abbess Regentrud and queen Regentrud are one i
2 CONC n the same, she may have entered the abbey on her husbands death.
2 CONT
2 CONT This could explain why the name of Theudeberts wife is excluded in the communion book of St. Peter in respect for her position as abbess. However, much of this is mere speculation based on the individuals named being one in the sa
2 CONC me.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following lineages have been suggested:
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter of the Seneschal (and Pfalzgraf) Hugobert and Irmina of Oeren. Daughter of Dagobert I Daughter of Childebert III
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Regintrud and first husband
2 CONT
2 CONT Pilitrud
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Regintrud and Treudebert
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugbert of Bavaria, heir to the duchy Swanachild, wife of Charles Martel Guntrud, wife of Liutprand
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------- Alternate birth location: Franconia, Germany <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
