1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 923
2 PLAC Georgia
2 ADDR
1 OCCU Bagrationi of Iberia, King of Iberia
1 TITL King
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Adarnase IV (Georgian: ადარნასე) (died 923) was a member of the Georgian Bagratid dynasty of Tao-Klarjeti and prince of Iberia/Kartli, responsible for the restoration of kingship, which had been in abeyance since i
2 CONC t had been abolished by Iran in the sixth century, in 888.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The numbering of successive rulers in the early Bagratid period is very confused in that it moves between the different branches of the family. Hence, Adarnase, known as “IV” for being the second Adarnase as the prince of Iberia, 
2 CONC is also known as “II” as a sovereign of Tao-Klarjeti and “I” as the king of Iberia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adarnase was the only son of David I, the prince of Iberia with the Byzantine title of curopalates, who was murdered by his cousin Nasra in 881. As Adarnase was still a minor, the Byzantine emperor – pursuant to the policy of divi
2 CONC sion – appointed as curopalates, not Adarnase, but his cousin Gurgen. Nasra’s subsequent attempt to dispossess Adarnase of patrimonial inheritance was defeated with the help of the Armenian king Ashot I in 888. Allied with the res
2 CONC urgent Armenians, Adarnase then launched, from his base in Lower Tao, a policy of expansion. Not being a curopalates and having Armenia's example before him, Adarnase assumed the title of king and subsequently defeated his rival c
2 CONC uropalates Gurgen. The Byzantine government adapted itself to the circumstances and, upon Gurgen death in 891, recognized Adarnase as curopalates.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Switching alliances
2 CONT
2 CONT Adarnase rewarded Ashot of Armenia’s assistance with steadfast loyalty which continued into the reign of Ashot’s successor Smbat I whom Adarnase aided to win the crown in dynastic struggles in 890 and later joined him against Ahme
2 CONC d ibn-'Isâ of Diyarbakır, the Caliph’s former governor of Armīniya. In turn, Smbat recognized Adarnase’s royal status and personally crowned him in 899. The two men collaborated in defeating, in 904, the Abasgian king Constantine 
2 CONC III, their common relative, who competed with Adarnase for hegemony in Inner Iberia (Shida Kartli) and with Smbat in Gogarene. Adarnase captured Constantine and turned him over to Smbat. But the latter, inclined to balance Adarnas
2 CONC e’s growing power and extend Armenian influence to west Georgia, freed his captive. This move turned Adarnase against Smbat and the ensuing break and enmity weakened the both monarchs: Adarnase was dispossessed by Constantine of I
2 CONC nner Iberia (Shida Kartli) in 904, while Smbat was defeated and tortured to death by Yusuf, a Sajid ruler of Azerbaijan in 914.[1] As a result of these events, Adarnase was relegated to his portion of the Bagratid hereditary lands
2 CONC  in Tao.[2] He rebuilt the church of Bana in Tao and made it a bishop's seat.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Adarnase's wife is not known. He was survived by four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT David II of Iberia (died 937)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ashot II of Tao (died 954)
2 CONT
2 CONT Bagrat Magistros (died 945)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sumbat I of Iberia (died 958)
2 CONT
2 CONT A daughter married to Constantine III of Abasgia
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ a b Suny, Ronald Grigor (1994), The Making of the Georgian Nation, pp. 30-31. Indiana University Press, ISBN 0253209153
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ a b c Toumanoff, Cyril (1967). Studies in Christian Caucasian History, pp. 490-493. Georgetown University Press.
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Thomson, Robert W. (1996), Rewriting Caucasian History, p. 247. Oxford University Press, ISBN 0198263732
