2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT # https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adelperga Adelperga and Arechis had five children, Romuald (761/2–787), Grimoald (before 773 – April 806), Gisulf (d. before 806), Theoderada (d. after 787) and Adelchisa (b. after 773, d. after 817
2 CONC ), abbess of San Salvatore d'Alife. … When Arechis died on 26 August 787, Adelperga acted as regent, because Charlemagne hesitated to release Adelperga's son Grimoald, whom he kept as a hostage. Grimoald was released and acceded a
2 CONC s duke in February 787. He arrived in Benevento in 788, thus discontinuing Adalperga's regency.[8]
2 CONT # Monasteries: San Salvatore/Santa Giulia, Brescia < [https://www.unive.it/pag/19478/ link] > The monastery of S. Salvatore in Brescia was founded in 757 by the Lombard King Desiderius and his wife Queen Ansa. Later it became bett
2 CONC er known as Sta Giulia. Its first abbess was Desiderius’ and Ansa’s daughter Anselberga. It attracted the daughters, sisters and nieces of the most elevated aristocratic family both in the Lombard kingdom and, after its end in 774
2 CONC , under the rule of the Carolingians in Italy, then that of the Emperor Berengar. … While the authority of the abbesses was in theory restricted to the monastery, in practice they wielded considerable economic, political and socia
2 CONC l influences on account of both the enormous amount of property they owned, and of their social and political status in relation to the rulers and powerful men with whom they were associated.
