1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [Before he [Tiberius of the Justinian dynasty] died he gave orders that his son-in-law, named Germanus, should be raised to the imperial throne. Now he had formerly been patrician. But owing to his humility of hear
2 CONC t he refused to be emperor. Thereupon Maurice, who was of the province of Cappadocia, was made emperor." ]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to The Emperor Maurice and His Historian: Theophylact Simocatta on Persian and Balkan warfare (1988) by Michael Whitby, Germanus was a patrician and governor of the praetorian prefecture of Africa. He was chosen by the d
2 CONC ying Tiberius II as a viable heir for his throne in 582. "In a dual ceremony on 5 August Germanus ... and Maurice were elevated to the rank of Caesar and betrothed to Tiberius' two daughters, Charito and Constantina.".
2 CONT
2 CONT [116] Tiberius, however, was full of charity. He gave a quarter of his total wealth to the  poor, and eliminated [some] taxes throughout his realm. He was a decent, optimistic, and  sincere man who reigned for four years and then 
2 CONC took ill. Now some claim that he ruled  for only one year. But do not believe them, for we have confirmed from many books that  he ruled for four years . He gave his daughter [Constantina] in marriage to the Augustus  Maurice, mak
2 CONC ing him emperor. [Maurice] hailed from the village of Arabissus (Arp'sus)  in Cappadocia. Then Tiberius died, leaving a good portion of the land grieving. And  Maurice became emperor [582-602]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rome, however, rebelled against Maurice and seated Germanus (Garamios) as their own  emperor. Previously he had been successful in warfare against the Persians. Then the  Persian king Khosrov died and his son Hurmazd [V, 579-590] 
2 CONC reigned.
