1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Doda''' (nickname for Chrothildis)  was first thought of as the daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/Wandregisis/6000000010918502385?through=6000000000957281690 ‘'''Wandregis'''’] and [https://www.geni.com/
2 CONC people/Farahild/6000000000700960321?through=6000000000957281690 '''Farahild'''].  Allegedly connected to over 25  pairs of parents.   Modern insights, (C. Settipani) conclude that she was a daughter of Ansegisel of Metz and his wi
2 CONC fe Begga. -discussion is not closed.
2 CONT * Not the same as [https://www.geni.com/people/Saint-Dode-of-Metz/6000000006296799111 '''Saint Dode of Metz''']
2 CONT *Not the same as [https://www.geni.com/people/Amelberga-Of-Maubeuge/6000000179077764895 '''Amalberga of Maubeuge''']
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Clotilda''' ('''Chrothildis'''). Her parentage is unknown. Settipani speculates [https://www.geni.com/people/Ansigisel-of-Metz-Mayor-of-the-Palace-of-Austrasia/5535400391550125499 '''Dux Ansegisel'''] had a daughter Doda who ma
2 CONC rried Théodéric III, and was the mother of [https://www.geni.com/people/Bertrade-de-Prüm/6000000001842551452 '''Bertrade of Prüm'''], (Gen-Medieval-L, Nov. 16, 1999).
2 CONT
2 CONT aka..Regent Clothilde / Chrothildis
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: ABT 0640 in Heristal, Belgium
2 CONT Death: after 3 Jun 0692
2 CONT Father: Ansegisel Mayor of Palace Austrasia b: ABT 0602 in Metz, France (perhaps) 
2 CONT Mother: St. Beggue of Landen b: ABT 0613 in Landen, Belgium (perhaps)
2 CONT Marriage: Theuderic I King of Franks b: ABT 0636 in Metz, France
2 CONT 692 Regent Queen Dowager Clothilde of Neustria and Bourgogne (France)
2 CONT
2 CONT Regent for a few months for son Childéric. She is also known as Rothilde, Chrothéchildis or Doda (d. 694/9).
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Merovingian Nobility:
2 CONT FRANKS, MEROVINGIAN NOBILITY
2 CONT v5.0 Updated 03 April 2025
2 CONT D.      FAMILY of ARNULF
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/FRANKSMaiordomi.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. ANSEGISEL ([612]-killed [662]).  
2 CONT The Gesta Episcoporum Mettensis names (in order) "duos filios Anschisum et Chlodolfum" as sons of Arnulf "iuventutis suæ tempore ex legitimi matrimonii copula" but does not name their mother[65].  The Vita Chrodegangi Episcopi Met
2 CONC tensis; names "Anchisæ" as second son of "Arnulfum sanctum"[66].  
2 CONT
2 CONT “…necnon et domesticorum Flodulfi, Ansigisili, Bettelini, Gariberti” consented to a donation to the monastery of Stabulo and Malmédy by King Sigebert III in a charter dated to [648][67].  "Childericus rex Francorum, Emnehildis et 
2 CONC Bilihildis…reginæ…Gundoino duce et Hodone domestico" confirmed the property of the monastery of Stablo and Malmedy on the advice of "Grimoaldo, Fulcoaldo, Adregisilo, Bobone ducibus, Chlodulfo, Ansegisilo, Gariberto domesticis" by
2 CONC  charter dated 6 Sep 667[68], although the presence of "Grimoaldo" in the document ten years after the attested death of the only known Duke Grimoald suggests that the document may have been subject to some alteration.   He was ki
2 CONC lled by a nobleman Gundoen[69].
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([643/44]) BEGGA, daughter of PEPIN [I] "l'Ancien" or "de Landen", maior domus of King Clotaire II & his wife Itta --- (-693).  The Cronica Hohenburgensis names "huius soror [beata Gerdrudis] Begga" as wife of "Angiso sancti Arn
2 CONC ulfi filio"[70].  Sigeberto's Vita Landiberto episcopi Traiectensis names "Pippinus…principes Francorum…sanctæ Beggæ matris eius"[71].  She founded the Abbey of Andenne, near Namur, 691 with nuns from the Abbey of Nivelle.  The An
2 CONC nales Xantenses record the death in 698 of "Sancta Begga mater Pippini ducis"[72].  Ansegisel & his wife had [two] children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >a) PEPIN [II] "le Gros" or "d'Herstal" ([645]-Jupille, near Liège 16 Dec 714, bur Metz, basilique de Saint-Arnoul).  The Gesta Episcoporum Mettensis names "Anschisus" as father of "Pippinum"[73]. 
2 CONT "Pippinus filius Ansegisili quondam necnon…matrone mea Plectrudis" donated property to the church of St Arnulf at Metz by charter dated 20 Feb 691[74].  He defeated his adversaries at Tertry, Somme in Jun 687 before becoming maior
2 CONC  domus of Austrasia in [688/90]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >'''b) [CHROTHECHILDIS [Rotilde] [Doda] (-692 or after). Settipani approves the theory of Maurice Chaume according to which the wife of King Theoderic III was the daughter of Ansegisel[75].  However, this does not appear to be dir
2 CONC ectly supported by primary source evidence. "Theudericus rex Francorum" donated property at the request of "regine nostre Chrodochilde…et…Berchario maiorem domos nostre" to the abbey of St Denis by charter dated 30 Oct 688[76].  "
2 CONC Chrotechildis regina" is named mother of King Clovis III in the Cartulaire of Saint-Bertin[77].  She was regent for her son King Chlodovech III until 692.  The epitaph of King Theoderic III and his wife bore the inscription "rex T
2 CONC heodericus…cum coniuge  Doda", assumed to be another name by which Rotilde was known[78].  m THEODERIC III King of the Franks in Neustria, son of CLOVIS II King of the Franks in Neustria & his wife Bathildis --- ([651]-[2 Sep 690/
2 CONC 12 Apr 691], bur Arras, basilique Saint-Vaast).]'''  
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family 
2 CONT ===From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Merovingian Kings:
2 CONT FRANKS, MEROVINGIAN KINGS
2 CONT v5.0 Updated 03 April 2025
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MEROVINGIANS.htm#_Toc359685964
2 CONT
2 CONT THEODERICH, son of CLOVIS II King of the Franks in Neustria & his wife Bathildis -- ([651]-[2 Sep 690/12 Apr 691], bur Arras, basilique Saint-Vaast).  The Liber Historiæ Francorum names (in order) "Chlotharium, Childericum atque T
2 CONC heudericum" as the three sons of "Chlodoveus…ex Balthilde regina eius", recording in the following paragraph that Theoderich succeeded his brother after a reign of 4 years[469].  The parentage of "Theodorici regis" is given in the
2 CONC  Cartulaire de Saint-Bertin[470].  He succeeded his brother in 673 as THEODERICH III King of the Franks in Neustria and Burgundy, under the protection of the maior domus Ebroin. … 
2 CONT
2 CONT m '''CHROTECHILDIS''' [Rotilde] [Doda], daughter of --- (-692 or after).  "Theudericus rex Francorum" donated property at the request of "regine nostre Chrodochilde…et…Berchario maiorem domos nostre" to the abbey of St Denis by ch
2 CONC arter dated 30 Oct 688[475].  "Chrotechildis regina" is named as mother of King Clovis III in the Cartulaire of Saint-Bertin[476].  She was regent for her son King Chlodovech III until 692.  The necrology of Arras Saint-Vaast name
2 CONC s "Theodericus rex Galliæ, Doda uxor regina"[477].  The epitaph of King Theoderich III and his wife bore the inscription "rex Theodericus…cum coniuge  Doda", assumed to be another name by which Rotilde was known[478].  Settipani a
2 CONC pproves the theory of Maurice Chaume that the wife of King Theoderich III was Doda, daughter of maior domus Ansegisel[479].  The primary source evidence which provides the evidence for this theory has not yet been identified.  As 
2 CONC noted in the document MEROVINGIAN NOBILITY, Doda was the possible name of Ansegisel’s mother, based only on a later source. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Theoderich III & his wife had [three] children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT *1.         CHLODOVECH [Clovis] ([678]-695, bur Choisy-au-Bac, near Compiègne, basilique Saint-Etienne).  … He succeeded his father in 690 as CLOVIS III King of the Franks, under the authority of Pépin d'Herstal [Carolingian], but
2 CONC  died after "having reigned four years"[483].  The Chronicon Sancti Medardi Suessionensis records the death in 695 of “Clodoveus puer Rex” and the accession of “Childebertus frater eius”[484]. 
2 CONT * 2.         CHILDEBERT (-14 Apr 711, bur Choisy-au-Bac, near Compiègne, basilique Saint-Etienne).  "Childebertus rex Francorum" names "geneture nostro Theuderico condam rige" in his deed dated 14 Mar 697[485].  "Hildebertus" is n
2 CONC amed brother of King Clovis III in the Cartulaire of Saint-Bertin[486].  The Liber Historiæ Francorum names "Childebertus frater eius [=Chlodoveus rex]" when recording that he succeeded his brother[487].  He succeeded his brother 
2 CONC in 695 as CHILDEBERT III King of the Franks, under the authority of Pépin d'Herstal [Carolingian]. m [ERMENECHILDIS], daughter of --King Childebert III & his wife had one child:
2 CONT ** a)         DAGOBERT ([697/98]-[3 Sep/31 Dec] 715
2 CONT * 3.         [BERTRADA [Berta].  Abbess of Prüm.  Settipani suggests that Bertrada, abbess of Prüm and great grandmother of Charlemagne, was the daughter of King Theoderich III, basing this on the transmission of the names Charibe
2 CONC rt and Theoderich into the Carolingian family[503].  m ---.]  '''Speculative''': see https://www.geni.com/people/Bertrade-de-Prum/6000000001842551452
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From the French Wikipedia page on Clodilde dite Doda:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clotilde_dite_Doda
2 CONT
2 CONT Translated to English:
2 CONT
2 CONT Clotilde (actually Chrothildis), also known as Doda (d. 3 June 694) was Queen of the Franks and wife of Merovingian King Thierry III (c.651-690/691).
2 CONT
2 CONT Her Name
2 CONT
2 CONT Although the Liber Historiae Francorum called her Clotilde, in contemporary charters and diplomas, she was called Chrotechildis or Chrothildis or Rothilde, as observed by Karl August Eckhardt. The name also appeared upon the name 
2 CONC of their graves: "Theodoric, King of the Franks and the Queen Doda". The precise epitaph says: "King Theodoric lies in repose with his wife Doda."
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly before 678, she married Thierry III (c.651-690/691), who became King of the Franks of Neustria in 673, King of the Franks in Burgundy in 675, and King of the Franks in Austrasia in 687 (all terms ending at his death in 691
2 CONC ). From this marriage was born:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Clovis IV (c.678-695), King of the Franks
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Childebert IV (d. 711), King of the Franks
2 CONT
2 CONT Two other children are suggested as being born from this marriage:
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Clotaire IV (d. 718), King of the Franks. The question of kinship between Theirry III and Clotaire IV is rather complicated, partly because no contemporary document specifies its existence, and the few later sources that mentio
2 CONC n it often conflict with each other, as well as with other known data. It seems that, given the confusion induced by these other inconsistencies, that Clotaire IV would be most easily explained as the son of Thierry III and Clotil
2 CONC de (Dode).
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Bertrade, founder of the Abbey of Prum and mother of Caribert Comte de Laon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Widowhood
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband, although a king who controlled many Mayors of the Palace - Ebroin (673), Leodegar (675-676), Ebroin again (676-680), Waratton (680-686), and Berchaire (686-687) -, he was nonetheless defeated by Pepin the Younger, May
2 CONC or of the Palace of Austrasia, who united the three Frankish kingdoms and dominated over Thierry III.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thierry died in 691. Clotilde (Doda) survived to become regent on behalf of her son Clovis until 5 June 692, the date in which she died. She was buried beside her husband in the Abbey St-Vaast-d'Arras.
2 CONT
2 CONT Filiation
2 CONT
2 CONT Her parentage is not mentioned by contemporary texts. The Chronicon of Aldemar de Charbannes wrote at the end of the 10th century that in 717 "Charles Martel seized Plectrude, his stepmother, and ... placed Clotarius, his cousin, 
2 CONC on the throne, son of Clodoveus, who himself was son of Dagobert. Although the parentage of Clotaire IV is the result of confusion with Clotaire III, the reference of kinship between Clotaire IV and Charles Martel is seen as accur
2 CONC ate. Since neither Charles nor his parents were Merovingian, the relationship probably involves the mother of Clotaire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other arguments along this direction:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Several close relatives of Bertrade de Prum have Merovingian names, which argues for a relationship between Bertrade de Prum and the Merovingians
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Pepin and his wife Bertrade de Laon each had half of two villas, one at Rommersheim, and the other at Rheinbach, each of which received their respective halves from their fathers. This implies a sharing of property with a relat
2 CONC ively new generation. Caribert, father of Bertrade de Laon, held property with his mother Bertrade de Prum.
2 CONT
2 CONT One explanation has been advanced, whereas Plectrude, stepmother of Charles Martel, was a close relative of Bertrade de Prum, she shared villas with her, as they were sisters. But this reasoning does not explain the transmission o
2 CONC f the name of the Comte to Merovingian names, and does not consider the existence of other sisters of Plectrude. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Maurice Chaume proposed another solution, that Bertrade de Laon was the daughter of Thierry III and Clotilde (Doda), who was herself a daughter of Ansegisel and Begga, and therefore the younger sister of Pepin's father Charles Mar
2 CONC tel. This proposal also explains the kinship between Clotaire IV and Charles Martel, and why Clotilde (Doda) became regent upon the death of her husband, as the victorious Mayor of the Palace was her brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT But onomastics suggest a variation of this solution that is slightly different: Chrothildis is composed of the roots "Chrot" (found in Chrodoare, the likely mother of St. Arnulf) and "Hildes" (which is the name of the wife of Clod
2 CONC ulf). Clotilde (Doda) would be the daughter of Hilda and Clodulf, and therefore a cousin of Pepin the Younger, rather than his sister. The name Doda may also refer to that of his grandmother, the wife of St. Arnulf.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ben notes: This is NOT St. Clotilde. St. Clotilde lived about a century before this individual.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT
2 CONT # https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chrothildis Chrothildis (7th-century – 692 CE) (also called Clothild, Chrodhild, Clotilde)[1][2][3] was a Frankish queen consort by marriage to king Theuderic III.[4] After Theuderic III's death she
2 CONC  was formally the nominal regent for her son Clovis IV during his minority from his succession in 691 (when he was nine) until her own death in 692.[3]
2 CONT ##  Fox, Yaniv (2014-09-18). Power and Religion in Merovingian Gaul: Columbanian Monasticism and the Formation of the Frankish Aristocracy. Cambridge University Press. ISBN 978-1-107-06459-1. < [https://books.google.com/books?id=p
2 CONC 7lUBAAAQBAJ&dq=%22Theuderic+III%22+marriage&pg=PA342 GoogleBooks] > 
2 CONT ##Fouracre, Paul; Gerberding, Richard A. (1996). Late Merovingian France: History and Hagiography, 640-720. Manchester University Press. ISBN 978-0-7190-4791-6. < [https://books.google.com/books?id=uifpAAAAIAAJ&dq=%22Theuderic+III
2 CONC %22+marriage&pg=PA163 GoogleBooks] > 
2 CONT ## Venning, Timothy (2017-10-12). A Chronology of Early Medieval Western Europe: 450–1066. Routledge. ISBN 978-1-351-58916-1. < [https://books.google.com/books?id=WqU5DwAAQBAJ&dq=%22Theuderic+III%22+Clothild&pg=RA1-PA693 GoogleBoo
2 CONC ks] > 
