1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Aspasia Annia Regilla,[1] full name Appia Annia Regilla Atilia Caucidia Tertulla [2][3] (Greek:Ἀσπασία Ἄννια Ῥήγιλλα, 125-160), was a wealthy, aristocratic and influential Roman woman, who was a distant relative of
2 CONC  several Roman emperors and empresses. She was the wife of the prominent Greek Herodes Atticus.[3][4] Contents  [hide] 1 Genealogy 2 Marriage and Early Life with Herodes Atticus 3 Greece and Olympia 4 Posthumous honors and legacy 
2 CONC 5 Children 6 References 7 Sources Genealogy[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Regilla was born into an aristocratic family of consular rank. She was a member of the gens Annia, of the venerable branch of the Annii Regilli.[5] Regilli means "Little Kings".[5] Her father was Appius Annius Trebonius Gallus, a 
2 CONC distinguished Roman Senator and one of the serving consuls in the year 139. Her mother was a Roman aristocrat called Atilia Caucidia Tertulla.[3][6] Regilla's brother, Appius Annius Atilius Bradua, served as an ordinary consul in 
2 CONC 160.[3][6][7] The paternal grandparents of Regilla was the senator Appius Annius Trebonius Gallus and his unnamed wife.,[4] while her maternal grandparents of Regilla was the Roman Senator, Governor Marcus Appius Bradua and the ar
2 CONC istocratic woman Caucidia Tertulla [3][8] Her mother’s brother was Marcus Atilius Metilius Bradua Caucidius Tertullus…Bassus.[9][10] Her uncle served as a polyonymous Proconsul of the Africa Province under the Roman Emperor Antoni
2 CONC nus Pius (138-161).[10] Her grandfathers were both consular colleagues in the year 108.[4] Through her paternal grandfather, Regilla was related to the Roman Senator Marcus Annius Verus, who was a brother-in-law of Roman Emperor H
2 CONC adrian and father of the Roman Empress Faustina the Elder, wife of the Roman Emperor Antoninus Pius.[5] Faustina the Elder was the mother of Roman Empress Faustina the Younger and aunt of Roman Emperor Marcus Aurelius.[5] Regilla 
2 CONC was related to the Roman Empress Lucilla, Roman Emperor Commodus and Roman Empress of the 3rd century, Annia Faustina. Marriage and Early Life with Herodes Atticus[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT During her father’s consulship, her father had betrothed Regilla to the prominent Greek Herodes Atticus.[11] Herodes Atticus was a very wealthy, influential, aristocratic Athenian who was a friend to the Roman Emperor Antoninus Pi
2 CONC us and was a tutor to the Emperor’s adopted son Marcus Aurelius. Herodes Atticus and Regilla married in 139. Regilla was about 14 years old and Herodes Atticus was 40 years old. When Regilla married Herodes Atticus, Annius Gallus 
2 CONC gave his daughter a dowry.[12] With this dowry Regilla bought with her husband a villa on the third mile of the Appian Way just south of Rome [12] and together they controlled a large area of land, which was known as the "Triopio"
2 CONC  (from Triopas, King of Thessaly), and later, in medieval times, as Capo di Bove. Regilla and Herodes Atticus only lived in Italy for a while. In a few years after their marriage, Herodes Atticus, Regilla and their children left I
2 CONC taly and moved to Greece. Although she was directly cut off from her immediate influential family, relatives and friends in Italy, Regilla became a part of the highest Greek circle of society, in particular in Athens. Greece and O
2 CONC lympia[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Regilla spent her remaining years living and travelling around Greece. When she lived in Athens, she was awarded as a priestess of the goddess Tyche and the venerable, long-established office of priestess of Demeter Chamyne in Oly
2 CONC mpia, Greece in 153. As a priestess of Demeter Chamyne, Regilla was the only woman officially present at the Olympic Games. Regilla sat on or near the altar of Demeter Chamyne on the north side of the stadium in Olympia opposite o
2 CONC n the stand of the judges to view the competitions. Being a priestess serving Demeter Chamyne, was a great honor and was quite physically demanding. Regilla using her own considerable influence and wealth erected a great nymphaeum
2 CONC  (a monumental fountain) at Olympia. Her husband constructed an aqueduct that fed the nymphaeum. It was the fountain the Regilla built, that made the cool refreshing water available to the people in this hot, dusty area. In the ce
2 CONC ntre of the nymphaeum, stood a life sized statue of a bull. On that bull there is an honorific inscription carved on the statue dedicated to Regilla stating: “Regilla, priestess of Demeter, dedicated the water and the things aroun
2 CONC d the water to Zeus”. At Olympia, bulls were sacrificed to the Temple of Zeus and Zeus was the chief divinity of Olympia. The fountain that Regilla built had two levels of niches bearing statues and a columnar façade. Statues have
2 CONC  survived from the monument. The central niche on each level held a statue of Zeus, surrounding by portrait statues on each side. The lower level contained the Roman Emperor Antoninus Pius, the ancestors and descendants of the emp
2 CONC eror. These included the previous Roman Emperor Hadrian; Hadrian’s cousin-wife Vibia Sabina; Roman Empress Faustina the Elder, the daughter of the elder Faustina and Antoninus Pius, Faustina the Younger and Antoninus Pius’ adopted
2 CONC  son and son-in-law, future Roman Emperor Marcus Aurelius. This level also showed several children of Marcus Aurelius and Faustina the Younger, in particular their first daughter Annia Aurelia Galeria Faustina (aged about 7 but sh
2 CONC own as a young adolescent). On the upper level, paralleling the imperial family, were statues of Regilla, Herodes Atticus, Regilla’s parents, Regilla’s maternal grandfather and Herodes Atticus’ parents. There were four statues of 
2 CONC the children of Regilla and Herodes Atticus who were: Elpinice, Athenais, Atticus Bradua and Regillus. The life sized statue of the bull and the statues from the fountain are on display at the Archaeological Museum of Olympia.[2] 
2 CONC Regilla is depicted in a traditional garment and pose that was suited for noble women, elite matrons and empresses. Due to the height, gleaning marble of the fountain, the monument stood out among the surrounding buildings. This f
2 CONC ountain brought great honor to Regilla and her family, in particular in emphasizing their links and relations to the ruling imperial family in Rome. It is not known for certain how the contemporaries and peers of Regilla viewed he
2 CONC r and viewed her magnificent donation to Olympia. In the year of Regilla’s brother being consul, Regilla was kicked to death in the abdomen by a freedman of Herodes Atticus named Alcimedon. Appius Annius Atilius Bradua brought cha
2 CONC rges in Rome against his brother-in-law, alleging that Herodes Atticus had been responsible for her death; Marcus Aurelius had exonerated his old tutor. Posthumous honors and legacy[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT After Regilla died, Herodes Atticus was stricken with grief. Herodes Atticus made various dedications to the memory of his wife. He offered Regilla’s jewels to the goddesses, Ceres and Proserpina. The building that is believed to 
2 CONC be her tomb, known as the Temple of Rediculum, was built in the grounds of the villa she owned with Herodes Atticus, in what is now the Caffarella Park close to the Appian Way and is still well-preserved.[13] Atticus also had rais
2 CONC ed on the boundary line of the property two columns bearing this inscription in Greek and Latin: “To the memory of Annia Regilla, wife of Herodes, the light and soul of the house, to whom these lands once belonged”. In an agricult
2 CONC ural village located in the Datça Peninsula, was a temple dedicated to Faustina the Younger under the title of the New Ceres, a burial place for the family was placed under the protection of Minerva and Nemesis and lastly a grove 
2 CONC sacred to the memory of Regilla. In 161, Herodes Atticus to honor her memory erected a monument in Athens called The Odeon. Regilla and Herodes Atticus from the 2nd century until the present day are still considered as great benef
2 CONC actors in Greece, in particular in Athens. Regilla and Herodes Atticus are commemorated, with two streets named after them Herodou Attikou Street and Rēgíllēs Street and a Square named after them in downtown Athens. In Rēgíllēs St
2 CONC reet is the residence of the Prime Minister of Greece. In Rome, Regilla and Herodes Atticus are commemorated, with two streets named after them. The streets are located in the Quarto Miglio suburb close to the area of the Triopio.
2 CONC  Children[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Regilla bore Herodes Atticus six children, whom three survived to adulthood. Their children were: Son, Claudius – born and died in 141 [3] Daughter, Elpinice – born as Appia Annia Claudia Atilia Regilla Elpinice Agrippina Atria Po
2 CONC lla, 142-165 [3] Daughter, Athenais – born as Marcia Annia Claudia Alcia Athenais Gavidia Latiaria, 143-161 [3] Son, Atticus Bradua – born as Tiberius Claudius Marcus Appius Atilius Bradua Regillus Atticus, born 145 [3] Son, Regil
2 CONC lus – born as Tiberius Claudius Herodes Lucius Vibullius Regillus, 150-155 [3] Unnamed child who died with Regilla or died even perhaps three months later in 160 [3] References[edit]
