0 @N6000000002187600329@ NOTE Arshak I of Iberia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Arshak I (Arsaces) (d. 81 BC) was king of Caucasian Iberia (93-81 BC) and founder of the Arsacid dynasty in Iberia, unrelated to the Arsacid Dynasty of Parthia and the later Arshakuni Dynasty of Armenia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Son of King Artavasdes I of Armenia, he was raised to the Iberian throne after King Farnadjom of Iberia had been deposed and killed in 93 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Under his rule, Iberia became a client state of Armenia and lost some of the western provinces to Colchis.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was succeeded as king of Iberia by his son Artag.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External link
1 CONT Georgian Kings 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Pharnajom King of Caucasian Iberia
1 CONT c. 93 BC–81 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Artag  
0 @N6000000003949970908@ NOTE prince, of the family of the king of Medie [Ref: Settipani LGA p90] Strabon, contemporary geographer, writes c.18/19 on the continuity of Atropatides: "Of the during proclaims king, Atropates organised the Medie in independant sta
1 CONC te and the dynasty originating from himself maintained there even has our days, its successors having known to contract marriages with the Armenie royal families and of Syria, and more recently of Persia" Before their ascession to
1 CONC  the Persian throne, both Artabanos II and Vonones II reigned in Medie; therefore both belonged to the local dynasty of Atropatides.
1 CONT Further, their descendants have arsacide names, based on their mother being an Arsacide princess.
