1 CONC uded by which Tiridates was acknowledged as a Roman client king in Armenia. The power of Vologeses was further weakened by an attack by the nomadic Dahae and Shakas, a rebellion of the Hyrcanians, an invasion by Alani tribesmen in
1 CONC  Media and Armenia, and theusurpation of his son Vardanes II. Vologeses' reign was also marked by a decided reaction against Hellenism; he built Vologesias near Ctesiphon with the intention of drawing to the new town the inhabitan
1 CONC ts and trade of the Greek city Seleucia on the Tigris. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97, VOLOGESES I]
