0 @N6000000002187600307@ NOTE Tigranes the Great
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Tigranes the Great (ruled 95 BC-56 BC) (also called Tigranes II and sometimes Tigranes I) was a king of Armenia. He was the son of Artavasdes I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Around 100 BC the Parthians placed him on the throne of Armenia, in return for "seventy valleys" in Armenia (Strabo 11.14.15). He rapidly built up his power, allying with Mithridates VI of Pontus and marrying his daughter Cleopatr
1 CONC a. Ultimately the two kings' attempts to control Cappadocia resulted in Roman intervention, in the person of Lucius Cornelius Sulla.
1 CONT
1 CONT Tigranes also expanded his domain into Parthia, going into Media as far as Ecbatana and to Arbela in Assyria, capturing northern Mesopotamia. In 83 BC he took over Syria, Phoenicia, and Cilicia, effectively putting an end to the S
1 CONC eleucid dynasty. Many of the inhabitants of conquered cities were sent to his new metropolis Tigranocerta.
1 CONT
1 CONT But his empire was not a lasting one. In 69 BC he warred with Rome, eventually losing Tigranocerta to Lucullus and being separated from Mithridates by Pompey in 66 BC. Tigranes' son went over to Pompey, and as they approached Arta
1 CONC xata, Tigranes himself surrendered, gave up all his territories except Armenia, and finished out his life as a tributary of Rome.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was suceeded as king of Armenia by his son, Artavasdes II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Philip I and Antiochus XII Ruler of the Seleucid Empire Succeeded by:
1 CONT Antiochus XIII 
0 @N6000000003949970731@ NOTE Mithridates of Armenia (flourished 1st century) was an Iberian prince and a king of Armenia under the protection of the Roman Empire.  Mithridates was installed by his brother Pharasmanes I of Iberia who, encouraged by Tiberius, i
1 CONC nvaded Armenia and captured its capital Artaxata in 35. When the Parthian prince Orodes attempted to dispossess Mithridates of his newly-acquired kingdom, Pharasmanes assembled a large army, with which he totally defeated the Part
1 CONC hians in a pitched battle (Tacitus, Annals. vi. 32-35). At a later period (c. 37), the new emperor Caligula had Mithridates arrested, but Claudius restored him on the Armenian throne c. 42. Subsequently, Mithridates's relations wi
1 CONC th Pharasmanes deteriorated and the Iberian king instigated his son, Rhadamistus, to invade Armenia and overthrow Mithridates in 51. Betrayed by his Roman commanders, Mithridates surrendered, but was put to death by Rhadamistus, w
1 CONC ho usurped the crown.
0 @N6000000003319677629@ NOTE
1 CONT wtf 4:565 Vipsania Agrippina (I) Major
0 @N6000000003635729076@ NOTE [4979] d 33 AD
0 @N6000000002188286900@ NOTE Agrippina the Elder
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Roman imperial dynasties
1 CONT Julio-Claudian dynasty 
1 CONT Agrippina the Elder, wife of Germanicus 
1 CONT Augustus 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Natural - Julia the Elder 
1 CONT    Adoptive - Gaius Caesar, Lucius Caesar, Agrippa Postumus, Tiberius 
1 CONT Tiberius 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Natural - Julius Caesar Drusus 
1 CONT    Adoptive - Germanicus 
1 CONT Caligula 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Natural - Julia Drusilla 
1 CONT    Adoptive - Tiberius Gemellus 
1 CONT Claudius 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Natural - Claudia Antonia, Claudia Octavia, Britannicus 
1 CONT    Adoptive - Nero 
1 CONT Nero 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Natural - Claudia Augusta 
1 CONT
1 CONT (Vipsania) Agrippina (Classical Latin: AGRIPPINA•GERMANICI[1]), (14 BC – 18 October 33), most commonly known as Agrippina Major or Agrippina "the Elder", was one of the most prominent women in the Roman Empire in the early 1st cen
1 CONC tury AD. She was the daughter of Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa by his third wife Julia the Elder, was a granddaughter of Augustus and wife of Germanicus. She was mother to the emperor Caligula and grandmother to Nero.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Early life 
1 CONT 2 Traveling wife 
1 CONT 3 Time in Rome 
1 CONT 4 Exile and death 
1 CONT 5 Personality 
1 CONT 6 See also 
1 CONT 7 References 
1 CONT 7.1 Ancient Sources 
1 CONT 7.2 Secondary Sources 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life
1 CONT Agrippina was born in Athens, Greece. In 5 she had married Germanicus, her second cousin and step-grandson of the Emperor Augustus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Agrippina had nine children by Germanicus, three of whom died young. The six who survived to adulthood were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Nero Caesar 
1 CONT Drusus Caesar 
1 CONT Caligula, future Emperor 
1 CONT Agrippina the Younger, mother of Emperor Nero 
1 CONT Julia Drusilla 
1 CONT Julia Livilla 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Traveling wife
1 CONT The well regarded Germanicus was a candidate for the succession and had won fame campaigning in Germania and Gaul, where he was accompanied by Agrippina. This was most unusual for Roman wives, as convention required them to stay a
1 CONC t home, and earned her a reputation as a model for heroic womanhood. She bore him two children in Gaul, a boy and Agrippina the Younger in the Rhine frontier.
1 CONT
1 CONT Agrippina and Germanicus travelled to the Near East in 19, incurring the displeasure of the emperor Tiberius. Germanicus quarrelled with Gnaeus Calpurnius Piso, the governor of Syria, and died in Antioch in mysterious circumstance
1 CONC s. It was widely suspected that Germanicus had been poisoned – perhaps on the orders of Tiberius himself – and Agrippina returned to Rome to avenge his death. She boldly accused Piso of the murder of Germanicus. According to Tacit
1 CONC us (Annals 3.14.1), the prosecution could not prove the poisoning charge, but other charges of treason seemed likely to stick, and Piso committed suicide.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Time in Rome
1 CONT
1 CONT Agrippina landing at Brundisium with the Ashes of Germanicus, Oil on canvas, c. 1768.From 19 to 29, Agrippina remained in Rome, becoming increasingly involved with a group of senators who opposed the growing power of Tiberius' fav
1 CONC ourite Sejanus. Her relations with the emperor became increasingly fraught as she made it clear that she believed that he was responsible for the death of Germanicus. The climate was further poisoned by the "inveterate hatred" tha
1 CONC t Tiberius' mother felt for her (Tacitus, Annals 4.12), since Agrippina's ambition, to be the mother of emperors and thus Rome's first woman, was an open secret. In 26, the emperor rejected her request that she be allowed to marry
1 CONC  again.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Exile and death
1 CONT
1 CONT Cinerary urn of Agrippina, in the tabularium.Agrippina and her sons Nero and Drusus were arrested in 29 on the orders of Tiberius. They were tried by the Senate and Agrippina was banished to the island of Pandataria (now called Ve
1 CONC ntotene) in the Tyrrhenian Sea off the coast of Campania where her mother had once been banished. There she was treated with great brutality, losing an eye from the blow of a centurion and later undergoing forcible feeding (Sueton
1 CONC ius, Tib.53). She died on 18 October 33 in suspicious circumstances. Her death, according to Suetonius the result of voluntary starvation (ibid), was probably hastened by her realisation that the fall of Sejanus had "led to no aba
1 CONC tement of horrors" (Tacitus, Annals 6.25). Tacitus also mentions malnutrition as a likely cause. After her death Tiberius accused her of "having had Asinius Gallus as a paramour and being driven by his death to loathe existence" (
1 CONC Annals 6.25). At Tiberius' prompting the Senate decreed that her birthday should be marked as a day of ill omen (Suet.ibid.).
1 CONT
1 CONT Drusus died of starvation after being imprisoned in Rome and Nero Caesar either committed suicide or was murdered after his trial in 29. Only two of her children are of historical importance: Agrippina the Younger and Gaius Julius
1 CONC  Caesar Augustus Germanicus, who succeeded Tiberius under the name of Caligula. Despite Tiberius' enmity towards Caligula's elder brothers, he nonetheless made Caligula and his cousin Tiberius Gemellus joint heirs to his property.
1 CONT
1 CONT There is a portrait of her in the Capitoline Museums at Rome and a bronze medal in the British Museum showing her ashes being brought back to Rome by order of Caligula.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Personality
1 CONT Agrippina was widely regarded by contemporaries as being a woman of the highest character and exemplary Roman morals, notwithstanding a profound arrogance and a vaulting ambition: Tacitus' verdict is of a woman who "could not endu
1 CONC re equality and loved to domineer, [and who] with her masculine aspirations was far removed from the frailties of women" (Annals 6.25).
1 CONT
1 CONT A superficial assessment views Agrippina as the innocent victim of tyranny. In reality, however, Agrippina herself had done much to provoke her fate. Her constant dwelling on her birth (e.g. Annals 1.40) and her being the "sole su
1 CONC rviving offspring of Augustus" (Annals 3.4) was not merely an insult to Tiberius, Augustus’ son by adoption, but also to Livia, who was Julia Augusta only by testamentary adoption; her attitude also implied a challenge to Tiberius
1 CONC ' own position.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT Wikimedia Commons has media related to: 
1 CONT Agrippina MajorTacitus, Annals i.-vi. 
1 CONT Suetonius, The Twelve Caesars 
1 CONT Julio-Claudian Family Tree 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ E. Groag, A. Stein, L. Petersen - e.a. (edd.), Prosopographia Imperii Romani saeculi I, II et III (PIR), Berlin, 1933 - V 463 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Ancient Sources
1 CONT Suetonius, De vita Casearum - On the Life of the Caesars Tiberius iii.52.3, 53 and Caligula iv.23.1 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Secondary Sources
1 CONT Robin Seager, Tiberius, London (Eyre Methuen) 1972 
1 CONT E. Klebs, H. Dessau, P. Von Rohden (ed.), Prosopographia Imperii Romani, 3 vol., Berlin, 1897-1898. (PIR1) 
0 @N6000000002187712471@ NOTE Aelia Paetina
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Aelia Paetina or Paetina was a Roman woman who lived in the 1st century. Her biological father was consul of 4, Sextus Aelius Catus while her mother is unknown. She was born into the family of the Aelii Tuberones, and thus apparen
1 CONC tly descended from the consul of 11 BC. Her father may have died when she was very young, as she was raised by her relative Praetorian Guard Prefect Lucius Seius Strabo (the biological father of her adoptive brother or relative Lu
1 CONC cius Aelius Sejanus, commander of the Praetorian Guard under Tiberius.
1 CONT
1 CONT Aelia Paetina married the future Roman Emperor Claudius in 28, as his second wife. Their only child was their daughter Claudia Antonia, born in 30. Claudius divorced Paetina after October 31, after when her adoptive brother fell f
1 CONC rom power and was murdered. According to Suetonius, Claudius divorced Paetina for slight offences.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 48, after the death of Roman Empress and Claudius’ third wife Valeria Messalina, Claudius considered remarrying for the fourth time. Claudius’ freedman Tiberius Claudius Narcissus supported a remarriage to Paetina. He suggested
1 CONC  to Claudius to remarry her and reminded him they had a child together. Narcissus also stated that Paetina would cherish Claudia Octavia and Britannicus, as her own children. (Claudia Octavia and Britannicus were Claudius’ childre
1 CONC n with Messalina).
1 CONT
1 CONT Another Freedman, Gaius Julius Callistus, was against Claudius remarrying Paetina and stated to Claudius that he divorced her before; Callistus said that remarrying Paetina would make her more arrogant. Callistus suggested Lollia 
1 CONC Paulina.
1 CONT
1 CONT Another Freedman, Marcus Antonius Pallas, recommended Claudius' niece, Agrippina the Younger. Agrippina was chosen, with dire implications for Claudius' children.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources:
1 CONT Suetonius, The Twelve Caesars, Claudius. 
1 CONT Tacitus, The Annals of Imperial Rome. 
0 @N6000000002187712482@ NOTE Claudia Antonia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Antonia (30–66 AD) was Claudius' only child from his second marriage to Aelia Paetina. Until 37 AD, she was raised by her grandmother Antonia Minor. From then until 43 AD, she was raised by her father.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 43 AD, in an arranged marriage, she first married Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus, a man of the highest birth. According to Suetonius he was murdered several years later, because he was caught in bed with his favourite boyfriend. Howeve
1 CONC r Cassius Dio states that Messalina (out of her fear, of Pompeius being a rival to Britannicus) ordered his execution, so that Antonia could marry Messalina's half-brother to strengthen the Julian blood-line. His name was Faustus 
1 CONC Cornelius Sulla Felix.
1 CONT
1 CONT Faustus Sulla and Antonia married later that year. They had a son, who lived up to his second birthday. The child was a weak one of little strength.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 58 AD, Faustus Sulla was exiled and murdered in 62 AD on Nero's orders. Antonia in 65 AD, participated in the failed revolt of Gaius Calpurnius Piso. Tacitus stated that Antonia would marry Piso, if he became Emperor. However T
1 CONC acitus was not sure if this proposal was a rumour or fact.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of Empress Poppaea Sabina, Nero asked her to marry him. When Antonia refused, Nero had her charged with an attempt of rebellion and executed her.
1 CONT
1 CONT With her death, passed the last living grandchild of Nero Claudius Drusus and Antonia Minor.
0 @N6000000000437173766@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> II, "The Younger"
0 @N6000000002187712493@ NOTE Agrippina the Younger
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Julia Vipsania Agrippina Minor or Agrippina Minor (Latin for "the younger") (November 7, AD 15 – March 59), often called "Agrippinilla" to distinguish her from her mother, was the daughter of Germanicus and Agrippina Major. She wa
1 CONC s sister of Caligula, granddaughter and great-niece to Tiberius, niece and wife of Claudius, and the mother of Nero. She was born at Oppidum Ubiorum on the Rhine, afterwards named in her honour Colonia Agrippinae (modern Cologne, 
1 CONC Germany).
1 CONT
1 CONT Agrippina was first married to (1st century AD) Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus. From this marriage she gave birth to Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus, who would become Roman Emperor Nero. Her husband died in January, 40. While still marri
1 CONC ed, Agrippina participated openly in her brother Caligula's decadent court, where, according to some sources, at his instigation she prostituted herself in a palace. While it was generally agreed that Agrippinilla, as well as her 
1 CONC sisters, had ongoing sexual relationships with their brother Caligula, incest was an oft-used criminal accusation against the aristocracy, because it was impossible to refute successfully. As Agrippina and her sister became more p
1 CONC roblematic for their brother, Caligula sent them into exile for a time, where it is said she was forced to dive for sponges to make a living. In January, 41, Agrippina had a second marriage to the affluent Gaius Sallustius Crispus
1 CONC  Passienus. He died between 44 and 47, leaving his estate to Agrippina.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a widow, Agrippina was courted by the freedman Pallas as a possible marriage match to her own uncle, Emperor Claudius, and became his favourite councillor, even granted the honor of being called Augusta (a title which no other 
1 CONC queen had ever received). They were married on New Year's Day of 49, after the death of Claudius's first wife Messalina. Agrippina then proceeded to persuade Claudius to adopt her son, thereby placing Nero in the line of successio
1 CONC n to the Imperial throne over Claudius's own son, Britannicus. A true Imperial politician, Agrippina did not reject murder as a way to win her battles. Many ancient sources credited her with poisoning Claudius in 54 with a plate o
1 CONC f poisened mushrooms, hence enabling Nero to quickly take the throne as emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT For some time, Agrippina influenced Nero as he was relatively ill-equipped to rule on his own. But Nero eventually felt that she was taking on too much power relative to her position as a woman of Rome. He deprived her of her hono
1 CONC urs and exiled her from the palace, but that was not enough. Three times Nero tried to poison Agrippina, but she had been raised in the Imperial family and was accustomed to taking antidotes. Nero had a machine built and attached 
1 CONC to the roof of her bedroom. The machine was designed to make the ceiling collapse — the plot failed with the machine. According to the historians Tacitus and Suetonius, Nero then plotted her death by sending for her in a boat cons
1 CONC tructed to collapse, intending to drown Agrippina. However, only some of the crew were in on the plot; their efforts were hampered by the rest of the crew trying to save the ship. As the ship sank, one of her handmaidens thought t
1 CONC o save herself by crying that she was Agrippina, thinking they would take special care of her. Instead the maid was instantly beaten to death with oars and chains. The real Agrippina realised what was happening and in the confusio
1 CONC n managed to swim away where a passing fisherman picked her up. Terrified that his cover had been blown, Nero instantly sent men to charge her with treason and summarily execute her. Legend states that when the Emperor's soldiers 
1 CONC came to kill her, Agrippina pulled back her clothes and ordered them to stab her in the belly that had housed such a monstrous son.
1 CONT
1 CONT See also: Julio-Claudian Family Tree
1 CONT
