1 CONT Barrett, Anthony. Agrippina: Sex, Power, and Politics in the Early Empire. Yale University Press. New Haven, 1999. 
1 CONT Dio Cassius. Historia Romanum. Books LX-LXII. 
1 CONT Levick, Barbara. Claudius. Yale University Press. New Haven, 1990. 
1 CONT Momigliano, Arnaldo. Claudius: the Emperor and His Achievement. Trans. W.D. Hogarth. W. Heffer and Sons. Cambridge, 1934. 
1 CONT Oost, S.V., "The Career of M. Antonius Pallas", American Journal of Philology, 79 (1958). 113-139. 
1 CONT Scramuzza, Vincent. The Emperor Claudius. Harvard University Press. Cambridge, 1940. 
1 CONT Suetonius. Twelve Caesars. Life of Claudius. 
1 CONT Suetonius. Twelve Caesars. Life of Titus. 
1 CONT Tacitus. Annals. Books XI-XIII. 
0 @N6000000002187712451@ NOTE Plautia Urgulanilla
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Plautia Urgulanilla (fl. first century) was the first wife of the future Roman Emperor Claudius. They married around 9, when Claudius was 18 years old. According to Suetonius, they divorced in 24 on grounds of adultery by Plautia 
1 CONC and suspicion of her involvement in the murder of her sister-in-law.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her father was Marcus Plautius Silvanus, a general who was consul for the year 2 BC. Urgulanilla was named for her grandmother, Urgulania, a close friend of Livia.
1 CONT
1 CONT She gave birth to a son, Claudius Drusus (who died young), and a daughter, Claudia, who was born five months after the divorce. As Claudia was widely known to be the illegitimate daughter of the freedman Boter, Claudius repudiated
1 CONC  the child and had her laid at Urgulanilla's doorstep.[1] Her adopted nephew was Tiberius Plautius Silvanus Aelianus.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ Suetonius. Claud. 27. 
0 @N6000000002187712549@ NOTE Antonia Minor
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Julia Antonia Minor ("the younger") (30 January/31 January 36 BC - May-October 37 AD) was the daughter of Mark Antony and Octavia.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT She married Drusus -- the son of the empress Livia and brother of the emperor Tiberius -- and their children were Germanicus, Livilla, and the emperor Claudius. She was the grandmother of Caligula and Agrippina the Younger, and th
1 CONC e great-grandmother of Nero.
1 CONT
1 CONT She never remarried after the death of her husband in 9 BC. When her son Germanicus died in 19 AD, she was forbidden to go to the funeral by orders of Tiberius and Livia. After the death of Livia in 29 AD, she took care of Germani
1 CONC cus and Agrippina the Elder's youngest children---Caligula and Drusilla.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 31 AD she exposed a plot between her daughter Livilla and Sejanus, Tiberius's Praetorian Prefect. This led to Sejanus's downfall and to the death of Livilla. Tiberius handed Livilla over to her formidable mother. According to T
1 CONC acitus, Antonia imprisoned Livilla in her rooms, effectively starving her to death. Claudius, her biggest disappointment (she once called him a "monster") was the only one of her children to survive her.
1 CONT
1 CONT She committed suicide in 37 AD on Caligula's orders after expressing unhappiness over the murder of her youngest grandson, Tiberius Gemellus. There is a passage in Suetonius's "Life of Gaius" that mentions how Caligula may have gi
1 CONC ven her poison himself.
