0 @N6000000002188286927@ NOTE Drusus Caesar
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Drusus Julius Caesar, also referred to as Drusus III (7 – 33), was a member of a noble family of Ancient Rome. He was a son of Germanicus and Agrippina the Elder.
1 CONT
1 CONT Drusus married Aemilia Lepida, daughter of Marcus Aemilius Lepidus, his second cousin. Tacitus reports that during their marriage "she had pursued her husband with ceaseless accusations". In 36, she was charged with adultery with 
1 CONC a slave and committed suicide, "since there was no question about her guilt" (Tacitus, Annals 6.40).
1 CONT
1 CONT Drusus' paternal grandparents were Nero Claudius Drusus and Antonia Minor. His maternal grandparents were Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa and Julia the Elder. He was adopted by his paternal great-uncle Tiberius after Tiberius' own son, J
1 CONC ulius Caesar Drusus, had died.
1 CONT
1 CONT Drusus Caesar was later accused of plotting against Tiberius and was imprisoned in 30, a year after his mother Agrippina the Elder and his brother Nero Caesar were arrested. He starved to death in prison in 33, reduced to chewing 
1 CONC the stuffing of his bed (Annals 6.23).
1 CONT
1 CONT His siblings included four brothers (Tiberius and Gaius Julius, who died young; Nero, and Caligula) and three sisters (Julia Livilla, Julia Drusilla and Agrippina the younger).
0 @N6000000002188286937@ NOTE Caligula
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Caligula 
1 CONT Emperor of the Roman Empire 
1 CONT
1 CONT Bust of Gaius Cæsar in the Louvre 
1 CONT Reign 16 March 37 AD –
1 CONT 24 January 41 AD
1 CONT (Consul from 39) 
1 CONT Full name Gaius Julius Caesar Augustus Germanicus 
1 CONT Born August 31, 12(12-08-31) AD 
1 CONT Died January 24, 41 (aged 28) AD 
1 CONT Predecessor Tiberius 
1 CONT Successor Claudius 
1 CONT Wife/wives 1) Junia Claudilla, 33–34 
1 CONT  2) Livia Orestilla, 37 or 38 
1 CONT  3) Lollia Paulina, 38 
1 CONT  4) Caesonia, ?–41 
1 CONT Issue Julia Drusilla 
1 CONT Dynasty Julio-Claudian 
1 CONT Father Germanicus 
1 CONT Mother Agrippina the Elder 
1 CONT
1 CONT Gaius Julius Caesar Augustus Germanicus (August 31, 12 – January 24, 41), more commonly known by his nickname Caligula (pronounced /k?'l?gj?l?/, meaning "little [soldier's] boots"), was a Roman Emperor who reigned from 16 March 37
1 CONC  until his assassination on 24 January 41. Caligula was the third emperor of the Roman Empire, and a member of the Julio-Claudian dynasty which descended from Augustus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Caligula's father, Germanicus, was the adopted son of emperor Tiberius and one of Rome's most beloved generals. The young Gaius earned his nickname, meaning little soldier's boot, while travelling with his father on military campa
1 CONC igns in Germania. When Germanicus died in Antioch in 19, his mother Agrippina the Elder returned to Rome with her six children, where she became entangled in an increasingly bitter feud with Tiberius. During the course of the 20s 
1 CONC and 30s, many of Caligula's relatives, including Agrippina and two elder brothers, died in mysterious circumstances. Caligula withdrew to the island of Capri in 31, where Tiberius himself had retired since 26, and eventually succe
1 CONC eded his adoptive grandfather upon his death on 16 March 37.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Caligula was popular with the Roman public throughout his reign, the scarce surviving sources focus upon anecdotes of his alleged cruelty, extravagance and sexual perversity, presenting him as an insane tyrant. While the 
1 CONC reliability of these sources has been difficult to assess, what is known is that during his brief reign, Caligula worked to increase the authority of the Principate, but struggled to maintain his position in the face of several co
1 CONC nspiracies to overthrow him. He focused much of his attention on ambitious construction projects, and annexed Mauretania but failed to conquer Britain.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 24 January 41, Caligula was assassinated as the result of a conspiracy involving members of his own bodyguard and the Roman Senate. The conspirators' attempt to use the opportunity to restore the Roman Republic was thwarted, as
1 CONC  the same day the Praetorian Guard declared Caligula's uncle Claudius emperor in his place.
1 CONT
