0 @N6000000002187718049@ NOTE Claudia Marcella
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Claudia Marcella was the name of both daughters of Octavia the sister of Caesar Augustus, and her first husband, the consul Gaius Claudius Marcellus.
1 CONT
1 CONT The elder, Claudia Marcella Major (b. 44 BC), was married to Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa in 28 BC to cement a political alliance between Agrippa and her uncle. Their daughter Vipsania Marcella was born in Rome in 27 BC. In 21 BC Agri
1 CONC ppa divorced Claudia to marry Augustus' daughter Julia Caesaris. She then married Iullus Antonius, the son of Mark Antony, who was later exiled for adultery with Julia. Their son Lucius Antonius was born in 20 or 19 BC. After Iull
1 CONC us' exile, Claudia married Sextus Appuleius, the consul of AD 14. In AD 17 their daughter Appuleia Varilla was charged with adultery and speaking insultingly about the imperial family. Although she was acquitted, her loved Manlius
1 CONC  was banished from Italy and Africa.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her younger sister, Claudia Marcella Minor (b. 43 BC), was married first to the consul Aemilius Lepidus Paullus. Their daughter Claudia Pulchra was born in 14 BC. After Paullus' death in 13 BC, Claudia married the consul Marcus Va
1 CONC lerius Messalla Messallinus. Through their son, Marcus Valerius Messalla Barbatus, she was the grandmother of the emperor Claudius' wife Messalina.
0 @N6000000002188289313@ NOTE Lucius Vipsanius Agrippa
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Lucius Vipsanius Agrippa is the father of another Lucius Vipsanius Agrippa, Roman Politician and General Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa and distinguished Roman woman Vipsania Polla.
1 CONT
1 CONT The family of Lucius Vipsanius originated from the Italian countryside and were of humble and plebs origins. The family was of equestrian rank and were wealthy. However, the Roman aristocracy considered them undistinguished and un
1 CONC sophisticated. Little is known of the life of Lucius Vipsanius Agrippa.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Pantheon, built by the Roman Emperor Hadrian in Rome in 118, was based on the design by his son Marcus. The name of Lucius Vipsanius and his son are inscribed on the building.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Source
1 CONT http://www.livius.org/vi-vr/vipsanius/agrippa.html
1 CONT
1 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucius_Vipsanius_Agrippa"
0 @N6000000002188289328@ NOTE Vipsania Polla
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Vipsania Polla was the daughter of Lucius Vipsanius Agrippa, (a man of equestrian rank) and sister to another Lucius Vipsanius Agrippa and Roman General and Politician Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa. Polla lived during the late Roman Re
1 CONC public and in the reign of Rome’s first Emperor Augustus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although this Roman woman was a distinguished woman, little is known on the life of Vipsania Polla. All we know is that Polla finished the construction of a monument called the Porticus Vipsania. The Porticus Vipsania, was a map o
1 CONC f the Roman Empire, which was engraved in Marble. Marcus Agrippa started the construction of this map, before his death in 12 BC. The Porticus Vipsania was once displayed in Rome, not far the Via Flaminia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although the Porticus Vipsania hasn’t survived, the description of this map is mentioned in Natural History by Pliny the Elder and through the Peutinger Map.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Source:
1 CONT http://www.livius.org/vi-vr/vipsanius/agrippa.html
0 @N6000000003222440751@ NOTE Murdered
1 CONT
1 CONT Clark, Christopher A., "Ancestors of Constantine I The Great, Emperor of Rome," www.familytreemaker.com/users/c/l/a/Christopher-A-Clark-/GENE11-0001.html, Oct. 20, 2000
0 @N6000000002187624610@ NOTE Nestled in the highlands of modern-day Kurdish Iraq, Armenia and northern Iran, two millennia ago this land sheltered the proud Jewish kingdom of Adiabene, with its capital at Arbela, nominally part of the Assyrian province of the
1 CONC  Parthian Empire.1 
1 CONT
1 CONT In the midst of changing alliances between Claudius Caesar, Herod and the remnants of the old Jerusalem Priesthood, a Jewish kingdom east of the Tigris was born in the first century CE that had a direct and benevolent effect on th
1 CONC e Israelites living in the Jewish Kingdom of Judea, and throughout the great sweep of the eastern Roman Empire. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Jerusalem at the time was under the thrall of the Roman procurator Fadus and thus subject to the changing whims of the Roman senate, emperor and oligarchs. Jews throughout the land of Judea had suffered under the increasing power 
1 CONC of the Roman empire's interests and had seen their holy Priesthood whittled and gutted to house a pro-Roman theocracy. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In Antiquities of The Jews: Book Twenty, Josephus cites the ongoing battles of consolidation between Jews and non-Jews surrounding their kingdom, including the case of the Perean Jews versus the Philadelphians: 
1 CONT
1 CONT But [Cuspius] Fadus, as soon as he was come procurator into Judea, found quarrelsome doings between the Jews that dwelt in Perea, and the people of Philadelphia about their borders, at a village called Mia, that was filled with me
1 CONC n of a warlike temper; for the Jews of Perea had taken up arms without the consent of their principal men, and had destroyed many of the Philadelphians.2 
1 CONT Eager to save face, Fadus had the three leaders of the Jewish revolt against the Mians captured, murdering one, named Hannibal.3 The other two, named Areram and Eleazar, were banished.4 An additional partisan named Tholomy was cap
1 CONC tured and killed by the Romans, but not before he had inflicted demonstrable damage onto the Idumeans and their kindred Arabians in and around Judea.5 
1 CONT
1 CONT http://www.khazaria.com/adiabene/lissner1.html
0 @N6000000002187597696@ NOTE John Hyrcanus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT John Hyrcanus (Yohanan Girhan) (reigned 134 BCE - 104 BCE, died 104 BCE) was a Hasmonean (Maccabeean) leader of the 2nd century BC. Apparently the name "Hyrcanus" was taken by him as a reignal name upon his accession to power.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Simon Maccabaeus and hence the nephew of Judas Maccabaeus, Jonathan Maccabaeus and their siblings, whose story is told in the deuterocanonical books of 1 Maccabees and 2 Maccabees, and in the Talmud. John was not
1 CONC  present at a banquet at which his father and his two brothers were murdered, purportedly by his brother-in-law Ptolemy. He attained to his father's former offices, that of high priest and king (although some Jews never accepted a
1 CONC ny of the Hasmoneans as being legitimate kings, as they were not lineal descendants of David).
1 CONT
1 CONT John Hyrcanus apparently combined an energetic and able style of leadership with the zeal of his forebears. He was known as a brave and brilliant military leader. He is credited with the forced conversion of the Idumeans to Judais
1 CONC m, which was unusual for a Jewish leader; Judaism was not typically spread by the sword. He also set out to resolve forcibly the religious dispute between the Jews and the Samaritans; during his reign he destroyed the Samaritan te
1 CONC mple on Mount Gerizim (although their descendants still worship among its ruins), which served further to deepen the already-historic hatred and rivalry between the two groups. Many historians believe that the apocryphal book of J
1 CONC ubilees was written during his reign; some would suggest even at his behest. Some writers, particularly Christian ones, have dated the division of Judaism into the parties of Pharisees and Sadducees to his era; most Jewish writers
1 CONC  and some Christian ones suggest that this split actually well predates him. Some historians would go so far as to identify him, as a priest, predominantly with the Sadducee party, which was closely associated with the Temple wors
1 CONC hip and the priestly class.
1 CONT
1 CONT John Hyrcanus represented in some ways the highest point of the Hasmonean Dynasty. The restored Jewish "kingdom" approached its maximum limits of both territory and prestige. Upon his death, his offices were divided among his heir
1 CONC s; his son Aristobulus succeeded him as high priest; his wife as "Queen regnant". The son, however, soon came to desire the essentially unchecked power of his father; he shortly ordered his mother and his brothers imprisoned. This
1 CONC  event seems to mark the beginning of the decline of the Hasmonean Dynasty; in just over four decades they were removed from power by the Roman Republic and none of them ever began to approach the level of power or prestige that h
1 CONC ad pertained to John Hyrcanus or his predecessors.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Simon Hasmonean Ruler and High Priest of Judaea
1 CONT 134–104 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Aristobulus 
0 @N6000000002187597715@ NOTE Aristobulus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT For other people with this name, see Aristobulus (disambiguation) 
1 CONT Aristobulus (reigned 104-103 BC) was a king of the Hebrew Hasmonean Dynasty, and the eldest of the five sons of King John Hyrcanus. He was the first of the Hasmonean rulers to call himself "king." According to the Hebrew Scripture
1 CONC s, only descendants of Judah, or, more specifically, the House of David, were qualified to be kings of Israel. All of Aristobulus' predecessors used the title of "nasi"/"prince".
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the directions of John Hyrcanus, the government of the country after his death was to be placed in the hands of his wife, and Aristobulus was originally to receive only the high-priesthood. He was not however satisfie
1 CONC d with this, so he cast his mother into prison and allowed her to starve there. By this means he came into the possession of the throne, which, however, he did not long enjoy, as after a year's reign he died of a painful illness (
1 CONC 103 BC). He was hostile to the Pharisees and pursued them with ruthlessness.
1 CONT
1 CONT Aristobulus' successor was his eldest brother, Alexander Jannæus, who, together with his two brothers, was freed from prison by Queen Shelomit [Salome] Alexandra, the widow of Aristobulus and sister to Shimon ben Shetach (the lead
1 CONC ing Jewish sage of that time).
