0 @N6000000002187624146@ NOTE Ptolemy IV Philopator
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Under the reign of Ptolemy IV Philopator ( Greek: ?t??eµa??? F???p?t??, reigned 221-204 BC), son of Ptolemy III and Berenice II of Egypt, the decline of the Ptolemaic kingdom began.
1 CONT
1 CONT His reign was inaugurated by the murder of his mother, and he was always under the dominion of favourites, male and female, who indulged his vices and conducted the government as they pleased. Self-interest led his ministers to ma
1 CONC ke serious preparations to meet the attacks of Antiochus III the Great on Coele-Syria including Judea, and the great Egyptian victory of Raphia (217), where Ptolemy himself was present, secured the northern borders of the kingdom 
1 CONC for the remainder of his reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT The arming of Egyptians in this campaign had a disturbing effect upon the native population of Egypt, leading to the secession of Upper Egypt under pharaohs Harmachis (also known as Hugronaphor) and Ankmakis, (also known as Chaonn
1 CONC ophris) thus creating a kingdom that occupied much of the country and lasted nearly twenty years.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philopator was devoted to orgiastic forms of religion and literary dilettantism. He built a temple to Homer and composed a tragedy, to which his vile favourite Agathocles added a commentary. He married (about 215 BC) his sister Ar
1 CONC sinoë III, but continued to be ruled by his mistress Agathoclea, sister of Agathocles.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy IV is a major protagonist of the apocryphal 3 Maccabees, which describes purported events following the Battle of Raphia, in both Jerusalem and Alexandria.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ptolemy Philopator I at LacusCurtius — (Chapter VII of E. R. Bevan's House of Ptolemy, 1923)
1 CONT     * Ptolemy IV — (Egyptian Royal Genealogy)
1 CONT     * The great revolt of the Egyptians:205–186 BC (2004)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy III Euergetes Lagidae Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy V Epiphanes
