1 CONT Notable relatives
1 CONT Gaius Marius ( married to his Aunt Julia) 
1 CONT Lucius Cornelius Sulla (possibly through Marriage) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Possible male lovers
1 CONT Roman society viewed the passive role during sex, regardless of gender, to be a sign of submission or inferiority. Indeed, Suetonius says that in Caesar's Gallic triumph, his soldiers sang that, "Caesar may have conquered the Gaul
1 CONC s, but Nicomedes conquered Caesar".[27] According to Cicero, Bibulus, Gaius Memmius (whose account may be from firsthand knowledge), and others (mainly Caesar's enemies), he had an affair with Nicomedes IV of Bithynia early in his
1 CONC  career. The tales were repeated by some Roman politicians as a way to humiliate and degrade him. It is possible that the rumors were spread only as a form of character assassination. Caesar himself, according to Cassius Dio, deni
1 CONC ed the accusations under oath.[28]
1 CONT
1 CONT Mark Antony charged that Octavian had earned his adoption by Caesar through sexual favors. Suetonius described Antony's accusation of an affair with Octavian as political slander. The boy Octavian was to become the first Roman emp
1 CONC eror following Caesar's death.[29]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Chronology
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Honours
1 CONT Was voted the title Divus, or "god," after his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his life, he received many honours, including titles such as Pater Patriae (Father of the Fatherland), Pontifex Maximus (Highest Priest), and Dictator. The many titles bestowed on him by the Senate are sometimes cited as a 
1 CONC cause of his assassination, as it seemed inappropriate to many contemporaries for a mortal man to be awarded so many honours.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a young man he was awarded the Corona Civica (civic crown) for valor while fighting in Asia minor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Perhaps the most significant title he carried was his name from birth: Caesar. This name would be awarded to every Roman emperor, and it became a signal of great power and authority far beyond the bounds of the empire. The title b
1 CONC ecame the German Kaiser and Slavic Tsar/Czar. The last tsar in nominal power was Simeon II of Bulgaria whose reign ended in 1946; for two thousand years after Julius Caesar's assassination, there was a least one head of state bear
1 CONC ing his name.
1 CONT
1 CONT Note, however, that Caesar was an ordinary name of no more importance than other cognomen like Cicero and Brutus. It did not become an Imperial title until well after Julius Caesar's death.
1 CONT
