1 CONC eek leadership in the eastern Mediterranean. Ptolemy II was no man of peace, but neither was he one of the warlike Hellenistic soldier-kings. A prudent and enlightened ruler, he found his strength in diplomatic abilityand his sati
1 CONC sfaction in a vast curiosity of mind.
0 @N6000000001355636512@ NOTE Birth: in 308 BC, Island of Cos [Kos, Greece]
1 CONT Death: in 246 BC 1
1 CONT Event: Ancestor M
1 CONT Event: Ruled 285 - 246 BC, Pharaoh of Egypt 1
1 CONT Event: Throne Name User-ka-en-ra Mery-amun 2
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT Ptolemy II PHILADELPHUS (Greek: Brother-loving) (b. 308 BC, Cos--d. 246), king of Egypt (285-246 BC), second king of the Ptolemaic dynasty,who extended his power by skillful diplomacy, developed agriculture and commerce, and made 
1 CONC Alexandria a leading centre of the arts and sciences.
1 CONT Life
1 CONT Reigning at first with his father, Ptolemy I Soter, he became sole ruler in 283-282 and purged his family of possible rivals. This dynasticstrife led also to the banishment of his first wife, Arsinoe I, daughter of King Lysimachus
1 CONC  of Thrace. Ptolemy then married his sister, Arsinoe II, an event that shocked Greek public opinion but was celebratedby the Alexandrian court poets. Taking advantage of the difficulties of the rival kingdoms of the Seleucids and 
1 CONC Antigonids, Ptolemy II extended his rule in Syria, Asia Minor, and the Aegean at their expense and asserted at the same time his influence in Ethiopia and Arabia. Egyptian embassies to Rome as well as to India reflect the wide ran
1 CONC ge of Ptolemy's political and commercial interests.
1 CONT While a new war with the Seleucids (from 274 to 270) did not affect the basic position of the rival kingdoms, the so-called Chremonidean War (268?-261), stirred up by Ptolemy against Antigonus II Gonatas, king of Macedonia, result
1 CONC ed in the weakening of Ptolemaic influence in the Aegean and brought about near disaster to Ptolemy's allies Athens and Sparta. Ptolemy was no more successful in the Second Syrian War (c.260-253), fought against the coalition of t
1 CONC he Seleucid king AntiochusII and Antigonus Gonatas. The unsuccessful course of the military operations was compensated for, to a certain degree, by the diplomatic skill of Ptolemy, who first managed to lure Antigonus into concludi
1 CONC ng a separate peace (255) and then brought the war with the Seleucid Empireto an end by marrying his daughter, Berenice--provided with a huge dowry--to his foe Antiochus II. The magnitude of this political masterstroke can be gaug
1 CONC ed by the fact that Antiochus, before marrying the Ptolemaic princess, had to dismiss his former wife, Laodice. Thus freed for the moment from Seleucid opposition and sustained by the considerable financial means provided by the E
1 CONC gyptian economy, Ptolemy II devoted himself again to Greece and aroused new adversaries to Antigonid Macedonia. While the Macedonian forces were bogged down in Greece, Ptolemy reasserted his influence in the Aegean, making good th
1 CONC e setback suffered during the Chremonidean War. He further improved his position byarranging for the marriage of his son (and later successor) Ptolemy III Euergetes to the daughter of King Magas of Cyrene, who had proved so far a 
1 CONC very troublesome neighbour. Not aiming at outright hegemony (even less imperialistic conquest) in the Hellenistic world of the eastern Mediterranean, Ptolemy II tried nonetheless to secure for Egypt asgood a position as possible, 
1 CONC holding at large his rivals beyond a wide buffer zone of foreign possessions. Without being completely successful, he managed to let his allies bear the brunt of the heaviest reverses, healing his own military wounds with diplomat
1 CONC ic remedies. The influence on Ptolemy of his wife and sister Arsinoe II, particularly in foreign affairs, was certainly substantial, though not as extensive asclaimed by some contemporary authors.
1 CONT Influence.
1 CONT Ptolemy II's record in domestic affairs is no less impressive. From pharaonic times onward, agriculture and the work of artisans in Egypt had been highly organized. Under Ptolemy's supervision and with the help of Greek administra
1 CONC tors, this system developed into a kind of planned economy. The peasant masses of the Nile Valley provided cheap labour, so that the introduction of slavery on a broad basis was never considered an economic necessity in Ptolemaic 
1 CONC Egypt. Ptolemy II became a master at the fiscal exploitation of the Egyptian countryside; the capital, Alexandria, served as the main trading and export centre. PtolemyII displayed a vivid interest in Greek as well as in Egyptian 
1 CONC religion, paid visits to the sanctuaries in the countryside, and spent large sums erecting temples. Anxious to secure a solid position for, and religious elevation of, his dynasty, the King insisted upon divine honours not only fo
1 CONC r his parents but also for his sister and wife Arsinoe II and himself as theoi adelphoi ("brother gods"). He thus became one of the most ardent promoters of the Hellenistic ruler cult, which in turn was to have a far-reaching infl
1 CONC uence on the cult of the Roman emperors.
1 CONT Under Ptolemy II, Alexandria also played a leading role in arts and science. Throughout the whole Mediterranean world the King acquired a reputation for being a generous patron of poets and scholars. Surrounding himself with a hos
1 CONC t of court poets, such as Callimachus and Theocritus, he expanded the library and financed the museum, a research centre founded as a counterweight to the more antimonarchial Athenian schools. Learning there was not confined to ph
1 CONC ilosophy and literature but extended also to include mathematics and natural sciences. The age of Ptolemy II coincided with the apex of Hellenistic civilization; its vigour and glamour were a result of the still fresh forces of Gr
1 CONC eek leadership in the eastern Mediterranean. Ptolemy II was no man of peace, but neither was he one of the warlike Hellenistic soldier-kings. A prudent and enlightened ruler, he found his strength in diplomatic abilityand his sati
1 CONC sfaction in a vast curiosity of mind.
0 @N6000000002187624204@ NOTE Ptolemy II Philadelphus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy II Philadelphus (Greek: ?t??eµa??? F???de?f??, 309–246 BC), was the king of Ptolemaic Egypt from 281 BC to 246 BC. He was of a delicate constitution. E.J.Bickermann (Chronology of the Ancient World, 2nd ed. 1980) gives the
1 CONC  date of his death as January 29.
1 CONT
1 CONT His half-brother Ptolemy Ceraunus found compensation by becoming king in Macedonia in 281 BC, and perished in the Gallic invasion of 280-79 BC (see Brennus).
1 CONT
1 CONT He began his reign as co-regent with his father Ptolemy I from 288 BC–285 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy II maintained a splendid court in Alexandria. Not that Egypt held aloof from wars. Magas of Cyrene opened war on his half-brother (274 BC), and the Seleucid king Antiochus I Soter, desiring Coele-Syria with Judea, attacked
1 CONC  soon after. Two or three years of war followed. Egypt ended as the undisputed naval power of the eastern Mediterranean; the Ptolemaic sphere of power extended over the Cyclades to Samothrace, and the harbours and coast towns of C
1 CONC ilicia Trachea ("Rough Cilicia"), Pamphylia, Lycia and Caria were largely in Ptolemy's hands.
1 CONT
1 CONT The victory won by Antigonus II Gonatas, king of Macedonia, over his fleet at Cos (between 258 and 256 BC) did not long interrupt his command of the Aegean. In a second war with the Seleucid kingdom, under Antiochus II Theos (afte
1 CONC r 260 BC), Ptolemy sustained losses on the seaboard of Asia Minor and agreed to a peace by which Antiochus married his daughter Berenice (ca 250 BC).
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy's first wife, Arsinoë I, daughter of Lysimachus, was the mother of his legitimate children. After her repudiation he married, probably for political reasons, his full-sister Arsinoë II, the widow of Lysimachus, by an Egypt
1 CONC ian custom abhorrent to Greek morality.
1 CONT
1 CONT The material and literary splendour of the Alexandrian court was at its height under Ptolemy II. Pomps and gay religions flourished. Ptolemy deified his parents and his sister-wife, after her death (270 BC), as Philadelphus. This 
1 CONC surname was used in later generations to distinguish Ptolemy II himself, but properly it belongs to Arsinoë only, not to the king.
1 CONT
1 CONT Callimachus, made keeper of the library, Theocritus, and a host of lesser poets, glorified the Ptolemaic family. Ptolemy himself was eager to increase the library and to patronize scientific research. He had the strange beasts of 
1 CONC far off lands sent to Alexandria. But, an enthusiast for Hellenic culture, he seems to have shown but little interest in the native religion.
1 CONT
1 CONT The tradition preserved in the pseudepigraphical Letter of Aristeas which connects the Septuagint translation of the Old Testament into Greek with his patronage is probably not historical. Ptolemy had many brilliant mistresses, an
1 CONC d his court, magnificent and dissolute, intellectual and artificial, has been justly compared with the Versailles of Louis XIV.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Alexandrian Pleiad
1 CONT     * Ptolemaic period - period of Egyptian history during the Ptolemaic dynasty.
1 CONT     * Ptolemais - towns and cities named after members of the Ptolemaic dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ptolemy Philadelphus at LacusCurtius — (Chapter III of E. R Bevan's House of Ptolemy, 1923)
1 CONT     * Ptolemy II software
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemaios II
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy I Soter Lagidae Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy III Euergetes
0 @N6000000002187624420@ NOTE Magas of Cyrene
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Magas of Cyrene (r. 276 - 250 BCE) was a Greek king of Cyrene (today's Libya). He managed to wrestle independence for Cyrene from the Greek Ptolemaic dynasty in Egypt.
1 CONT
1 CONT Magas was the son of Berenice and Philip, a Macedonian noble man, before Berenice remarried with the powerful Ptolemy I, founder of the Greek Ptolemaic dynasty in Egypt. Magas was a half-brother to their son, Ptolemy II Philadelph
1 CONC os, the second Ptolemy ruler of Egypt.
1 CONT
1 CONT Magas received the governorship of Cyrene from his mother Berenice. Following the death of Ptolemy I however, Magas tried on several occasions to wrestle independence for Cyrene, until he crowned himself king around 276 BCE.
1 CONT
1 CONT Magas then married Apama, the daughter of the Seleucid king Antiochus I, and used his marital alliance to foment a pact to invade Egypt. He opened hostilities against his half brother Ptolemy II Philadelphos in 274 BCE, attacking 
1 CONC Egypt from the west, as Antiochus I was attacking Palestine. However Magas had to cancel his operations due to an internal revolt of the Libyan nomad Marmaridae. In the east, Antiochus I suffered defeat against the armies of Ptole
1 CONC my Philadelphos.
1 CONT
1 CONT Magas at least managed to maintain the independence of Cyrene until his death in 250 BCE, upon which the kingdom was almost immediately reabsorbed by Ptolemaic Egypt.
