0 @N6000000002188108861@ NOTE Gaius Julius Caesar Strabo Vopiscus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Gaius Julius Caesar Strabo Vopiscus (c.130 BC - 87 BC) was the younger son to Lucius Julius Caesar II and his wife Poppilia and younger brother to Lucius Julius Caesar III. His cognomina indicate he was possibly cross-eyed, and th
1 CONC e surviving member of a set of twins.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 103 BC, he was on a committee to supervise the implementation of the Lex frumentaria, an agrarian bill, proposed by tribune Lucius Appuleius Saturninus. Strabo became a priest in 99 BC; a quaestor in 96 BC and an aedile in 90 B
1 CONC C.
1 CONT
1 CONT When the war between Sulla and Marius started, Strabo stood for the consulship, although he needed to be a praetor before. Sulla supported him and this caused great civil unrest.
1 CONT
1 CONT Along with his brother he was killed fighting in the streets at the beginning of the Civil War by partisans of Marius in 87 BC. According to Livy, their heads were exposed on the speaker’s platform.
1 CONT
1 CONT Caesar Strabo Vopiscus wrote at least 3 tragedies with Greek themes. These plays were Adrastus, Tecmesa and Teutras. Only fragments of the plays survive. According to Cicero, he was an orator known for his wit and humor. Cicero pu
1 CONC blished a dialogue called De Oratore, in which Strabo explains why humor is important in speech.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was an uncle to Lucius Julius Caesar IV, Julia Antonia and a great-uncle to Mark Antony, Gaius Antonius, Lucius Antonius and Lucius Julius Caesar V.
0 @N6000000002188289137@ NOTE Publius Mucius Scaevola
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Publius Mucius Scaevola (c. 115 BC) was a prominent Roman politician and jurist. He was tribune in 141 BC, praetor in 136 BC, and consul in 133 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his consulship, Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Serapio opposed Tiberius Gracchus running for a second consecutive tribuneship. Mucius said that he would allow Gracchus to run since that seemed to be the wishes of the peopl
1 CONC e and he did not want to resort to violence to prevent him from running. Scipio though continued to protest and led the mob that killed Gracchus. Mucius did not condemn Scipio for these actions despite his earlier comments.
1 CONT
1 CONT He later became Pontifex Maximus in 131 BC after the death of his brother, Publius Licinius Crassus Dives Mucianus. He is the last Pontifex Maximus to have published the Annales Maximi, the list of events of the year.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Gaius Fulvius Flaccus and Publius Cornelius Scipio Aemilianus Africanus Consul of the Roman Republic
1 CONT with Lucius Calpurnius Piso Frugi
1 CONT 133 BC Succeeded by
1 CONT Publius Popillius Laenas and Publius Rupilius 
0 @N6000000002187624215@ NOTE Lysimachus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Lysimachus (c. 360 BC–281 BC, Greek: ??s?µa???) was a Macedonian officer and "successor" (diadochus) of Alexander the Great, later a king (306 BC) in Thrace and Asia Minor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Son of Agathocles, he was from Pella in Macedonia. During Alexander's Persian campaigns he was one of his immediate bodyguard and distinguished himself in India. After Alexander’s death (323 BC) he was appointed to the government 
1 CONC of Thrace and the Chersonese. For a long time he was chiefly occupied with fighting against the Odrysian king Seuthes III.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 315 BC he joined Cassander, Ptolemy and Seleucus against Antigonus, who, however, diverted his attention by stirring up Thracian and Scythian tribes against him. In 309 BC, he founded Lysimachia in a commanding situation on the
1 CONC  neck connecting the Chersonese with the mainland. He followed the example of Antigonus in taking the title of king. In 302 when the second affiance between Cassander, Ptolemy and Seleucus was made, Lysimachus, reinforced by troop
1 CONC s from Cassander, entered Asia Minor, where he met with little resistance. On the approach of Antigonus he retired into winter quarters near Heraclea, marrying its widowed queen Amastris, a Persian princess. Seleucus joined him in
1 CONC  301 BC, and at the battle of Ipsus Antigonus was defeated and slain. His dominions were divided among the victors, Lysimachus receiving the greater part of Asia Minor.
1 CONT Coin of Lysimachus. Greek inscription reads ??S???OS ??S?????? ([coin] of King Lysimachus).
1 CONT Enlarge
1 CONT Coin of Lysimachus. Greek inscription reads ??S???OS ??S?????? ([coin] of King Lysimachus).
1 CONT
1 CONT Feeling that Seleucus was becoming dangerously great, Lysimachus now allied himself with Ptolemy, marrying his daughter Arsinoe II of Egypt. Amastris, who had divorced herself from him, returned to Heraclea. When Antigonus’s son D
1 CONC emetrius I of Macedon renewed hostilities (297 BC), during his absence in Greece, Lysimachus seized his towns in Asia Minor, but in 294 BC concluded a peace whereby Demetrius was recognized as ruler of Macedonia. He tried to carry
1 CONC  his power beyond the Danube, but was defeated and taken prisoner by the Getae king Dromichaetes (Dromihete), who, however, set him free on amicable terms. Demetrius subsequently threatened Thrace, but had to retire in consequence
1 CONC  of a rising in Boeotia, and an attack from Pyrrhus of Epirus.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 288 BC Lysimachus and Pyrrhus in turn invaded Macedonia, and drove Demetrius out of the country. Pyrrhus was at first allowed to remain in possession of Macedonia with the title of king, but in 285 BC he was expelled by Lysimac
1 CONC hus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Domestic troubles embittered the last years of Lysimachus’s life. Amastris had been murdered by her two sons; Lysimachus treacherously put them to death. On his return Arsinoe asked the gift of Heraclea, and he granted her request
1 CONC , though he had promised to free the city. In 284 BC Arsinoe, desirous of gaining the succession for her sons in preference to Agathocles (the eldest son of Lysimachus), intrigued against him with the help of her brother Ptolemy K
1 CONC eraunos; they accused him of conspiring with Seleucus to seize the throne, and he was put to death.
1 CONT
1 CONT This atrocious deed of Lysimachus aroused great indignation. Many of the cities of Asia revolted, and his most trusted friends deserted him. The widow of Agathocles fled to Seleucus, who at once invaded the territory of Lysimachus
1 CONC  in Asia. In 281 BC, Lysimachus crossed the Hellespont into Lydia, and at the decisive Battle of Corupedium was killed. After some days his body, watched by a faithful dog, was found on the field, and given up to his son Alexander
1 CONC , by whom it was interred at Lysimachia.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Arrian, Anabasis v. 13, vi. 28
1 CONT     * Justin xv. 3, 4, xvii. I
1 CONT     * Quintus Curtius V. 3, x. 30
1 CONT     * Diodorus Siculus xviii. 3
1 CONT     * Polybius v. 67
1 CONT     * Plutarch, Demetrius, 31. 52, Pyrrhus, 12
1 CONT     * Appian, Syriaca, 62
1 CONT     * Connop Thirlwall, History of Greece, vol. viii. (1847)
1 CONT     * J. P. Mahaffy, Story of Alexander’s Empire
1 CONT     * Droysen, Hellenismus (2nd ed., 1877)
1 CONT     * Adolf Holm, Griechische Geschichte, vol. iv. (1894)
1 CONT     * Benediktus Niese, Geschichte der griechischen und makedonischen Staaten, vols. i. and ii. (1893, 1899)
1 CONT     * Karl Julius Beloch, Griechische Geschichte vol. iii. (1904)
1 CONT     * Hunerwadel, Forschungen zur Gesch. des Könige Lysimachus (1900)
1 CONT     * Possenti, Il Re Lisimaco di Tracia (1901)
1 CONT     * Ghione, "Note sul regno di Lisimaco" (Atti d. real. Accad. di Torino, xxxix.)
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT {{succession box|title=Governor of Thrace|before=—|after=became king|years=323–306 BC
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Alexander IV King of Thrace
1 CONT 306–281 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy Keraunos
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Antigonus I Monophthalmus King of Asia Minor
1 CONT 301–281 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Seleucus I Nicator
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Demetrius I Poliorcetes King of Macedon
1 CONT with Pyrrhus of Epirus
1 CONT 288–281 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy Keraunos
0 @N6000000002187624280@ NOTE Berenice I of Egypt
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Berenice I, daughter of Lagus, was first the wife of Philip, an obscure Macedonian nobleman, with whom she gave birth to the future Magas of Cyrene. Upon Philip's death, she came to Egypt as a lady-in-waiting to Eurydice, bride of
1 CONC  Ptolemy I, Alexander's general and founder of the Ptolemaic dynasty. Berenice caught the eye of the king. Her son with Ptolemy I, Ptolemy II Philadelphos, was recognized as heir in preference to Eurydice's children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy gave her name to the new port he built on the Red Sea, Berenice. King Pyrrhus of Epirus also gave the name Berenicis to a new city. Her son Ptolemy II Philadelphos decreed divine honours to her on her death. (See Theocritu
1 CONC s, Idylls xv. and xvii.)
1 CONT
1 CONT This part of the entry was originally from the 1911 Encyclopedia Britannica.
1 CONT
1 CONT She exerted influnce on all state matters, and fought beside Ptolemy on the battlefield. After Ptolemy's death, her sons were killed in a war with her brother-in-law. She led against him to avenge her children, and personally kill
1 CONC ed him. She then repeatedly drove her chariot over his body. She is the mother of Arsinoe II.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT Salmonson, Jessica Amanda.(1991) The Encyclopedia of Amazons. Paragon House. Page 33. ISBN
0 @N6000000001353854616@ NOTE Appointed 290 BC, Queen of Egypt 2
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT Berenice I (fl. c. 317-c. 275 BC), queen of Egypt, third wife of Ptolemy I Soter, and mother of Arsinoe II and Ptolemy II Philadelphus.
1 CONT Berenice arrived in Egypt in the retinue of Eurydice, Ptolemy's second queen, whom he married as part of a political agreement with her father, Antipater of Macedonia. About 317 Ptolemy married Berenice. Probably because she was n
1 CONC ot of royal blood, a genealogy was fabricated tomake her a half sister of the king. In 308 Berenice gave birth to Ptolemy II Philadelphus, and in 290 Ptolemy made her queen of Egypt. In 285 Ptolemy II was made coregent and success
1 CONC or to his father, bypassingEurydice's children. Ptolemy II's second wife was his sister Arsinoe II, also the child of Berenice. [Encyclopædia Britannica CD '97, BERENICE I]
1 CONT ----------
1 CONT Berenice, daughter of Lagus, [was the] wife of an obscure Macedonian solider and subsequently of Ptolemy Soter, with whose bride, Eurydice,she came to Egypt as a lady in waiting. Her son, Ptolemy Philadelphus, was recognized as he
1 CONC ir over the heads of Eurydice's children. So great was her ability and her influence that Pyrrhus of Epirus gave the name Berenicis to a new city. Her son Philadelphus decreed divine honours to her on her death.
