0 @N6000000001354417930@ NOTE Mithradates I, also called ARSACES VI (fl. 2nd century BC), king of Parthia (reigned 171-138 BC); he succeeded his brother Phraates I.
1 CONT Before 160 Mithradates I seized Media from the Seleucid ruler Timarchus. Turning to the east, he won two provinces, Tapuria and Traxiana, from the Bactrian king Eucratides. Mithradates then captured the province of Elymais (ancien
1 CONC t Elam) and invaded Babylonia (142 or 141). The Seleucid king Demetrius II Nicator recaptured Babylon (141 or 140) but was defeated and held by Mithradates in honourable captivity. Generally, Mithradates was regarded as a mild rul
1 CONC er, and his epithet Philhellene ("Greek-loving") indicates that he tried to conciliate his Greek subjects.
