0 @N6000000003949970637@ NOTE Armenian: ?????? ?, Khosrov Ben), the son of Tiridates III, was a member of the Arshakuni Dynasty and the king of Armenia from 330 – 339 AD. He was a man of short stature, thus his name. He founded the city of Dvin which later bec
1 CONC ame the Armenian capital.
1 CONT During his reign, two generals, Vatche Mamikonian and Vahan Amatuni, distinguished themselves for their valor in battle, often coming to help the king. During these years, pro-Sassanid and anti-Mamikonian sentiment grew in Armenia
1 CONC  and so did anti-Roman sentiment. Pro-Sassanid groups gained popularity so much so that they were successful in assassinating Catholicos St. Aristaces I, son of Gregory the Illuminator.
1 CONT Shapur II, Sassanid king of the Persians, invaded Armenia twice and did gain some territory. Vatche Mamikonian was killed in those battles and was later named a saint by the Armenian Apostolic Church for his sacrifice.
0 @N6000000002187821910@ NOTE Bagrat I Bagratuni
1 CONT b. circa 290, #6396
1 CONT Pedigree 
1 CONT       Presiding Prince of the Bagratids.. Sources: 1. Toumanoff, C. 'The Orontids of Armenia' in 'Studies in Christian Caucasian History' (1963) pp.337-342. ; 2. Toumanoff, C. 'The Early Bagratids' in La Museon, (1949), pp.21-54. 
1 CONC Bagrat I Bagratuni was born circa 290. He was the son of Smbat I Bagratuni. Great prince of the aspetut'iwn in 353.1 He was selected by the unanimous consent of the bishops, the king and the ashxarhax, to be a delegate in a group 
1 CONC of nobles to accompany the newly appointed Catholicos, Nerses, to the capital city of Cappadocia, Caesarea in 353.2  
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT      Child of Bagrat I Bagratuni: 
1 CONT   Smbat II Bagratuni+   b. c 315, d. a 374 
1 CONT
1 CONT [S327] Robert Bedrosian (translator), editor, P'awstos Buzandac'i's The History of the Armenians (23 Washington Street, Long Branch, New Jersey 07740-5931: Sources of the Armenian Tradition, 1985), pg. 86. Hereinafter cited as HoA
1 CONC  : Buzandac'i's (5th C). 
1 CONT [S327] Robert Bedrosian (translator), HoA : Buzandac'i's (5th C), Ch. 1
0 @N6000000002187712812@ NOTE Pompeius Strabo
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Gnaeus Pompeius Strabo (died 87 BC), whose cognomen means "cross eyed", is often referred to in English as Pompey Strabo to distinguish him from Strabo the geographer. Strabo lived in the Roman Republic. Strabo was born and raised
1 CONC  into a noble family in Picenum (modern Marche & Abruzzo) a rural district in Northern Italy, off the Adriatic Coast. Strabo's mother was called Lucilia. Lucilia’s family originated from Suessa Aurunca (modern Sessa Aurunca) and s
1 CONC he was a sister of satire poet Gaius Lucilius. Lucilius was a friend of Roman general Scipio Aemilianus Africanus. Strabo’s paternal grandfather was Gnaeus Pompeius, while his father was Sextus Pompeius. His elder brother was Sext
1 CONC us Pompeius and his sister was Pompeia. His brother was the father of another Sextus Pompeius and Quintus Pompeius, the first of whom was the father of another Sextus Pompeius, Consul in 35 BC, in turn the father of still another 
1 CONC Sextus Pompeius.
1 CONT
1 CONT Strabo became the first of his branch of the gens Pompeia to achieve senatorial status in Rome, despite the anti-rural prejudice of the Roman Senate. After proving his military talent, Strabo climbed the cursus honorum and became 
1 CONC promagistrate in Sicily 93 BC and consul in the year 89 BC, in the midst of the Social War.
1 CONT
1 CONT Strabo commanded Roman forces against the Italian Allies in the northern part of Italy. His three Roman legions were instrumental in Rome's victory. After his consulship and the war, Strabo retired to Picenum with all of his veter
1 CONC an soldiers. He remained there until 87 BC, when he responded to dictator Lucius Cornelius Sulla's request for help against general Gaius Marius. Strabo had the habit of playing both ends against the middle in the intense politics
1 CONC  of the period. Sulla arranged to remove Strabo from command and replace him with a handpicked confederate. Strabo left camp on "personal business" while his soldiers killed the replacement. Later, when Strabo died of plague, a mo
1 CONC b dragged his body through the streets until a tribune interceded. His son took the legions back to Picenum once again.
1 CONT
1 CONT Strabo married and his wife is an unnamed Roman woman. He had a least two children: a son, Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus, also known as Pompey the Great or Pompey the triumvir who married Julia (the daughter of dictator Gaius Julius Caes
1 CONC ar) as his fourth wife and a daughter called Pompeia.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources:
1 CONT Microsoft Encarta Encyclopaedia 2002 
1 CONT http://www.ancientlibrary.com/smith-bio/1930.html 
1 CONT http://www.ancientlibrary.com/smith-bio/2808.html 
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Lucius Julius Caesar and Publius Rutilius Lupus Consul of the Roman Republic
1 CONT with Lucius Porcius Cato
1 CONT 89 BC Succeeded by
1 CONT Lucius Cornelius Sulla and Quintus Pompeius Rufus 
0 @N6000000002188289298@ NOTE Gnaeus Pompeius
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Gnaeus Pompeius (ca. 75 - April 12, 45 BC), also known as Pompey the Younger, also spelled Cneius, Gneius ...was a Roman politician and general from the late Republic (1st century BC).
1 CONT
1 CONT Gnaeus Pompeius was the oldest son of Pompey the Great (Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus) by his third wife, Mucia Tertia. Both he and his younger brother Sextus Pompeius grew up in the shadow of their father, one of Rome's best generals an
1 CONC d an originally non-conservative politician who drifted to the more traditional faction when Julius Caesar became a threat. When Caesar crossed the Rubicon in 49 BC, thus starting a civil war, Gnaeus followed his father in their e
1 CONC scape to the East, as did most of the conservative senators. Pompey's army lost the Battle of Pharsalus in 48 BC, and Pompey himself had to run for his life, only to be murdered in Egypt on September 29 of the same year.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the murder, Gnaeus and his brother Sextus joined the resistance against Caesar in the Africa Province. Together with Metellus Scipio, Cato the Younger and other senators, they prepared to oppose Caesar and his army to the en
1 CONC d. Caesar defeated Metellus Scipio and Cato, who subsequently committed suicide, at the Battle of Thapsus in 46 BC. Gnaeus escaped once again, this time to the Balearic Islands, where he joined Sextus. Together with Titus Labienus
1 CONC , former general in Caesar's army, the Pompey brothers crossed over to the Hispania (the Iberian Peninsula, comprising modern Spain and Portugal), where they raised yet another army.
1 CONT
1 CONT Caesar soon followed and, on March 17, 45 BC, the armies met in the battle of Munda. Both armies were large and led by able generals. The battle was closely fought, but eventually a cavalry charge by Caesar turned events to his si
1 CONC de. In the battle and the panicked escape that followed, Titus Labienus and an estimated 30,000 men of the Pompeian side died. Gnaeus and Sextus managed to escape once again. However, this time, supporters were difficult to find b
1 CONC ecause it was by now clear Caesar had won the civil war. Within a few weeks, Gnaeus Pompeius was caught and executed for treason. Sextus Pompeius was able to keep one step ahead of his enemies, and survived his older brother for a
1 CONC  number of years. He married a woman called Claudia Pulchra, who survived him and they had no children.
