0 @N6000000002187597061@ NOTE Saint Narses
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT (Redirected from Narses the Great)
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT For other historical figures with similar names, see Narses (disambiguation). 
1 CONT Saint Nerses I the Great was an Armenian Catholicos (or Patriarch) who lived in the fourth century. He was the father of another catholicos, St. Sahak I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Born of the royal Gregorid stock, he spent his youth in Caesarea where he married Sanducht, a Mamikonian princess. After the death of his wife, he was appointed sword-bearer to King Arsaces II of Armenia. A few years later, having
1 CONC  entered the ecclesiastical state, he was elected catholicos in 353.
1 CONT
1 CONT His patriarchate marks a new era in Armenian history. Till then the Church had been more or less identified with the royal family and the nobles; Nerses brought it into closer connection with the people. At the Council of Ashtisha
1 CONC t he promulgated numerous laws on marriage, fast days, and Divine worship. He built schools and hospitals, and sent monks throughout the land to preach the Gospel.
1 CONT
1 CONT Some of these reforms drew upon him the king's displeasure, and he was exiled, supposedly to Edessa. It was probably at some point during the later part of Arsaces's reign that Nerses went to Constantinople to ensure the Emperor's
1 CONC  support of Armenia against the Persians.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon the accession of pro-Arian King Papes (369) he returned to his see. Papes proved a dissolute and unworthy ruler and Nerses forbade him entrance to the church. Under the pretence of seeking a reconciliation, Papes invited Ners
1 CONC es to his table and reportedly poisoned him in 373.
0 @N6000000003949970411@ NOTE Nerses "the Great", Katholikos of Armenia was the ancestor of Sahakanoyš Souren-Pahlav; a member of the Gregorid family (confirmed by contemporary evidence of high quality), and the line can then be traced back without difficulty 
1 CONC to the Gregorid Armenian patriarch Nerses the Great.3 Nerses "the Great", Katholikos of Armenia was of, well founded, Arsacid descent through his mother and/or grandmother, and from there, virtually certain of a descent from king 
1 CONC Phriapites of Parthia in the early second century BC.3 He was a soldier of king Arshak who raised his steel sword in service to the king and considered himself unworthy of the honor of being chosen as patriarch.4 He was worthy of 
1 CONC the epithet by which he was called, "the marvelous," for he was a rightous and blessed man.5 He was a tall man, of pleasing size and captivating beauty, so much so that his equal in good looks could not be found in the world. Ever
1 CONC yone looking at him found him desirable, amazing and venerable, and he displayed enviable courage in military training.
0 @N6000000002187821872@ NOTE Isaac I Bagratuni1
1 CONT b. circa 340, d. after 387, #6392
1 CONT Pedigree 
1 CONT      Sources: 1. Toumanoff, C. 'The Orontids of Armenia' in 'Studies in Christian Caucasian History' (1963) pp.337-342. ; 2. Toumanoff, C. 'The Early Bagratids' in La Museon, (1949), pp.21-54. Presiding Prince of the Bagratids. Is
1 CONC aac I Bagratuni was born circa 340. The placement of Isaac II as son of Smbat II seems to fit historically and chronologically but has not been proven. He was the son of Smbat II Bagratuni. He died after 387.  
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT      Child of Isaac I Bagratuni: 
1 CONT   Smbat III Bagratuni+   b. c 370, d. a 421 
1 CONT
1 CONT [S197] Toby Dills, "A Descendant of Antiquity," gedcom file from e-mail address (e-mail address) to Robert Stewart, 5 Feb 1999. Hereinafter cited as "Descendant of Antiquity". 
0 @N6000000002187712679@ NOTE Appius Claudius Caecus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Appius Claudius Caecus ("Appius Claudius the Blind," c. 340 BC-273 BC) was a Roman politician from a wealthy patrician family.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a censor in 312 BC during the Second Samnite War. He sought support from the lower classes, allowing sons of freed slaves to serve in the Senate, and extending voting privileges to men in the rural tribes who did not own la
1 CONC nd. During the war he advocated the founding of Roman colonies throughout Latium and Campania to serve as fortifications against the Samnites and Etruscans.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his term as censor, he built the Appian Way (Latin: Via Appia), an important and famous road between Rome and Capua, as well as the first aqueduct in Rome, the Aqua Appia. He also published for the first time a list of lega
1 CONC l procedures and the legal calendar, knowledge of which, until that time, had been reserved for the pontifices, the priests. He was also concerned with literature and rhetoric, and instituted reforms in Latin orthography.
1 CONT
1 CONT He later served as consul twice, in 307 BC and 296 BC, and in 292 BC and 285 BC he was appointed dictator. In 280 BC, after he had gone blind (because of a curse, according to Livy), he gave a famous speech against Cineas, an envo
1 CONC y of Pyrrhus of Epirus, declaring that Rome would never surrender. This is the first recorded political speech in Latin, and is the source of the saying "every man is the architect of his own fortune".
1 CONT
1 CONT Appius Claudius Caecus is used in Cicero's Pro Caelio as a stern and disapproving ancestor to Clodia. Cicero speaks as Caecus in a scathing prosopopoeia, where he is incensed at her for associating with Caelius, a member of the mi
1 CONC ddle equestrian class instead of the upper patrician class. Caecus's achievments, such as the building of the Appian Way and the Aqua Appia, are mentioned as being defiled by Clodia's actions.
1 CONT
1 CONT His son was the consul Publius Claudius Pulcher, his grandson the consul Appius Claudius Caudex.
1 CONT
1 CONT See also: Claudius (gens)
0 @N6000000002187600040@ NOTE Lucius Julius Libo
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Lucius Julius Libo was a member of the influential Julii clan. This patrician family was always of the most distinguished blood, however they had long since fallen out of the inner Roman elite. The Julii were active in politics si
1 CONC nce the Punic Wars.
1 CONT
1 CONT Libo descended from the Julii who fled from Alba Longa to Rome. This city was destroyed by the Roman king Tullus Hostilius. His ancestors claim their descent from the goddess Venus and the first Roman king Romulus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Libo was consul in 267 BC. His son of the same name was father to Numerius Julius Caesar, born before 300 BC. Numerius was a grandfather to Sextus Julius Caesar I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Caesar is Latin for hairy. This surname was originally a nickname given to the family. Over time the nickname became a surname. The surname came about because the family's male members had hairy foreheads.
0 @N6000000003051250768@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Lucius Julius Libo", retrieved 12 Jul 08]
1 CONT Lucius Julius Libo was a member of the influential Julii clan. This patrician family was always of the most distinguished blood, however they had long since fallen out of the inner Roman elite. The Julii were active in politics si
1 CONC nce the Punic Wars.
1 CONT
1 CONT Libo descended from the Julii who fled from Alba Longa to Rome. This city was destroyed by the Roman king Tullus Hostilius. His ancestors claim their descent from the goddess Venus and the first Roman king Romulus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Libo was consul in 267 BC. His son of the same name was father to Numerius Julius Caesar, born before 300 BC. Numerius was a grandfather to Sextus Julius Caesar I.
