0 @N6000000002187718740@ NOTE Nicomedes II Epiphanes
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT This article contains information that has not been verified and thus might not be reliable. If you are familiar with the subject matter, please check the article for inaccuracies and modify as needed, citing sources.
1 CONT Nicomedes II, Epiphanes, was the king of Bithynia, from 149 to 91 BC. He was fourth in descent from Nicomedes I and was the son of Prusias II.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was so popular with the people that his father sent him to Rome to limit his influence. However in Rome, he also gained favor from the Roman Senate, forcing Prusias to send an emissary with secret orders to assassinate him. But
1 CONC  the emissary revealed the plot, and persuaded the prince to rebel against his father.
1 CONT
1 CONT Supported by Attalus II, king of Pergamum, he was completely successful, and ordered his father to be put to death at Nicomedia. During his long reign Nicomedes adhered steadily to the Roman alliance, and assisted them against Ari
1 CONC stonicus of Pergamum.
1 CONT
1 CONT He made himself for a time master of Paphlagonia, and in order to have a claim on Cappadocia married Laodice (the widow of Ariarathes VI), who had fled to him when Mithradates the Great endeavoured to annex the country.
1 CONT
1 CONT When her two sons died, Nicomedes brought forward an impostor as a claimant to the throne; but the plot was detected. The Romans refused to recognize the claim, and required Nicomedes to give up all pretensions to Cappadocia and t
1 CONC o abandon Paphlagonia.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Prusias II King of Bithynia
1 CONT  Succeeded by:
1 CONT Nicomedes III 
0 @N6000000002188198571@ NOTE Demetrius II of India
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Demetrius II was a Greco-Bactrian king, probably during the mid-2nd century BCE. His coins are few and often crude, and he seems to have been a relatively young man. His identity has been hard to ascertain. Some modern studies [1]
1 CONC  assume from numismatical clues and portrait likeness that Demetrius II was a relative of Eucratides the Great.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 The enigma of Demetrius of India
1 CONT     * 2 See also
1 CONT     * 3 References
1 CONT     * 4 Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The enigma of Demetrius of India
1 CONT
1 CONT However, the history records keep only one reference to a king Demetrius contemporary with Eucratides, and this reference is highly problematic and may even clash with the view mentioned above.
1 CONT
1 CONT Roman historian Justin names a "Demetrius, king of the Indians", (Justin, xli) who was an enemy of Eucratides the Great. Justin's Demetrius beleaguered the warlike Eucratides with an army of 60,000 men against the latters garrison
1 CONC  of 300, but still — according to the probably exaggerated account — eventually was defeated. This episode is referred to as occurring at the end of the reign of Eucratides, hence dating the death of Justin's Demetrius around 150 
1 CONC BC. Justin's Demetrius may have been a relative of the Indian king Apollodotus I or a fugitive prince of the Euthydemid dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, the king of Justin's quote is not easily reconciled with the numismatical evidence, and views are divided as for how to interpret the various coinage series bearing the name Demetrius.
1 CONT
1 CONT The best account is probably done by numismatican Bopearachchi (1991); he singles out three kings named Demetrius. Demetrius I reigned c. 200- 185 BCE, well before the rise of Eucratides, and Demetrius III was an Indian king who i
1 CONC s thought to have ruled much later, around 100 BC. There remains Demetrius II, who is said – rather tentatively – to have reigned around 170 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bopearachchi does however continue to identify Demetrius II with Justin’s Demetrius of India, notwithstanding the fact that Justin's quote suggests a later reign. Furthermore, Bopearachchi’s Demetrius II clearly reigned in Bactria
1 CONC  and not in India, as he strikes no coins whatsoever with Indian legends.
1 CONT
1 CONT Therefore, the identity of Justin's Demetrius remains uncertain. The following hypotheses may be considered:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * The account of Justin, who is a 2nd hand source, is confused. Either Demetrius II was not king of India but Bactria, or the account of the war is mixed up, or the king's name is wrong, Justin having confused the name of anot
1 CONC her Indo-Greek king with that of the legendary Demetrius I. The Indo-Greek king Apollodotus I would have suited well in with the description. His name is known to have been misunderstood in ancient sources [1], and may have been m
1 CONC istaken for Apollodorus of Artemita, who was one of Justin's sources, and therefore altered.
1 CONT     * Bopearachchi's Demetrius III could be placed earlier - this king's coins are few and rather peculiar - and Demetrius III was in fact Justin's Demetrius who ruled half a century earlier.
1 CONT
1 CONT Even if Justin's Demetrius of India existed, this does not exclude that Eucratides also had a son named Demetrius, which was a common dynastic name at the time. The prince may have been named after the Seleucid Demetrius I Soter.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Indo-Greek Kingdom
1 CONT     * Seleucid Empire
1 CONT     * Greco-Buddhism
1 CONT     * Indo-Scythians
1 CONT     * Indo-Parthian Kingdom
1 CONT     * Kushan Empire
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * "The Greeks in Bactria and India", W.W. Tarn, Cambridge University Press.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT     *
1 CONT          1. ^ "Demetrios II of Bactria and Hoards from Ai Khanoum" by L.M. Wilson (Oriental Numismatic Society newsletter nr 180)
1 CONT     * ? . Periplus of the Erythraean Sea, misnaming Apollodotus I
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Eucratides Indo-Greek Ruler
1 CONT (Bactria)
1 CONT (c.155-150 BCE) Succeeded by:
1 CONT Heliocles I?
0 @N6000000002188198545@ NOTE Menander I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Menander I (known as Milinda in Sanskrit and Pali) was one of the rulers of the Indo-Greek Kingdom in northern India and present-day Pakistan from 155 or 150 to 130 BC.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 A renowned Indo-Greek king
1 CONT     * 2 Menander and Buddhism
1 CONT           o 2.1 The Milinda Pañha
1 CONT           o 2.2 Other Indian accounts
1 CONT           o 2.3 "Menander the Just, Follower of the Dharma"
1 CONT           o 2.4 The question of Menander's change of title
1 CONT     * 3 Menander's death
1 CONT           o 3.1 Succession
1 CONT     * 4 Legacy
1 CONT           o 4.1 Buddhism
1 CONT           o 4.2 Representation of the Buddha
1 CONT           o 4.3 Geography
1 CONT     * 5 Notes
1 CONT     * 6 See also
1 CONT     * 7 References
1 CONT     * 8 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] A renowned Indo-Greek king
1 CONT
1 CONT His territories covered the eastern dominions of the divided Greek empire of Bactria (from the areas of the Panjshir and Kapisa) and extended to the modern Pakistani province of Punjab with diffuse tributaries to the south and eas
1 CONC t, probably as far as Mathura.
1 CONT
1 CONT His capital is supposed to have been Sagala, a very prosperous city in northern Punjab (modern Sialkot).
1 CONT
1 CONT He is one of the few Bactrian kings mentioned by Greek authors, among them Apollodorus of Artemita, quoted by Strabo, who claims that the Greeks from Bactria were even greater conquerors than Alexander the Great, and that Menander
1 CONC  was one of the two Bactrian kings, with Demetrius, who extended their power farthest into India:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "The Greeks who caused Bactria to revolt grew so powerful on account of the fertility of the country that they became masters, not only of Ariana, but also of India, as Apollodorus of Artemita says: and more tribes were subdue
1 CONC d by them than by Alexander-- by Menander in particular (at least if he actually crossed the Hypanis towards the east and advanced as far as the Imaüs), for some were subdued by him personally and others by Demetrius, the son of E
1 CONC uthydemus the king of the Bactrians; and they took possession, not only of Patalena, but also, on the rest of the coast, of what is called the kingdom of Saraostus and Sigerdis. In short, Apollodorus says that Bactriana is the orn
1 CONC ament of Ariana as a whole; and, more than that, they extended their empire even as far as the Seres and the Phryni." (Strabo 11.11.1 [1])
1 CONT
1 CONT Silver drachm of Menander I (155-130 BC).Obv: Greek legend, ??S???OS SO????S ????????? (BASILEOS SOTEROS MENANDROY) lit. "Of Saviour King Menander". Rev: Kharosthi legend: MAHARAJA TRATASA MENADRASA "Saviour King Menander". Athena
1 CONC  advancing right, with thunderbolt and shield. Taxila mint mark.
1 CONT Silver drachm of Menander I (155-130 BC).
1 CONT Obv: Greek legend, ??S???OS SO????S ????????? (BASILEOS SOTEROS MENANDROY) lit. "Of Saviour King Menander".
1 CONT Rev: Kharosthi legend: MAHARAJA TRATASA MENADRASA "Saviour King Menander". Athena advancing right, with thunderbolt and shield. Taxila mint mark.
1 CONT
1 CONT Strabo also suggests that these Greek conquests went as far as the capital Pataliputra in northeastern India (today Patna):
1 CONT
1 CONT     "Those who came after Alexander went to the Ganges and Pataliputra" (Strabo, 15.698).
1 CONT
1 CONT The Indian records also describe Greek attacks on Mathura, Panchala, Saketa, and Pataliputra. This is particularly the case of some mentions of the invasion by Patanjali around 150 BC, and of the Yuga Purana, which describes India
1 CONC n historical events in the form of a prophecy:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "After having conquered Saketa, the country of the Panchala and the Mathuras, the Yavanas (Greeks), wicked and valiant, will reach Kusumadhvaja. The thick mud-fortifications at Pataliputra being reached, all the provinces will
1 CONC  be in disorder, without doubt. Ultimately, a great battle will follow, with tree-like engines (siege engines)." (Gargi-Samhita, Yuga Purana chapter, No5).
1 CONT
1 CONT In the West, Menander seems to have repelled the invasion of the dynasty of Greco-Bactrian usurper Eucratides, and pushed them back as far as the Paropamisadae, thereby consolidating the rule of the Indo-Greek kings in the norther
1 CONC n part of the Indian Subcontinent.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Milinda Panha gives some glimpses of his military methods:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "Has it ever happened to you, O king, that rival kings rose up against you as enemies and opponents?
1 CONT     -Yes, certainly.
1 CONT     -Then you set to work, I suppose, to have moats dug, and ramparts thrown up, and watch towers erected, and strongholds built, and stores of food collected?
1 CONT     -Not at all. All that had been prepared beforehand.
1 CONT     -Or you had yourself trained in the management of war elephants, and in horsemanship, and in the use of the war chariot, and in archery and fencing?
1 CONT     -Not at all. I had learnt all that before.
1 CONT     -But why?
1 CONT     -With the object of warding off future danger."
1 CONT     (Milinda Panha, Book III, Chap 7)
1 CONT
1 CONT Silver drachm of middle-aged Menander.Obv: Greek legend, ??S???OS SO????S ????????? (BASILEOS SOTHROS MENANDROY) lit. "Of Saviour King Menander". Rev: Kharosthi legend: MAHARAJA TRATASA MENADRASA "Saviour King Menander". Athena ad
1 CONC vancing left, with thunderbolt and shield.
1 CONT Silver drachm of middle-aged Menander.
1 CONT Obv: Greek legend, ??S???OS SO????S ????????? (BASILEOS SOTHROS MENANDROY) lit. "Of Saviour King Menander".
1 CONT Rev: Kharosthi legend: MAHARAJA TRATASA MENADRASA "Saviour King Menander". Athena advancing left, with thunderbolt and shield.
1 CONT
1 CONT His reign was long and successful. Generous findings of coins testify to the prosperity and extension of his empire (with finds as far as Britain): the finds of his coins are the most numerous and the most widespread of all the In
1 CONC do-Greek kings. Precise dates of his reign, as well as his origin, remain elusive however. Guesses among historians have been that Menander was either a nephew or a former general of the Greco-Bactrian king Demetrius I, but the tw
1 CONC o kings are now thought to be separated by at least thirty years. Menander's predecessor in Punjab seems to have been the king Apollodotus I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Menander's empire survived him in a fragmented manner until the last Greek king Strato II disappeared around 10.
1 CONT
1 CONT Menander was the first Indo-Greek ruler to introduce the representation of Athena Alkidemos ("Athena, saviour of the people") on his coins, probably in reference to a similar statue of Athena Alkidemos in Pella, capital of Macedon
1 CONC . This type was subsequently used by most of the later Indo-Greek kings.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Menander and Buddhism
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The Milinda Pañha
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Milinda Panha
1 CONT
1 CONT According to tradition, Menander embraced the Buddhist faith, as described in the Milinda Pañha, a classical Pali Buddhist text on the discussions between Milinda and the Buddhist sage Nagasena. He is described as constantly accom
1 CONC panied by a guard of 500 Greek ("Yonaka") soldiers, and two of his counsellors are named Demetrius and Antiochus.
1 CONT A coin of Menander I with a Buddhist eight-spoked wheel. Obv: Greek legend, ??S???OS SO????S ????????? (BASILEOS SOTEROS MENANDROY) lit. "Of Saviour King Menander" with eight-spoked wheel. Rev: Kharosthi legend MAHARAJA TRATASA ME
1 CONC NADRASA "Saviour King Menander", with palm of victory.
1 CONT A coin of Menander I with a Buddhist eight-spoked wheel.
1 CONT Obv: Greek legend, ??S???OS SO????S ????????? (BASILEOS SOTEROS MENANDROY) lit. "Of Saviour King Menander" with eight-spoked wheel.
1 CONT Rev: Kharosthi legend MAHARAJA TRATASA MENADRASA "Saviour King Menander", with palm of victory.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the Milindanpanha, Menander is introduced as
1 CONT
1 CONT     "King of the city of Sâgala in India, Milinda by name, learned, eloquent, wise, and able; and a faithful observer, and that at the right time, of all the various acts of devotion and ceremony enjoined by his own sacred hymns c
1 CONC oncerning things past, present, and to come. Many were the arts and sciences he knew--holy tradition and secular law; the Sânkhya, Yoga, Nyâya, and Vaisheshika systems of philosophy; arithmetic; music; medicine; the four Vedas, th
1 CONC e Purânas, and the Itihâsas; astronomy, magic, causation, and magic spells; the art of war; poetry; conveyancing in a word, the whole nineteen. As a disputant he was hard to equal, harder still to overcome; the acknowledged superi
1 CONC or of all the founders of the various schools of thought. And as in wisdom so in strength of body, swiftness, and valour there was found none equal to Milinda in all India. He was rich too, mighty in wealth and prosperity, and the
1 CONC  number of his armed hosts knew no end." (The Questions of King Milinda, Translation by T. W. Rhys Davids, 1890).
1 CONT
1 CONT Buddhist tradition relates that, following his discussions with Nagasena, Menander adopted the Buddhist faith:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "May the venerable Nâgasena accept me as a supporter of the faith, as a true convert from to-day onwards as long as life shall last!" (The Questions of King Milinda, Translation by T. W. Rhys Davids, 1890).
1 CONT
1 CONT He then handed over his kingdom to his son and retired from the world:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "And afterwards, taking delight in the wisdom of the Elder, he handed over his kingdom to his son, and abandoning the household life for the houseless state, grew great in insight, and himself attained to Arahatship!" (The Que
1 CONC stions of King Milinda, Translation by T. W. Rhys Davids, 1890)
1 CONT
1 CONT There is however little besides this testament to indicate that Menander in fact abdicated his throne in favor of his son. Based on numismatic evidence, Sir Tarn believes that he in fact died, leaving his wife Agathocleia to rule 
1 CONC as a regent, until his son Strato could rule properly in his stead. Despite the success of his reign, it is clear that after his death, his "loosely hung" empire splintered into a variety of Indo-Greek successor kingdoms, of vario
1 CONC us size and stability.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Other Indian accounts
1 CONT Indian relief of probable Indo-Greek king, possibly Menander, with Buddhist triratana symbol on his sword. Bharhut, 2nd century BC. Indian Museum, Calcutta (drawing).
1 CONT Indian relief of probable Indo-Greek king, possibly Menander, with Buddhist triratana symbol on his sword. Bharhut, 2nd century BC. Indian Museum, Calcutta (drawing).
1 CONT
1 CONT     * A 2nd century BC relief from a Buddhist stupa in Bharhut, in eastern Madhya Pradesh (today at the Indian Museum in Calcutta), represents a foreign soldier with the curly hair of a Greek and the royal headband with flowing en
1 CONC ds of a Greek king, and may be a depiction of Menander. In his left hand, he hold a branch of ivy, symbol of Dionysos. Also parts of his dress, with rows of geometrical folds, are characteristically Hellenistic in style. On his sw
1 CONC ord appears the Buddhist symbol of the three jewels, or Triratana.
1 CONT
1 CONT     * A Buddhist reliquary found in Bajaur bears a dedicatory inscription referring to "the 14th day of the month of Karttika" of a certain year in the reign of "Maharaja Minadra" ("Great King Menander"):
1 CONT
1 CONT         "Minadrasa maharajasa Katiassa divasa 4 4 4 11 pra[na]-[sa]me[da]... (prati)[thavi]ta pranasame[da]... Sakamunisa"
1 CONT         "On the 14th day of Karttika, in the reign of Maharaja Minadra, (in the year ...), (the corporeal relic) of Sakyamuni, which is endowed with life... has been established" [2]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * According to an ancient Indian source, the Mahavamsa, Greek monks seem to have been active proselytizers of Buddhism during the time of Menander: the Yona (Greek) Mahadhammarakkhita (Sanskrit: Mahadharmaraksita) is said to h
1 CONC ave come from “Alasandra” (thought to be Alexandria of the Caucasus, the city founded by Alexander the Great, near today’s Kabul) with 30,000 monks for the foundation ceremony of the Maha Thupa ("Great stupa") at Anuradhapura in S
1 CONC ri Lanka, during the 2nd century BC:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "From Alasanda the city of the Yonas came the thera ("elder") Yona Mahadhammarakkhita with thirty thousand bhikkhus." (Mahavamsa, XXIX [3])
1 CONT
1 CONT These elements tend to indicate the importance of Buddhism within Greek communities in northwestern India, and the prominent role Greek Buddhist monks played in them, probably under the sponsorship of Menander.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] "Menander the Just, Follower of the Dharma"
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article Menander the Just
1 CONT
1 CONT Coin of Menander the Just. Obv: Menander wearing a diadem. Greek legend: ??S???OS ??????? ????????? (King Menander the Just). Rev: Winged figure bearing diadem and palm, with halo, probably Nike. The Kharoshthi legend reads MAHARA
1 CONC JASA DHARMIKASA MENADRASA (Great King, Menander, follower of the Dharma, Menander).
1 CONT Coin of Menander the Just.
1 CONT Obv: Menander wearing a diadem. Greek legend: ??S???OS ??????? ????????? (King Menander the Just).
1 CONT Rev: Winged figure bearing diadem and palm, with halo, probably Nike. The Kharoshthi legend reads MAHARAJASA DHARMIKASA MENADRASA (Great King, Menander, follower of the Dharma, Menander).
1 CONT
1 CONT Most historians and numismatists consider that Menander changed his royal title and coins types at a later stage of his life [4], and this has been associated to his supposed conversion to Buddhism as described in the Milinda Panh
1 CONC a.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although his first coins bear the mention "Of King Menander the Saviour" (Greek: ??S???OS SO????S ????????? (BASILEOS SOTEROS MENANDROY)/ Kharosthi: MAHARAJA TRATASA MENADRASA), later coins adopt the new title "Of King Menander th
1 CONC e Just" (Greek: ??S???OS ??????? ?????????), to which, for the first time in any coinage corresponds the Pali title "Great King, follower of the Dharma" on the reverse (Kharoshthi: MAHARAJASA DHARMIKASA MENADRASA), a possible refe
1 CONC rence to his conversion to Buddhism.
1 CONT
1 CONT The profiles of the king between the former and later coins are quite consistent. Based on the age difference between the rendering of the profiles on the coins, this change would have happened around the age of 50. None of the pr
1 CONC ofiles on the coins of Menander "the saviour" would indicate an age beyond 50, while all the known coins of Menander "the Just" would tend to suggest a man beyond that age.
1 CONT Coin of Menander the Just (Indian standard). Obv: Athena standing, with spear and palm-branch, shield at her feet, making a benediction gesture with the right hand, similar to the Buddhist vitarka mudra. The Greek legend reads ??S
1 CONC ???OS ??????? ????????? (Of King Menander the Just). Rev: Buddhist lion. Kharoshti legend reads MAHARAJASA DHARMIKASA MENADRASA (Great King, follower of the Dharma, Menander).
1 CONT Coin of Menander the Just (Indian standard).
1 CONT Obv: Athena standing, with spear and palm-branch, shield at her feet, making a benediction gesture with the right hand, similar to the Buddhist vitarka mudra. The Greek legend reads ??S???OS ??????? ????????? (Of King Menander the
1 CONC  Just).
1 CONT Rev: Buddhist lion. Kharoshti legend reads MAHARAJASA DHARMIKASA MENADRASA (Great King, follower of the Dharma, Menander).
1 CONT
1 CONT The coins of Menander the Just are filled with Buddhist symbolism, and present interesting evolutions of his previous coin types:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * When the goddess Athena is represented, she is not the warlike "Athena Alkidemos" of the earlier coins, but instead she has dropped her shield in a peaceful attitude, holds a palm of victory, and makes a benediction gesture 
1 CONC identical to the Buddhist vitarka mudra.
1 CONT     * The goddess Nike, holding a palm of victory, is now crowned by a religious halo.
1 CONT     * On some of his coins, Menander represents himself from head to toe, a first for an Indo-Greek king. He has abandoned his spear-throwing warlike stance, and instead makes the vitarka mudra.
1 CONT     * The seated figure of Zeus is represented with a Buddhist wheel at his feet, echoing the only other known occurrence of the Buddhist wheel in Indo-Greek coinage, on an earlier coin type of Menander.
1 CONT     * Finally, the seated Buddhist lion (as on the Pillars of Ashoka and numerous Buddhist representations) is depicted on many of these coins, a unique phenomenon in Indo-Greek coinage (although the standing lion later appears ag
1 CONC ain with Artemidoros).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The question of Menander's change of title
1 CONT
1 CONT Problematically, the two coin categories of Menander ("Of Menander the Saviour" and "Of Menander the Just") have no mint marks in common and the types are quite different, leading to suggestions (Bopearachchi) that "Menander the J
1 CONC ust" may be a later, different, king, relabeled Menander II for convenience, and possibly the grandson of "Menander the Saviour", relabeled "Menander I".
1 CONT
1 CONT Curiously however, almost none of the coins of Agathokleia and Strato I, unanimously acknowledged to be "Menander the Saviour"'s successors, have any common mint marks with him either [5]. On the contrary the western king Zoilos I
1 CONC  appropriated most of Menander I's mint marks. An explanation would be that following Menander's death, his widow Agathokleia managed to flee to the East with her child (the future Strato I) and establish a new realm, setting up s
1 CONC everal new mints in the process (Bopearachchi), a rather extraordinary accomplishment for someone in her situation.
1 CONT Coin of Menander the Just. Obv: King Menander in armour, standing, armed with spear and sword. Forming a benediction with the right hand, similar to Buddhist vitarka mudra. Greek legend: ??S???OS ??????? ????????? (Of King Menande
1 CONC r the Just). Rev: Buddhist lion. Kharoshti legend reads MAHARAJASA DHARMIKASA MENADRASA (Menander, King of the Dharma).
1 CONT Coin of Menander the Just.
1 CONT Obv: King Menander in armour, standing, armed with spear and sword. Forming a benediction with the right hand, similar to Buddhist vitarka mudra. Greek legend: ??S???OS ??????? ????????? (Of King Menander the Just).
1 CONT Rev: Buddhist lion. Kharoshti legend reads MAHARAJASA DHARMIKASA MENADRASA (Menander, King of the Dharma).
1 CONT
1 CONT Alternatively Menander I himself may have relocated to the Punjab, where he became "Menander the Just", "follower of the Dharma", until he transmitted his new realm to Agathokleia and Strato. All the coins of Agathokleia as Queen-
1 CONC Mother, where, middle-aged, she is alone on her coins with the bow and arrow quiver on the reverse, and arguably correspond to some of her first coin types, have mint marks identical to those of "Menander (II) the Just".[6] Simila
1 CONC rly, the youngest coins type of Strato I also have mint marks identical to those of "Menander (II) the Just".[7]. All these coins also share many similarities in the types and titles, at least in the first part of their rule: the 
1 CONC "Dharmikasa" titles, the king and Athena forming the Vitarka mudra.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to this scenario, Menander I had to abandon most of his western territories (and therefore most, or all, of his mints) at the end of his reign, possibly as the Greco-Bactrians, led by Zoilos I, invaded northwestern India
1 CONC  as they fled from the Yuezhi following the destruction of Ai-Khanoum around 140 BC. Menander relocated in a territory centered on eastern Punjab, probably with Sagala (modern Sialkot) as his capital as described in the Milinda Pa
1 CONC nha, possibly extending from Taxila in the West (as suggested by some of his mint marks [8]) to Mathura in the east (as suggested by coin hoards [9]). Menander would have had to establish new mints in these territories. He would a
1 CONC lso have persisted in his adoption of Buddhism (one of Menander I's coins already displays the Dharma wheel) and changed his title to "Menander the Just", "follower of the dharma". This relocation beyond the Indus proved long-last
1 CONC ing, as eastern Indo-Greeks were to continue ruling from Sagala until around 10.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Milinda Panha would tend to support this view, since it describes Menander as being somewhat cornered by numerous enemies into a circumscribed territory:
1 CONT
1 CONT     After their long discussion "Nagasaka asked himself "though king Milinda is pleased, he gives no signs of being pleased". Menander says in reply: "As a lion, the king of beasts, when put in a cage, though it were of gold, is s
1 CONC till facing outside, even so do I live as master in the house but remain facing outside. But if I were to go forth from home into homelessness I would not live long, so many are my enemies" (Milinda Panha, Book III, Chapter 7, quo
1 CONC ted in Boppearachchi [10]
1 CONT
1 CONT After "Menander the Just"'s death, Agathokleia and Strato I would then naturally have inherited several of his mints, as apparently they adopted the "Menander making a vitarka mudra gesture" design and the titles "Saviour" and "Ju
1 CONC st" ("follower of the Dharma") during the first few years of their coinage, before Strato reverted to the embattled Athena Alkidemos design and a more neutral "Saviour Epiphanes" title.
1 CONT
1 CONT The extent of the coinage of "Menander the Just" is also rather small (around 10 types are known), in effect very much smaller than the coinage of "Menander the Saviour" (more than 200 types, and thousands of coins recovered to th
1 CONC is day), suggesting a shorter rule and a reduced economic base.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Menander's death
1 CONT
1 CONT Plutarch (Praec. reip. ger. 28, 6) reports that Menander died in camp while on campaign, thereby differing with the version of the Milindapanha. Plutarch gives Menander as an example of benevolent rule, contrasting him with dislik
1 CONC ed tyrants such as Dionysus, and goes on explaining that his subject towns disputed about the honour of his burial, ultimately sharing his ashes among them and placing them in "monuments" (possibly stupas), in a manner reminiscent
1 CONC  of the funerals of the Buddha [11].
1 CONT
1 CONT     "But when one Menander, who had reigned graciously over the Bactrians, died afterwards in the camp, the cities indeed by common consent celebrated his funerals; but coming to a contest about his relics, they were difficultly a
1 CONC t last brought to this agreement, that his ashes being distributed, everyone should carry away an equal share, and they should all erect monuments to him." (Plutarch, "Political Precepts" Praec. reip. ger. 28, 6 [12])
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Succession
1 CONT
1 CONT Most historians and numismatist agree that Menander was succeeded by his Queen Agathokleia, who acted as regent to their son Strato I until he became an adult and took over the crown. In Agathokleia's earliest coins, where she app
1 CONC ears alone, with a seated Herakles, her Pali title appears as a combination of the two successive titles held by Menander, "Saviour" and "Follower of the Dharma":
1 CONT
1 CONT     Obverse: Agathokleia in war attire, in profile. Greek legend ??S???SS?S T???????? ?G?T??????S (BASILISSES THEOTROPOU AGATHOKLEIAS) ("Of Queen Agathokleia, Godlike")
1 CONT     Reverse: Pali "Maharajasa tratarasa dharmikasa Agathukriae" ("Great Queen, saviour and follower of the Dharma, Agathokleia")
1 CONT
1 CONT Drachm of Agathokleia at the beginning of her regency. Obv: Diademed bust of Agathokleia, with drapery and necklace. Greek legend ??S???SS?S T???????? ?G?T??????S (BASILISSES THEOTROPOU AGATHOKLEIAS) "Of Godlike Queen Agathokleia"
1 CONC . Rev: King in military outfit, diademed, holding spear, bow, arrow and shield in his left hand, and extending his right hand in a benediction gesture. Kharoshthi legend "Maharajasa tratarasa dhramikasa Stratasa", "Great King, sav
1 CONC iour and follower of the dharma, Strato".
1 CONT Drachm of Agathokleia at the beginning of her regency.
1 CONT Obv: Diademed bust of Agathokleia, with drapery and necklace. Greek legend ??S???SS?S T???????? ?G?T??????S (BASILISSES THEOTROPOU AGATHOKLEIAS) "Of Godlike Queen Agathokleia".
1 CONT Rev: King in military outfit, diademed, holding spear, bow, arrow and shield in his left hand, and extending his right hand in a benediction gesture. Kharoshthi legend "Maharajasa tratarasa dhramikasa Stratasa", "Great King, savio
1 CONC ur and follower of the dharma, Strato".
1 CONT
1 CONT The next coins, mentioning Strato for the first time, as he was still a child, also show Agathokliea on the obverse, and adopt on the reverse Menander II's depiction of a king in armour making the vitarka mudra. In these early coi
1 CONC ns also the title adopted for the future king Strato on the obverse is "Maharajasa tratarasa dhramikasa Stratasa" ("King Strato, saviour and follower of the dharma"), apparently the combination of both titles which had been held b
1 CONC y Menander:
1 CONT
1 CONT     Obverse: Profile of Agathokleia in peace attire, as regent. Greek legend BASILISSIS AGATOKLEIAS ("Queen Agathokleia")
1 CONT     Reverse: King in armour, forming the Vitarka Mudra. Pali legend "Maharajasa tratarasa dharmikasa Stratasa" ("Great king, saviour and followers of the Dharma, Strato")
1 CONT
1 CONT The first coins of Strato only, where he looks like an adolescent, show Athena in a peaceful attitude, holding Nike over her extended arm, in a parallel to the peaceful Athena of Menander II. Here again his Pali title is "Maharaja
1 CONC sa tratarasa Dhramikasa Stratasa" ("Great saviour king Strato, follower of the Dharma"). On these coins, his mint-marks are the same as mint-marks of Menander II [13].
1 CONT
1 CONT After a few years, Strato I and Agathokleia again reverted to the warlike Athena design on their coins, probably as a symbol of their conflicts with the Western Indo-Greek kings. Soon, Strato also abandoned the title "Follower of 
1 CONC the Dharma":
1 CONT
1 CONT     Obverse: ??S???OS ???F????S SO????S S????O??S (BASILEOS EPIPHANOUS SOTEROS STRATONOS) "Of Saviour king Strato, manifest"
1 CONT     Reverse: Pali "Maharajasa pracachasa tratarasa Stratasa" "Great king, manisfest and saviour, Strato"
1 CONT
1 CONT He held this last title until the end of his reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT Agathokleia did not manage to keep the empire of her husband intact. The western parts became independent under king Zoilos I. Some of her subjects may have been reluctant to accept an infant king with a queen regent. The later ki
1 CONC ng Heliokles II also over-struck some of Agathokleia's coins.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Legacy
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Buddhism
1 CONT
1 CONT Main article: Greco-Buddhism
1 CONT Vitarka Mudra gestures on Indo-Greek coinage. Top: Divinities Tyche and Zeus. Bottom: Depiction of Indo-Greek kings Nicias and Menander II.
1 CONT Vitarka Mudra gestures on Indo-Greek coinage. Top: Divinities Tyche and Zeus. Bottom: Depiction of Indo-Greek kings Nicias and Menander II.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the reign of Menander I, his son Strato I and several subsequent Indo-Greek rulers, such as Amyntas, Nicias, Peukolaos, Hermaeus, and Hippostratos, depicted themselves or their Greek deities forming with the right hand a sym
1 CONC bolic gesture identical to the Buddhist vitarka mudra (thumb and index joined together, with other fingers extended), which in Buddhism signifies the transmission of the Buddha's teaching. At the same time, right after the death o
1 CONC f Menander, several Indo-Greek rulers also started to adopt on their coins the Pali title of "Dharmikasa", meaning "follower of the Dharma" (the title of the great Indian Buddhist king Ashoka was Dharmaraja "King of the Dharma"). 
1 CONC This usage was adopted by Strato I, Zoilos I, Heliokles II, Theophilos, Peukolaos and Archebios.
1 CONT
1 CONT Altogether, the conversion of Menander to Buddhism suggested by the Milinda Panha seems to have triggered the use of Buddhist symbolism in one form or another on the coinage of close to half of the kings who succeeded him. Especia
1 CONC lly, all the kings after Menander who are recorded to have ruled in Gandhara (apart from the little known Demetrius III) display Buddhist symbolism in one form or another.
1 CONT
1 CONT Both because of his conversion and because of his unequaled territorial expansion, Menander may have contributed to the expansion of Buddhism in Central Asia. Although the spread of Buddhism to Central Asia and Northern Asia is us
1 CONC ually associated with the Kushans, a century or two later, there is a possibility that it may have been introduced in those areas from Gandhara "even earlier, during the time of Demetrius and Menander" (Puri, "Buddhism in Central 
1 CONC Asia").
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Representation of the Buddha
1 CONT
1 CONT Main article: Greco-Buddhist art
1 CONT One of the first known representations of the Buddha, Gandhara.
1 CONT One of the first known representations of the Buddha, Gandhara.
1 CONT
1 CONT The anthropomorphic representation of the Buddha is absent from Indo-Greek coinage, suggesting that the Indo-Greek kings may have respected the Indian an-iconic rule for Buddhist depictions, limiting themselves to Buddhist symboli
1 CONC sm only. Consistently with this perspective, the actual depiction of the Buddha would be a later phenomenon, usually dated to the 1st century, emerging from the sponsorship of the syncretic Kushan Empire and executed by Greek, and
1 CONC , later, Indian and possibly Roman artists. Datation of Greco-Buddhist statues is generally uncertain, but they are at least firmly established from the 1st century.
1 CONT
1 CONT Another possibility is that the Indo-Greeks may not have considered the Buddha strictly as a God, but rather as an essentially human sage or philosopher, in line with the traditional Nikaya Buddhist doctrine. Just as philosophers 
1 CONC were routinely represented in statues (but certainly not on coins) in Antiquity, the Indo-Greek may have initiated anthropomorphic representations of the Buddha in statuary only, possibly as soon as the 2nd-1st century BC, as advo
1 CONC cated by Foucher and suggested by Chinese murals depicting Emperor Wu of Han worshipping Buddha statues brought from Central Asia in 120 BC (See picture) ). An Indo-Chinese tradition also explains that Nagasena, also known as Mena
1 CONC nder's Buddhist teacher, created in 43 BC in the city of Pataliputra a statue of the Buddha, the Emerald Buddha, which was later brought to Thailand.
1 CONT
1 CONT Stylistically, Indo-Greek coins generally display a very high level of Hellenistic artistic realism, which declined drastically around 50 BC with the invasions of the Indo-Scythians, Yuezhi and Indo-Parthians. The first known stat
1 CONC ues of the Buddha are also very realistic and Hellenistic in style and are more consistent with the pre-50 BC artistic level seen on coins.
1 CONT Detail of Asia in the Ptolemy world map. The "Menander Mons" are in the center of the map, at the east of the Indian subcontinent, right above the Malaysian Peninsula.
1 CONT Detail of Asia in the Ptolemy world map. The "Menander Mons" are in the center of the map, at the east of the Indian subcontinent, right above the Malaysian Peninsula.
1 CONT
1 CONT This would tend to suggest that the first statues were created between 130 BC (death of Menander) and 50 BC, precisely at the time when Buddhist symbolism appeared on Indo-Greek coinage. From that time, Menander and his successors
1 CONC  may have been the key propagators of Buddhist ideas and representations: "the spread of Gandhari Buddhism may have been stimulated by Menander's royal patronage, as may have the development and spread of Gandharan sculpture, whic
1 CONC h seems to have accompanied it" (Mc Evilly, "The shape of ancient thought", p378)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Geography
1 CONT
1 CONT In Classical Antiquity, from at least the 1st century, the "Menander Mons", or "Mountains of Menander", came to designate the mountain chain at the extreme east of the Indian subcontinent, today's Naga Hills and Arakan, as indicat
1 CONC ed in the Ptolemy world map of the 1st century geographer Ptolemy.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Demetrius II of India Indo-Greek ruler
1 CONT (Paropamisadae, Arachosia, Gandhara, Punjab)
1 CONT 155/150-130 BC Succeeded by
1 CONT (In Paropamisadae, Arachosia:)
1 CONT Zoilos I
1 CONT
1 CONT (In Gandhara, Punjab:)
