0 @N6000000002188289013@ NOTE Faustus Cornelius Sulla (senator)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Faustus Cornelius Sulla (78 BC-47 BC) was a Roman senator. Faustus was eldest surviving son of the Dictator of Rome Lucius Cornelius Sulla, born in Arrentium (another source states he was born in Greece where his mother had fled b
1 CONC etween 86 and 81 BC). He was known simply as Faustus, the only Roman to be called that since Faustus Valerius in the time of Numa Pompilius.
1 CONT
1 CONT Faustus Cornelius Sulla was the head of the branch of the Patrician family Cornelius cognimated Sulla, a family consisting only of himself, and his cousin Publius Cornelius Sulla, adherent to Faustus' nephew by marriage Julius Cae
1 CONC sar. As a member of one of the most ancient patrician families, the Cornelii, and the son of the great dictator, promising things were prophesied for the young Sulla, doubly so as, after his father's death, he was brought up by hi
1 CONC s guardian, his father's friend Lucullus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Faustus married Pompeia Magna, daughter of the notable Pompey. They had three children. His career as an advocate was cut short, however, by the civil war between Pompey and Caesar. He, as Lucullus' ward and Pompey's son-in-law, s
1 CONC ided with the former and was killed in a minor skirmish with Caesarian troops in 47 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children
1 CONT by Pompeia Magna 
1 CONT Faustus (II) Cornelius Sulla 
1 CONT Lucius Cornelius Sulla Felix Ahenobarbus 
1 CONT Cornelia Sulla married Lucius Scribonius Libo 
1 CONT By a Peasant woman 
1 CONT Gnaeus Ulpius 
0 @N6000000002188289060@ NOTE Lucius Cornelius Cinna (suffect consul 32 BC)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Lucius Cornelius Cinna (flourished 1st century BC) was the son of an elder Lucius Cornelius Cinna, who was a supporter of politician Gaius Marius and his mother is an unnamed Roman woman. His sister was Cornelia Cinna minor, was t
1 CONC he first wife of dictator Gaius Julius Caesar and was the maternal uncle of their daughter Julia Caesaris. Cinna was a member of the Cornelius (gens), who were of patrician status.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cinna in 78 BC, allied himself with Marcus Aemilius Lepidus in overthrowing the Roman constitution of dictator Lucius Cornelius Sulla. After the defeat and death of Lepidus in Sardinia, Cinna accompanied Marcus Perperna to join ge
1 CONC neral Quintus Sertorius in Spain. Caesar used Cinna, against various Roman Senators in the Roman Senate and was able to recall Cinna from exile back to Rome.
1 CONT
1 CONT Due to his father’s association with Marius and Sulla’s constitutions reforms, he was unable to promote his career, until Caesar’s rise. When Caesar became dictator, Cinna promoted to the praetorship. Although Cinna, strongly disa
1 CONC pproved of Caesar’s way of governing, he wouldn’t associate himself with the senators who wanted Caesar killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Caesar was assassinated in March 44 BC, the senators were in such rage of Cinna, they accidentally murdered tribune of the plebs Helvius Cinna, thinking it was he. When the murder of the tribune took place, Cinna was walking
1 CONC  in Caesar’s funeral procession. Cinna during the political chaos didn’t promote his career, even not claiming a Roman province, for him to govern. Cicero praises him for this act. Cinna in 32 BC, served as a suffect consul.
1 CONT
1 CONT After 47 BC, Cinna married Pompeia Magna, the daughter of triumvir Pompey and from his third wife Mucia Tertia. Cinna married Pompeia has her second husband. Pompeia married Cinna as his first wife. Pompeia had become a widow, who
1 CONC  her husband Faustus Cornelius Sulla had died in battle. Cinna became a stepfather to Pompeia’s children from her first marriage. Pompeia bore Cinna two children who were: a son Gnaeus Cornelius Cinna Magnus and a daughter Corneli
1 CONC a Pompeia Magna. His wife died before 35 BC and beyond this no more is known on Cinna.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Microsoft Encarta Encyclopaedia 2002 
1 CONT http://www.ancientlibrary.com/smith-bio/0763.html 
1 CONT http://www.ancientlibrary.com/smith-bio/0764.html 
1 CONT http://www.ancientlibrary.com/smith-bio/2806.html 
0 @N6000000002188289094@ NOTE Gnaeus Cornelius Cinna Magnus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Gnaeus Cornelius Cinna Magnus (born after 47 BC and before 35 BC-?) was the son of suffect consul Lucius Cornelius Cinna and Pompeia Magna. His sister was Cornelia Pompeia Magna.
1 CONT
1 CONT His maternal grandparents were Roman triumvir Pompey and Mucia Tertia, while paternal grandparents were consul Lucius Cornelius Cinna and an unnamed Roman woman. Cinna is the only grandson of Pompey, that has the name ‘Magnus’ and
1 CONC  is the only grandson that has partly his name.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cinna became a supporter to Roman triumvir Mark Antony. He was promoted to a priesthood. In 4, Cinna and Aemilia Lepida, the granddaughter of triumvir Marcus Aemilius Lepidus were involved in a conspiracy against the Roman Emperor
1 CONC  Augustus. Cinna and Lepida were pardoned by the emperor. Cinna served as a consul in 5.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cinna is the main character of many Latin plays and of a tragedy called Cinna by the famous 17th century French playwright Corneille.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources:
1 CONT http://www.ancientlibrary.com/smith-bio/0763.html 
1 CONT http://www.ancientlibrary.com/smith-bio/0764.html 
1 CONT http://www.ancientlibrary.com/smith-bio/2806.html 
0 @N6000000002188198449@ NOTE Strato I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Strato I was an Indo-Greek king who ruled between c. 125-110 BCE in Northern India. He was the son of the great king Menander I and his queen Agathokleia, who acted as regent during his early years. The western parts of the Indo-G
1 CONC reek empire, including Paropamisadae and Arachosia, gained independence after the death of Menander I.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Territorial losses
1 CONT     * 2 Notes
1 CONT     * 3 See also
1 CONT     * 4 References
1 CONT     * 5 See also
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Territorial losses
1 CONT
1 CONT Strato's territory extended from the mid-Punjab at the Jhelum River in the West to Mathura in the East, retaining the capital of his father in Sagala (modern Sialkot) in the northern Punjab, or possibly to the city of Bucephala (P
1 CONC lutarch, p. 48 n. 5). The area of Gandhara, west of river Jhelum, also belonged to the kingdom but seems to have been lost during the latter part of Strato's reign, perhaps to the Western king Antialcidas. According to archaeologi
1 CONC cal evidence, Strato I was apparently the last Greek king to hold Mathura, which seems to have been lost to Indian kingdoms around 100 BCE.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the life of Strato I, various types of coins were minted, some of them bearing the mention "Dharmika" ("Of the "Dharma") [1].
1 CONT
1 CONT Strato I was also the only Indo-Greek king to appear bearded, probably to indicate that he was no longer an infant.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Agathokleia Indo-Greek Ruler
1 CONT (Gandhara, Punjab)
1 CONT (125-110 BCE) Succeeded by:
1 CONT Heliokles II
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ?  Legends on the coins of Agathokliea and Strato:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Period 1: Coins where Agathokleia is alone on the obverse, and has a seated Herakles on the reverse:
1 CONT
1 CONT Obverse: Greek BASILISSIS TEOTROPOU AGATOKLEIAS "Queen Agathokleia, Godlike" Reverse: Pali "Maharajasa tratarasa dharmikasa Agathukriae" "Queen Agathokleia, saviour and follower of the dharma"
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Period 2: Coins where only Agathokleia is shown, as regent, with king on the reverse forming the Vitarka Mudra:
1 CONT
1 CONT Obverse: Greek BASILISSIS AGATOKLEIAS ("Queen Agathokleia") Reverse: Pali "Maharajasa tratarasa dharmikasa Stratasa" ("The Great king Strato, saviour and followers of the Dharma")
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Period 3: Coins with Strato as an adolescent, accompanied by Athena peacefully holding Nike
1 CONT
1 CONT Obverse: Greek BASILEOS SOTIROS KAI DIKAIOU STRATONOS "Saviour king Strato, the Just" Reverse: Pali "Maharajasa tratarasa Dhramikasa Stratasa "Great saviour king Strato, follower of the Dharma"
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Period 4: Coins where Strato as an adolescent appears jointly with Agatokleia
1 CONT
1 CONT Obverse: Greek BASILEOS SOTIROS STRATONOS KAI AGATHOKLEIA "Saviour king Strato, and Agathokleia" Reverse: Pali "Maharajasa tratarasa Stratasa/ Agathukriae" "Great saviour king Strato, and Agathokleia"
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Period 5: King Strato alone, with Athena Alkidemos
1 CONT
1 CONT Obverse: Greek BASILEOS SOTIROS STRATONOS "Saviour king Strato" Reverse: Pali "Maharajasa tratarasa Stratasa" "Great saviour king Strato"
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Period 6: King Strato alone, with Athena Alkidemos
1 CONT
1 CONT Obverse: Greek BASILEOS SOTIROS STRATONOS "Saviour king Strato" Reverse: Pali "Maharajasa tratarasa dhramikasa Stratasa" "Great saviour king Strato, follower of the dharma"
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Period 7: King Strato alone, with Athena Alkidemos
1 CONT
1 CONT Obverse: Greek BASILEOS SOTIROS DIKAIOU STRATONOS "Saviour and just king Strato" Reverse: Pali "Maharajasa tratarasa dhramikasa Stratasa" "Great saviour king Strato, follower of the dharma"
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Period 8: King Strato alone, with Athena Alkidemos
1 CONT
1 CONT Obverse: Greek BASILEOS EPIPHANOUS SOTIROS STRATONOS "Saviour and divine king Strato" Reverse: Pali "Maharajasa pracachasa tratarasa Stratasa" "Great saviour king Strato, epiphane"
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Indo-Greek Kingdom
1 CONT     * Greco-Buddhism
1 CONT     * Indo-Scythians
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * "The Greeks in Bactria and India" W.W. Tarn, Cambridge University Press
0 @N6000000002187600784@ NOTE Darius II of Persia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Darius II, originally called Ochus and often surnamed Nothus (from Greek ?????, meaning 'bastard'), was emperor of Persia from 423 BC to 404 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Artaxerxes I, who died shortly after December 24, 424 BC, was followed by his son Xerxes II. After a month and a half Xerxes was murdered by his brother Secydianus or Sogdianus (the form of the name is uncertain). His illegitimate
1 CONC  brother, Ochus, satrap of Hyrcania, rebelled against Sogdianus, and after a short fight killed him, and suppressed by treachery the attempt of his own brother Arsites to imitate his example. Ochus adopted the name Darius (in the 
1 CONC chronicles he is called Nothos, meaning "the bastard"). Neither Xerxes II nor Secydianus occurs in the dates of the numerous Babylonian tablets from Nippur; here the reign of Darius II follows immediately after that of Artaxerxes 
1 CONC I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Of Darius's reign we know very little (a rebellion of the Medes in 409 BC is mentioned in Xenophon), except that he was quite dependent on his wife Parysatis. In the excerpts from Ctesias some harem intrigues are recorded, in whic
1 CONC h he played a disreputable part. As long as the power of Athens remained intact he did not meddle in Greek affairs; even the support which the Athenians in 413 BC gave to the rebel Amorges in Caria would not have roused him, had n
1 CONC ot the Athenian power been broken in the same year before Syracuse. He gave orders to his satraps in Asia Minor, Tissaphernes and Pharnabazus, to send in the overdue tribute of the Greek towns, and to begin a war with Athens; for 
1 CONC this purpose they entered into an alliance with Sparta. In 408 BC he sent his son Cyrus to Asia Minor, to carry on the war with greater energy. In 404 BC Darius II died after a reign of nineteen years, and was followed by Artaxerx
1 CONC es II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Based on an article from a 1911 encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Sogdianus Great King of Media and Persia
1 CONT 423–404 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Artaxerxes II 
1 CONT King of Egypt
1 CONT 423–404 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Amyrtaeus 
0 @N6000000002187600795@ NOTE Parysatis
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Parysatis was the illegitimate daughter of Artaxerxes I, Emperor of Persia and Andia of Babylon. She was the half-sister of Xerxes II, Sogdianus and Darius II. She married her half-brother Darius and had two sons, Artaxerxes III a
1 CONC nd Cyrus the Younger. When her husband died, she supported her younger son Cyrus. When Cyrus was defeated in the Battle of Cunaxa she blamed the starpt Tissapharnes for the death of her son. She later had Tissapharnes assassainate
1 CONC d.
0 @N6000000002187600822@ NOTE Died: 3 Sept 401 in battle of Kounaxa
0 @N6000000002187821836@ NOTE Tirots I Bagratuni1
1 CONT b. circa 400, d. after 451, #6388
1 CONT Pedigree 
1 CONT       Also called Prince Tirots I of the Bagratids. Sources: 1. Toumanoff, C. 'The Orontids of Armenia' in 'Studies in Christian Caucasian History' (1963) pp.337-342. ; 2. Toumanoff, C. 'The Early Bagratids' in La Museon, (1949), 
1 CONC pp.21-54. Tirots I Bagratuni was born circa 400. The placement of Tirots I as son of Smbat III seems to fit historically and chronologically but has not been proven. He was the son of Smbat III Bagratuni. Presiding Prince of the B
1 CONC agratids circa 451. He died after 451.  
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT      Child of Tirots I Bagratuni: 
1 CONT   Isaac II Bagratuni+   b. c 425, d. 483 
1 CONT
1 CONT [S197] Toby Dills, "A Descendant of Antiquity," gedcom file from e-mail address (e-mail address) to Robert Stewart, 5 Feb 1999. Hereinafter cited as "Descendant of Antiquity". 
