0 @N6000000002188327590@ NOTE Constantine II (emperor)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantine II
1 CONT Emperor of the Roman Empire
1 CONT Constantine II as Caesar
1 CONT Reign 1 March 317 - 337 (as Caesar in the west under his father);
1 CONT 337 - 340 (joint emperor with Constantius II and Constans, over Gaul, Hispania, and Britannia, in 340 in failed competition with Constans);
1 CONT Full name Flavius Claudius Constantinus
1 CONT Born 316
1 CONT Arles
1 CONT Died 340
1 CONT Aquileia
1 CONT Predecessor Constantine I
1 CONT Successor Constantius II and Constans
1 CONT Dynasty Constantinian
1 CONT Father Constantine I
1 CONT Mother Fausta
1 CONT
1 CONT Flavius Claudius Constantinus, known in English as Constantine II, (316 – 340) was Roman Emperor from 337 to 340. The eldest son of Constantine I and Fausta, he was born at Arles, and was raised as a Christian.
1 CONT
1 CONT On March 1, 317, Constantine was made Caesar, and at the age of seven, in 323, took part in his father's campaign against the Sarmatians. At the age of ten he became commander of Gaul, after the death of his half-brother Crispus. 
1 CONC An inscription dating to 330 records the title of Alamannicus, so it is probable that his generals won a victory over Alamanni. His military career continued when Constantine I elected his son field commander during the 332 campai
1 CONC gn against the Goths.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following the death of his father in 337, Constantine II became emperor jointly with his brothers Constantius II and Constans. After the division of the empire, made by the three brothers in September of the same year in Pannonia,
1 CONC  he ruled over Gaul, Britannia and Hispania.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was involved in the struggle between the different Christian streams. The Western portion of the empire leaned towards Trinitarism and against Arianism, and Constantine freed Athanasius and allowed him to return to Alexandria. 
1 CONC This action also put some burden on Constantius II, who was a supporter of Arianism.
1 CONT
1 CONT At first, he was the guardian of his younger brother Constans, whose portion was Italia, Africa and Illyricum. As Constans came of age, Constantine would not relinquish the guardianship and in 340 he marched against Constans in It
1 CONC aly, but was defeated at Aquileia and died in battle. Constans came to control his deceased brother's realm.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Wikimedia Commons logo Media related to Constantine II (emperor) from the Wikimedia Commons.
1 CONT     * DiMaio, Michael, and Robert Frakes, "Constantine II (337-340 A.D.)", D.I.R.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Constantine I Roman Emperor
1 CONT 337–340
1 CONT Co-Emperor with: Constantius II and Constans Succeeded by
1 CONT Constantius II and Constans
0 @N6000000002188327612@ NOTE Constans
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Constans
1 CONT Emperor of the Roman Empire
1 CONT Bronze coin bearing the
1 CONT profile of Constans
1 CONT Reign 337-350, jointly with Constantine II (until 340) and Constantius II
1 CONT Full name Flavius Julius Constans
1 CONT Born 320
1 CONT Died 350
1 CONT southeastern Gaul
1 CONT Predecessor Constantine I
1 CONT Successor Magnentius
1 CONT Dynasty Constantinian
1 CONT Father Constantine I
1 CONT Mother Fausta
1 CONT
1 CONT Flavius Julius Constans (320-350), was a Roman Emperor who ruled from 337 to 350. Constans was the third and youngest son of Constantine I and Fausta, Constantine's second wife.
1 CONT Bust of Constans
1 CONT Bust of Constans
1 CONT
1 CONT In 337 he succeeded his father, jointly with his older brothers Constantine II and Constantius II, receiving Italy, Pannonia and Africa as his portion. Constantine II, who ruled over Gaul, Spain and Britain, attempted to take adva
1 CONC ntage of his youth and inexperience by invading Italy in 340, but Constans defeated Constantine at Aquileia, where the older brother died. The invasion was the effect of brotherly tensions between the two emperors. Constantine II 
1 CONC was, at first, Constans's guardian. As Constans grew older, Constantine II never relinquished that position.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 341-2, Constans led a successful campaign against the Franks and in the early months of 343 visited Britain. The source for this visit, Julius Firmicus Maternus, does not give a reason for this but the quick movement and the da
1 CONC nger involved in crossing the channel in the dangerous winter months, suggests it was in response to a military emergency of some kind, possibly to repel the Picts and Scots.
1 CONT
1 CONT Regarding religion, Constans was tolerant of Judaism but promulgated an edict banning pagan sacrifices in 341. He suppressed Donatism in Africa and supported Nicene orthodoxy against Arianism, which was championed by his brother C
1 CONC onstantius the latter. Constans called the Council of Serdica, which unsuccessfully tried to settle the conflict.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 350, the general Magnentius declared himself emperor with the support of the troops on the Rhine frontier, and later the entire Western portion of the Roman Empire. Constans lacked any support beyond his immediate household, an
1 CONC d was forced to flee for his life. Magnentius' supporters cornered him in a fortification in Helena, southwestern Gaul, where he was killed by Magnentius's assassins.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT Wikimedia Commons has media related to:
1 CONT Constans
1 CONT
1 CONT     * DiMaio, Michael, "Constans I (337-350 A.D.)", De Imperatoribus Romanis site.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Constantine I Roman Emperor
1 CONT 337-350
1 CONT with Constantius II
1 CONT and Constantine II Succeeded by
1 CONT Magnentius
1 CONT (usurper)
0 @N6000000002188327601@ NOTE Constantius II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantius II
1 CONT Emperor of the Roman Empire
1 CONT Constantius II coin, celebrating
1 CONT his 15th year of reign.
1 CONT Reign 1) Caesar under his father
1 CONT 2) full co-emperor 337 - October 5, 361 with Constans and Constantine II (and in competition with others)
1 CONT Full name Flavius Iulius Constantius
1 CONT Born 7 August 317(317-08-07)
1 CONT Sirmium (Sremska Mitrovica, Serbia)
1 CONT Died 5 October 361 (aged 44)
1 CONT Tarsus in Cilicia
1 CONT Predecessor Constantine I
1 CONT Successor Julian
1 CONT Wife/wives daughter of Julius Constantius
1 CONT Eusebia
1 CONT Faustina
1 CONT Issue by 3)Flavia Maxima Constantia, who later married Gratian
1 CONT Dynasty Constantinian
1 CONT Father Constantine I
1 CONT Mother Fausta
1 CONT
1 CONT Flavius Iulius Constantius, known in English as Constantius II, born in Sirmium (now Sremska Mitrovica, Serbia), (7 August 317 - October 5, 361) was a Roman Emperor (337 - 361) of the Constantinian dynasty.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Biography
1 CONT           o 1.1 Rise to power
1 CONT           o 1.2 Struggles against usurpers and foreign threats
1 CONT           o 1.3 Death and succession
1 CONT           o 1.4 Religious issues
1 CONT     * 2 Notes
1 CONT     * 3 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Biography
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Rise to power
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantius was the second of the three sons of Constantine I and his second wife Fausta. Constantius was born in Sirmium now called Sremska Mitrovica (in Panonia) and named Caesar by his father. He married three times, first to a
1 CONC  daughter of Julius Constantius, then to Eusebia, and last to Faustina, who gave birth to a posthumous daughter called Constantia, who later married Emperor Gratian.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Constantine died in 337, Constantius II led the massacre of his relatives descended from the second marriage of his grandfather Constantius Chlorus and Theodora,[1] leaving himself, his older brother Constantine II, his young
1 CONC er brother Constans and two cousins (Gallus and his half-brother Julian) as the only surviving males related to Constantine. The three brothers divided the Roman Empire among them, according to their father's will. Constantine II 
1 CONC received Britannia, Gaul and Hispania; Constans ruled Italia, Africa, and Illyricum; and Constantius ruled the East.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Struggles against usurpers and foreign threats
1 CONT Constantius II coin, celebrating his 15th year of reign.
1 CONT Constantius II coin, celebrating his 15th year of reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT This division changed when Constantine II died in 340, trying to overthrow Constans in Italy, and Constans became sole ruler in the Western half of the empire. The division changed once more in 350 when Constans was killed in batt
1 CONC le by forces loyal to the usurper Magnentius. Until this time, Constantius was preoccupied with fighting the Sassanid Empire, and he was forced to elevate his cousin Gallus to Caesar of the East to assist him, while he turned his 
1 CONC attention to this usurper.
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantius eventually met and crushed Magnentius in the Battle of Mursa Major, one of the bloodiest battles in Roman history, in 351. Magnentius committed suicide in 353, and Constantius soon after put his cousin Gallus to death.
1 CONC  However, he still could not handle the military affairs of both the Eastern and German frontiers by himself, so in 355 he elevated his last remaining relative, Julian, to the rank of Caesar.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 11 August 355, the magister militum Claudius Silvanus revolted in Gaul. Silvanus had surrendered to Constantius after the battle of Mursa Major. Constantius had made him magister militum in 353/353, with the purpose of blocking
1 CONC  the German threats, a feat that Silvanus achieved by bribing the German tribes with the money he had collected. A plot organized by members of Constantius' court led the emperor to recall Silvanus. After Silvanus revolted, he rec
1 CONC eived a letter by Constantius that recalled him to Milan, but which made no reference to the revolt. Ursicinus, who should have replaced Silvanus, bribed some troops, and Silvanus was killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Arbitio was the main general (magister militum) of Constantius. He was given a consulship in 355.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death and succession
1 CONT
1 CONT As Julian was hailed Augustus by the army in Gaul, in 361, Constantius saw no alternative but to face the usurper with violent force. As the two armies sought engagement, Constantius died from a fever near Tarsus on October 5th, 3
1 CONC 61, and Julian was proclaimed Augustus throughout the Roman Empire. Many of his ministers were put to trial at the Chalcedon tribunal
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Religious issues
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantius took an active part in the affairs of the Christian church — convening one council at Rimini and its twin at Seleuca, which met in 359 and 360. "Unfortunately for his memory the theologians whose advice he took were ul
1 CONC timately discredited and the malcontents whom he pressed to conform emerged victorious," writes the historian A.H.M. Jones. " The great councils of 359-60 are therefore not reckoned oecumenical in the tradition of the church, and 
1 CONC Constantius II is not remembered as a restorer of unity, but as a heretic who arbitrarily imposed his will on the church."[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT Jones also notes that Constantius "appears in the pages of Ammianus as a conscientious emperor but a vain and stupid man, an easy prey to flatterers. He was timid and suspicious, and interested persons could easily play on his fea
1 CONC rs for their own advantage."[3] Ammianus also notes that Constantius II repeatedly murdered Roman aristocrats on the merest hint that they might be seeking the Empire themselves, including killing one because he had a dream about 
1 CONC him, and another because he had a purple tablecloth which might have been fashioned into the robe of an Emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ X. Lucien-Brun, "Constance II et le massacre des princes," Bulletin de l'Association Guillaume Budé ser. 4 (1973): 585-602; Joe W. Leedom, "Constantius II: Three Revisions," Byzantion 48 (1978): 132-145, and Michael DiMaio
1 CONC  and Duane Arnold, "Per Vim, Per Caedem, Per Bellum: A Study of Murder and Ecclesiastical Politics in the Year 337 A.D," Byzantion, 62(1992), 158ff. Cited in DiMaio and Frakes.
1 CONT    2. ^ A.H.M. Jones, The Later Roman Empire, 284-602: a Social, Economic and Administrative Survey (Baltimore: Johns Hopkins University, 1986), p. 118.
1 CONT    3. ^ Jones, Later Roman Empire, p. 116.
1 CONT
