1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Secondary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Morris, John; Arnold Hugh Martin Jones, and John Robert Martindale (1992). The prosopography of the later Roman Empire. Cambridge University Press, pp. 696-697. ISBN 0-521-07233-6. 
1 CONT     * DiMaio, Michael, "Constantina (daughter of Constantine I)", De Imperatoribus Romanis
0 @N6000000002188289341@ NOTE Sextus Aelius Catus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sextius Aelius Catus (consul of 4 AD) was father of Aelia Paetina, second wife of the emperor Claudius from 28 AD to about 31 AD (when Aelia's adoptive brother Sejanus fell from power). His only known grandchild was Aelia and Clau
1 CONC dius's daughter Claudia Antonia, born in 30.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sextus Aelius Catus was possibly descended from Sextus Aelius Q.f. Paetus Catus, consul of 198 BC and later a censor, or from Quintus Aelius Tubero, consul 11 BC. His daughter Aelia Paetina is said to belong to the Aelii Tuberones
1 CONC , which implies a descent from the consul of 11 BC.
0 @N6000000002187617206@ NOTE Xerxes II of Persia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Xerxes II was a Persian king and the son and successor of Artaxerxes I. After a reign of forty-five days, he was assassinated in 424 BC by his brother Sogdianus, who in turn was murdered by Darius II. He is an obscure historical f
1 CONC igure known primarily from the writings of Ctesias. He was reportedly the only legitimate son of Artaxerxes I and his Queen Damaspia. He is known to have served as Crown Prince.
1 CONT
1 CONT The last inscription mentioning Artaxerxes I being alive can be dated to December 24, 424 BC. Xerxes apparently succeeded to the throne but two of his illegitimate brothers claimed it for themselves. The first was Sogdianus, son b
1 CONC y concubine Alogyne of Babylon. The second was Darius II, son by concubine Cosmartidene of Babylon. He was married to their common half-sister Parysatis, daughter of Artaxerxes I and his concubine Andia of Babylon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Xerxes was apparently only recognized in Persia and Sogdianus in Elam. Ochus' first inscription as Darius II can be dated to January 10, 423 BC. He was already satrap of Hyrcania and was soon recognized by Media, Babylonia and Egy
1 CONC pt. Xerxes II only ruled forty five days. He was reportedly murdered while drunk by Pharnacyas, and Menostanes on Sogdianus' orders. Sogdianus apparently gained the support of his regions. Sogdianus was killed a few months later. 
1 CONC Darius II became the sole ruler of the Persian Empire and would reign till 404 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Achaemenid dynasty
1 CONT Born:  ??; Died: 424 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Artaxerxes I King (Shah) of Persia
1 CONT 424 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Sogdianus 
1 CONT Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT 424 
0 @N6000000002187617187@ NOTE Sogdianus of Persia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sogdianus, king of Persia (424 - 423 BC). He is an obscure historical figure known primarily from the writings of Ctesias. He was reportedly an illegitimate son of Artaxerxes I by his concubine Alogyne of Babylon.
1 CONT
1 CONT The last inscription mentioning Artaxerxes I being alive can be dated to December 24, 424 BC. His death resulted in at least three of his sons proclaiming themselves Kings. The first was Xerxes II, who was reportedly his only legi
1 CONC timate son by Queen Damaspia and was formerly Crown Prince. He was apparently only recognized in Persia. The second was Sogdianus himself, possibly recognized in Elam. The third was Ochus, son of Artaxerxes I by his concubine Cosm
1 CONC artidene of Babylon and satrap of Hyrcania. Ochus was also married to their common half-sister Parysatis, daughter of Artaxerxes I and his concubine Andia of Babylon. The first inscription of Ochus as Darius II can be dated to Jan
1 CONC uary 10, 423 BC. He seems to have been recognized by Media, Babylonia and Egypt.
1 CONT
1 CONT This chaotic state of affairs would prove short-lived. Xerxes II only ruled for forty-five days. He was reportedly murdered while drunk by Pharnacyas and Menostanes on Sogdianus' orders. Sogdianus apparently gained the support of 
1 CONC his regions. He was himself killed by Arbarios, commander of the cavalry. He had only reigned for six months and fifteen days. Darius II became the sole ruler of the Persian Empire and would reign till 404 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Achaemenid dynasty
1 CONT Born:  ??; Died: 424 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Xerxes II King (Shah) of Persia
1 CONT 424–423 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Darius II 
1 CONT Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT 424–423 
