1 CONT   93. ^ Barnes, Constantine and Eusebius, 36–37; Elliott, Christianity of Constantine, 50–53; Lenski, "Reign of Constantine" (CC), 66–67; Odahl, 94–95. It has traditionally been asserted, on the basis of this panegyric, that Const
1 CONC antine professed some variety of Apolline faith, or identified himself with Apollo. These views have recently been challenged (Elliott 1996, 51). The essentially political and impersonal nature of the panegyrics could preclude per
1 CONC sonal involvement on the part of Constantine. Barnes states that "It is not necessary to believe that Constantine ever saw such a vision" (Barnes 1981, 36). Lenski states, contrariwise, that Constantine indeed "believed he had see
1 CONC n a divine vision" (Lenski 2006, 67).
1 CONT   94. ^ The most detailed accounts of Galerius' illness are given in Lactantius 31–35 and Eusebius, Historia Ecclesiastica 8.16. Both describe a particularly painful disease, but are inconsistent in its details. Jones and Lenski i
1 CONC dentify it as a degenerative bowel disease, specifically cancer of the bowels in Jones' case (Jones 1948, 66; Lenski 2006, 68). Odahl identifies the disease as cancer of the penis (Odahl 2004, 95–96, 316). Elliott suspects that th
1 CONC e gruesome facts supplied by the Christian authors are greatly exaggerated (Elliott 1996, 43).
