0 @N6000000002187572916@ NOTE Petronius Maximus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Petronius Maximus on a coin.Petronius Maximus (c.396 - 455), was a Roman aristocrat, and briefly Western Roman Emperor for part of 455.
1 CONT
1 CONT His senatorial career was as follows. His earliest known office was praetor, held c.411; around 415 he served as a tribunus et notarius, which was an entry position to the imperial bureaucracy, and led to his serving as Comes sacr
1 CONC arum largitionum (Count of the Sacred Largess) between 416 and 419, as well as Urban Prefect between the years 419 and 433. In 433 he was consul, in 439 Praetorian Prefect of Italy, and in 443 he was consul a second time. When he 
1 CONC was granted the title of Patrician in 445, he was briefly the most honored of all non-Imperial Romans, until the third consulate of Aëtius, generalissimo of the Western empire, the following year.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is clear that the enmity between Maximus and Aetius led to the catastrophic events that brought down the Western Roman Empire. According to the account of John of Antioch, Maximus and the eunuch Heraclius persuaded the emperor 
1 CONC Valentinian III to have Aëtius killed – which he did by his own hands. The historian John further alleges that Valentinian's death (March 16, 455) at the hands of Optila and Thranstila was also at Maximus' instigation.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following Valentinian's death, there was no one obvious successor to the throne: Maximus competed with one Maximianus, son of Domninus, who had been a bodyguard of Aëtius, and with the future emperor Majorian, who had the backing 
1 CONC of the empress Licinia Eudoxia. Maximus managed to defeat his rivals by gaining control of the palace and forced Eudoxia to marry him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Maximus quickly appointed Avitus as Master of Soldiers, and sent him on a mission to Toulouse to gain the support of the Visigoths; however, by the time Avitus arrived, Maximus was dead, and the mission pointless. Within two month
1 CONC s of Maximus gaining the throne, word came that Gaiseric, king of the Vandals, had arrived in Italy, news that panicked the inhabitants of Rome. In the disorder Maximus was killed, either by a mob or by "a certain Roman soldier na
1 CONC med Ursus".
1 CONT
1 CONT Three days after Maximus' death on April 22, Gaiseric entered Rome with his army. While the Vandals looted the city and captured people as slaves or hostages, in response to the pleas of Pope Leo I, they desisted from more destruc
1 CONC tive behavior that accompanied the sack of a city – arson, torture, and murder.
0 @N6000000009304860859@ NOTE #Générale##Générale#<dooghe.23><HR>
0 @N6000000002188327534@ NOTE Minervina
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Minervina was the wife or mistress of Constantine. She had one son, Crispus. When Constantine wanted to strengthen his bonds with the Tetrarchs, he set apart Minervina and married Fausta, daughter of Maximian.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Pohlsander, Hans, "Constantine I (306 - 337 A.D.)", De Imperatoribus Romanis site.
0 @N6000000002188327496@ NOTE Crispus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Flavius Julius Crispus, also known as Flavius Claudius Crispus and Flavius Valerius Crispus was a Caesar of the Roman Empire. He was the first-born son of Constantine I and Minervina.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Life account
1 CONT           o 1.1 Birth
1 CONT           o 1.2 Question of legitimacy
1 CONT           o 1.3 Education
1 CONT           o 1.4 Career
1 CONT           o 1.5 Execution
1 CONT     * 2 References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life account
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Birth
1 CONT
1 CONT Crispus' year and place of birth are uncertain. He is considered likely to have been born between 299 and 305, somewhere in the Eastern Roman Empire. His mother Minervina was either a concubine or a first wife to Constantine. Noth
1 CONC ing else is known about Minervina. His father served as a hostage in the court of Eastern Roman Emperor Diocletian in Nicomedia. Thus securing the loyalty of Caesar of the Western Roman Empire Constantius Chlorus, father of Consta
1 CONC ntine and grandfather of Crispus.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 307, Constantine allied to the Italian Augusti, and this alliance was sealed with the marriage of Constantine to Fausta, daughter of Maximian and sister of Maxentius.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Question of legitimacy
1 CONT
1 CONT The marriage of Constantine to Fausta has caused modern historians to question the status of his relation to Minervina and Crispus. If Minervina was his legitimate wife, Constantine would have needed to secure a divorce before mar
1 CONC rying Fausta. This would have required an official written order signed by Constantine himself, but no such order is mentioned by contemporary sources.
1 CONT
1 CONT This silence in the sources has led many historians to conclude that the relationship between Constantine and Minervina was informal and to assume her to have been an unofficial lover. It should be noted however that Minervina may
1 CONC  have already been dead by 307. A widowed Constantine would need no divorce order.
1 CONT
1 CONT Neither the true nature of the relationship between Constantine and Minervina nor the reason Crispus came under the protection of his father will ever probably be known. The offspring of an illegitimate affair could have caused dy
1 CONC nastical problems and would likely be dismissed, but Crispus was raised by his father in Gaul. This can be seen as evidence of a loving and public relationship between Constantine and Minervina which gave him a reason to protect h
1 CONC er son.
1 CONT
1 CONT One should note that the story of Minervina is quite similar to that of Helena of Constantinople. Helena of Constantinople was the beloved first wife of Constantius Chlorus and the mother of Constantine. Constantius later had to d
1 CONC ivorce her for political reasons, specifically, to marry Flavia Maximiana Theodora (daughter or step-daughter to Maximian, sister of Maxentius and Fausta) in order to secure his alliance with his new father-in-law. Constantine in 
1 CONC his turn may have had to put aside Minervina, if she still lived, in order to secure an alliance with the same man. Constantius did not however dismiss Constantine as his son, and perhaps Constantine chose to follow the example of
1 CONC  his father.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Education
1 CONT
1 CONT Whatever the reason, Constantine kept Crispus at his side. Surviving sources are unanimous in declaring him a loving, trusting and protective father to his first son. Constantine even entrusted his education to Lactantius, among t
1 CONC he most important Christian teachers of that time, who probably started teaching Crispus before 317. There is no evidence that Lactantius managed to implant the values of Christianity to his young pupil. On the contrary it seems t
1 CONC hat Crispus was never baptised during his lifetime.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Career
1 CONT
1 CONT By 317, there were two remaining Augusti in control of the Roman Empire. Constantine reigned as an Western Roman Emperor and his brother-in-law Licinius as an Eastern Roman Emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 1 March 317, the two co-reigning Augusti jointly proclaimed three new Caesars. Crispus alongside his younger half-brother Constantine II and his first cousin Licinius iunior. Constantine II was the older son of Fausta but was p
1 CONC robably about a month old at the time of his proclamation. Thus only Crispus assumed actual duties.
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantine apparently believed in the abilities of his son and appointed Crispus as Commander of Gaul. The new Caesar soon held residence in Augusta Treverorum (modern Trier), regional capital of Germania.
1 CONT
1 CONT In January 322, Crispus was married to a young woman called Helena. Helena bore him a son in October, 322. There is no surviving account of the name or later fate of the son. Eusebius of Caesarea reported that Constantine was prou
1 CONC d of his son and very pleased to become a grandfather.
1 CONT
1 CONT Crispus was leader in victorious military operations against the Franks and the Alamanni in 318, 320 and 323. Thus he secured the continued Roman presence in the areas of Gaul and Germania. Crispus joined his father in visiting Ro
1 CONC me during 322, and received the warmest and most enthusiastic welcome by the crowds.
1 CONT
1 CONT The soldiers adored him thanks to his strategic abilities and the victories to which he had led the Roman legions. Perhaps more importantly, Crispus was neither a Christian nor in favour of the new religion. Therefore Crispus was 
1 CONC likely seen as the secret hope of all Pagans in the Western Roman Empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT Crispus spent the following years assisting Constantine in the war against by then hostile Licinius. In 324, Constantine appointed Crispus as the commander of his fleet which left the port of Piraeus to confront the rival fleet of
1 CONC  Licinius. The subsequent Battle of Hellespont was fought in at the straits of Bosporus. The 200 ships under the command of Crispus managed to utterly beat the enemy forces which were at least double in number. Thus Crispus achiev
1 CONC ed his most important and difficult victory which further established his reputation as a brilliant soldier and general.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following his navy activities, Crispus was assigned part of the legions loyal to his father. The other part was commanded by Constantine himself. Crispus led the legions assigned to him in another victorious battle outside Chrysop
1 CONC olis against the armies of Licinius.
1 CONT
1 CONT The two victories were his contribution to the final triumph of his father over Licinius. Constantine was the only Augustus left in the Empire. He honoured his son for his support and success by depicting his face in imperial coin
1 CONC s, statues, mosaics, cameos, etc. Eusebius of Caesaria wrote for Crispus that he is "an Imperator most dear to God and in all regards comparable to his father."
1 CONT
1 CONT Crispus was the most likely choice for an heir to the throne at the time. His siblings Constantine II, Constantius II and Constans were far too young and inexperienced.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Execution
1 CONT
1 CONT In 326, Crispus life came to a sudden end: On his father's orders he was tried by a local court at Pola, Istria, condemned to death and executed. Soon afterwards, Constantine had his wife Fausta killed, she was suffocated in an ov
1 CONC er-heated bath.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT The reason for this act remains unclear and historians have long debated Constantine's motivation.
1 CONT
1 CONT Zosimus in the 5th century and Joannes Zonaras in the 12th century both reported that Fausta, step-mother of Crispus, was extremely jealous of him. She was reportedly afraid that Constantine would put aside the sons she bore him. 
1 CONC So, in order to get rid of Crispus, Fausta set him up. She reportedly told the young Caesar that she was in love with him and suggested an illegitimate love affair. Crispus denied the immoral wishes of Fausta and left the palace i
1 CONC n a state of a shock. Then Fausta said to Constantine that Crispus had no respect for his father, since the Caesar was in love with his father's own wife. She reported to Constantine that she dismissed him after his attempt to rap
1 CONC e her. Constantine believed her and, true to his strong personality and short temper, executed his beloved son. A few months later, Constantine reportedly found out the whole truth and then killed Fausta.
1 CONT
1 CONT This version of events has become the most widely accepted, since all other reports are even less satisfactory.
1 CONT
1 CONT That Fausta and Crispus could have plotted treason against Constantine is rejected by most historians. as they would have nothing to gain considering their positions as favourites of Constantine. In any case, such a case would not
1 CONC  have been tried by a local court as Crispus' case clearly was.
1 CONT
1 CONT Another view suggests that Constantine killed Crispus because as an illegitimate son, he would cause a crisis in the order of succession to the throne. However, Constantine had kept him at his side for twenty years without any suc
1 CONC h decision. Constantine also had the authority to appoint his younger, legitimate sons as his heirs. Nevertheless, Crispus' status as a legitimate or illegitimate son remains uncertain.
1 CONT
1 CONT Some reports claimed that Constantine was envious of the success of his son and afraid of him. This seems improbable, given that Constantine had twenty years of experience as emperor while Crispus was still a young caesar. Similar
1 CONC ly, there seems to be no evidence that Crispus had any ambitions to harm or displace his father.
1 CONT
1 CONT So while the story of Zosimus and Zonaras seems the most believable one, there are also problems relating to their version of events.
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantine's reaction suggests that he suspected Crispus of a crime so terrible that death was not enough. Crispus also suffered damnatio memoriae, meaning his name was never mentioned again and was deleted from all official docu
1 CONC ments and monuments. Crispus, his wife Helena and their son were never to be mentioned again in historical records. The eventual fate of Helena and her son is a mystery.
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantine may have been eventually convinced of Crispus' innocence. But he did not restore his son's innocence and name, as he probably would have on learning of his son's innocence. Perhaps Constantine's pride, or shame at havi
1 CONC ng executed his son, prevented him from publicly admitting having made a mistake.
1 CONT
1 CONT Beyond doubt there was a connection between the executions of Crispus and Fausta. Both happened too close in time to be coincidental. Such agreement among different sources connecting the two deaths is extremely rare in itself. A 
1 CONC number of modern historians have suggested that Crispus and Fausta really did have an illegitimate affair. When Constantine found out, his reaction was for executing both of them. What delayed the death of Fausta may have been a p
1 CONC regnancy. Since the years of birth for the two known daughters of Constantine and Fausta remain unknown, one of their births may have delayed their mother's execution.
1 CONT
1 CONT The story of Zosimus and Zonaras listed above is suspiciously similar to the legend of Hippolytus of Athens, casting Crispus in the role of the youth, Constantine in the role of Theseus and Fausta in the role of Phaedra. One wonde
1 CONC rs if the legend provided inspiration for two historians attempting to resolve a historical mystery.[citation needed]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Roman Emperors - DIR Fausta
0 @N6000000002188327580@ NOTE Fausta
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Fausta Flavia Maxima, Roman Empress, (289-326A.D.) She was the daughter of the Roman Emperor Maximianus. To seal the alliance between them for control of the Tetrarchy, Maximianus married her to Constantine I in 307. Constantine a
1 CONC nd Fausta had been betrothed since 293.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fausta had a part in her father's downfall. In 310 Maximian died as a consequence of an assassination plot against Constantine. Maximian decided to involve his daughter Fausta, but she revealed the plot to her husband, and the ass
1 CONC assination was disrupted. Maximian died, by suicide or by assassination, in July of that same year.
1 CONT
1 CONT Empress Fausta was held in high esteem by Constantine and proof of his favour was that in 323 she was proclaimed Augusta, previously she held the title of Nobilissima Femina. However 3 years later Fausta was put to death by Consta
1 CONC ntine. Although the real reasons are not clear, Constantine put her to death following the execution of Crispus, his eldest son by a previous marriage to Minervina, in 326. According to the ancient sources, she accused Crispus of 
1 CONC rape, and Constantine had Crispus executed. Fausta was later executed by suffocation in an over-heated bath,[1] when her charge was discovered to be false. Modern commentators have tended to ignore the allegation of rape and to se
1 CONC ek some other explanation for what happened. It has often been argued that Fausta wanted to get rid of Crispus who was a dangerous rival for her sons in the competition to succeed Constantine. The Emperor ordered the damnatio memo
1 CONC riae of his wife. Significantly her sons once in power never revoked this order.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her sons became Roman Emperors: Constantine II reigned 337 - 340, Constantius II reigned 337 - 361, and Constans reigned 337 - 350. She also bore three daughters Constantina, Helena and Fausta. Of these, Constantina married her co
1 CONC usins, firstly Hannibalianus and secondly Constantius Gallus, and Helena married Emperor Julian. Apparently a genealogical claim that her daughter Fausta became mother of Emperor Valentinian I is without foundation (Valentinian I 
1 CONC and children of Constantine I's second marriage were born in years close to each other, i.e they were of the same generation).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Roman Emperors - DIR Fausta
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Bibliography
1 CONT
1 CONT J.W. Drijvers, 'Flavia Maxima Fausta: Some Remarks', Historia 41 (1992) 500- 506.
1 CONT
1 CONT D. Woods, ‘On the Death of the Empress Fausta’, Greece & Rome 45 (1998), 70-86.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Wikimedia Commons logo Media related to Fausta from the Wikimedia Commons.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Galeria Valeria Empress of Rome
1 CONT 307–326
1 CONT (with Galeria Valeria, Valeria Maximilla and Flavia Julia Constantia)
0 @N6000000002188327650@ NOTE Flavia Maximiana Theodora
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Flavia Maximiana Theodora (known as Theodora) was the step-daughter of Maximian. Her parents were Afanius Hannibalianus and Eutropia, later wife of Maximian. Theodora's father was consul in 292, and praetorian prefect under Diocle
1 CONC tian. In 293, Theodora married Flavius Valerius Julius Constantius (later known as Constantius Chlorus), after he had divorced from his first wife, Helena, to strengthen his political position.
1 CONT
1 CONT The couple had six children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Flavius Dalmatius;
1 CONT     * Julius Constantius, father of Roman Emperor Julian;
1 CONT     * Hannibalianus (must have died before the imperial purges that occurred in 337 because he is not listed among its victims);
1 CONT     * Anastasia;
1 CONT     * Flavia Julia Constantia, wife of Roman Emperor Licinius;
1 CONT     * Eutropia, mother of Nepotianus.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
