0 @N6000000000437392029@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> King Name Suffix:<NSFX> of Burgundy
0 @N6000000002187718312@ NOTE Gundobad
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Gundobad, Patrician of the Western Roman Empire (472-473) also became King of the Burgundians (473-516), after his father Gundioc of Burgundy, though he had to fight off three brothers to seize his title.
1 CONT
1 CONT When the former Patrician Ricimer, who had been the power behind the throne for the Western Empire, died in 472, his nephew Gundobad seized the title. With his new power he elevated the current Count of the Domestics, Glycerius, t
1 CONC o the position of Western Roman Emperor. Gundobad, however left office in 473, as his father, Gundioc, had died and he had inherited Burgundy along with his three brothers; Godegisel, Chilperic II and Gundomar.
1 CONT
1 CONT But Gundobad was not content with his fractured portion of Burgundy, and turned against his brothers with hope of control over all the land.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 486 he killed Gundomar, though little is known of this encounter.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 493 he turned his sword against Chilperic, and drowned his wife. Gundobad exiled his two daughters, Chroma becoming a nun. The other, Clotilde, was found by the men of Clovis I, King of the Franks, who sent word to Gundobad, as
1 CONC king Clotilde's hand in marriage. Gundobad was too afraid to decline.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gundobad's battle with Godegisel raged long. Unknowingly, both called upon Clovis trying to persuade him to join forces against the other. Clovis sided with Godegisel, who had offered him his pleasure of tribute and crushed Gundob
1 CONC ad's force. Gundobad fled but King Clovis pursued him to Avignon. Gundobad feared the worst with Clovis's mighty army at the gates. But a man of wit called Aridius went from Gundobad to Clovis and charmed him into taking his advic
1 CONC e, which was to spare Gundobad but force him into paying a yearly tribute.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gundobad later broke his promise of tribute as he regained his power and besieged Godegisel, locked up in the city of Vienne. As famine devoured Vienne, Godegisel expelled the common people from the city for fear for himself. An o
1 CONC utraged expelled artisan seeking vengeance on Godegisel went to Gundobad, and with his help he navigated the aqueduct and broke into the city. He murdered Godegisel in 501 in an Arian church along with the bishop.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gundobad was now sole king of Burgundy. He made peace with the Franks, converted to Catholicism, and died peacefully succeeded by his son Sigismund in 516. He also had another son: Godomar. Godomar would succeed his brother after 
1 CONC his death in 524.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Sourced from Gregory of Tours' History of the Franks 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Gunderic King of Burgundy
1 CONT 473–516 Succeeded by
1 CONT Sigismund 
1 CONT
1 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gundobad"
0 @N6000000001353750011@ NOTE [blended.FTW]
1 CONT [mergebase.FTW]
1 CONT King of the Burgundians (?-516)
1 CONT Succeeded with his brother Godigisel as sole rulers of the Burgundians. In 500, Clovis I, King of the Franks, defeated him with the help ofGodigisel. This battle was followed by more between the two brothers.Gundobad, after killin
1 CONC g Godigisel while in an Arian church, then assumed both an alliance with the Franks and sole leadership of Burgundy. He died in 516 at peace with the four Frankish kings, and was succeeded by his son Sigismund.
0 @N6000000000437392042@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> King Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Burgundy
0 @N6000000001353845057@ NOTE Gunther, also called GUNDICAR, GUNDICARIUS, GUNNAR, GUNDAHAR, or GUNTHARIUS, Burgundian king (died 437) who was the hero of medieval legends.
1 CONT The historical Gunther led the Burgundians across the Rhine in the early 5th century, establishing a kingdom at Worms. He supported the imperial usurper Jovinus (411) and fell in battle against the Huns in 437.
1 CONT Gunther (called Gunnar) figures in the Eddaic poem Atlakvida, in which he is slain by Atli (Attila) the Hun and avenged by his sister, Atli's wife. In the 11th-century Latin poem (Waltharius), he and his warriors try unsuccessfull
1 CONC y to kill the hero (Walter of Aquitaine) and steal his treasure. The 12th-century German epic Nibelungenlied associates him with Siegfried, who helps Gunther to win Brunhild and in returnmarries Gunther's sister Kriemhild. When Si
1 CONC egfried is later killed onGunther's order, Kriemhild revenges his death by having Gunther and his followers slain while visiting the court of her second husband, Etzel (Attila)
0 @N6000000001354338006@ NOTE [blended.FTW]
1 CONT [mergebase.FTW]
1 CONT King of the Burgundians (?-516)
1 CONT Succeeded with his brother Godigisel as sole rulers of the Burgundians. In 500, Clovis I, King of the Franks, defeated him with the help ofGodigisel. This battle was followed by more between the two brothers.Gundobad, after killin
1 CONC g Godigisel while in an Arian church, then assumed both an alliance with the Franks and sole leadership of Burgundy. He died in 516 at peace with the four Frankish kings, and was succeeded by his son Sigismund.
0 @N6000000001355633052@ NOTE Gunther, also called GUNDICAR, GUNDICARIUS, GUNNAR, GUNDAHAR, or GUNTHARIUS, Burgundian king (died 437) who was the hero of medieval legends.
1 CONT The historical Gunther led the Burgundians across the Rhine in the early 5th century, establishing a kingdom at Worms. He supported the imperial usurper Jovinus (411) and fell in battle against the Huns in 437.
1 CONT Gunther (called Gunnar) figures in the Eddaic poem Atlakvida, in which he is slain by Atli (Attila) the Hun and avenged by his sister, Atli's wife. In the 11th-century Latin poem (Waltharius), he and his warriors try unsuccessfull
1 CONC y to kill the hero (Walter of Aquitaine) and steal his treasure. The 12th-century German epic Nibelungenlied associates him with Siegfried, who helps Gunther to win Brunhild and in returnmarries Gunther's sister Kriemhild. When Si
