0 @N6000000002187718323@ NOTE Gunther
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT For the character of the sitcom Friends see here. For the former model and musician who uses the alias Günther and sometimes Gunther, see Mats Söderlund. For the historian of science, see Robert Gunther. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Volsung Cycle
1 CONT
1 CONT Volsunga saga 
1 CONT Poetic Edda 
1 CONT Norna-Gests þáttr 
1 CONT Artifacts 
1 CONT Andvarinaut 
1 CONT Gram 
1 CONT Dwarves 
1 CONT Andvari 
1 CONT Hreidmar 
1 CONT Otr 
1 CONT Regin 
1 CONT Dragon 
1 CONT Fafnir 
1 CONT People 
1 CONT Volsung 
1 CONT Sigmund 
1 CONT Signy 
1 CONT Sinfjötli 
1 CONT Helgi Hundingsbane 
1 CONT Sigurd 
1 CONT Brynhild 
1 CONT Gudrun 
1 CONT Attila 
1 CONT Gunnar 
1 CONT Locations 
1 CONT Gautland 
1 CONT Hunaland 
1 CONT Related 
1 CONT Nibelungenlied 
1 CONT Hagbard and Signy 
1 CONT Gunther (in Latin Gundaharius and in Anglicized Old Norse Gunnar) was a king of the Burgundians west of the Rhine from at least 411 to his death in 437. Legendary tales about Gundaharius appear in Latin, medieval German, Old Norse
1 CONC , and Old English texts, especially concerning his relations with Siegfried (in Old Norse Sigurd) and the death of Gunther from treachery in the hall of Attila the Hun.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Historical information 
1 CONT 2 In legend 
1 CONT 3 Forms of the name 
1 CONT 4 See also 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Historical information
1 CONT According to Olympiodorus (11), in 411 Jovinus was proclaimed tyrannos of Moundiakon in Germania Secunda through the support of the Alan Goar and of Guntiarios who was called commander of the Burgundians.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the defeat of Jovinus in 413, the Burgundians were permitted by the Western Roman Empire to settle in Gaul, on the Rhine and it may have been then that Gundaharius first established the Burgundian Kingdom west of the Rhine w
1 CONC ith Worms as its capital.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 435 the Burgundians attempted to thrust farther westward but were defeated by the Western Roman general Aëtius. In 437 the Huns (perhaps with the support of Aetius) attacked and destroyed the Burgundian kingdom. Gundaharius met
1 CONC  his death and the surviors were allowed by the Romans to settle in Savoy. Attila himself seems to have had no part in this since, at least according to Priscus, in 435 Attila was negotiating peace with Romans at Margus in upper M
1 CONC oesia and until 439 was occupied with subduing the nations of Scythia and warring on the Sorosgi.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gundaharius' successor was Gunderic, whose relation to Gundaharius is unknown.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the Lex Burgundionum', issued by the Burgundian king Gundobad (c. 480–516), it is decreed that those who were free under the kings Gibica, Gundomar, Gislaharius, and Gundaharius will remain free. In later Germanic legend Gunthe
1 CONC r appears directly as the son of Gibica or Gibeche/Gjúki. In German accounts his brothers are named Gernot and Giselher (all three called kings in the Nibelungenlied). Norse tradition knows of a younger brother named Gotthorm (Got
1 CONC þormr) which might dervie from Gundomar. This suggests that historically the king Gibica of the Bungrundian Laws might have been the father of three kings Gundomar, Gislaharius, and Gundaharius who shared the kingdom, presumably w
1 CONC ith Gundaharius being the high king. But if so, the order of the names here is puzzling. One would expect Gundaharius to be named immediately after Gibica.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT In legend
1 CONT In the Waltharius Gibicho and his son Guntharius are kings of the Franks, whereas the king of the Burgundians is named Heriricus who is father to Hiltgunt, the heroine of the story. Hagen appears here as a kinsman of Gibicho and G
1 CONC untharius, but the relationship is not made explicit. In their combats with Waltharius, Guntharius loses a leg, Hagen loses half his face and one eye, and Waltharius loses a hand. But there is no hint in later tales that Gunther i
1 CONC s in any way maimed. Another version of the story of Waltharius and Hiltgunt appears in the Norse Thidreks saga, but in this account Gunther/Gunnar plays no part at all.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gunther otherwise only appears in tales relating to Siegfried/Sigurd and the fall of the Burgundians.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gunther/Gunnar seeks to make Brünhild/Brynhild his wife but can win her and master her only because the hero Siegfried/Sigurd aids him and takes his place. Siegfried/Sigurd marries Gunther/Gunnar's sister Kriemhild/Gudrún. An impa
1 CONC ssioned debate between Brünhild/Brynhild and Kriemhild/Gudrún about their respective status leads to the secret being revealed and to Gunther/Gunnar agreeing to Sigfried/Sigurd's murder.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the story of the fall of the Burgundians, in various texts, Gunther and his brothers, despite deep suspicions of treachery, accept an invitation from Etzel/Atli (= Attila) to visit his court. There Gunther and his brothers meet
1 CONC  their deaths.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Forms of the name
1 CONT The name appears in Olympiodorus as Guntiarios, in Prosper of Aquitaine's Chronica Minora Saec as Gundicharius, in the Leges Burgundionum as Gundaharius, in Ekkehard of St. Gall's Latin poem Waltharius as Guntharius, in the Old En
1 CONC glish poem Waldere as Guthere (Gúðere), in medieval German as Gunther, and in Old Norse as Gunnar (accurate Old Norse nominative form Gunnarr). Reconstructed Germanic forms such as Gundahari, Gundahar, and Gundacar are sometimes u
1 CONC sed.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT See also Burgundians, Nibelung, Volsunga saga and
1 CONT
1 CONT This holm is named "Gunnar's Holm" after Gunnar Hámundarson from Hlíðarendi, one of the main protagonists of Njál's saga, Gunnar is the most attractive and unreservedly admired of Icelandic saga heroes
