0 @N6000000000337167914@ NOTE (Research):Clotilda, Saint Clotilda, Saint Pronounced As: kltild , d. 545, Frankish queen. She converted her husband, Clovis I, to Christianity and built with him in Paris the Church of the Apostles Peter and Paul, later renamed (
1 CONC 10th cent.) Sainte-Geneviève. After her husband's death she spent her life caring for the poor. Feast: June 3.
0 @N6000000000437062559@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Saint Name Suffix:<NSFX> Princess Of Burgundy "Clotilda was the younger daughter of King Chilperic II of the Burgundians. On her father'smurder by her uncles, she and her sister Chroma escaped Burgundy. Clotilda
1 CONC  married Clovis, King of the Franks, in 493 and had with him five children. She wasthe person primarily responsible for Clovis' conversion to Christianity, and,therefore, the conversion of all of France. At Clovis' death in 511, C
1 CONC lotilda went into a monastery at Tours where she stayed until her death in 545. She wascanonized a few years after her death, and her traditional feast day is June 3." ---------------------------------------- 1  NAME St. Clothilda
1 CONC  (Clotild) of/Burgundy/ 2  SOUR S033320 3  DATA 4  TEXT Date of Import: Jan 17, 2001 1NAME St. Clothilde /De Burgundy/ 1  DEAT 2  DATE 3 JUN 548 2  PLAC Tours,Ingre-et-Loire, France
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [De La Pole.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources: RC 303, 349; AF; Kraentzler 1636, 1667, 1778, 1780; "The Franks" by Edward James; Pfafman. Franks:Chrodechildis, or Clotild in modern French; a Catholic Christian, daughter ofa former Burgundian king. Her family memberswe
1 CONC re not Christians. They were Arians, who did not believe in the equality ofpersons in the Trinity. She probably was responsible for pushing Clovis into becoming a Christian. The Church ofthe Holy Apostles--where Clotild, Clovis an
1 CONC d their daughter were buried--was later named after St. Genevieve, who also was buried there. K: Saint Clothildede Bourgogne. K-1657: Chrotechilde von Altburgund (Old Bourgogne). Pfafman: Clothilda (Clothild) of Burgundy. Second w
1 CONC ife of Clovis. Canonized after her death.Clotilda was responsible for the conversion of her husband to Christianity.After he died in 511 she entered a monastery at Tours. She was canonized a fewyears after her death. Her remains w
1 CONC ere buried in the Church of Saint Genevivein Paris. Her tradition al feast day is June 3. "Clotilda, Saint," Microsoft(R) Encarta. Copyright (c ) 1994 MicrosoftCorporation. Copyright (c) 1994 Funk& Wagnall's Corporation
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources: RC 303, 349; AF; Kraentzler 1636, 1667, 1778, 1780; "The Franks" by Edward James; Pfafman. Franks: Chrodechildis, or Clotild in modern French; a Catholic Christian, daughter of a former Burgundian king. Her family members
1 CONC were not Christians. They were Arians, who did not believe in the equality ofpersons in the Trinity. She probably was responsible forpushing Clovis into becoming a Christian. The Church of the Holy Apostles--where Clotild, Clovis 
1 CONC and their daughter were buried--was later named after St.Genevieve, who also was buried there. K: Saint Clothilde de Bourgogne. K-1657: Chrotechilde von Altburgund (Old Bourgogne). Pfafman: Clothilda (Clothild) of Burgundy. Second
1 CONC  wife of Clovis. Canonized after her death.Clotilda was responsible for the conversion of her husband to Christianity. After he died in 511she entered a monastery at Tours. She was canonized a few years after her death. Her remain
1 CONC s were buried in the Church of Saint Genevive in Paris. Her tradition al feast day is June 3. "Clotilda, Saint," Microsoft (R) Encarta. Copyright (c ) 1994 MicrosoftCorporation. Copyright (c) 1994 Funk & Wagnall's Corporation
