0 @N6000000007329688720@ NOTE [Chambers Biographical Dictionary] Merovingian King of the Franks.
1 CONT [Ancestral Safari, Wm. G. Cook, Parke's Newsletter 1991 #3] :   Dagobert
1 CONT I,  King of Austrasia (602-38). Wife Nantichild (fl. 629-39).
1 CONT [Ahnentafel by Philippe Houdry, from various sources, ver. 3 (Aug.   31,
1 CONT 1994) posted by Tom Camfield]: b. ca 611, liason 630, d. ca. 638
1 CONT Saint-Denis;  Frankish King 629-638.
0 @N6000000001353845701@ NOTE In 623 his father established him as king of the region east of the Ardennes, and in 626 revived for him the ancient kingdom of Austrasia, minus Aquitaine and Provence. As Dagobert was but yet a child, he was placed under the auth
1 CONC ority of the mayor of the palace, Pepin, and Arnulf, bishop of Metz. At the death of Clotaire II in 628, Dagobert wished to re-establish unity in the Frankish realm, and in 629 and 630 make expeditions into Neustria and Burgundy, 
1 CONC where he succeeded on the whole in securing the recognition of his authority. In Aquitaine he gave his brother Caribert the administration of the counties of Toulouse, Cahors, Agen, Pérogeux and Saintes; but at Caribert's death in
1 CONC  632 Dagobert became sole ruler of the whole of the Frankish territories south of the Loire. Under him the Merovingian monarchy attained its culminating point. He restored to the royal domain the lands that had beenusurped by the 
1 CONC great nobles and by the church; he maintained at Parisa luxurious, though, from the example he himself set, a disorderly court; he was a patron of the arts and delighted in the exquisite craftsmanship of his treasurer, the goldsmi
1 CONC th, St. Eloi. His authority was recognized through the length and breadth of the realm. The duke of theBasques came to his court to swear fidelity, and at his villa at Clichy the chief of the Bretons of Domnoné promised obedience.
1 CONC  He intervened in the affairs of the Visigoths of Spain and the Lombards of Italy,and was heard with deference. Indeed, as a sovereign, Dagobert was reckoned superior to the other barbarian kings. He entered into relations with th
1 CONC e eastern empire, and swore a "perpetual peace" with the emperor Heraclius; and it is probable that the two sovereigns took commonmeasures against the Slav and Burgundian tribes which ravaged in turnthe Byzantine state and the Ger
1 CONC man territories subject to the Franks.Dagobert protected the church and placed illustrious prelates at the head of the bishoprics---Eloi (Eligius) at Noyon, Ouen (Audoenus) at Rouen and Didier (Desiderius) at Cahors. His reign is 
1 CONC also marked by the creation of numerous monasteries and by renewed missionary activityin Flanders and among the Basques. He died on Jan 9, 639, as was buried at St. Denis. After his death the Frankish monarchy was again divided. I
1 CONC n 634 he had been obliged to give the Austrasians a special king in the person of his eldest son Sigebert, and at the birth of a secondson, Colvis, in 635, the Neustrians had immediately claimed him as king. Thus the unification o
1 CONC f the realm, which Dagobert had re-established with so much pains, was anulled.
0 @N6000000009304900194@ NOTE #Générale#Descendant du general gallo-romain Flavius Afranius
1 CONT Syagrius 430 - 486
1 CONT Profession : Noble de Dijon
1 CONT
1 CONT #Générale#Profession : Roi des Francs de 629 à 639
1 CONT
1 CONT crémation :        610
1 CONT
1 CONT #Générale#Roi de France en 629
1 CONT  Il est aussi connu sous le nom de Dagobert Ier Mérovingiens.
1 CONT  Il est aussi connu sous le nom de Roi Dagobert Ier d' Austrasie des Francs 1 .
1 CONT  Il est aussi connu sous le nom de Roi Dagobert Ier de Bourgogne 1 .
1 CONT  Il est aussi connu sous le nom de Roi Dagobert Ier de Neustrie 1 .
1 CONT  Il est couronné roi d'Austrasie en 0623.
1 CONT  Il est couronné roi de Neustrie et Bourgogne en 0629.
1 CONT  Il est couronné roi des Francs en 0632.
1 CONT  Il est inhumé à abbaye de Saint-Denis, France.
0 @N6000000002240782524@ NOTE He was the last of the Mervoingian dynasty to rule a realm united in more
1 CONT than name only. He moved his capital from Austrasia to Paris, a central
1 CONT location from which the kingdom could be governed more efficiently. Famed for
1 CONT his love of justice, he was nevertheless greedy and dissolute. The prosperity
1 CONT of his reign, and the revival of the arts during this period, can be judged
1 CONT by the rich contents of the tombs of the period and from the goldsmith's work
1 CONT at churches. He encouraged learning & founded Saint-Denis abbey.
0 @N6000000001355550421@ NOTE [blended.FTW]
1 CONT [mergebase.FTW]
1 CONT King of Austrasia 623-632
1 CONT King of the Franks 629-639
1 CONT In 623, Dagobert's father, Chlotar II, King of the Franks, made him king of Austrasia to please the leading Austrasian nobles: Mayor of thePalace Pepin I and Saint Arnulf, Bishop of Metz. When Chlotar died in629, Dagobert became s
1 CONC ole King of the Franks, and he moved his capital from Austrasia to Paris. Later, Dagobert left the council of Pepin for a more flexible Neustrian Mayor of the Palace. In 632, he was forced to put his three-year old son Sigebert on
1 CONC  the throne of Austrasia as the nobles were in revolt, however Pepin was not made his Mayor of the Palace. The Neustrian nobles then wished to unite with Burgundy, and so they urged Dagobert to put his son Clovis II as king of bot
1 CONC h those kingdoms, although he was only 5 years old and could be easily manipulated by the nobles. When Dagobert died in 639, the nobles of the kingdoms controlled both his sons, now puppet kings.
1 CONT [Fix.FTW]
